(window.KG_PARTS = window.KG_PARTS || []).push({"group":"kilkanakku_akam","chapters":{"aa:1":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":1,"title":"முல்லை payiram-10","section":"முல்லை","theme":"The mullai fifty's first decade: after the prefatory verse, the friend and the waiting wife read the rainy season, its cloud, thunder, jasmine and the herdsman's evening flute, as his promised return or as an assault on her alone (1-9); then the husband, returning, hurries his chariot to see her waiting (10)."},"aa:2":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":2,"title":"குறிஞ்சி 11-20","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Kurinji: the secret love of the millet field on the hill, from the sweet days of guarding and the hunter's pretext (11-15) to the end of the guarding and the dangers of his night path, which the friend turns into a plea for marriage (12, 16-19), and the veri rite the mother holds because no one knows the cause (20)."},"aa:3":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":3,"title":"மருதம் 21-30","section":"மருதம்","theme":"Marutham: the wife turns away the bard who pleads for her straying husband (21-23), then tells her friend of her anger, her faded beauty, the son his father passed by on the way to a new wedding, and the union she still finds sweet (24-30)."},"aa:4":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":4,"title":"பாலை 31-40","section":"பாலை","theme":"The paalai decade of separation: the woman wasting at the sight of spring (31) and fearing the road of robbers, ghouls and hero-stones (34, 35); the friend's comfort through beasts that care for their mates on that road (32, 36, 38); the mother doubting the eloping girl who has forgotten her toys and faltered at play (33, 37); and the man holding back, telling his heart of her sobs and of tomorrow's lonely bed (39, 40)."},"aa:5":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":5,"title":"நெய்தல் 41-50","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Neythal: on the sea shore the heroine grieves the shore lord's absence and the doubtful worth of his secret oath and signs (41-45, 49, 50), the friend presses him to marry by appeal to what is fitting and to his name (46, 48), and the hero is struck by the fisher girl guarding the drying catch (47)."},"ae:1":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":1,"title":"குறிஞ்சி 0-14","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Kurinji: the invocation to the elephant god (0), then the hill lovers' secret love, the friend's pleas to the man not to forget and to marry (1, 5, 7, 11), the girl's trust in his pledge (2, 4, 8, 9, 14), her care for him on the night path (10), the friend shielding her from the mother and the velan's rite (6, 12), and the coming that ends the mother's harrying (13)."},"ae:2":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":2,"title":"முல்லை 15-28","section":"முல்லை","theme":"Mullai: the wife waits through the rainy season; the evening, the thunder, the peacocks and the homecoming herdsmen each turn the promised season against her (15-24, 27-28)."},"ae:3":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":3,"title":"பாலை 29-42","section":"பாலை","theme":"Paalai: the road of separation, tigers, robbers, cairns and hero-stones, seen from home; the mother of an eloped girl (30), the friend before the man leaves (31, 36), the heroine's reproaches and fears (32-40), and omens of his return (41, 42)."},"ae:4":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":4,"title":"மருதம் 43-56","section":"மருதம்","theme":"Marutham: the wife refuses the husband's bard and his excuses, mocking the man who comes in behind their son and sending the go-between back to the other women's street (45, 47-51, 56), laments his false oath (44), and the friend and wife lay the blame on him for his straying, his withheld regard and the 'manliness' he claims (46, 52-55)."},"ae:5":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":5,"title":"நெய்தல் 57-68","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Neythal: on the sea shore the heroine grieves the shore lord's absence and coldness, turning to the sea, the heron, the stork and the anril as fellow sufferers, witnesses or messengers (57, 60, 62-66), presses through her friend for marriage as her mother grows suspicious (58), waits for his chariot (59), is overwhelmed at the sight of him (67), and the friend reassures her of his love (61) and reproaches him with the paired crab (68)."},"ki:1":{"book":"kainnilai","num":1,"title":"குறிஞ்சி 1-12","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Kurinji in the secret love: the girl's pallor, slipping bangles and wasting shoulders tell of the hill lord (1, 5, 7), her heart goes to him or pines bound by longing (3, 6), she wonders whether he will come or has turned from her (2, 9) and is sure he will come at midnight (10), while the friend comforts her (4), names him as the only cure (8), notes the house that keeps her (11) and warns of an angry kinsman's spear (12)."},"ki:2":{"book":"kainnilai","num":2,"title":"பாலை 13-24","section":"பாலை","theme":"Paalai: the man's journey through the hot wasteland seen from home; the report of his going melts her eyes (13, 14), the road's killers and beasts make her fear for him (16, 23), omens of lizard and forehead promise his return (18, 19), and her heart follows him while the friend turns its swimming into his homecoming (21, 22). Five verses (14 to 17, 20) survive only in pieces."},"ki:3":{"book":"kainnilai","num":3,"title":"முல்லை 25-36","section":"முல்லை","theme":"The mullai section of the Kainnilai (25-36, Pullankadanar), much of it lost in the manuscripts: in the rains the friend shows the waiting heroine the mullai's teeth, the red thonri, the stag with his doe and the plumed horses he will yoke as proof he will come; the heroine's evening, with the cowherds entering the common and blowing their pipes, brings pallor; in 34 he has come."},"ki:4":{"book":"kainnilai","num":4,"title":"மருதம் 37-48","section":"மருதம்","theme":"The marutham decade of the wife against the bard: she bars the go-between and the man (37, 38, 46, 47), mocks the husband as a dancer worked by others (42), sets the son as her own against the bard's praise and against the man's use of the boy to gain entry (39, 41), weighs her age against his old promises (45, 48), and hears the other women's boasts and the town's gossip of his affair (40, 43, 44)."},"ki:5":{"book":"kainnilai","num":5,"title":"நெய்தல் 49-60","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Neythal on the Pandya's shore: the heroine wastes in the shore lord's absence (49, 53, 58), turns from him to the stork that saw his oath (50), sends his envoy-bird away (51), recalls the drying-ground where he lied and took her beauty (54, 56), faces the mother's confinement that ends the night meetings (52, 59), is warned her bangles will slip if he leaves unembraced (55), and sees her pallor lift as his chariot comes (60)."},"kv:1":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":1,"title":"களவழி நாற்பது 1-20","section":"களவழி நாற்பது","theme":"The first twenty stanzas of Poigaiyar's Kalavazhi Narpathu: each paints one sight of the field where the Chola Sengan killed his enemies, elephants, blood, broken drums and scavengers, each in a single simile drawn mostly from hills, sea, rain and the farmer's tank, and each closes on the same refrain of the field where he killed."},"kv:2":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":2,"title":"களவழி நாற்பது 21-41","section":"களவழி நாற்பது","theme":"Poykaiyar's battlefield of the Chola Senganan, the red-eyed wrathful Maal, lord of the Kaveri's watered land: each poem fixes one sight of the field in a simile, mostly of falling elephants (sinking mountains, snakes struck by thunder, a god's ploughing), with severed limbs, umbrellas and drums, the wailing kin, the taking of Kalumalam and the killing of the Vanji king, and each ends on the field where he killed his foes."},"kn:1":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":1,"title":"முல்லை 1-20 (1/2)","section":"முல்லை","theme":"The friend shows the waiting wife the rainy season he promised to return by, reading cloud, lightning, thunder and the flowers of the mullai woodland as proof that he will come (1-18); then the man, his king's work done, turns home (19-20)."},"kn:2":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":2,"title":"முல்லை 21-40 (2/2)","section":"முல்லை","theme":"The second half of the rains: the man, his work done, tells his charioteer and his heart that the flowering woodland calls him home (21, 22, 24, 28-33, 36), while the friend alternately shows the waiting wife the season as proof he will come and lets her own distress, or his broken word, show (23, 25-27, 34, 35, 37-40)."},"tn:1":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":1,"title":"குறிஞ்சி 1-16 (1/2)","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Kurinji of the secret love in Kanimedaviyar's quatrains: the hunter's pretext question at the millet field and his confession of it (1, 8, 9), the girl seen on the platform (3, 4), the friend forbidding the night path for its elephants, tigers, snakes and gods (6, 7, 10, 11, 13), the ripening millet that ends the meetings (5), the friend's disclosure of the elephant and flood rescues to the household (2, 12, 14), her plea against a bride price (15), and the lover's madal (16)."},"tn:2":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":2,"title":"குறிஞ்சி 17-31 (2/2)","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"The kurinji courtship's second half: the man's pleas, in which the girl's eyes are spears and arrows and her family hunters and clearers of the hill (17, 19, 21-23, 30); the friend's refusals and warnings, of leaf-garment and night visit, with the vengai that is both gold-flowered tree and tiger (20, 24-26, 28); and the end of the field-watch, the harvest, the mother's guard and the push to marry (18, 27, 29, 31)."},"tn:3":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":3,"title":"நெய்தல் 32-47 (1/2)","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Neythal in the time of the secret love: the man is struck at first sight by the fisher's daughter guarding drying fish (32, 33, 45, 47); the friend bars the night shore of sharks and wave-elephants, closes the day to gossip and presses for marriage (34-37, 43, 44, 46); the girl laments to the sleepless sea, to the crab and her own melting heart once he has gone (38, 40-42)."},"tn:4":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":4,"title":"நெய்தல் 48-62 (2/2)","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"The second half of the neythal section: the friend refuses the shore lord's night visits because the pearls light the village, the tide rises and both day and night carry danger (48, 49, 55, 59), turns the shore into a marshalled royal army to press for a fixed wedding day (50, 52-54), points out the grove and the house where they may meet (56, 58, 61), while the hero names the fisher girl who has maddened him (60), the heroine refuses him (57) and the friend praises the man who once helped them (62)."},"tn:5":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":5,"title":"பாலை 63-92","section":"பாலை","theme":"Paalai: the dry spring of murukku, kongam and pungu that marks the season of return (63-67, 79-80); the man who cannot leave for her glance (68); the friend's and the heroine's arguments for and against going for wealth, from manly duty to youth that will not come back (82-87, 91-92); and the eloped girl seen on the road by villagers and travellers, and mourned and sought by her mother among trees and diviners (65, 69-73, 75, 81, 88-90)."},"tn:6":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":6,"title":"முல்லை 93-108 (1/2)","section":"முல்லை","theme":"The first half of Kanimethaviyar's mullai: the rains and the evening come and the man does not. Clouds that drank the sea, the konrai and the kodal announce the season (93, 98, 104, 107), the evening and moonlight wound (94, 96, 97), the woman counters her friend's counsel that the season is false or that she should bear it (99, 103, 106), and the paired deer and clinging creepers set off her loneliness (102, 105)."},"tn:7":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":7,"title":"முல்லை 109-123 (2/2)","section":"முல்லை","theme":"Mullai in the rains, second half: the wife waits as the konrai, kodal, kurundham and jasmine bloom and the thunder rolls, refuses the friend's plea that it is not yet the season (118), counts the kodal's whole life passing (119), finds every sight and sound an enemy (121, 122), while the friend urges readiness and reproaches the flowers (112, 116); the last poem turns to the rival women, burned in this life for scorning her (123)."},"tn:8":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":8,"title":"மருதம் 124-139 (1/2)","section":"மருதம்","theme":"The first half of the marutham section: the wife refusing the bard who comes as the straying man's go-between (124 to 127, 131 to 133, 136), mocking his lute, his modes and his lies; the man's field town, its paddy and lotus likened to a dancing or bathing woman's jewels, as the place she will not enter (128, 129); her doubt at his return (134) and wonder at his servitude to another woman (135); and the field town described as the father's town of a little boy with a toy chariot (124, 125, 137 to 139)."},"tn:9":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":9,"title":"மருதம் 140-payiram (2/2)","section":"மருதம்","theme":"Marutham, the close of the book (140-153) and its preface: the husband's straying and the wife's sulk, told through the field buffalo raiding the pond, the bard turned away, the son carried home as a passport, and the eyes that never see him in any phase of the quarrel."},"ti:1":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":1,"title":"குறிஞ்சி 1-10","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Kurinji in the time of the secret love: the friend forbids the night path and the field, naming the elephants, the armed kin and the town's gossip (1, 5-8), while the heroine, pale and with loosening bangles, waits for the hill lord to come, until the harvest ends and her mother keeps her from the field (2-4, 9, 10)."},"ti:2":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":2,"title":"பாலை 11-20","section":"பாலை","theme":"The paalai decade of Kannan Senthanar: the man leaves for wealth across a waste of fire, stone, bowmen and vultures (11, 12, 15, 17, 18, 19), the woman watches her bangles and colour fail while the season he swore by comes (13, 14), and the lovers elope, the man praising the road and the mother fearing it (16, 20)."},"ti:3":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":3,"title":"முல்லை 21-30","section":"முல்லை","theme":"The mullai decade of the Thinaimozhi Aimpathu (21-30, Kannan Chenthanar): in the rainy season the friend points the waiting heroine to the season's signs (the kaayaa, kodal and mullai in bloom, the thunder like kings' drums, the cloud drinking the sea, the deer with his doe, the herds dripping milk) as the token the absent man set for his return, and promises that her pallor will go; in 21 he has already come."},"ti:4":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":4,"title":"மருதம் 31-40","section":"மருதம்","theme":"Marutham: the wife and her friend answer the straying husband of the field town and refuse his bard (31-39), measuring what his love gave and took through the lily, the bee and the crushed cane; the last verse names a girl of the town by her family's flowers (40)."},"ti:5":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":5,"title":"நெய்தல் 41-50","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Neythal: the heroine and her friend on the shore of backwaters and screw-pine, complaining of trysts that proved false and of pallor and slipping bangles, pressing the sea lord to come and marry (41-48, 50); and the man's first-sight doubt whether the girl of the njazhal grove is peacock, woman or sea goddess (49)."}},"verses":{"aa:payiram":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":1,"t":["பண்புள்ளி நின்ற பெரியார் பயன்தெரிய","வண்புள்ளி மாறன் பொறையன் புணர்த்தியாத்த","ஐந்திணை ஐம்பதும் ஆர்வத்தின் ஓதாதார்","செந்தமிழ் சேரா தவர்"],"p":"","r":"The prefatory verse says that Maran Poraiyan of generous fame bound together the Ainthinai Aimpathu so that great men of good character might see its worth, and that those who do not recite it with eagerness have not attained pure Tamil.","n":"It praises a book, not a king, and makes the fifty poems a test of belonging to செந்தமிழ்: the five landscapes are offered as the curriculum of correct Tamil, not only as love poetry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","kalvi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu payiram","lb":"AApayiram","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:1"},"aa:1":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":2,"t":["மல்லர்க் கடந்தான் நிறம்போல் திரண்டெழுந்து","செல்வக் கடம்பமார்ந்தான் வேல்மின்னி - நல்லாய்","இயங்கெயில் எய்தவன தார்பூப்ப ஈதோ","மயங்கி வலனேருங் கார்"],"p":"","r":"Good lady, the friend says, see: the rain cloud has gathered, massed and dark like the colour of Mal who overcame the wrestlers, flashing with lightning that is the spear of Murugan who wears the kadamba, making the konrai bloom as the garland of Shiva who shot the moving forts, and it rises turning to the right.","n":"Three gods each lend the storm one attribute: Mal, god of the mullai, its colour; Murugan, god of the hills, its lightning; Shiva its konrai. The cloud rising on the right (வலன் ஏரும்) is the auspicious omen, so the friend shows the season as a sacred sign of his return rather than only as weather.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 1","lb":"AA1","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மல்லர்க் கடந்தான்","மால்",1,"deyvam","simile","Mal"],["கடம்பமார்ந்தான்","முருகன்",2,"deyvam","simile","Murugan"],["எய்தவன","சிவன்",3,"deyvam","simile","Shiva"],["தார்","தார்",3,"maram","simile","garland (konrai)"],["கார்","கார்",4,"pira","scene","rain cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:1"},"aa:2":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":3,"t":["அணிநிற மஞ்ஞை அகவ இரங்கி","மணிநிற மாமலைமேல் தாழ்ந்து - பணிமொழி!","கார்நீர்மை கொண்ட கலிவானம் காண்தொறும்","பீர்நீர்மை கொண்டன தோள்"],"p":"","r":"As the beautiful peacock calls, the roaring sky, which has taken on the nature of rain, thunders and hangs low over the sapphire-dark mountain; every time she sees it, the heroine tells her soft-spoken friend, her shoulders take on the yellow of the peerkku flower.","n":"The two halves are built on the same phrase: the sky takes on the nature of rain (கார்நீர்மை கொண்ட) and her shoulders take on the nature of the peerkku (பீர்நீர்மை கொண்டன), so the season's fulfilment and her pallor arrive as one event. The speaker could also be the friend observing the heroine's shoulders; the vocative பணிமொழி fits either.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","paruvam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 2","lb":"AA2","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",1,"pul","scene","peacock"],["கலிவானம்","வானம்",3,"pira","scene","sky"],["பீர்நீர்மை","பீர்",4,"maram","scene","ridge-gourd flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:1"},"aa:3":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":4,"t":["மின்னும் முழக்கும் இடியும்மற் றின்ன","கொலைப்படை சாலப் பரப்பிய முல்லை","முகைவென்ற பல்லினாய்! இல்லையோ? மற்று","நமர்சென்ற நாட்டுள்இக் கார்"],"p":"","r":"Lightning, thunder and the thunderbolt, such killing weapons this rain has spread wide; she asks her friend, whose teeth outshine jasmine buds, whether there is no such rain in the land where their man has gone.","n":"The question is an accusation turned inside out: if the rain were in his land too, he would have read it and come, so either it is not there or he has not heeded it. The season meant to be his signal falls on her alone as an attacking army.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 3","lb":"AA3","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","scene","jasmine bud"],["கார்","கார்",4,"pira","scene","rain cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:1"},"aa:4":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":5,"t":["உள்ளார்கொல் காதலர் ஒண்டொடி நம்திறம்","வள்வார் முரசின் குரல்போல் இடித்துரறி","நல்லார் மனங்கவரத் தோன்றிப் பணிமொழியைக்","கொல்வாங்குக் கூர்ந்த(து)இக் கார்"],"p":"","r":"Does our lover not think of how it is with us, she asks her bright-bangled friend: this rain, roaring like the voice of a strapped war-drum and appearing so as to seize the hearts of good women, has grown keen as if to kill the soft-spoken one.","n":"The war-drum makes the thunder a summons to battle aimed at women (நல்லார் மனங்கவர), and the soft-spoken one it would kill is the speaker herself named in the third person; it may instead be the friend speaking of the heroine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 4","lb":"AA4","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முரசின்","முரசு",2,"parai","simile","drum"],["கார்","கார்",4,"pira","scene","rain cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:1"},"aa:5":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":6,"t":["கோடுயர் தோற்றம் மலைமேல் இருங்கொண்மூக்","கூடி நிரந்து தலைபிணங்கி - ஓடி","வளிகலந்து வந்துறைக்கும் வானம்காண் தோறும்","துளிகலந்து வீழ்தருங் கண்"],"p":"","r":"Each time she sees the sky, where dark clouds gather on the high-peaked mountain, spread in rows with their heads tangling, run on mingled with the wind and pour down, her eyes fall too, mingled with drops.","n":"The verbs carry over: what the clouds do in three lines (கலந்து, வந்து உறைக்கும்) her eyes do in one (துளிகலந்து வீழ்தரும்), so the poem never says 'I weep like the rain' but makes her tears another fall of the same storm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","mazhai","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 5","lb":"AA5","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["இருங்கொண்மூக்","கொண்மூ",1,"pira","scene","rain clouds"],["வளிகலந்து","வளி",3,"pira","scene","wind"],["துளிகலந்து","துளி",4,"pira","scene","drops"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:1"},"aa:6":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":7,"t":["முல்லை நறுமலர் ஊதி இருந்தும்பி","செல்சார் வுடையார்க் கினியவாய் - நல்லாய்மற்(று)","யாரும்இல் நெஞ்சினேம் ஆகி யுறைவேமை","ஈரும் இருள்மாலை வந்து"],"p":"","r":"The dark bee humming after sipping the fragrant jasmine is sweet to those who have someone to lean on, she tells her good friend, but on us, who live with hearts that have no one, the dark evening comes and saws.","n":"The same hour is sweet or cruel by company alone (செல்சார்வு உடையார்க்கு), so the evening's cruelty is in her solitude. The bee at the jasmine is a near miss for iraicchi: it acts like a lover, but the poem itself states the contrast, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 6","lb":"AA6","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",1,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["இருந்தும்பி","தும்பி",1,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:1"},"aa:7":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":8,"t":["தேரோன் மலைமறைந்த செக்கர்கொள் புன்மாலை","ஆர்ஆனபின் ஆயன் உவந்தூதும் - சீர்சால்","சிறுகுழல ஓசை செறிதொடி! வேல்கொண்(டு)","எறிவது போலும் எனக்கு"],"p":"","r":"In the dull evening when the sun has hidden behind the hill and the sky holds its red glow, the sound of the fine little flute the herdsman plays in joy once the cattle have fed, she tells her friend of close-set bangles, is to her like a spear thrown at her.","n":"The herdsman plays because he is glad (உவந்து ஊதும்) and his flute is called fine (சீர்சால்): the music is not harsh, it wounds because it announces a homecoming that is not hers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 7","lb":"AA7","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஆயன்","ஆயன்",2,"seythi","scene","herdsman"],["சிறுகுழல","குழல்",3,"yaazh","simile","flute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:1"},"aa:8":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":9,"t":["பிரிந்தவர் மேனிபோல் புல்லென்ற வள்ளி","பொருந்தினர் மேனிபோல் பொற்பத் - திருந்திழாய","வானம் பொழியவும் வாரார்கொல் இன்னா","கானம் கடந்துசென் றார்"],"p":"","r":"The valli creeper, dull like the bodies of those who are parted, has grown lovely like the bodies of those united; she asks her well-adorned friend whether, even though the sky now pours, the man who crossed the cruel forest will not come.","n":"The usual direction of the simile is reversed: human bodies are the vehicle and the plant the tenor. The creeper has passed from the parted state to the united one; she has not, so the plant shows her the change the season promised her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 8","lb":"AA8","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வள்ளி","வள்ளி",1,"maram","simile","valli creeper"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:1"},"aa:9":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":10,"t":["வருவர் வயங்கிழாய் வாள்ஒண்கண் நீர்கொண்(டு)","உருகி யுடன்றழிய வேண்டா - தெரிதியேல்","பைங்கொடி முல்லை அவிழ்அரும்(பு) ஈன்றன","வம்ப மறையுறக் கேட்டு"],"p":"","r":"He will come, the friend tells the heroine of shining jewels; she need not melt and waste away with her bright sword-like eyes full of tears: if she will look, the green-stemmed jasmine has put forth opening buds, having heard the secret of the new rain.","n":"The jasmine is said to have heard the secret (மறை) of the coming rain before anyone saw it, so the friend offers it as inside knowledge of the season. It is a near miss for iraicchi: the flower's answering of the season's message suggests the man will answer too, but it is offered as evidence, not as an act a person of the situation does, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 9","lb":"AA9","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",3,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:1"},"aa:10":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":11,"t":["நூல்நின்ற பாக! தேர் நொவ்விதாச் சென்றீக","தேன்நவின்ற கானத்து எழில்நோக்கித் - தான்நவின்ற","கற்புத்தாழ் வீழ்த்துக் கவுண்மிசைக் கையூன்றி","நிற்பாள் நிலையுணர்கம் யாம்"],"p":"","r":"Charioteer skilled by the rule, the returning man says, let the chariot go swiftly; looking at the beauty of the forest where bees hum, let us learn the state of her who stands with the bolt of her practised chastity drawn shut and her hand pressed to her cheek.","n":"Chastity is a door bar she has let fall, so her waiting is a house closed from within; the hand on the cheek is the posture of that waiting. He wants to see her before she sees him, to know her state as it is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","ninaivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 10","lb":"AA10","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தேன்நவின்ற","தேன்",2,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:1"},"aa:11":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":12,"t":["பொன்னிணர் வேங்கை சவினிய பூம்பொழிலுள்","நன்மலை நாடன் நலம்புனைய -மென்முலையாய்","போயின சின்னாள் புனத்து மறையினால்","ஏயினார் இன்றி இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The friend reminds the soft-breasted girl that the few days they spent hidden in the millet field, while the lord of the good hill dressed her beauty in the flowering grove of gold-clustered vengai, have gone by, sweet because no one was there to give them orders.","n":"போயின, 'they have gone', makes the memory a farewell: the secret days lasted only while the girls were sent to the field, and ஏயினார் இன்றி, with none to command, names the freedom that the return home will take away. The colophon gives no thurai; the speaker and addressee are inferred from மென்முலையாய் and the shared 'we'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","punarchi","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 11","lb":"AA11","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["பூம்பொழிலுள்","பொழில்",1,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:2"},"aa:12":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":13,"t":["மாலவரை வெற்ப! வணங்கு குரல்ஏனல்","காவல் இயற்கை ஒழிந்தேம்எம் - தூஅருவி","பூக்கண் கழூஉம் புறவிற்றாய்ப் பொன்விளையும்","பாக்கம் இதுஎம் இடம்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the great hill that their natural task of guarding the bending millet is over, and points him to their home: this village, where the clean waterfall washes the flowers on the slope and gold is found, is where they live.","n":"With the guarding over, the field can no longer be the meeting place; telling him where the house is, பாக்கம் இது எம் இடம், moves his courting from the field to the family's door, which is where marriage is asked. பாக்கம் and புறவு are usually words of the shore and the woodland; here they only name a hill village and its slope.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 12","lb":"AA12","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["அருவி","அருவி",2,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:2"},"aa:13":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":14,"t":["கானக நாடன் கலவான்என் தோளென்று","மானமர் கண்ணாய்! மயங்கல்நீ - நானம்","கலந்திழியும் நன்மலைமேல் வாலருவி யாடப்","புலம்பும் அகன்றுநில் லா"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the doe-eyed girl not to be bewildered by the thought that the man of the forest country will not come to embrace her shoulders: when they bathe in the white waterfall that comes down the good hill mingled with musk, her loneliness will not stay.","n":"The bathing place in the falls is a meeting place, so the comfort is practical: the loneliness will leave because he will be there. The water that comes 'mixed with musk' carries the scent of his hill down to her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 13","lb":"AA13","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மானமர்","மான்",2,"maa","scene","deer"],["நானம்","நானம்",2,"pira","scene","musk"],["வாலருவி","வால் அருவி",3,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:2"},"aa:14":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":15,"t":["புனைபூந் தழையல்குல் பொன்னன்னாய்! சாரல்","தினைகாத் திரும்தேம்யாம் ஆக - வினைவாய்த்து","மாவினவு வார்போல வந்தவர் நம்மாட்டுத்","தாம்வினவ லுற்றதொன் றுண்டு"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the gold-like girl in her leaf skirt that while they were guarding the millet on the slope the man came, like hunters who come asking after a beast, and that there is something else he has come to ask them.","n":"The friend names the hunter's question about the animal as a manner of coming (போல), so she has already seen through the pretext: his real question, ஒன்றுண்டு, is left unsaid for the girl to guess. வினைவாய்த்து in line 2 is hard to construe; the reading takes it with his coming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 14","lb":"AA14","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["தழை","தழை",1,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["தினை","தினை",2,"unavu","scene","millet"],["மா","மா",3,"maa","simile","game beast"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:2"},"aa:15":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":16,"t":["வேங்கை நறுமலர் வெற்பிடை யாங்கொய்து","மாந்தளிர் மேனி வியர்ப்பமற்(று) - ஆங்(கு) எனைத்தும்","பாய்ந்தருவி ஆடினே மாகப் பணிமொழிக்குச்","சேந்தனவாம் சேயரிக்கண் தாம்"],"p":"","r":"The friend explains that the red-lined eyes of the gentle-spoken girl have reddened because they plucked fragrant vengai flowers on the hill till their mango-shoot bodies sweated and then bathed again and again in the leaping waterfall.","n":"The poem speaks of the girl in the third person (பணிமொழிக்கு) and offers a cause for her red eyes, which suggests the friend is answering an elder who has noticed them; the bath in the falls is an innocent explanation for eyes reddened by love or weeping. The addressee is inferred: the colophon gives no thurai.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 15","lb":"AA15","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","ornament","kino flowers"],["மாந்தளிர்","மா தளிர்",2,"maram","scene","mango shoots"],["பாய்ந்தருவி","அருவி",3,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:2"},"aa:16":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":17,"t":["கொடுவரி வேங்கை பிழைத்துக் கோட் பட்டு","மடிசெவி வேழம் இரீஇ - அடியோசை","அஞ்சி ஒதுங்கும் அதருள்ளி ஆரிருள்","துஞ்சா சுடர்த்தொடி கண்"],"p":"","r":"Remembering the path where an elephant with folded ears, having escaped the curve-striped tiger, sits down hurt and shrinks aside in fear at the sound of footsteps, the girl with bright bangles cannot sleep through the thick dark.","n":"வேங்கை here is the tiger, not the tree. The elephant that has survived the tiger and still flinches at a footfall measures the path the man takes at night; a reader may hear the girl's own fear in it, but the beast only shows the danger, so it is scene, not iraicchi. Telling him she lies awake is a way of asking him to stop the night visits and marry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kan","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 16","lb":"AA16","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",2,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:2"},"aa:17":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":18,"t":["மஞ்சிவர் சோலை வளமலை நன்னாட!","எஞ்சாது நீவருதி யென்றெண்ணி - அஞ்சித்","திருஒடுங்கும் மென்சாயல் தேங்கோதை மாதர்","உருஒடுங்கும் உள்ளுருகி நின்று"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the rich hill with cloud-climbed groves that, thinking he will come without fail and afraid for him, the girl with the honeyed garland loses her bloom and soft grace and her body shrinks as she stands melting within.","n":"The wasting here comes not from his absence but from his coming: எஞ்சாது நீ வருதி, you come without fail, is what she fears. His faithfulness to the night meetings is itself the harm, so only marriage would end it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","kuri","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 17","lb":"AA17","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["மஞ்சிவர்","மஞ்சு",1,"pira","scene","cloud"],["சோலை","சோலை",1,"maram","scene","grove"],["கோதை","கோதை",3,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:2"},"aa:18":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":19,"t":["எறிந்தெமர் தாம்உழுத ஈர்ங்குரல் ஏனல்","மறந்தும் கிளியனமும் வாரா - கறங்கருவி","மாமலை நாட! மடமொழி தன்கேண்மை","நீமறவல் நெஞ்சத்துக் கொண்டு"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the great hill with roaring falls that now not even the parrot flocks come, even by mistake, to the moist-eared millet that their kin cleared and ploughed, and asks him not to forget, but to keep in his heart, his bond with the gentle-spoken girl.","n":"மறந்தும், 'even by forgetting', and நீ மறவல், 'do not forget', answer each other across the poem. With no parrots to chase, the girls will not be sent to the field, so the meetings there end; the friend asks that the bond outlast the field.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","ninaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 18","lb":"AA18","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கிளி","கிளி",2,"pul","iraicchi","parrot"],["கறங்கருவி","கறங்கு அருவி",2,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கிளி","not_confirmed","0/2","the man, whose meetings in the field are ending too, must not stop coming and forget her as the parrots forget the field"]],"ch":"aa:2"},"aa:19":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":20,"t":["நெடுமலை நன்னாட! நீள்வேல் துணையாக","கடுவிசை வாலருவி நீந்தி - நடுஇருள்","இன்னா அதர்வர ஈர்ங்கோதை மாதராள்","என்னாவாள் என்னும்என் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the tall hill that when he comes at midnight along the cruel path, with only his long spear for company, swimming across the fierce white waterfall, her heart asks what will become of the girl with the cool garland.","n":"The friend does not say she fears for him: her heart asks what will become of the girl if harm meets him. His danger is counted as the girl's, which is the friend's way of pressing for marriage without naming it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","nenju","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 19","lb":"AA19","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வாலருவி","வால் அருவி",2,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["கோதை","கோதை",3,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:2"},"aa:20":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":21,"t":["வெறிகமழ் வெற்பன்என் மெய்ந்நீர்மை கொண்ட(து)","அறியான்மற்(று) அன்னோ! அணங்(கு) அணங்கிற்(று) என்று","மறிஈர்த்(து) உதிரம்தூய் வேலன் தரீஇ","வெறியோ(டு) அலம்வரும் யாய்"],"p":"","r":"The girl says the man of the fragrant hill has taken her body's true nature, but the velan, not knowing this, declares that a spirit has seized her, drags a kid and scatters its blood, and her mother, having brought him, whirls distraught in the veri rite.","n":"அறியான் is masculine: the one who does not know is the velan, whose whole office is to know the cause. அணங்கு அணங்கிற்று, 'a spirit has seized her', is true in a way he cannot see: the afflicting beauty is the hill man's. Spoken to the friend, the complaint is meant to reach the man and hurry the marriage, though the colophon gives no thurai to say he overhears.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","anangu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 20","lb":"AA20","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["அணங்கு","அணங்கு",2,"deyvam","scene","afflicting spirit"],["மறி","மறி",3,"maa","scene","goat kid"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",3,"seythi","scene","diviner"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:2"},"aa:21":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":22,"t":["கொண்டுழிப் பண்டம் விலையொரீஇக் கொல்சேரி","நுண்துளைத் துன்னூ விற்பாரின் - ஒன்றானும்","வேறல்லை பாண! வியலூரன் வாய்மொழியைத்","தேற எமக்குரைப்பாய் நீ"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the bard that in pressing her to trust the word of the man of the wide town he is no different from those who hawk fine-eyed needles in the smiths' own street, taking any goods offered for them.","n":"The smiths' street is where needles are made, so selling them there is the proverbial wasted errand: she knows the man's word better than any go-between can describe it. The bard is mocked, not answered.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","oodal","vaaimai","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 21","lb":"AA21","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["கொல்சேரி","கொல் சேரி",1,"seythi","simile","smiths' street"],["துன்னூ","துன்னூசி",2,"seythi","simile","needle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:3"},"aa:22":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":23,"t":["போதார்வண்(டு) ஊதும் புனல்வயல் ஊரற்குத்","தூதாய்த் திரிதரும் பாண்மகனே - நீதான்","அறி(வு)அயர்ந்(து) எம்இல்லுள் என்செய்ய வந்தாய்","நெறிஅதுகாண் எங்கையர் இற்கு"],"p":"","r":"The wife asks the bard who roams as envoy for the man of the watered fields where bees hum at the blossoms what he has come into her house to do, out of his wits, and points him to the road to her 'younger sisters'' houses.","n":"எங்கையர், 'our younger sisters', is the wife's barbed courtesy for the other women. The bee that moves among blossoms is only the town's scene here; read as the husband moving among women it would make one match, short of a hidden comparison.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 22","lb":"AA22","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["போதார்வண்(டு)","வண்டு",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["போதார்வண்(டு)","போது",1,"maram","scene","blossom"],["பாண்மகனே","பாண்மகன்",2,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"aa:3"},"aa:23":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":24,"t":["யாணர் அகல்வயல் ஊரன் அருளுதல்","பாண! பரிந்துரைக்க வேண்டுமோ - மாண","அறிவ(து) அறியும் அறிவினார் கேண்மை","நெறியே உரையாதோ மற்று"],"p":"","r":"The wife asks the bard whether the grace of the man of the wide, rich fields needs to be pleaded for, since the bond of those who know what ought to be known will itself show the way.","n":"The argument turns the bard's office against itself: if the man felt the bond, no envoy would be needed; that an envoy comes proves the bond is missing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","natpu","arul","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 23","lb":"AA23","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாண","பாணன்",2,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:3"},"aa:24":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":25,"t":["கோலச் சறுகுருகின் குத்(து)அஞ்சி ஈர்வாளை","நீலத்துப் புக்கொளிக்கும் ஊரற்கு - மேல்எலாம்","சார்தற்குச் சந்தனச்சாந்(து) ஆயினேம் இப்பருவம்","காரத்தின் வெய்யஎம் தோள்"],"p":"","r":"For the man of the town where the vaalai fish, afraid of the stab of the handsome little heron, hides among the blue lilies, our shoulders were once sandal paste to lean on; in this season they are hotter than caustic.","n":"The fish fleeing the heron's beak into the lilies maps onto the husband who, dreading the wife's sharp words, hides in other women's houses; her shoulders, once his cooling sandal, now burn him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 24","lb":"AA24","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சறுகுருகின்","சிறு குருகு",1,"pul","ullurai","heron"],["ஈர்வாளை","வாளை",1,"pira","ullurai","vaalai fish"],["நீலத்துப்","நீலம்",2,"maram","ullurai","blue lily"],["சந்தனச்சாந்(து)","சந்தனம்",3,"maram","simile","sandal paste"],["காரத்தின்","காரம்",4,"pira","simile","caustic"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","சறுகுருகின் ஈர்வாளை நீலத்துப்","confirmed","1/1","the husband who keeps to other women's houses for fear of his wife's anger"]],"ch":"aa:3"},"aa:25":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":26,"t":["அழல்அவிழ் தாமரை ஆய்வய லூரன்","விழைதரு மார்பம் உறுநோய் - விழையின்","குழலும் குடுமிஎன் பாலகன் கூறும்","மழலைவாய் கட்டுரை யால்"],"p":"","r":"Should the pain of longing for the desirable chest of the man of the fine-field town, where lotuses open like flame, come upon her, the wife says it is eased by the earnest prattle of her little son with the curling tuft.","n":"The sentence leaves its verb unsaid, so the boy's chatter may either console her without the man or soften her toward him; either way the child, not the bard, is the go-between who works.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","oodal","kaamanoy","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 25","lb":"AA25","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","scene","lotus"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:3"},"aa:26":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":27,"t":["பெய்வளைக் கையாய்! பெருநகை ஆகின்ற","செய்வய லூரன் வதுவை விழ(வு)இயம்பக்","கைபுனை தேரேறிச் செல்வானைச் சென்றிவன்","எய்தி இடருற்ற வாறு"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her bangled friend to see what happened when, as the drums of the man's new wedding, already the town's great joke, were sounding, this little one ran after him mounting his decorated chariot, reached him and came to grief.","n":"இவன், 'this one', is most naturally the son (as in 28), turned away at the chariot by a father bound for another bride; it could also be the bard, who went to the wedding and was rebuffed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","makkal","alar","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 26","lb":"AA26","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["பெய்வளைக்","வளை",1,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:3"},"aa:27":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":28,"t":["தணவயல் ஊரன் புலக்கும் தகையமோ","நுண்ணுறல் போல நுணங்கிய ஐங்கூந்தல்","வெண்மரல் போல நிறந்திரிந்து வேறாய","வண்ணம் உடையேம்மற்(று) யாம்"],"p":"","r":"The wife asks whether she is still the kind the man of the cool fields would quarrel with, now that her five-part hair has thinned like fine grains and her skin has turned pale like the white marul fibre, changed in colour.","n":"To sulk at someone is to still want her; she says she has faded past even being worth his anger, so his absence is not oodal but neglect.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nalam","pasalai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 27","lb":"AA27","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["வெண்மரல்","மரல்",3,"maram","simile","marul fibre"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:3"},"aa:28":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":29,"t":["ஒல்லென்(று) ஒலிக்கும் ஒலிபுனல் ஊரற்கு","வல்லென்(று) என்நெஞ்சம் வாட்கண்ணாய் - நில்லென்னா(து)","ஏக்கற்றாங்(கு) என்மகன் தான்நிற்ப என்னானும்","நோக்கான்தேர் ஊர்ந்து கண்டு"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her sword-eyed friend that her heart has hardened against the man of the town of loud-sounding waters, because, while her son stood there longing, he did not tell the chariot to stop, saw him and drove on without a glance.","n":"The charge is precise: he saw (கண்டு) and did not look (நோக்கான்). The son's mute yearning (ஏக்கற்று) is the appeal that fails, and her hardening follows from his hardness to the child, not to her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kannottam","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 28","lb":"AA28","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["ஒலிபுனல்","புனல்",1,"pira","scene","water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:3"},"aa:29":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":30,"t":["ஒல்லென் ஒலிபுனல் ஊரன் வியன்மார்பம்","புல்லேன்யான் என்பேன் புனையிழையாய்! - புல்லேன்","எனக்கோர் குறிப்பும் உடையனோ ஊரன்","தனக்(கு)ஏவல் செய்தொழுகு வேன்"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend that she would say 'I will not embrace his wide chest', but does the man of the loud waters care at all that she refuses? She will go on doing his bidding.","n":"The refusal is withdrawn not from desire but because it has no audience: oodal is a weapon only against someone who notices it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","orutalai","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 29","lb":"AA29","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["ஒலிபுனல்","புனல்",1,"pira","scene","water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:3"},"aa:30":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":31,"t":["குளிரும் பருவத்தே யாயினும் தென்றல்","வளியெறியின் மெய்யிற்(கு) இனிதாம் - ஒளியிழாய்!","ஊடி யிருப்பினும் ஊரன் நறுமேனி","கூடல் இனிதாம் எனக்கு"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her bright-jewelled friend that as the south wind is sweet to the body when it blows even in the cold season, so union with the man's fragrant body is sweet to her even while she sulks.","n":"The comparison is pointed: the south wind belongs to a warm season, so its sweetness out of season is like a union that pleases against her own quarrel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 30","lb":"AA30","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["தென்றல்","தென்றல்",1,"pira","scene","south wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:3"},"aa:31":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":32,"t":["உதிரம் துவரிய வேங்கை உகிர்போல்","எதிரி முருக்கரும்ப வீர்ந்தண்கார் நீங்க - எதிருநர்க்(கு)","இன்பம் பயந்த இளவேனில் காண்தொறும்","துன்பம் கலந்தழியும் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"","r":"Each time she sees the early summer, when the murukku puts out buds like a tiger's claws dyed with blood and the cool rains have gone, a season that gives joy to lovers who are together, the woman says her heart, mixed with grief, wastes away.","n":"The poem names its own contrast: the season brings joy to those who meet (எதிருநர்க்கு), so her grief is measured against the pleasure the same sight gives the united. The blood-dyed claw turns the spring flower into an image of a wound.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 31","lb":"AA31","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maa","simile","tiger"],["முருக்கரும்ப","முருக்கு",2,"maram","simile","palasa"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:4"},"aa:32":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":33,"t":["விலங்கல் விளங்கிழாய்! செல்லாரோ வல்லர்","அழற்பட்(டு) அசைந்த பிடியை - எழிற்களிறு","கற்சுனைச் சேற்றிடைச் சின்னீரைக் கையாற்கொண்(டு)","உச்சி ஒழுக்கஞ் சுரம்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the bright-jewelled woman that the man is able to go across the wasteland where a handsome tusker takes up in his trunk the little water in the mud of a rock pool and pours it over the head of his mate, worn out by the heat.","n":"Line 1 is hard to construe, so whether the friend means 'he is strong enough to go' or 'will he not come back?' is open; either way the tusker's care is what she offers as her argument.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 32","lb":"AA32","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எழிற்களிறு","களிறு",2,"maa","iraicchi","tusker"],["பிடியை","பிடி",2,"maa","iraicchi","cow elephant"],["கற்சுனைச்","சுனை",3,"pira","scene","rock pool"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","எழிற்களிறு","confirmed","1/1","the man who passes such care on his road will be moved to the same care for the wife he left"]],"ch":"aa:4"},"aa:33":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":34,"t":["பாவையும் பந்தும் பவளவாய்ப் பைங்கிளியும்","ஆயமும் ஒன்றும் இவைநினையாள் - பால்போலும்","ஆய்ந்த மொழியினாள் செல்லுங்கொல் காதலன்பின்","காய்ந்து கதிர்தெறூஉங் காடு"],"p":"","r":"The mother says the girl of chosen words sweet as milk no longer thinks of her doll, her ball, her green parrot with its coral beak or her playmates; will she go after her lover through the forest that the sun's rays scorch?","n":"The things she forgets are all of childhood, so their being forgotten is the sign that she has left it; the milk of her speech keeps her a child in her mother's words even as she goes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 33","lb":"AA33","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பைங்கிளியும்","கிளி",1,"pul","scene","parrot"],["பால்போலும்","பால்",2,"unavu","simile","milk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:4"},"aa:34":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":35,"t":["கோட்டமை வல்லில் கொலைபிரியா வன்கண்ணர்","ஆட்டிவிட்(டு) ஆறலைக்கும் அத்தம் பலநீந்தி","வேட்ட முனைவயின் சேறீரோ ஐய! நீர்","வாள்தடங்கண் மாதரை நீத்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man whether he will go, leaving the woman of wide sword-bright eyes, across many wastelands where hard-eyed men with curved strong bows, never parted from killing, harry and rob travellers, to the far place he longs for.","n":"The danger she names is his, not the woman's: the friend tries to hold him back by the robbers of the road rather than by the grief at home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 34","lb":"AA34","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["வன்கண்ணர்","வன்கண்ணர்",1,"seythi","scene","robbers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:4"},"aa:35":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":36,"t":["கொடுவில் எயினர்தம் கொல்படையால் வீழ்ந்த","தடிநிணம் மாந்திய பேஎய் - நடுகல்","விரிநிழல் கண்படுக்கும் வெங்கானம் என்பர்","பொருள்புரிந்தார் போய சுரம்"],"p":"","r":"The woman says that the wasteland the man bent on wealth has gone into is, people say, a hot forest where a ghoul, gorged on the cut fat of men felled by the killing weapons of the curved-bowed Eyinar, sleeps in the wide shade of a hero-stone.","n":"Everything on that road is at rest, the dead under the stone and the sated ghoul in its shade, while she has only report (என்பர்) to go on and no rest at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","nadukal","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 35","lb":"AA35","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["எயினர்தம்","எயினர்",1,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["தடிநிணம்","நிணம்",2,"unavu","gift_food","fat"],["பேஎய்","பேய்",2,"pira","scene","ghoul"]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:4"},"aa:36":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":37,"t":["கடி(து)ஓடும் வெண்தேரை நீராம்என்(று) எண்ணிப்","பிடியோ(டு) ஒருங்கோடித் தாள்பிணங்கி வீழும்","வெடியோடும் வெங்கானம் சேர்வார்கொல் நல்லாய்","தொடியோடி வீழத் துறந்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the good woman whether he will go to the hot forest of cracking heat, where an elephant takes the swift white mirage for water, runs to it together with his mate and falls with tangled legs, leaving her so that her bangles slip and fall.","n":"The mirage deceives the pair but does not part them: they run and fall together, which is the reproach the friend leaves unspoken against the man who goes alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 36","lb":"AA36","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வெண்தேரை","வெண்தேர்",1,"pira","scene","mirage"],["பிடியோ","பிடி",2,"maa","iraicchi","cow elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பிடியோ","confirmed","1/1","even beasts fooled by the waste do not part from their mates, yet the man leaves her"]],"ch":"aa:4"},"aa:37":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":38,"t":["தோழியர் சூழத் துறைமுன்றில் ஆடுங்கால்","வீழ்பவள் போலத் தளருங்கால் - தாழாது","கல்லதர் அத்தத்தைக் காதலன் பின்போதல்","வல்லவோ மாதர் நடை"],"p":"","r":"The mother asks whether the girl, who would falter as if about to fall when she played by the ford in front of the house with her friends around her, can now keep pace without lagging behind her lover on the stony paths of the wasteland.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 37","lb":"AA37","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:4"},"aa:38":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":39,"t":["சுனைவாய்ச் சிறுநீரை எய்தாதென் றெண்ணிப்","பிணைமான் இனிதுண்ண வேண்டிக் - கலைமாத்தன்","கள்ளத்தின் ஊச்சும் சுரமென்பர் காதலர்","உள்ளம் படர்ந்த நெறி"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that the road the lover's heart has taken is, people say, a wasteland where a stag, judging that the little water in the pool will not be enough, wants the doe to drink her fill and only pretends to drink.","n":"The stag's love is shown by a deceit (கள்ளத்தின் ஊச்சும்), and the poem sets it on the road the man's heart (உள்ளம்) has gone down, as if his heart had chosen the path where it would be taught tenderness. The words could also be the woman's own, said with the opposite edge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 38","lb":"AA38","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சுனைவாய்ச்","சுனை",1,"pira","scene","pool"],["பிணைமான்","பிணை மான்",2,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["கலைமா","கலை மா",2,"maa","iraicchi","stag"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கலைமா","confirmed","1/1","the man whose heart has gone that way will see such care and his heart will turn back to the wife he left"]],"ch":"aa:4"},"aa:39":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":40,"t":["மடவைகாண் நன்நெஞ்சே மாண்பொருள் மாட்டோ டப்","புடைபெயர் போழ்தத்து மாற்றாள் - படர்கூர்ந்து","விம்மி யுயிர்க்கும் விளங்கிழையாள் ஆற்றுமோ","நம்மில் பிரிந்த இடத்து"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his good heart that it is foolish: when they stir to set out after great wealth, she of bright ornaments cannot hold back, and sobs and sighs with swelling grief; how will she bear it once they have gone from her?","n":"He argues from what he sees at the moment of setting out to what must follow after, so the sobbing he has watched becomes the measure of a grief he will not be there to see.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 39","lb":"AA39","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:4"},"aa:40":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":41,"t":["இன்(று)அல்கல் ஈர்ம்புடையுள் ஈர்ங்கோதை தோள்துணையா","நன்கு வதிந்தனை நன்னெஞ்சே! - நாளைநாம்","குன்றதர் அத்தம் இறந்து தமியமாய்","என்கொலே சேக்கும் இடம்"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his good heart that tonight it slept well in the cool place with the shoulders of her of the cool garland for companion; tomorrow, having crossed the wasteland of hill paths, alone, where will they lie down?","n":"The cool (ஈர்ம், ஈர்ங்) of tonight is set against a tomorrow that has no place named at all; the question is the whole argument against going.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 40","lb":"AA40","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஈர்ங்கோதை","கோதை",1,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:4"},"aa:41":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":42,"t":["தெண்கடற் சேர்ப்பன் பிரியப் புலம்(பு) அடைந்(து)","ஒள்தடங்கண் துஞ்சற்க ஒள்ளிழாய் - நண்படைந்த","சேவலும் தன்அருகில் சேக்குமால் என்கொலோ","பூந்தலை அன்றில் புலம்பு"],"p":"","r":"Since the lord of the clear sea's shore has gone away, the heroine tells her bright-jewelled friend that her wide bright eyes, sunk in grief, will not sleep: the flower-crested anril cries out though its mate has come close and rests beside it, so what wonder if she, whose mate is gone, keeps awake?","n":"The optative துஞ்சற்க can be read as the heroine's vow of wakefulness or as the friend's gentle irony; either way the argument runs from the lesser case to the greater: a bird that cries with its mate beside it licenses a parted woman's sleepless crying.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 41","lb":"AA41","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["சேவலும்","சேவல்",3,"pul","iraicchi","male bird"],["அன்றில்","அன்றில்",4,"pul","iraicchi","anril bird"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","அன்றில்","confirmed","1/1","if a joined bird laments, the heroine parted from her man cannot be asked to sleep"]],"ch":"aa:5"},"aa:42":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":43,"t":["கொடுந்தாள் அலவ! குறையாம் இரப்பேம்","ஒடுங்கா ஒலிகடல் சேர்ப்பன் - நெடுந்தேர்","கடந்த வழியைஎம் கண்ணாரக் காண","நடந்து சிதையாதி நீ"],"p":"","r":"The heroine begs the crooked-legged crab, as a favour, not to walk over and blur the tracks the long chariot of the lord of the ever-roaring shore left behind, so that she may look at the path he took until her eyes are full.","n":"All she has left of him is the wheel-rut in the sand, and the only creature that can take it from her is the crab, so she pleads with it as with a person (குறையாம் இரப்பேம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 42","lb":"AA42","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அலவ","அலவன்",1,"pira","scene","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:5"},"aa:43":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":44,"t":["பொரிப்புறப் பல்லிச் சினையீன்ற புன்னை","வரிப்புற வார்மணல்மேல் ஏறித் - தெரிப்புறத்","தாழ்கடற் தண்சேர்ப்பன் தார்அகலம் நல்குமேல்","ஆழியால் காணாமோ யாம்"],"p":"","r":"Climbing the long striped sand where the punnai has put out buds like the eggs of a speckled lizard, the heroine says that if the cool lord of the low-lying sea is to grant her his garlanded chest, she and her friend will surely see it in the circle drawn in the sand.","n":"ஆழி is taken as the circle a waiting woman draws in the sand to divine whether the lover will come; it can also mean the sea, which would change the last line to seeing him by the sea, so the reading is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 43","lb":"AA43","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பல்லிச்","பல்லி",1,"pira","scene","lizard"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene","punnai tree"],["தார்அகலம்","தார்",3,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:5"},"aa:44":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":45,"t":["கொண்கன் பிரிந்த குளிர்பூம் பொழில்நோக்கி","உண்கண் சிவப்ப அழுதேன் ஒளிமுகம்","கண்டன்னை எவ்வம்யா தென்னக் கடல்வந்தென்","வண்டல் சிதைத்ததென் றேன்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that she wept till her kohl-lined eyes reddened, gazing at the cool flowering grove the shore lord had left, and when her mother saw her face and asked what grieved her, she answered that the sea had come and wrecked her sand-house.","n":"The lie is also the truth in code: it was the man of the sea, the கொண்கன், who came and broke up her girlhood play, so the excuse names the real cause in terms only the friend can read.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","pirivu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 44","lb":"AA44","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பொழில்நோக்கி","பொழில்",1,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"aa:5"},"aa:45":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":46,"t":["ஈர்ந்தண் பொழிலுள் இருங்கழித் தண்சேர்ப்பன்","சேர்ந்தென் செறிவளைத்தோள் பற்றித் தெளித்தமை","மாந்தளிர் மேனியாய்! மன்ற விடுவனவோ","பூந்தண் பொழிலுள் குருகு"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend, whose body is tender as a mango shoot, that the cool lord of the dark backwater came to her in the moist grove, took her bangled arm and swore his faith, and asks whether the herons of that flowering grove will really let the oath go by.","n":"The herons are called on as the only witnesses of a secret oath; they are the subject of her question, not a carrier of a further sense, so they are not tagged iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","vaaimai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 45","lb":"AA45","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பொழிலுள்","பொழில்",1,"maram","scene","grove"],["மாந்தளிர்","மாந்தளிர்",3,"maram","scene","mango shoot"],["குருகு","குருகு",4,"pul","scene","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:5"},"aa:46":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":47,"t":["ஓதம் தொகுத்த ஒலிகடல் தண்முத்தம்","பேதை மடவார்தம் வண்டல் விளக்கயரும்","கானலம் சேர்ப்ப! தகுவதோ என்தோழி","தோள்நலம் தோற்பித்தல் நீ"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the seaside grove, where innocent young girls light their sand-houses with cool pearls heaped up by the tide of the roaring sea, whether it befits him to make her friend's shoulders lose their beauty.","n":"The girls treat the sea's pearls as playthings, which sets the heroine's guarded girlhood against his careless use of her beauty; read as ullurai it gives only that one pair (precious things spent lightly), so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","nalam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 46","lb":"AA46","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முத்தம்","முத்து",1,"pira","scene","pearl"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"aa:5"},"aa:47":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":48,"t":["பெருங்கடல் உள்கலங்க நுண்வலை வீசி","ஒருங்குடன் தன்னைமார் தந்த கொழுமீன்","உணங்கல்புள் ஒப்பும் ஒளியிழை மாதர்","அணங்காகும் ஆற்ற எமக்கு"],"p":"","r":"The hero says that the bright-jewelled girl who scares the birds off the fat fish her brothers brought in together, casting their fine nets till the great sea was stirred to its depths, has become a tormenting goddess to him beyond bearing.","n":"The brothers' nets, cast together, place the girl inside her watchful kin from the first line, which is why the sight of her is fear as much as desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anangu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 47","lb":"AA47","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["வலை","வலை",1,"seythi","scene","fishing net"],["தன்னைமார்","தன்னைமார்",2,"seythi","scene","fishermen brothers"],["கொழுமீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","scene","fish"],["உணங்கல்புள்","உணங்கல்",3,"unavu","scene","drying fish"],["உணங்கல்புள்","புள்",3,"pul","scene","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:5"},"aa:48":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":49,"t":["எக்கர் இடுமணல்மேல் ஓதம் தரவந்த","நித்திலம் நின்றிமைக்கும் நீள்கழித் தண்சேர்ப்ப","மிக்க மிகுபுகழ் தாங்குபவோ தற்சேர்ந்தார்","ஒற்கம் கடைப்பிடியா தார்"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the cool lord of the long backwater, where pearls brought by the tide onto the heaped sand of the dunes stay and glitter, whether men who do not take up the distress of those who have come to depend on them can carry great renown.","n":"The pearl the tide leaves on the dune keeps shining where it was set down; the friend wants the girl who has come to him to be kept so. That is one pair only, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","pugazh","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 48","lb":"AA48","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நித்திலம்","நித்திலம்",2,"pira","scene","pearl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:5"},"aa:49":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":50,"t":["கொடுமுள் மடல்தாழைக் கூம்பவிழ்ந்த ஒண்பூ","இடையுள் இழுதொப்பத் தோன்றிப் -புடையெலாம்","தெய்வம் கமழும் தெளிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்பன்","செய்தான் தெளியாக் குறி"],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that the lord of the clear sea's shore, where the bright flower of the thorny-fronded thaazhai opens among its leaves like a lump of butter and the whole shore round about smells as if a god dwelt there, gave her a sign she cannot trust.","n":"The place of the tryst is so fragrant it seems holy, while the man's signal there is the one thing on that shore that is not clear (தெளி-கடல் against தெளியாக் குறி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","vaaimai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 49","lb":"AA49","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மடல்தாழைக்","தாழை",1,"maram","simile","screw-pine"],["இழுதொப்பத்","இழுது",2,"unavu","simile","butter"],["தெய்வம்","தெய்வம்",3,"deyvam","scene","god"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:5"},"aa:50":{"book":"ainthinai_aimpathu","num":51,"t":["அணிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்பன் தேர்ப்பரிமாப் பூண்ட","மணிஅரவம் என்றெழுந்து போந்தேன் - கனிவிரும்பும்","புள்ளரவம் கேட்டுப் பெயர்ந்தேன் ஒளியிழாய்","உள்ளுருகு நெஞ்சினேன் ஆய்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her bright-jewelled friend that she rose and went out, thinking it the sound of the bells on the horses yoked to the chariot of the cool lord of the lovely sea, and turned back, her heart melting within her, when she heard it was only the noise of fruit-seeking birds.","n":"The birds that crave fruit mirror her own craving: she too goes out at a sound, and both are disappointed waiters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","ninaivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 50","lb":"AA50","poet":"மாறன் பொறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பரிமாப்","பரிமா",1,"maa","scene","horse"],["புள்ளரவம்","புள்",3,"pul","scene","fruit-eating birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"aa:5"},"ae:0":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":1,"t":["எண்ணும் பொருளினிதே எல்லாம் முடித்தெமக்கு","நண்ணுங் கலையனைத்தும் நல்குமால் - கண்ணுதலின்","முண்டத்தான் அண்டத்தான் மூலத்தான் நலஞ்சேர்","கண்டத்தான் ஈன்ற களிறு"],"p":"","r":"The invocation asks that the elephant born of the god with the eye in his forehead, he of the skull, of the whole universe, of the root of things and of the fair dark throat, bring every purpose we hold sweetly to its end and grant us all the arts.","n":"The elephant god is never named; he is known only as the offspring (ஈன்ற களிறு) of Siva, who is drawn by four epithets ending in -த்தான். The prayer asks for success in what one intends and for learning, the two things a poet beginning a book needs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 0","lb":"AE0","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",4,"deyvam","scene","the elephant god"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:1":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":2,"t":["அவரை பொருந்திய பைங்குரல் ஏனல்","கவரி கடமா கதூஉம் படர்சாரல்","கானக நாட! மறவல் வயங்கிழைக்(கு)","யானிடை நின்ற புணை"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the forest land, whose spreading slopes see the yak and the gaur graze on the green-eared millet twined with avarai beans, not to forget that she stood as the raft between him and the girl of bright jewels.","n":"The friend claims her own part: the love was crossed to on her as on a raft, so forgetting the girl would also be forgetting the one who carried him. The beasts eating a guarded millet field invite a hidden reading of the man taking what is watched over, but only one correspondence can be named, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","thunai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 1","lb":"AE1","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அவரை","அவரை",1,"maram","scene","field bean"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கவரி","கவரி",2,"maa","scene","yak"],["கடமா","கடமா",2,"maa","scene","gaur"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:2":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":3,"t":["மன்றத் துறுகல் கருங்கண முசுஉகளும்","குன்றன நாடன் தெளித்த தெளிவினை","நன்றென்று தேறித் தெளிந்தேன் தலையளி","ஒன்றுமற்(று) ஒன்றும் அனைத்து"],"p":"","r":"The girl says that she trusted the assurance given by the lord of the hills, where a troop of black-faced langurs leaps on the boulder of the village common, thought it good and was set at rest; his grace, she says, is one and the same from first to last.","n":"Three forms of one root, தெளித்த, தெளிவினை, தெளிந்தேன், make the verse a small study of trust: he made her clear, she weighed it (தேறி) and then was clear. The last line is the doubtful one; it is read here as saying his kindness is the same throughout.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 2","lb":"AE2","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முசுஉ","முசு",1,"maa","scene","langur"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:3":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":4,"t":["மன்றப் பலவின் களைவிளை தீம்பழம்","உண்டுவந்து மந்தி முலைவருடக் - கன்றமர்ந்(து)","ஆமா சுரக்கும் அணிமலை நாடனை","யாமாப் பிரிவ(து) இலம்"],"p":"","r":"The girl says they cannot part from the lord of the lovely mountain, where a female monkey, having eaten the sweet ripe fruit of the jackfruit on the common, strokes the udder of a wild cow, and the cow, as glad as with her own calf, lets her milk flow.","n":"The milk is not taken but given: the cow lets it down (சுரக்கும்) because the touch feels like her calf's, so tenderness flows across kinds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 3","lb":"AE3","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பலவின்","பலா",1,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",2,"maa","iraicchi","female monkey"],["ஆமா","ஆமா",3,"maa","iraicchi","wild cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","ஆமா","confirmed","1/2","a heart once touched with tenderness gives itself and cannot be withdrawn, so the girl cannot part from the man who has won her"]],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:4":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":5,"t":["சான்றவர் கேண்மை சிதைவின்றாய் ஊன்றி","வலியாகிப் பின்னும் பயக்கும் மெலிவில்","கயந்திகழ் சோலை மலைநாடன் கேண்மை","நயந்திகழும் என்னும்என் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"","r":"The girl says her heart tells her that, as the friendship of the worthy stands firm without decay, grows into strength and keeps yielding good afterwards, so the friendship of the lord of the mountain, with its groves bright with pools, will shine with goodness and never weaken.","n":"The first two lines are a maxim of the ethics books, set down whole before the love situation; the girl's trust rests on ranking her lover among the சான்றவர், and it is her heart, not her friend, that makes the argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","saandror","nenju","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 4","lb":"AE4","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கயந்திகழ்","கயம்",3,"pira","scene","pool"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:5":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":6,"t":["பொன்னிணர் வேங்கை கமழும் நளிசோலை","நன்மலை நாட! மறவல் வயங்கிழைக்கு","நின்னல(து) இல்லையால் ஈயாயோ கண்ணோட்டத்(து)","இன்னுயிர் தாங்கும் மருந்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the good mountain, whose dense groves smell of the vengai's golden clusters, not to forget that the girl of bright jewels has no medicine but him to keep her sweet life, and asks whether he will not give it out of kindness.","n":"Line 2 repeats verse 1's மறவல் வயங்கிழைக்கு, so the friend's plea has a refrain. The gift she asks for is named by the Kural's word for regard toward one's own, கண்ணோட்டம், making his return a duty of kindness rather than a favour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","marundhu","kannottam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 5","lb":"AE5","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene","vengai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:6":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":7,"t":["காய்ந்தீயல் அன்னை! இவளோ தவறிலள்","ஓங்கிய செந்நீர் இழிதரும் கான்யாற்றுள்","தேன்கலந்து வந்த அருவி முடைந்தாடத்","தாம்சிவப் புற்றன கண்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother not to rage, since the girl is not at fault: her eyes turned red because she bathed again and again in the waterfall that came down mixed with honey in the red, swollen water of the forest stream.","n":"The excuse is precise enough to be believed: red flood water, honey in the stream, long bathing. What reddened the eyes is left unsaid, and the friend's defence is itself the sign that the girl has been weeping for her lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","thunai","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 6","lb":"AE6","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தேன்","தேன்",3,"unavu","scene","honey"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:7":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":8,"t":["வெறிகமழ் தண்சுனைத் தண்ணீர் துளும்பக்","கறிவளர் தேமா நறுங்கணி வீழும்","வெறிகமழ் தண்சோலை நாட! ஒன்(று) உண்டோ","அறிவின்கண் நின்ற மடம்"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the fragrant cool groves, where the cool water of a fragrant spring brims over and the sweet ripe fruit of the mango on which pepper vine climbs falls into it, whether any folly can stand where knowledge stands.","n":"The rebuke is a compliment turned against him: he is wise, so his delay must be the one folly wisdom cannot hold. The ripe fruit falling into the brimming spring has been read as the girl ready for marriage; it is one match of mood only, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","pedhaimai","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 7","lb":"AE7","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கறி","கறி",2,"maram","scene","pepper vine"],["தேமா","தேமா",2,"maram","scene","sweet mango"],["நறுங்கணி","கனி",2,"maram","scene","ripe fruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:8":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":9,"t":["கேழல் உழுத கரிபுனக் கொல்லையுள்","வாழை முதுகாய் கடுவன் புதைத்தயரும்","தாழருவி நாடன் தெளிகொடுத்தான் என்தோழி","நேர்வனை நெஞ்(சு) ஊன்று கோல்"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that the lord of the land of falling waterfalls, where in the burnt clearing the boar has ploughed a male monkey buries mature plantains to ripen and then rests, has given her his pledge, and that pledge is the staff her heart leans on.","n":"The monkey's stored fruit and the girl's stored promise both wait to ripen; the comfort is in having put something by.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","thunai","nenju","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 8","lb":"AE8","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கேழல்","கேழல்",1,"maa","scene","boar"],["வாழை","வாழை",2,"maram","scene","plantain"],["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",2,"maa","iraicchi","male monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கடுவன்","confirmed","1/1","the man has laid his pledge by as the monkey lays by its fruit, so she can rest on it until it ripens into marriage"]],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:9":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":10,"t":["பெருங்கை இருங்களிறு ஐவனம் மாந்திக்","கருங்கால் மராம்பொழில் பாசடைத் துஞ்சும்","சுரும்(பு)இமிர் சோலை மலைநாடன் கேண்மை","பொருந்தினார்க்கு ஏமாப்(பு) உடைத்து"],"p":"","r":"The bond with the mountain lord of the bee-loud groves, where a great-trunked dark bull elephant eats hill paddy and sleeps in the green-leaved shade of the black-stemmed kadamba grove, gives safety to those who join with it.","n":"ஏமாப்பு உடைத்து is the didactic books' word for lasting protection; here it is said of a lover's bond.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","thunai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 9","lb":"AE9","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இருங்களிறு","களிறு",1,"maa","iraicchi","bull elephant"],["ஐவனம்","ஐவனம்",1,"unavu","gift_food","hill paddy"],["மராம்","மராம்",2,"maram","scene","kadamba tree"],["சுரும்","சுரும்பு",3,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இருங்களிறு","confirmed","1/1","as the elephant sleeps secure in the grove's shade, the woman joined to this man may rest without fear"]],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:10":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":11,"t":["குறையொன்(று) உடையேன்மன் தோழி நிறையில்லா","மன்னுயிர்க்(கு) ஏமம் செயல்வேண்டும் இன்னே","அராவழங்கு நீள்சோலை நாடனை நம்மில்","இராவாரல் என்ப(து) உரை"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that she has one want: to keep safe a life that is dear to her, so tell the lord of the long groves where snakes move that he must not come to their house by night.","n":"The refusal is made out of care: the danger is to him on the snake path, and asking him to stop coming is how she protects him. அரா and இரா sit two syllables apart in line 3 and 4, the snake and the night together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","acham","uyir","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 10","lb":"AE10","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["அராவழங்கு","அரா",3,"pira","scene","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:11":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":12,"t":["பிரைசங் கொளவீழ்ந்த தீந்தேன் இறாஅல்","மரையான் குழவி குளம்பில் துகைக்கும்","வரையக நாட! வரையால் வரின்எம்","நிரைதொடி வாழ்தல் இவள்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the mountain land, where the calf of a wild cow tramples with its hooves a honeycomb of sweet honey that has fallen under its weight, that if he keeps coming without marrying her, the girl of the rows of bangles will not live.","n":"வரையக நாட and வரையால் play on one sound: lord of the hills (வரை), you who do not wed (வரை). The trampled honeycomb has been read as her ruined sweetness; it gives only one correspondence, a near miss for ullurai, and stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","uyir","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 11","lb":"AE11","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இறாஅல்","இறால்",1,"unavu","scene","honeycomb"],["மரையான்","மரையான்",2,"maa","scene","wild cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:12":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":13,"t":["வார்குரல் ஏனல் வளைலாயக் கிளைகவரும்","நீரால் தெளிதிகழ் காநாடன் கேண்மையே","ஆர்வத்தின் ஆர முயங்கினேன் வேலனும்","ஈர வலித்தான் மறி"],"p":"","r":"The girl says she has embraced to the full, with longing, her bond with the lord of the forest land where flocks of curved-beaked parrots carry off the long ears of millet, and now the velan has firmly resolved to cut a goat kid.","n":"The two halves are set side by side without comment: she knows the cause of her sickness, an embrace she chose, while the diviner the mother called readies a sacrifice for a god's seizure. The irony is all in the juxtaposition.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 12","lb":"AE12","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கிளைகவரும்","கிளை",1,"pul","scene","flock of parrots"],["வேலனும்","வேலன்",3,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["மறி","மறி",4,"maa","gift_food","goat kid"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:13":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":14,"t":["இலையடர் தண்குளவி ஏய்ந்த பொதும்பில்","குலையுடைக் காந்தள் இனவண்(டு) இமிரும்","வரையக நாடனும் வந்தான்மற்(று) அன்னை","அலையும் அலைபோயிற்(று) இன்று"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the lord of the mountain land, where swarms of bees hum on the clustered kaanthal in a bower thick with the leaves of cool wild jasmine, has come at last, and their mother's harrying has gone away today.","n":"அலையும் அலைபோயிற்று: the harrying itself has gone off, a play on அலை. The bees finding the kaanthal mirror his coming, but they carry a mood and are not tested as iraicchi. That he came to ask for her is inferred from the mother's anger ending.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 13","lb":"AE13","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["குளவி","குளவி",1,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",2,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["இனவண்","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bees"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:14":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":15,"t":["கொல்லைப் புனத்த அகில்சுமந்து கல்பாய்ந்து","வானின் அருவி ததும்பக் கவினிய","நாடன் நயமுடையன் என்பதனால் நீப்பினும்","வாடல் மறந்தன தோள்"],"p":"","r":"The girl says that because they say the lord of the land, where a stream carrying eaglewood from the clearing leaps over rocks and the waterfall from the sky brims in beauty, is a man of goodness, her shoulders have forgotten to waste even if he goes away.","n":"The usual sign of separation, wasting shoulders, is cancelled by reputation: what others say of his goodness (என்பதனால்) is enough to keep her body whole. Her confidence in the bond, not the waiting, is the matter, so uri is taken as punarthal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","nayan","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 14","lb":"AE14","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அகில்","அகில்",1,"maram","scene","eaglewood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:1"},"ae:15":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":16,"t":["செங்கதிர்ச் செல்வன் சினங்கரந்த போழ்தினால்","பைங்கொடி முல்லை மணங்கமழ -வண்(டு)இமிரக்","காரோ(டு) அலமரும் கார்வானம் காண்தொறும்","நீரோ(டு) அலமரும் கண்"],"p":"","r":"When the red-rayed sun has put away its heat, the green-stemmed jasmine smells sweet and the bees hum, she says that every time she sees the rain sky tossing with its clouds, her eyes toss with tears.","n":"The two closing lines repeat one verb, அலமரும், for the sky and the eyes, and only the noun changes (காரோடு, நீரோடு): the sky's clouds and her tears are matched sound for sound rather than through a simile marker. The sun is not set but has 'hidden its anger', the cooling hour of mullai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kan","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 15","lb":"AE15","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["வண்(டு)","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:2"},"ae:16":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":17,"t":["தடமென் பணைத்தோளி! நீத்தாரோ வாரார்","மடநடை மஞ்ஞை அகவக் - கடல்முகந்து","மின்னோடு வந்த(து) எழில்வானம் வந்தென்னை","என்னாதி என்பாரும் இல்"],"p":"","r":"She tells her friend of broad, soft, bamboo-like shoulders that the man who left has not come, though the soft-stepping peacock calls and the lovely sky that drew up the sea has come with its lightning, and there is no one who asks her what is wrong.","n":"The season has come (வந்தது) and the man has not (வாரார), and the last line turns from him to the household: her worst complaint is that no one even asks, so her grief is also isolation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 16","lb":"AE16","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",2,"pul","scene","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:2"},"ae:17":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":18,"t":["தண்ணுறங் கோடல் துடுப்பெடுப்பக் காரெதிரி","விண்ணுயர் வானத்(து) உரும்உரற்றத் - திண்ணிதின்","புல்லுநர் இல்லார் நடுங்கச் சிறுமாலை","கொல்லுநர் போல வரும்"],"p":"","r":"As the cool glory lily lifts its spoon-shaped buds to greet the rains and thunder roars in the high sky, the little evening comes like killers, so that those who have no one to hold them close tremble.","n":"The victims are named as a class, புல்லுநர் இல்லார், 'those without someone to embrace them firmly', so the speaker counts herself among them without saying 'I'. The evening is called small (சிறுமாலை) and still comes as a killer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 17","lb":"AE17","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கோடல்","கோடல்",1,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["உரும்உரற்றத்","உரும்",2,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:2"},"ae:18":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":19,"t":["கதழுறை வானம் சிதற இதழ்அகத்துத்","தாதிணர்க் கொன்றை எரிவளர்ப்பப் பாஅ","இடிப்பது போலும் எழில்வானம் நோக்கித்","துடிப்பது போலும் உயிர்"],"p":"","r":"As the sky scatters its swift rain and the konrai with pollen in its petals blazes like a growing fire, she looks at the lovely sky that seems to thunder as it spreads, and says her life seems to throb.","n":"The two last lines rhyme இடிப்பது and துடிப்பது, each with போலும்: the thunder's beat outside is matched by the beat of her life inside, the same device as verse 15.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 18","lb":"AE18","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",2,"maram","scene","konrai"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:2"},"ae:19":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":20,"t":["ஆலி விருப்புற்(று) அகவிப் புறவெல்லாம்","பீவி பரப்பி மயில்ஆலச் - சூலி","விரிகுவது போலும்இக் கார்அதிர ஆவி","உருகுவது போலும் எனக்கு"],"p":"","r":"As the peacock, longing for the raindrops, calls and spreads its plumes over all the woodland and dances, and this rain cloud thunders as if a pregnant one were bursting, she says her life seems to melt.","n":"The peacock's joy and the cloud's fullness (சூலி, 'the pregnant one') are both images of fruition, set against her melting. Reading சூலி as the heavy cloud like a woman in labour is our reading of a compressed line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir","paruvam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 19","lb":"AE19","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மயில்ஆலச்","மயில்",2,"pul","scene","peacock"],["ஆலி","ஆலி",1,"pira","scene","raindrops"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:2"},"ae:20":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":21,"t":["இனத்த வருங்கலை பொங்கப் புனத்த","கொடிமயங்கு முல்லை தளிர்ப்ப இடிமயங்கி","யானும் அவரும் வருந்தச் சிறுமாலை","தானும் புயலும் வரும்"],"p":"","r":"As the stags coming with their herd leap up, the jasmine twined with the creepers of the clearing puts out shoots, and thunder rolls, the little evening comes together with the storm, so that she and he both suffer.","n":"யானும் அவரும் வருந்த: she imagines him suffering too, so the poem is not a reproach. The stags arrive (வரும்) with their herd and rejoice at the hour he cannot.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 20","lb":"AE20","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வருங்கலை","கலை",1,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வருங்கலை","not_confirmed","0/1","the beasts come home in company at dusk, which the man who left has not done, and so both of them suffer apart"]],"ch":"ae:2"},"ae:21":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":22,"t":["காரிகை வாடத் துறந்தாரும் வாராமுன்","கார்கொடி முல்லை எயிறீனக் - காரோ(டு)","உடன்பட்டு வந்தலைக்கும் மாலைக்கோ எம்மின்","மடம்பட்டு வாழ்கிற்பார் இல்"],"p":"","r":"Before even the man who left her, letting her beauty fade, has come, the rain-fed jasmine shows its teeth, and the evening comes with the rain cloud to torment her: no one, she says, lives in such folly as we do.","n":"The jasmine 'bearing teeth' is the usual figure for its buds, but next to her self-mockery it also grins at her. The closing line can mean that no one is as foolish as she, who still lives, or who let him go; the poem does not choose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","pedhaimai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 21","lb":"AE21","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","மடன்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:2"},"ae:22":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":23,"t":["கொன்றைக் குழலூதிக் கோவலர் பின்னுரைத்துக்","கன்றமர் ஆயம் புகுதா - இன்று","வழங்கிய வந்தன்று மாலையாம் காண","முழங்கிவில் கோலிற்று வான்"],"p":"","r":"The herdsmen blow their konrai flutes and follow talking behind, the herd with its calves goes into the fold, and today, as she watches, the evening that comes every day has come, and the sky has thundered and drawn its bow.","n":"Nothing is said of the man or of her pain: the evening is only watched (யாம் காண). The herdsmen and the herd coming home are a near miss for iraicchi; the act fits, but the poem never names the absent man, so the sense it would carry has nothing in the poem to attach to. The sky 'drawing a bow' arms the evening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 22","lb":"AE22","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொன்றைக்","கொன்றை",1,"maram","scene","konrai pod flute"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",1,"seythi","scene","herdsmen"],["கன்றமர்","கன்று",2,"maa","scene","calf"],["ஆயம்","ஆயம்",2,"maa","scene","herd"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ae:2"},"ae:23":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":24,"t":["தேரைத் தழங்குகுரல் தார்மணி வாயதிர்ப்ப","ஆர்கலி வானம் பெயல்தொடங்கிக் - கார்கொள","இன்(று)ஆற்ற வாரா விடுவார்கொல் காதலர்","ஒன்றாலும் நில்லா வளை"],"p":"","r":"As the frogs' clamouring voices and the bells on the garlands ring out and the roaring sky begins to rain, she asks whether her lover will fail to come today to comfort her when the rains set in, for her bangles will not stay on at all.","n":"Whose bells ring (தார்மணி) is not said: cattle bells fit the mullai evening, chariot-horse bells would be the sound she hopes for, and either is possible. The slipping bangles are her wasting body's evidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 23","lb":"AE23","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தேரைத்","தேரை",1,"pira","scene","frog"],["தார்மணி","தார் மணி",1,"pira","scene","garland bells"],["வளை","வளை",4,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:2"},"ae:24":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":25,"t":["கல்லேர் புறவின் கவினிப் புதன்மிசை","முல்லை தளவொடு போதவிழ - எல்லி","அலை(வு)அற்று விட்டன்று வானமும் உன்கண்","முலைவற்று விட்டன்று நீர்"],"p":"","r":"On the stony woodland, grown lovely, jasmine opens its buds on the bushes together with the wild jasmine; in the night the sky has stopped its tossing, the friend says, but the water of your kohl-lined eyes has not stopped running down to your breasts.","n":"The rhyme அலைவற்று / முலைவற்று sets the sky that has ceased against the eyes that have not. The second half is compressed and our reading of முலைவற்று is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 24","lb":"AE24","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["தளவொடு","தளவம்",2,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:2"},"ae:27":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":26,"t":["கார்ப்புடைப் பாண்டில் கமழப் புறவெல்லாம்","ஆர்ப்போ(டு) இனவண்(டு) இமிர்ந்தாட - நீர்த்தின்றி","ஒன்றா(து) அலைக்கும் சிறுமாலை மாறுழந்து","நின்றாக நின்றது நீர்"],"p":"","r":"As the paandil of the rains smells sweet and the swarms of bees hum and dance with a din over all the woodland, the little evening torments her without mercy, never relenting, and, fighting it, her tears stand as they have always stood.","n":"நீர்த்தின்றி, 'without fitness', makes the evening a wrongdoer; the closing line is obscure and we read it as her tears never ceasing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 27","lb":"AE27","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பாண்டில்","பாண்டில்",1,"maram","scene","paandil flower"],["இனவண்(டு)","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:2"},"ae:28":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":27,"t":["குருந்தலை வான்படலை சூடிச் சுரும்பார்ப்ப","ஆயன் புகுதரும் போழ்தினான் ஆயிழாய்!","பின்னொடு நின்று பெயரும் படுமழைகொல","என்னொடு பட்ட வகை"],"p":"","r":"She asks her well-jewelled friend whether what has happened to her is like the pouring rain that lingers behind and then moves away, at the hour when the herdsman comes home wearing a fine garland headed with kurunthu flowers while bees hum.","n":"The herdsman comes home garlanded at the very hour she asks her question, so his return frames her lover's absence. Reading the lingering rain as a figure for the man who stayed and left is ours; the line can also be read of the rain itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 28","lb":"AE28","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருந்தலை","குருந்து",1,"maram","ornament","kurunthu"],["சுரும்பார்ப்ப","சுரும்பு",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["ஆயன்","ஆயன்",2,"seythi","scene","herdsman"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:2"},"ae:29":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":28,"t":["பொறிகிளர் சேவல் வரிமரல் குத்த","நெறிதூர் அருஞ்சுரம்நரம் உன்னி - அறிவிட்(டு)","அலர்மொழி சென்ற கொடியக நாட்ட","வலனுயர்ந்து தோன்றும் மலை"],"p":"","r":"The speaker thinks of the hard wasteland whose paths are choked, where a speckled cock pecks at the striped maral, and of the mountain of the land of the one the gossip went out about, which now shows itself rising high on the right.","n":"A hill seen rising on the right (வலன்) is the auspicious side, so the closing image may be an omen of the man's good journey or return, as in 41 and 42; but line 2 (அருஞ்சுரம்நரம்) and line 3 (அறிவிட்டு, கொடியக) are hard to construe, and the speaker and situation are our guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","alar","nimittam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 29","lb":"AE29","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சேவல்","சேவல்",1,"pul","scene","cock"],["வரிமரல்","மரல்",1,"maram","scene","bowstring hemp"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:30":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":29,"t":["ஒல்லோம்என்(று) ஏங்கி உயங்கி இருப்பாளோ","கல்லிவர் அத்தம் அரிபெய் சிலம்(பு)ஒலிப்பக்","கொல்களி(று) அன்னான்பின் செல்லுங்கொல் என்பேதை","மெல்விரல் சேப்ப நடந்து"],"p":"","r":"A mother whose daughter has gone off with a man wonders whether the girl sits pining, sighing that she cannot go on, or whether she walks behind that man like a killer elephant across the stony wasteland, the pebbles in her anklets ringing and her soft toes reddening.","n":"The anklet is the unmarried girl's ornament (the silambu is removed at the wedding), so its ringing on the desert road says she has gone before her marriage rite. The addressee is no one present: the mother speaks to herself. மெல்விரல் may be fingers or toes; walking makes toes the likelier sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 30","lb":"AE30","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["சிலம்(பு)ஒலிப்பக்","சிலம்பு",2,"pira","ornament","anklet"],["கொல்களி(று)","களிறு",3,"maa","simile","bull elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:31":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":30,"t":["பொரிபுற ஓமைப் புகர்படு நீழல்","வரிநுகல் யானை பிடியோ(டு) உறங்கும்","எரிமயங்கு கானம் செலவுரைப்ப நில்லா","அரிமயங்கு உண்கண்ணுள் நீர்"],"p":"","r":"Under the dappled shade of the rough-barked omai a striped-trunked elephant sleeps with its mate, and when the man speaks of leaving through that fire-hazed forest the tears in the heroine's kohl-lined, red-streaked eyes will not stay.","n":"The speaker is most likely the friend telling the man what his talk of going has already done: the tears come at the word, before any parting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 31","lb":"AE31","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஓமைப்","ஓமை",1,"maram","scene","omai tree"],["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","iraicchi","elephant"],["பிடியோ(டு)","பிடி",2,"maa","iraicchi","cow elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","யானை","confirmed","1/1","even the beasts of the road he means to cross keep to their mates in the heat, so the man should not leave her to cross it"]],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:32":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":31,"t":["எழுத்துடைக் கல்நிரைக்க வாயில் விழுத்தொடை","அம்ஆ(று) அலைக்கும் சுரநிறைத்(து) அம்மா","பெருந்தரு தாளாண்மைக்(கு) ஏற்க அரும்பொருள்","ஆகும்அவர் காதல் அவா"],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that the road is lined with lettered stones where archers with fine arrows harass the lovely way and fill the wasteland, and yet, fitting his labour that wins great things, his longing in love has become longing for hard-won wealth.","n":"The lettered stones are hero-stones of men killed by the road's archers, so the danger and the motive are set side by side. ஏற்க, 'fitting', may be praise of his effort or a bitter concession; the poem leaves it open. Lines 1 and 2 are hard to divide (வாயில், அம்ஆ(று)), so the scene is read only in outline.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pirivu","muyarchi","nadukal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 32","lb":"AE32","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["விழுத்தொடை","தொடை",1,"seythi","scene","arrows"]],"en":[["herostone","எழுத்துடைக் கல்",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:33":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":32,"t":["வில்லுழுது உண்பார் கடுகி அதரலைக்கும்","கல்சூழ் பதுக்கையார் அத்தத்தில் பாரார்கொல்","மெல்லியல் கண்ணோட்டம் இன்றிப் பொருட்(கு)இவர்ந்து","நில்லாத வுள்ளத் தவர்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks whether the man, whose heart without kindly regard for his soft girl climbs after wealth and will not stay, does not see on the road the men who live by ploughing with the bow, rushing out to harass travellers among the stone-ringed cairns.","n":"The cairns are the stone heaps over travellers the robbers killed, so 'do they not see?' means: he can see the death on the road and still goes, but cannot see her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","selvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 33","lb":"AE33","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["வில்லுழுது","வில்",1,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["பதுக்கையார்","பதுக்கை",2,"pira","scene","stone cairn"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:34":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":33,"t":["நீரல் அருஞ்சுரைத்(து) ஆமான் இனம்வழங்கும்","ஆரிடை அத்தம் இறப்பர்கொல் ஆயிழாய்!","நாணினை நீக்கி உயிரோ(டு) உடன்சென்று","காணப் புணர்ப்பதுகொல் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether the man will cross the waterless hard road where herds of wild cattle roam, and whether her heart, throwing off shame, will go along with her life and contrive that she see him.","n":"உயிரோடு உடன்சென்று: the heart will leave with her life, so following him and dying are the same act; shame is the only thing that keeps her body at home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","nenju","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 34","lb":"AE34","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆமான்","ஆமான்",1,"maa","scene","wild cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:35":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":34,"t":["பீரிவர் கூரை மறுமனைச் சேர்ந்(து) அல்கிக்","கூருகிர் எண்கின் இருங்கினை கண்படுக்கும்","நீரில் அருஞ்சுரம் உன்னி அறியார்கொல்","ஈரமில் நெஞ்சில் அவர்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks whether the man with a heart without moisture does not know the waterless hard road, where the sharp-clawed bear's dark kin settle in an abandoned house overgrown with gourd vine and sleep.","n":"If இருங்கினை stands for இருங்கிளை (the dark kin), the bears that sleep together in a deserted house are set against a man who leaves his own house: a near miss for iraicchi, kept as scene because the word is in doubt. The 'moistureless heart' answers the waterless road.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 35","lb":"AE35","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பீரிவர்","பீர்",1,"maram","scene","ridge gourd"],["எண்கின்","எண்கு",2,"maa","scene","bear"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:36":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":35,"t":["சூரல் புறவின் அணில்பிளிற்றும் சூழ்படப்பை","ஊர்கெழு சேவல் இதலொடு - போர்தினைக்கும்","தேரொடு கானம் தெருளிலார் செல்வார்கொல்","ஊரிடு கவ்வை ஒழித்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether the man without sense will drive his chariot away through the forest, where squirrels squeal in the cane of the scrub and the village cock fights the quail round the garden plots, rather than ending the gossip the town has raised.","n":"ஒழித்து can mean that he goes leaving the gossip behind for her to bear, or that he should end it by marrying; either way the journey is set against marriage. The scrub with its village, cock and quail is a mullai woodland on the paalai road. The fighting cock and quail may hint at the town's quarrel, but one match of mood is not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","varaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 36","lb":"AE36","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சூரல்","சூரல்",1,"maram","scene","cane"],["அணில்பிளிற்றும்","அணில்",1,"maa","scene","squirrel"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",2,"pul","scene","cock"],["இதலொடு","இதல்",2,"pul","scene","quail"],["தேரொடு","தேர்",3,"pira","scene","chariot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:37":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":36,"t":["கொடுவரி பாயத் துணையிழந்(து) அஞ்சி","கடுவுணங்கு பாறைக் கடவு தெவுட்டு","நெடுவரை அத்தம் இறப்பர்கொல் கோண்மாப்","படுபகை பார்க்குஞ் சுரம்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks whether the man will cross the tall-hilled road, where a creature that lost its mate to the leaping tiger crowds in fear at the sun-baked rock, the wasteland where the beast of prey watches for its kill.","n":"துணையிழந்து அஞ்சி, a beast that has lost its mate and fears, is the fate she fears for herself, but the creature only suffers and does nothing a lover does, so it carries a mood, not an iraicchi. Line 2 (கடவு தெவுட்டு) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 37","lb":"AE37","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["கொடுவரி","கொடுவரி",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["கோண்மாப்","கோண்மா",3,"maa","scene","beast of prey"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:38":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":37,"t":["கோளவல் கொடுவரி நல்வய மரக்குழுமும்","தாள்வீ பதுக்கைய கானம் இறந்தார்கொல்","ஆள்வினையின் ஆற்ற அகன்றவா நன்றுணரா","மீளிகொள் மொய்ம்பி னவர்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks whether he has crossed the forest of cairns and fallen blossom where the strong striped tiger, skilled at the kill, roars, he of a hero's strength who went far to finish his labour and does not understand what is good.","n":"The praise is barbed: மீளி, the hero's might, and ஆள்வினை, manly labour, are the virtues that make him blind to the good at home. Line 1 (நல்வய மரக்குழுமும்) is probably a misdivision of a line about the tiger's roar.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","pirivu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 38","lb":"AE38","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["கொடுவரி","கொடுவரி",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["தாள்வீ","வீ",2,"maram","scene","fallen flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:39":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":38,"t":["பேழ்வாய் இரும்புலி குஞ்சரம் கோட்பிழைத்துப்","பாழூர்ப் பொதியில் புகாப்பார்க்கும் ஆரிடைச்","சூழாப் பொருள்நசைக்கண் சென்றோர் அருள்நினைந்","வாழ்தியோ மற்றோ உயிர்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her own life whether it will live on, thinking of the grace of the man who went off in unreflecting desire for wealth on the road where a gape-mouthed tiger, having missed its elephant, watches the entrance of the common hall of a ruined village, or whether it will not.","n":"The addressee is her own life (உயிர்), set apart from her as the heart is in other poems. The tiger that failed with an elephant and now waits at a ruined village for weaker prey makes the road dangerous to the man; the 'grace' she must live on is the very thing his going denies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arul","selvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 39","lb":"AE39","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["இரும்புலி","புலி",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["குஞ்சரம்","குஞ்சரம்",1,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:40":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":39,"t":["முள்ளுடை மூங்கில் பிணங்கிய சூழ்படப்பை","புள்ளி வெருகுதன் குட்டிக்(கு) இரைபார்க்கும்","கள்ளர் வழங்கும் சுரமென்பர் காதலர்","உள்ளம் படர்ந்த நெறி"],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that the road her lover's heart set out on is, people say, a wasteland where robbers roam and where, in the plots ringed by tangled thorny bamboo, the spotted wildcat watches for prey for its kitten.","n":"She has only heard of the road (என்பர், 'they say'); it is his heart, not his body, she follows along it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 40","lb":"AE40","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["மூங்கில்","மூங்கில்",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["வெருகுதன்","வெருகு",2,"maa","iraicchi","wildcat"],["குட்டிக்(கு)","குட்டி",2,"maa","iraicchi","kitten"],["கள்ளர்","கள்ளர்",3,"seythi","scene","robbers"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வெருகுதன்","confirmed","1/1","the man too has gone out on the robbers' road to win what his household needs, so his going is provision as well as desertion"]],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:41":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":40,"t":["மன்ற முதுமரத்து ஆந்தை குரல்இயம்பக்","குன்றனம் கண்ணிக் குறும்(பு)இறந்து - சென்றவர்","கள்ளிய தன்மையர் போலும் அடுத்தடுத்(து)","ஒள்ளிய தும்மல் வரும்"],"p":"","r":"As the owl hoots from the old tree of the village common, bright sneezes come one after another, so the man who went over the hills and past the hill forts seems to be thinking of her in secret.","n":"A sneeze was taken as a sign that someone far away is thinking of you; repeated sneezes mean steady thought. கள்ளிய தன்மையர், 'of a stealing nature', suggests he thinks of her secretly or is stealing back unannounced; the owl's cry is a second omen whose sense the poem does not spell out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 41","lb":"AE41","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முதுமரத்து","முதுமரம்",1,"maram","scene","old tree"],["ஆந்தை","ஆந்தை",1,"pul","scene","owl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:42":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":41,"t":["பூங்கணிடம் ஆடும் கனவும் திருந்தின","ஓங்கிய குன்றம் இறந்தாரை யாம்நினைப்ப","வீங்கிய மெள்தோள் கவினிப் பிணைதீரப்","பாங்கத்துப் பல்லி படும்"],"p":"","r":"Her flower-like left eye throbs, her dreams have turned good, her swollen soft shoulders grow lovely again as she thinks of the man who crossed the high hills, and the lizard chirps at the favourable side.","n":"Four omens are heaped up: the throbbing left eye (good for a woman), dreams that come right, the body's own return to beauty, and the lizard's chirp from the proper quarter. The body recovers before the man arrives. பிணைதீர and மெள்தோள் (for மென்தோள்) are uncertain in the text.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","kanavu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 42","lb":"AE42","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பல்லி","பல்லி",4,"pira","scene","lizard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:3"},"ae:43":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":42,"t":["பேதையர் என்று தமரைச் செறுபவோர்","போதுறழ் தாமரைக்கண் ஊரனை நேர்நோக்கி","வாய்முடி யிட்டும் இருப்பஏர் மாண்ழாய்!","நோவதென் மார்(பு)யுஅறிம் இன்று"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her fair-jewelled friend that those who scold their own kin as simpletons still sit with sealed lips once they have looked the lord of the lotus town in the face; why then should she grieve, when today his chest is known for what it is?","n":"The point seems to be that even the sharp-tongued, who call others fools, fall silent before him, so his charm is no proof against what he does; but lines 3 and 4 are corrupt and the last clause is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 43","lb":"AE43","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாமரைக்கண்","தாமரை",2,"maram","scene","lotus"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:44":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":43,"t":["ஒள்ளிதழ்த் தாமரைப் போதுறழும் ஊரனை","உள்ளம்கொண்(டு) உள்ளானென்(று) யார்க்குரைக்கோ - ஒள்ளிழாய்","அச்சுப் பணிமொழி உண்டேனோ மேனாள் ஓர்","பொய்ச்சூள் எனஅறியா தேன்"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her bright-jewelled friend to whom she can say that the lord of the town where bright-petalled lotus buds crowd took her heart and now gives her no thought; long ago she drank in his soft humble words, not knowing they were a false oath.","n":"The complaint looks back from married neglect to the oath of courtship: what betrays her now is not a new act but the discovery that the first words were already false. அச்சுப் பணிமொழி is obscure; 'humble, trembling words' is a guess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","oodal","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 44","lb":"AE44","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",1,"maram","scene","lotus"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:45":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":44,"t":["ஆற்றல் உடையன் அரும்பொறி நல்லூரன்","மேற்றுச் சிறுதாய காய்வஞ்சி - போற்றுருவிக்","கட்டக முத்திற் புதல்வனை மார்பின்மேல்","பட்டஞ் சிதைப்ப வரும்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says with irony that the lord of the good town is a man of great resource: he comes home carrying their son on his chest, the boy's pearl-strung ornaments crushing the frontlet he wears.","n":"ஆற்றல் உடையன் is praise turned to mockery: his 'strength' is the cunning of coming in behind the child, whom no one can turn away. Line 2 (மேற்றுச் சிறுதாய காய்வஞ்சி போற்றுருவி) is obscure and is not construed here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 45","lb":"AE45","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வஞ்சி","வஞ்சி",2,"maram","scene","vanji tree"],["முத்திற்","முத்து",3,"pira","ornament","pearl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:46":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":45,"t":["அகன்பனை யூரனைத் தாமம் பிணித்த(து)","இகன்மை கருதி யிருப்பன் - முகன்அமரா","ஏதின் மகளிரை நோவ தெவன்கொலோ","பேதமை கண்டொழுகு வார்"],"p":"","r":"The woman says she will sit counting it an offence that the lord of the town of wide fields is bound by a garland, but asks why those who have watched his folly should blame the stranger women who show them no friendly face.","n":"She turns the blame from the rival women back on the man: the garland that binds him is his own choosing. The construal of the last two lines is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 46","lb":"AE46","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாமம்","தாமம்",1,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:47":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":46,"t":["போத்தில் கழுத்தில் புதல்வன் உணச்சான்றான்","மூத்தேம் இனியாம் வருமுலையார் சேரியுள்","நீத்துநீர் ஊனவாய்ப் பாண!நீ போய்மொழி","கூத்தாடி உண்ணினும் உண்"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the bard, his mouth greasy with meat, that the son has been fed at her breast and she has grown old, so let him go and speak in the street of the women whose breasts are just rising, and eat there, even if he must dance for his meal.","n":"She sets her own body, aged by nursing (மூத்தேம்), against the budding breasts of the street women, and makes the bard a hired dancer who eats where he performs. The opening words போத்தில் கழுத்தில் are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 47","lb":"AE47","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["ஊனவாய்ப்","ஊன்",3,"unavu","gift_food","meat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:48":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":47,"t":["யாணர்நல் லூரன் திறங்கிளப்பல் என்னுடை","பாண! இருக்க வதுகளை - நாணுடையான்","தன்னுற்ற எல்லாம் இருக்க இரும்பாண!","நின்உற்ற(து) உண்டேல் உரை"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her bard to leave off speaking of the ways of the lord of the rich good town: let that man of shame keep all that has befallen him, and let the great bard tell what has befallen himself, if anything has.","n":"நாணுடையான், 'the man of shame', is pure irony: the man without shame is given the virtue he lacks, and the bard is invited to talk about anything but his master.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","thoothu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 48","lb":"AE48","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:49":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":48,"t":["உழலை முருக்கிய செந்நோக்(கு) எருமை","பழனம் படிந்துசெய் மாந்தி - நிழல்வதியும்","தண்டுறை யூரன் மலரன்ன மால்புறப்","பெண்டிர்க்(கு) உரைபாண! உய்த்து"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the bard to lead his master off and tell the flower-like women of the other street what he is: the lord of the cool-ford town, where the red-eyed buffalo that broke its wooden clog sinks into the field pond, gorges on the crop and lies at ease in the shade.","n":"The flower-like women are meant to hear in the buffalo a portrait of the man they are getting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 49","lb":"AE49","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","ullurai","buffalo"],["மலரன்ன","மலர்",3,"maram","simile","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","எருமை","confirmed","1/1","the husband who has left home for the other women"]],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:50":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":49,"t":["பேதை புகலை புதல்வன் துணைச்சான்றோன்","ஓதை மலிமகிழ்நற்(கு) யாஅம் எவன்செய்தும்","பூவார் குழற்கூந்தல் பொன்னன்னார் சேரியுள்","ஓவாது செல்பாண! நீ"],"p":"","r":"The wife says it is folly for him to seek entry with the son as his backing: what can they do for the lord of loud revels? Let the bard go on without pause to the street of the gold-like women with flower-filled hair.","n":"ஓவாது செல், 'go without ceasing', tells the bard where his real work lies: he is as much a regular of that street as his master. Line 1 (பேதை புகலை புதல்வன் துணைச்சான்றோன்) is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","thoothu","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 50","lb":"AE50","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பூவார்","பூ",3,"maram","ornament","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:51":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":50,"t":["பொய்கைநல் லூரன் திறங்கிளத்தல் என்னுடைய","எவ்வம் எனினும் எழுந்தீக - வைகல்","மறுவில் பொலந்தொடி வீசும் அலற்றும்","சிறுவன் உடையேன் துணை"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the bard to get up and go, though to speak of the lord of the good pond-town is her own grief: she has as her companion her little son, who every day waves his flawless gold bangles and cries.","n":"துணை closes the poem: the son, not the husband, is named her partner, so the bard's plea has nothing to fill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","oodal","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 51","lb":"AE51","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பொலந்தொடி","பொலம் தொடி",3,"pira","ornament","gold bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:52":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":51,"t":["உண்டுறைப் பொய்கை வராஅல் இனம்இரியும்","தண்டுறை யூர! தருவதோ? - ஒண்டொடியைப்","பாராய் மனைதுறந்(து) அச்சேரிச் செல்வதனை","ஊராண்மை யாக்கிக் கொளல்"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the cool-ford town, where the shoal of varaal fish scatters in the drinking pond, whether it is fitting to pass by the bright-bangled wife without a look, leave the house for that street, and call it manliness before the town.","n":"The fleeing fish may hint at the household scattered by his going, as in the long marutham buffalo scenes, but the poem gives only one such match, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","aanmai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 52","lb":"AE52","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வராஅல்","வரால்",1,"pira","scene","varaal fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:53":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":52,"t":["வளவயல் ஊரன் மருளுரைக்கு மாதர்","வளைகிய சக்கரத்(து) ஆழி - கொளைபிழையா","வென்றிடை யிட்டு வருமேல்நின் வாழ்நாட்கள்","ஒன்றி அனைத்தும் உளேன்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker sets the woman's bewilderment at the deluding words of the lord of the fertile-field town against a pledge: if he comes back having won, as the wheel's rim never misses, she will be one with him through all his days.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 (the bent wheel and its unerring rim) are obscure, and the poem's situation cannot be fixed with confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 53","lb":"AE53","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:54":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":53,"t":["உள்நாட்டம் சான்றவர் தந்த நசையிற்றென்(று)","எண்ணார்க்குக் கண்ணோட்டம் தீர்க்குதும்என்(று) - எண்ணி","வழிபாடு கொள்ளும் வயவயல் ஊரன்","பழிபாடு நின்மே லது"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the strong-field town, who accepts the wife's devoted service, that if he thinks her merely the gift of the wise elders' wish and resolves to withhold kindly regard from those who do not reckon with him, the blame of it falls on him.","n":"The wife's வழிபாடு (worshipful service) is set against his கண்ணோட்டம் withheld: the poem turns an ethical term into the measure of a husband. The construal of lines 1 and 2 is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","pazhi","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 54","lb":"AE54","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:55":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":54,"t":["காதலில் தீரக் கழிய முயங்கல்மின்","ஓதம் துவன்றும் ஒலிபுனல் ஊரனைப்","பேதைப்பட்(டு) ஏங்கல்மின் நீயிரும் எண்ணிலா","ஆசை ஒழிய வுரைத்து"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the other women not to embrace the lord of the town of loud waters, where the flood crowds in, to the full end of love, nor to pine for him in folly; let them too speak so that their endless longing may leave them.","n":"நீயிரும், 'you too', puts the rivals in the wife's own case: they will be left as she was, so her warning is half scorn and half fellow-feeling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 55","lb":"AE55","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","parathai",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:56":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":55,"t":["தேன்கமழ் பொய்கை அகவயல் ஊரனைப்","பூங்கண் புதல்வன் மிதித்துழக்க - ஈங்குத்","தளர்முலை பாராட்டி என்னுடைய பாவை","வளர்முலைக் கண்ஞமுக்கு வார்"],"p":"","r":"The wife says the lord of the town of honey-scented ponds among the fields, letting their flower-eyed son trample and kick him here, praises her slackened breast, while he is the one who presses the budding breasts of his young 'doll'.","n":"தளர்முலை against வளர்முலை: the nursing mother's breast and the girl's rising breast meet in one line, and the domestic scene with the son is exposed as half of a double life. Reading பாவை as the young rival is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 56","lb":"AE56","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:4"},"ae:57":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":56,"t":["ஒழுகு நிரைக்கரை வான்குருகின் தூவி","உழிதரும் ஊதை எடுக்கும் துறைவனைப்","பேதையான் என்றுணரும் நெஞ்சும் இனி(து)உண்மை","ஊதியம் அன்றோ உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that the heart which has come to know the shore lord, of the bank where the roaming cold wind lifts the down of the white herons ranged along it, for a simple fool is itself a sweet thing, for is not the truth a gain to one's life?","n":"The down that the wind picks up and carries off is the lightest thing on the shore, and the poem sets it beside a man the heart now judges light; only one correspondence can be named, so it is scene, not ullurai. The bitterness is turned into consolation: knowing the truth, however hard, is a gain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","nenju","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 57","lb":"AE57","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:5"},"ae:58":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":57,"t":["என்னைகொல் தோழி! அவர்கண்ணும் நன்கில்லை","அன்னை முகனும் அதுவாகும் - பொன்னலர்","புன்னையம் பூங்கானல் சேர்ப்பனைத் தக்கதோ","நின்னல்ல(து) இல்லென்(று) உரை"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend what is to be done, since there is no kindness even in his eyes and her mother's face has turned the same way, and bids her tell the lord of the shore grove of golden-flowered punnai whether this is fitting, saying she has no one but him.","n":"The mother's face has 'become that too' (அதுவாகும்): the secret is close to discovery at home just as the lover cools, so the plea that she has none but him is a push toward marriage.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 58","lb":"AE58","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புன்னையம்","புன்னை",3,"maram","scene","punnai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:5"},"ae:59":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":58,"t":["இடுமணல் எக்கர் அகன்கானல் சேர்ப்பன்","கடுமான் மணியவரம் என்று- கொடுங்குழை","புள்ளரவம் கேட்டுப் பெயர்ந்தாள் சிறுகுடியர்","உள்ளரவம் நாணுவர் என்று"],"p":"","r":"Taking the noise of birds for the bells of the swift horses of the shore lord, of the wide grove with its heaped sand dunes, the girl of the curved earrings rose and then turned back when she heard what it was, afraid that the people of the little hamlet would make talk of her.","n":"Her turning back has two causes folded together: the sound was only birds, and going out to meet a chariot would give the hamlet its gossip; the waiting girl is caught between longing and the town's eyes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","alar","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 59","lb":"AE59","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கடுமான்","கடு மா",2,"maa","scene","horse"],["புள்ளரவம்","புள்",3,"pul","scene","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:5"},"ae:60":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":59,"t":["மணிநிற நெய்தல் இருங்கழிச் சேர்ப்பன்","அணிநலம் உண்டகன்றான் என்றுகொல் எம்போல்","திணிமணல் எக்கர்மேல் ஓதம் பெயரத்","துணிமுந்நீர் துஞ்சா தது"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks whether it is because the lord of the dark backwater of sapphire-blue neythal took his fill of her beauty and left that the sea, like her, does not sleep as its tide shifts over the packed sand dunes.","n":"She does not say the sea keeps her awake; she makes the sea a fellow sufferer and gives it her own cause, so the restless tide becomes a second heroine deserted by the same man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nalam","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 60","lb":"AE60","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","scene","neythal flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:5"},"ae:61":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":60,"t":["கண்ணுறு நெய்தல் கமழும் கொடுங்கழித்","தண்ணந் துறைவனோ தன்இலன் ஆயிழாய்!","வண்ணகைப் பட்டதனை ஆண்மை எனக்கருதிப்","பண்ணமைத் தேர்மேல் வரும்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the jewelled girl that the cool lord of the curved backwater, fragrant with neythal that meets the eye, is no longer his own master: holding it manliness to have been caught by her lovely smile, he comes on his well-made chariot.","n":"கண்ணுறு நெய்தல் can be the neythal that meets the eye or the neythal like eyes; and the joke is that his manliness consists in being captured, so the friend reassures the girl by mocking him gently.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","aanmai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 61","lb":"AE61","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","scene","neythal flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:5"},"ae:62":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":61,"t":["எறிசுறாக் குப்பை இனங்கலக்கத் தாக்கும்","ஏறிதிரைச் சேர்ப்பன் கொடுமை - யறியாகொல்","கானகம் நண்ணி அருள்அற் றிடக்கண்டும்","கானலுள் வாழும் குருகு"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks whether the heron living in the shore grove does not know the cruelty of the lord of the shore, whose leaping waves strike the shoals of sharks till their kinds are mixed, though it saw him go off to the forest land and turn graceless.","n":"The heron was the one witness of the secret union, and her question accuses it of keeping silent; the waves that throw the sharks into confusion carry the cruelty she names, but only one correspondence is stated, so it is not ullurai.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","kalaviyal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 62","lb":"AE62","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எறிசுறாக்","சுறா",1,"pira","scene","shark"],["குருகு","குருகு",4,"pul","scene","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:5"},"ae:63":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":62,"t":["நுண்ஞான் வலையில் பரதவர் போத்தந்த","பன்மீன் உணங்கல் கவரும் துறைவனைக்","கண்ணினாற் காண அமையுங்கொல் என்தோழி!","வண்ணந்தா என்கம் தொடுத்து"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether merely seeing with their eyes the lord of the shore, where the many fish the fishermen brought in their fine-corded nets are snatched as they dry, will be enough, and proposes that they accost him and say 'give back her colour'.","n":"The catch the fishermen laboured for is snatched while it dries, as her colour, won from her, was taken; only this one correspondence holds, so it stays scene, a near miss on ullurai condition 3.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","nalam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 63","lb":"AE63","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",1,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["உணங்கல்","உணங்கல்",2,"unavu","scene","dried fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ae:5"},"ae:64":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":63,"t":["சிறுமீன் கவுள்கொண்ட செந்தூவி நாராய்","இறுமென் குரலநின் பிள்ளைகட்கே யாகி","நெறிநீர் இருங்கழிச் சேர்ப்பன் அகன்ற","நெறியறிதி மீன்தபு நீ"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells the red-feathered stork that holds small fish in its cheek and lives only for its young, with its soft breaking cry, that it knows the path by which the lord of the dark backwater of winding water went away, it, the killer of fish.","n":"மீன்தபு நீ turns the praise into reproach: the devoted parent is also a killer, as the man who seemed gentle proved cruel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 64","lb":"AE64","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நாராய்","நாரை",1,"pul","iraicchi","stork"],["சிறுமீன்","சிறு மீன்",1,"pira","gift_food","small fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","நாராய்","not_confirmed","0/1","a bird that keeps faith with its own brood has seen the road of a man who did not keep faith with his"]],"ch":"ae:5"},"ae:65":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":64,"t":["தெண்ணீர் இருங்கழி வேண்டும் இரைமாந்திப்","பெண்ணைமேற் சேக்கும் வணர்வாய்ப் புணர்அன்றில்!","தண்ணந் துறைவற்(கு) உரையாய் மடமொழி","வண்ணம்தா என்று தொடுத்து"],"p":"","r":"The heroine bids the curve-beaked anril, which eats the food it wants in the clear-watered dark backwater and roosts joined with its mate on the palmyra, to accost the cool lord of the shore and say in tender words 'give back her colour'.","n":"மடமொழி may be the tender words she lends the bird or a vocative to a soft-spoken friend; the vocative mark on the anril in line 2 favours the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","thoothu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 65","lb":"AE65","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புணர்அன்றில்","அன்றில்",2,"pul","iraicchi","anril bird"],["பெண்ணைமேற்","பெண்ணை",2,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","புணர்அன்றில்","confirmed","2/2","the bird that never parts from its mate is the fitting voice to shame the man who parted from her"]],"ch":"ae:5"},"ae:66":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":65,"t":["அடும்பிவர் எக்கர் அலவன் வழங்கும்","கொடுங்கழிச் சேர்ப்பன் அருளான் எனத்தெளிந்து","கள்ள மனத்தான் அயல்நெறிச் செல்லுங்கொல்","நல்வளை சோர நடந்து"],"p":"","r":"Having made sure that the lord of the curved backwater, where crabs walk the dunes overgrown with adumbu creeper, will show no grace, the speaker asks whether that thief-hearted man will go off by another's path, walking away while her good bangles slip.","n":"Who 'makes sure' (தெளிந்து) is open: the girl, who has learnt he will not be kind, or the man, sure of his own coldness; அயல்நெறி may be another woman's way or simply a road away from her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 66","lb":"AE66","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அடும்பிவர்","அடும்பு",1,"maram","scene","adumbu creeper"],["அலவன்","அலவன்",1,"pira","scene","crab"],["நல்வளை","வளை",4,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ae:5"},"ae:67":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":66,"t":["கண்டதிரள் முத்தம் பயக்கும் இருமுந்நீர்ப்","பண்டங்கொள் நாவாய் வழங்கும் துறைவனை","முண்டகக் கானலுள் கண்டேன் எனத்தெளிந்தேன்","நின்ற உணர்விலா தேன்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that she, left standing bereft of her senses, became certain she had seen the lord of the shore, where ships laden with goods ply the great sea that yields clustered pearls, in the grove of mundakam.","n":"The certainty comes only after the sight, because the sight itself took her senses away; the pearl-yielding, trading sea sets him among wealth beyond her own shore.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 67","lb":"AE67","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",1,"pira","scene","pearls"],["நாவாய்","நாவாய்",2,"pira","scene","ships"],["முண்டகக்","முண்டகம்",3,"maram","scene","mundakam"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ae:5"},"ae:68":{"book":"ainthinai_ezhupathu","num":67,"t":["இவர்திரை நீக்கியிட்(டு) எக்கர் மணன்மேல்","கவர்கால் அலவன் தனபெடை யோடு","நிகரில் இருங்கழிச் சேர்ப்ப! என்தோழி","படர்பசலை ஆயின்று தோள்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the matchless dark backwater, where the forked-legged crab stays with its mate on the dune of sand the climbing waves have left, that her friend's shoulders have become overspread with pallor.","n":"The crab's line has no verb of its own (தனபெடை யோடு): it is simply with its mate, and the friend leaves the man to finish the thought.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","varaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 68","lb":"AE68","poet":"மூவாதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அலவன்","அலவன்",2,"pira","iraicchi","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","அலவன்","confirmed","1/1","even the crab stays beside its mate, yet he leaves her friend to pale"]],"ch":"ae:5"},"ki:1":{"book":"kainnilai","num":1,"t":["நுகர்தல் இவரும் கிளிகடி ஏனல்","நிகரில் மடமான் எரியும் அமர் சாரல்","கானக நாடன் கலந்தான் இவன் என்று","மேனி சிதையும் பசந்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that on the slope by the millet field where parrots eager to feed are driven off and the matchless young doe moves, the girl's body wastes and turns pale, as if announcing that the lord of the forest land is the one who joined with her.","n":"The pallor does the talking: the secret union is written on her body in a way the field-watch was meant to prevent, so the line works as a push toward marriage. இவன், 'this man', may point at the hero standing there, which is why the friend is taken as the speaker; the girl herself could equally be saying it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kalaviyal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 1","lb":"KI1","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கிளிகடி","கிளி",1,"pul","scene","parrot"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",1,"maram","scene","millet"],["மடமான்","மான்",2,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:1"},"ki:2":{"book":"kainnilai","num":2,"t":["வெந்த புனத்துக்கு வாச முடைத்தாகச்","சந்தனம் ஏந்தி அருவி கொணர்ந்திடூஉம்","வஞ்ச மலைநாடன் வாரான்கொல் தோழிஎன்","நெஞ்சம் நடுங்கி வரும்"],"p":"","r":"The girl asks her friend whether the deceitful hill lord, from whose hill the waterfall carries sandalwood down so that the burnt clearing grows fragrant, will not come, for her heart comes trembling.","n":"வஞ்ச, deceitful, sits right against the stream's kindness: the water brings sweetness to scorched ground unasked, while he, who scorched her, does not come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","vanjam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 2","lb":"KI2","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["சந்தனம்","சந்தனம்",2,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["அருவி","அருவி",2,"pira","iraicchi","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","அருவி","confirmed","1/1","the hill's own stream heals the scorched field as the hero ought to come and soothe the girl he has left burning, so his absence is the deceit"]],"ch":"ki:1"},"ki:3":{"book":"kainnilai","num":3,"t":["பாசிப் பசுஞ்சுனைப் பாங்கர் அழிமுதுநீர்","காய்சின மந்தி பயின்று கனிசுவைக்கும்","பாசம்பட் டோ டும் படுகல் மலைநாடற்கு","ஆசையின் தேம்பும் என் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"","r":"The girl says that her heart pines with longing for the lord of the hill of trap-stones, where by the green moss-grown spring the fierce female monkey, used to the place, tastes the fruit and, caught in the noose, runs.","n":"பாசம் is both the hunter's noose and attachment, so the snared monkey and the 'bond' of love are one word. The subject of the running in line 3 is not certain; if it is not the monkey, the hidden comparison weakens to a mood.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nenju","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 3","lb":"KI3","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பாசிப்","பாசி",1,"maram","scene","moss"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",2,"maa","ullurai","female monkey"],["கனிசுவைக்கும்","கனி",2,"maram","gift_food","fruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","மந்தி","not_confirmed","0/2","the girl herself, who tasted union and is now bound by longing"]],"ch":"ki:1"},"ki:4":{"book":"kainnilai","num":4,"t":["ஓங்கல் விழுப்பலவின் இன்பம் கொளீஇய","தீங்கனி மாவின் முசுப்பாய் மலைநாடன்","தான்கலந்து உள்ளாத் தகையானோ நேரிழாய்","தேங்கலந்த சொல்லின் தெளித்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the jewelled girl whether the hill lord, from whose hill the langur leaps off the sweet-fruited mango to take the pleasure of the tall jackfruit, is a man to join with her and then forget, he who assured her with words mixed with honey.","n":"The friend's comfort carries a shadow: the langur that leaves one sweet tree for a sweeter one is the very fickleness she says he lacks. It is a single mood with no second pair and no stated situation, so it is not tagged as ullurai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","ninaivu","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 4","lb":"KI4","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["விழுப்பலவின்","பலா",1,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["மாவின்","மா",2,"maram","scene","mango"],["முசுப்பாய்","முசு",2,"maa","scene","langur"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ki:1"},"ki:5":{"book":"kainnilai","num":5,"t":["இரசங்கொண்டு இன்தேன் இரைக்கும் குரலைப்","பிரசை இரும்பிடி பேணி வரூஉம்","முரசருவி ஆர்க்கும் மலைநாடற்கு என்தோள்","நிரையம்எனக் கிடந்த வாறு"],"p":"","r":"The girl says that for the lord of the hill where the waterfall roars like a drum and the dark she-elephant comes tending a honeycomb loud with bees and full of sweet honey, her shoulders lie as though in hell.","n":"Hell is a startling vehicle in a love quatrain and belongs to the later moral vocabulary of the didactic books. குரல் in line 1 may be the bees' hum or a millet ear, and the first two lines do not settle what the elephant carries.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["melivu","kaamanoy","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 5","lb":"KI5","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["இன்தேன்","தேன்",1,"unavu","scene","honey"],["பிரசை","பிரசம்",2,"pira","scene","honeycomb"],["இரும்பிடி","பிடி",2,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["முரசருவி","அருவி",3,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ki:1"},"ki:6":{"book":"kainnilai","num":6,"t":["மரையா உகளும் மரம்பயில் சோலை","உரைசால் மடமந்தி ஓடி உகளும்","புரைதீர் மலைநாடன் பூண்ஏந்து அகலம்","உரையா வழங்குமென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"","r":"The girl says that, unspoken, her heart goes to the jewelled broad chest of the faultless hill lord, from whose grove thick with trees the wild cow leaps and the famed young female monkey runs and leaps.","n":"உகளும் is repeated for the wild cow and the monkey, and the heart's going is the third leap; the creatures carry a mood of unchecked movement only, so they are scene. உரையா, without telling, makes the heart a secret traveller, which is the whole of kalavu.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 6","lb":"KI6","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மரையா","மரையா",1,"maa","scene","wild cow"],["மடமந்தி","மந்தி",2,"maa","scene","female monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ki:1"},"ki:7":{"book":"kainnilai","num":7,"t":["கல்வரை ஏறிக் கடுவன் கனிவாழை","எல்உறு போழ்தின் இனிய பழங்கவுள்கொண்டு","ஒல்என ஓடு மலைநாடன் தன்கேண்மை","சொல்லச் சொரியும் வளை"],"p":"","r":"The girl says that when the friendship of the hill lord is spoken of, her bangles slip off: in his land the male monkey climbs the rock, stuffs the sweet ripe banana into its cheek in the daylight and runs off with a rush.","n":"கேண்மை, friendship, is ironic after the picture of the monkey that grabs and bolts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","natpu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 7","lb":"KI7","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",1,"maa","iraicchi","male monkey"],["கனிவாழை","வாழை",1,"maram","gift_food","plantain"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கடுவன்","not_confirmed","0/1","the hero, like the monkey, took his pleasure and made off, which is why the mere mention of his friendship loosens her bangles"],["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ki:1"},"ki:8":{"book":"kainnilai","num":8,"t":["கருங்கை கதவேழம் கார்ப்பாம்புக் குப்பங்","கி...க்...கொண்...கரும்","பெருங்கல் மலைநாடன் பேணி வரினே","சுருங்கும் இவள்உற்ற நோய்"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that if the lord of the great rocky hill, where the black-trunked angry elephant and the dark snake are named in a line now partly lost, comes caring for the girl, the sickness she suffers will shrink.","n":"The cure named is the man, not a rite: பேணி வரினே, if he comes cherishing her, reads as coming to wed. The addressee may be the hero, urged to marry, or the foster mother, told the true cause of the illness; the poem does not decide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 8","lb":"KI8","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கதவேழம்","வேழம்",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கார்ப்பாம்புக்","பாம்பு",1,"pira","scene","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:1"},"ki:9":{"book":"kainnilai","num":9,"t":["காந்தள ரும்புகை என்று கதவேழம்","ஏந்தல் மருப்பிடைக் கைவைத்து இனனோக்கிப்","பாய்ந்தெழுந்து ஓடும் பயமலை நன்னாடன்","காய்ந்தான்கொல் நம்கண் கலப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The girl asks her friend whether the lord of the good fruitful hill, where the angry elephant, taking the kaanthal's bud for smoke, lays its trunk between its raised tusks, looks to its herd and leaps up and flees, has grown averse to union with them.","n":"The word division of line 1 is unsure (அரும்பு புகை, the bud as smoke, or அரும்பு உகை, the bud rising); the elephant's flight from a harmless flower is what the hidden comparison rests on.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kalaviyal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 9","lb":"KI9","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காந்தள","காந்தள்",1,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["கதவேழம்","வேழம்",1,"maa","ullurai","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","கதவேழம்","confirmed","1/1","the hero who keeps away"]],"ch":"ki:1"},"ki:10":{"book":"kainnilai","num":10,"t":["பொன்இணர் வேங்கைப் புனஞ்சூழ் மலைநாடன்","மின்னின் அனையவேல் ஏந்தி இரவினுள்","இன்னே வரும்கண்டாய் தோழி இடையாமத்து","என்னை இமைபொரு மாறு"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that the lord of the hill whose millet fields are ringed by vengai with golden clusters will come right now at midnight, bearing a spear like lightning, so how could her eyelids close.","n":"இமைபொரு, eyelids meeting, may mean she lies sleepless waiting or that only his coming lets her sleep; either way the midnight tryst is fixed in her mind before it happens.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 10","lb":"KI10","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene","vengai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:1"},"ki:11":{"book":"kainnilai","num":11,"t":["எறிகிளர் கேழல் கிளைத்திட்ட பூமி","பொறிகிளர் மஞ்ஞை புகன்று குடையும்","முறிகிளர் நன்மலை நாடன் வருமே","அரிதுரைத்திவ் இல்லில் நமக்கு"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether the lord of the good hill bright with tender leaves, where the spotted peacock delights in scratching the ground a fierce boar has dug up, will come, since coming is hard for him to this house of theirs.","n":"The peacock that takes its pleasure in ground the boar has turned is a picture of one creature enjoying another's labour; it has no single act a person of the situation does, so it stays scene. The house in line 4 suggests the girl is now kept indoors.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 11","lb":"KI11","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கேழல்","கேழல்",1,"maa","scene","boar"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",2,"pul","scene","peacock"],["முறிகிளர்","முறி",3,"maram","scene","tender leaves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:1"},"ki:12":{"book":"kainnilai","num":12,"t":["நாக நறுமலர்நாள் வேங்கைப் பூவிரவிக்","கேசம் அணிந்த கிளர்எழிலோன் ஆகம்","முடியுங் கொல் என்றுமுனிவான் ஒருவன்","வடிவேல்கை ஏந்தி வரும்"],"p":"","r":"The friend warns that a man, angry, comes with a sharp spear in hand, asking whether the chest of that bright handsome one who wears fragrant naagam flowers mixed with fresh vengai blossoms in his hair can be ended.","n":"Who the angry spearman is the poem does not say; he is most simply one of the girl's kin who has learned of the visits, and the warning is the friend's way of shutting the night path.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","kalaviyal","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 12","lb":"KI12","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["நாக","நாகம்",1,"maram","ornament","naagam tree"],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",1,"maram","ornament","vengai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:1"},"ki:13":{"book":"kainnilai","num":13,"t":["கடுகி அதர்அலைக்கும் கல்சூழ் பதுக்கை","விடுவில் எயினர்தம் வீளைஓர்த் தோடும்","நெடுவிடை அத்தம் செலவுரைப்பக் கேட்டே","வடுவிடை மெல்கின கண்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that when she heard him speak of leaving by the long wasteland, where travellers hurry among stone-ringed cairns and run at the whistle of the Eyinar loosing their bows, her eyes, shaped like split tender mango, went soft with tears.","n":"Nothing has happened yet except talk: the eyes give way at செலவுரைப்ப, the telling of the journey, so the poem shows the parting doing its harm before the man has moved. The first line is hard to construe (who hurries and harries the path), and the speaker is not named; the friend reporting to the man is the likeliest frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 13","lb":"KI13","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["எயினர்தம்","எயினர்",2,"seythi","scene","hunters"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:2"},"ki:14":{"book":"kainnilai","num":14,"t":["கதநாய் துரப்ப..................................","............................................ அவிழும்","புதல்மாறு வெங்கானம் போக்குரைப்ப நில்லா","முதன்..................."],"p":"","r":"Only fragments survive: fierce dogs give chase, something blossoms, and when the journey through the hot forest where the bushes have withered is spoken of, something will not stay; the end is lost.","n":"போக்குரைப்ப repeats the situation of verse 13 (செலவுரைப்ப), so the poem probably turned on her response to the news of his going; நில்லா may have described her tears or her strength failing, but the lines that would say so are lost. The speaker is a guess from verse 13.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 14","lb":"KI14","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கதநாய்","கத நாய்",1,"maa","scene","dog"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:2"},"ki:15":{"book":"kainnilai","num":15,"t":["...........................................","...........................................","கடுங்கதிர் வெங்கானம் பல்லாருட்கண் சென்றார்","கொடுங்கல் மலை...."],"p":"","r":"Only the last lines survive: the one who went has crossed the hot forest of fierce sunrays, a place many dread (or: in a company of many), and a hill of crooked rocks is named before the text breaks off.","n":"பல்லாருட்கண் can be divided as பல்லார் உட்கு (what many fear) or பல்லார் உள் கண் (among many), and the choice decides whether the line is about danger or company. The speaker is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 15","lb":"KI15","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:2"},"ki:16":{"book":"kainnilai","num":16,"t":["............. வுறையு மெல்லென் கடத்துக்","கடுஞ்சின வேங்கை கதழ்வேழம் சாய்க்கு","...........................................","........................................... நமர்"],"p":"","r":"Of a damaged verse, what remains says that in the wasteland the fierce tiger brings down the charging elephant, and that 'our man' is somewhere in the lost close.","n":"The tiger felling the elephant is the danger of the road at its most extreme: even the strongest beast is prey there, which is what makes the absent man's safety a question. Whether the lost close feared for him or promised his return cannot be told.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 16","lb":"KI16","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",2,"maa","scene","tiger"],["கதழ்வேழம்","வேழம்",2,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:2"},"ki:17":{"book":"kainnilai","num":17,"t":["கடமா இரிந்தோடும் கல்லதர் அத்தம்","மடமா இரும்பிடி வேழ மரு.............","....................ண்ட உண்கண் ணுள்நீர்",".........................................."],"p":"","r":"The stony wasteland where the wild gaur flee is named, with a young dark she-elephant and a tusker, and then the tears within kohl-lined eyes; the rest is lost.","n":"A she-elephant and a tusker named together is where Sangam paalai often places a pair of creatures acting for each other, the textbook iraicchi; here the line breaks at மரு.... and no act survives, so the pair stays scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 17","lb":"KI17","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கடமா","கடமா",1,"maa","scene","gaur"],["இரும்பிடி","பிடி",2,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["வேழ","வேழம்",2,"maa","scene","tusker"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:2"},"ki:18":{"book":"kainnilai","num":18,"t":["ஆமா சிலைக்கும் அணிவரை ஆரிடை","ஏமாண் சிலையார்க்கும் இனமா இரிந்துஓடும்","தாமாண்பில் வெஞ்சுரம் சென்றார் வரக்கண்டு","வாய்மாண்ட பல்லி படும்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that in the hard way among lovely hills, where wild cattle low and herds of beasts flee when the bow with its fine arrows twangs, the truthful-mouthed lizard has chirped as if it has seen the one who went through that graceless hot waste coming home.","n":"வாய்மாண்ட praises the lizard's mouth as truthful, so the chirp is offered as testimony rather than hope; the verb கண்டு even gives the lizard eyes, as though it had seen the return before anyone at home. The hill with its lowing wild cattle is a kurinji scene set on the paalai road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 18","lb":"KI18","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆமா","ஆ மா",1,"maa","scene","wild cattle"],["இனமா","இனம் மா",2,"maa","scene","herd beasts"],["பல்லி","பல்லி",4,"pira","scene","lizard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:2"},"ki:19":{"book":"kainnilai","num":19,"t":["அரக்கார்ந்த ஓமை அரிபடு நீழல்","செருக்கில் கடுங்களிறு சென்றுறங்கி நிற்கும்","பரற்கானம் பல்பொருட்குச் சென்றார் வருவர்","நுதற்கு இவர்ந் தேறும் ஒளி"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that the man who went for much wealth through the gravel forest, where the fierce proud tusker goes to stand asleep in the thin, broken shade of the lac-laden omai tree, will come back: the brightness climbing again onto her forehead says so.","n":"The proof is in her own body: the pallor is receding before he arrives, and the friend reads the returning light (இவர்ந்து ஏறும், climbing up) as a sign, not as an effect of his coming. The tusker sleeping upright in shade too broken to cool it is how the forest's heat is shown; it does nothing a lover does, so it is scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","pasalai","selvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 19","lb":"KI19","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஓமை","ஓமை",1,"maram","scene",""],["கடுங்களிறு","களிறு",2,"maa","scene","tusker"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:2"},"ki:20":{"book":"kainnilai","num":20,"t":["..............................................................","........................................... வீழ்க்கும்","ஓவாத வெங்கானம் சென்றார்........","..............வார் வருவார் நமர்"],"p":"","r":"A damaged verse: something is made to fall, the one who went has crossed the ceaseless hot forest, and the surviving close says that our man will come.","n":"வருவார் நமர் closes the verse with the promise verse 19 makes (வருவர்), so the speaker is probably the comforting friend, but the lost lines may have held the reason she gives.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 20","lb":"KI20","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:2"},"ki:21":{"book":"kainnilai","num":21,"t":["ஆந்தை குறுங்கலி கொள்ளநம் ஆடவர்","காய்ந்து கதிர்தெறூஉம் கானம் கடந்தார்பின்","ஏந்தல் இளமுலை ஈர்எயிற்றாய் என்நெஞ்சு","நீந்து நெடுவிடைச் சென்று"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend of young lifted breasts and moist teeth that, as the owl begins its short hooting and our man has crossed the forest where the rays burn and scorch, her heart goes after him, swimming the long way.","n":"The dry forest is crossed by swimming: the heart moves through the scorched land as if through water, so the metaphor carries the effort and the danger of drowning into a waterless place. The owl's short cry marks the night falling as she speaks.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 21","lb":"KI21","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஆந்தை","ஆந்தை",1,"pul","scene","owl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:2"},"ki:22":{"book":"kainnilai","num":22,"t":["கள்வர் திரிதரூஉம் கானம் கடந்தவர்","உள்ளம் பிரிந்தமை நீஅறிதி - ஒள்இழாய்","தொல்லை விடரகம் நீந்திப் பெயர்ந்தவர்","வல்லைநாம் காணும் வரவு"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the woman of bright jewels whether she really thinks the heart of the man who crossed the forest where robbers roam has parted from her, and says that he will cross back over the old mountain clefts and they will soon see him come.","n":"Line 2 can also be read as a statement, 'you know he parted from you in mind (by his own resolve)', which would make the comfort harder; the question reading fits the confident close. The friend uses the same verb of swimming that the woman used of her heart in verse 21, now for the man's crossing home.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 22","lb":"KI22","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கள்வர்","கள்வர்",1,"seythi","scene","robbers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:2"},"ki:23":{"book":"kainnilai","num":23,"t":["சிலையொலி வெங்கணையார் சிந்தியா நெஞ்சில்","கொலைபுரி வில்லொடு கூற்றுபோல் ஓடும்","இலையொலி வெங்கானத்து இப்பருவம் சென்றார்","தொலைவிலர்கொல் தோழி நமர்"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether the man who went in this very season, through the hot forest of rustling leaves where men with cruel arrows and twanging bows, their hearts empty of thought, run like Death with bows bent on killing, has come to no harm.","n":"தொலைவிலர்கொல் turns on தொலைவு: as ruin it asks whether he is unharmed; as distance it asks whether he is not far away now, i.e. near home. The fear of the archers favours the first. சிந்தியா நெஞ்சு, hearts that do not think, makes the killers' danger their thoughtlessness rather than malice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kootru","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 23","lb":"KI23","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["வெங்கணையார்","வெங்கணையார்",1,"seythi","simile","archers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:2"},"ki:24":{"book":"kainnilai","num":24,"t":["வெஞ்சுரம் தேரோட வெஃகிநின்று அத்தமாச்","சிந்தையான் நீர்என்று செத்துத் தவாஓடும்","பண்பில் அருஞ்சுரம் என்பவால் ஆய்தொடி","நண்பிலார் சென்ற நெறி"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend of chosen bangles that the road taken by the loveless one is, they say, the graceless, hard waste where, on the burning track as the mirage runs, the deer of the wilderness, craving, takes it in its mind for water and runs on without end.","n":"The deer that runs forever after false water is close to the man who left for gain, but the poem draws no second correspondence and does not apply it, so it fails the two-pair condition of ullurai and stays scene; a reader may still hear it. பண்பில் (graceless) of the road and நண்பிலார் (loveless) of the man are set in one rhyme, so the land's want of kindness becomes his. The speaker could also be the friend, since ஆய்தொடி usually addresses the heroine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 24","lb":"KI24","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அத்தமாச்","அத்தம் மா",1,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:2"},"ki:25":{"book":"kainnilai","num":25,"t":["கார்செய் புறவில் கவினிக் கொடிமுல்லை","கூர்எயிறு ஈனக் குருத்தரும்ப - ஓரும்","வருவர்நம் காதலர் வாள்தடங் கண்ணாய்","பருவரல் பைதல்நோய் கொண்டு"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine of wide sword-bright eyes that in the woodland the rains have made, the creeping mullai has grown lovely and put out sharp teeth as its shoots bud, so their lover will surely come: let her not take on this grieving, pallid sickness.","n":"The buds are the creeper's teeth, so the woodland already smiles; the friend's argument is that the season has kept its side of the promise and the sickness (பைதல்நோய்) has no ground left. ஓரும் is an expletive of assurance, not a verb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","kaamanoy","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 25","lb":"KI25","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கொடிமுல்லை","கொடி முல்லை",1,"maram","scene","jasmine creeper"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:3"},"ki:26":{"book":"kainnilai","num":26,"t":["குருதி மலர்த்தோன்றி கூர்முகை ஈன","........... சேவல் எனப்பிடவாம் ஏறி","பொருதீ எனவெருளும் பொன்நேர் நிறத்தாய்","அரிதவர் வாரா விடல்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine of gold-like colour that, now the blood-red thonri puts out its sharp buds, so red that a bird climbing the pidavam shies from them as from blazing fire, it is unlikely he will fail to come.","n":"Line 2 opens with a gap, so who takes the buds for what is partly guessed: சேவல் என may mean the creature takes the red bud for a cock's comb before shying from it as fire. அரிது ... வாரா விடல் is a double negative: his not coming is the hard thing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["paruvam","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 26","lb":"KI26","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மலர்த்தோன்றி","தோன்றி",1,"maram","scene","gloriosa lily"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",2,"pul","scene","male bird"],["பிடவாம்","பிடவம்",2,"maram","scene","pidavam shrub"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:3"},"ki:27":{"book":"kainnilai","num":27,"t":[".......................................................................",".............................. ஒல்கப் புகுதரு","கார்தரு மாலை கலந்தார் வரவுள்ளி","ஊர்தரு மேனி பசப்பு"],"p":"","r":"In the evening the rains bring, when something now lost comes in swaying, pallor spreads over her body as she thinks of the coming of the man who was one with her.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are lost but for ஒல்கப் புகுதரு, perhaps the herds or the cloud entering at dusk. The pallor rises from thinking of his coming (வரவுள்ளி), not of his going: expectation, not only absence, feeds it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","ninaivu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 27","lb":"KI27","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:3"},"ki:28":{"book":"kainnilai","num":28,"t":[".......................................................................","................. பெய்த புறவில் கடுமான்தேர்","ஒல்லைக் கடவாவார் இவர்காணின் காதலர்","சில்.........................................................."],"p":"","r":"In the woodland where the rain has poured, the friend says, their lover, if he saw these signs, would drive his chariot of swift horses back without delay; the rest is lost.","n":"இவர் is read as 'these' (the season's signs) rather than a person; with lines 1 and 4 lost, the conditional (if he saw) could also be a reproach that he has not seen them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["paruvam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 28","lb":"KI28","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கடுமான்தேர்","கடுமான் தேர்",2,"maa","scene","swift horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:3"},"ki:29":{"book":"kainnilai","num":29,"t":["........................................... குருந்தலரப்","பீடார் இரலை பிணைதழுவக் காடாரக்","கார்வானம் வந்து முழங்................","..........................................."],"p":"","r":"A fragment: the kurunthu blooms, the proud stag embraces his doe, and the rain-cloud comes over the forest and thunders; how the scene was applied to the lovers is lost.","n":"The stag embracing his doe is the classic iraicchi carrier of mullai, but with the closing lines lost the deletion test cannot be run, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 29","lb":"KI29","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருந்தலரப்","குருந்து",1,"maram","scene","kurunthu"],["இரலை","இரலை",2,"maa","scene","stag"],["பிணைதழுவக்","பிணை",2,"maa","scene","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:3"},"ki:30":{"book":"kainnilai","num":30,"t":[".............................................................","............................................................","கொன்றை கொடுகுழல் ஊதிய கோவலர்","மன்றம் புகுதரும் போழ்து"],"p":"","r":"A fragment: only the time survives, the hour when the cowherds who have blown their konrai pipes come into the village common.","n":"The pipe is made of the konrai pod (கொன்றை ... குழல்), the herders' own instrument; what was said to happen at that hour is lost with lines 1 and 2.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["maalai","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 30","lb":"KI30","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",3,"maram","scene","konrai pod"],["குழல்","குழல்",3,"yaazh","scene","pipe"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",3,"seythi","scene","cowherds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:3"},"ki:31":{"book":"kainnilai","num":31,"t":["............................................................","............................................................","............................................ வானம்","வந்து துளிவழங்கக் கண்டு"],"p":"","r":"A fragment: only the end survives, seeing the cloud come and give its drops.","n":"Three of four lines are lost; the speaker and the sense of 'having seen' cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["mazhai","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 31","lb":"KI31","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:3"},"ki:32":{"book":"kainnilai","num":32,"t":["காரெதிர் வானம் கதழ்எரி சி.........","............................................................",".............................லக மெழுநெஞ்சே சொல்லாயால்","கூர்எரி மாலைக் குறி"],"p":"","r":"A fragment: with the sky meeting the rains and swift fire of lightning, she asks her own heart whether it will not tell the sign set for the evening of piercing fire.","n":"The address to the heart (நெஞ்சே) survives but its first word is broken (மெழு); a returning hero urging his heart on would also fit. The evening called sharp fire repeats the lightning's fire of line 1, as if the evening itself burns.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kuri","nenju","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 32","lb":"KI32","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:3"},"ki:33":{"book":"kainnilai","num":33,"t":["தளையவிழ்தே......................................","..............................................................","உளையார் கலிநன் மாப்பூட்டி வருவார்","களையாரோ நீயுற்ற நோய்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine, as the buds loosen their bonds, that he will come yoking his fine, spirited, plumed horses: will he not take away the sickness she has suffered?","n":"Only the promise and its question survive; the horses' plumes (உளை) belong to the chariot of return, and the question form makes the cure his to bring, not hers to find.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["paruvam","kaamanoy","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 33","lb":"KI33","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாப்பூட்டி","மா",3,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:3"},"ki:34":{"book":"kainnilai","num":34,"t":["முல்லை எயிறுஈன........................",".....................................ன மல்கிக்","கடல்முகந்து கார்பொழியக் காதலர் வந்தார்","உடனியைந்த கெ....................................."],"p":"","r":"As the mullai put out its teeth and the clouds, having scooped the sea, pour their rain, the lover has come; the rest, about their being joined, is broken.","n":"Unlike its neighbours the verse reports the arrival as done (வந்தார்), so the season's signs are here a fulfilment, not a promise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","paruvam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 34","lb":"KI34","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",1,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:3"},"ki:35":{"book":"kainnilai","num":35,"t":["...........................................................","............................. ரடைப் பால்வாய் இடையர்","தெரிவிலர் தீங்குழல் ஊதும் பொழுதால்","அரித"],"p":"","r":"A fragment: at the hour when the herders, milk on their lips and heedless of others' grief, blow their sweet pipes, something is hard to bear.","n":"தெரிவிலர், they do not know, makes the herders innocent of the pain they cause; the pipe is sweet, and the torment is in her hearing it at that hour. பால்வாய் is read as milk-mouthed, the herders fresh from the milking; the last line breaks off at அரித.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 35","lb":"KI35","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["இடையர்","இடையர்",2,"seythi","scene","herdsmen"],["தீங்குழல்","குழல்",3,"yaazh","scene","pipe"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:3"},"ki:36":{"book":"kainnilai","num":36,"t":["பிடவங் குருந்தொடு பிண்டி மலர","மடவமயில் கூவ மந்திமா கூரத்","தடமலர்க் கோதையாய் தங்கார் வருவர்","இடபமெனக் கொண்டு தாம்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine wearing a garland of large flowers that, now the pidavam, kurunthu and asoka bloom, the young peacock calls and the monkeys shiver in the cold, he will not stay away: he will come, taking this as his sign.","n":"இடபம் in the last line is obscure: read as the sign or token (perhaps the zodiac bull marking the season's turn); the close turns on it. The female monkey belongs to the hills, a kurinji thing in a mullai catalogue.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","thelivu","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 36","lb":"KI36","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பிடவங்","பிடவம்",1,"maram","scene","pidavam"],["குருந்தொடு","குருந்து",1,"maram","scene","kurunthu"],["பிண்டி","பிண்டி",1,"maram","scene","asoka"],["மயில்","மயில்",2,"pul","scene","peacock"],["மந்திமா","மந்தி",2,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["கோதையாய்","கோதை",3,"maram","ornament","flower garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:3"},"ki:37":{"book":"kainnilai","num":37,"t":["கழனி உழவர் கலிஅஞ்சி ஓடித்","தழென மதஎருமை தண்கயம் பாயும்","பழன வயலூரன் பாணஎம் முன்னர்ப்","பொழெனப் பொய்கூறா தொழி"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the bard of the man of the field town, where a buffalo in rut plunges with a splash into the cool pond and the ploughmen run from the uproar, to stop pouring out lies in front of her.","n":"The two sound words pair the halves: the buffalo goes in தழென and the bard's lies come out பொழென, so the bard's rush of talk is heard as the same heedless plunge. As ullurai it is a near miss on condition 3: the buffalo's plunge maps onto the man's rush to other women, but the fleeing ploughmen have no clear partner in the love situation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","vaaimai","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 37","lb":"KI37","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["உழவர்","உழவர்",1,"seythi","scene","ploughmen"],["மதஎருமை","எருமை",2,"maa","iraicchi","buffalo"],["தண்கயம்","கயம்",2,"pira","scene","pond"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மதஎருமை","not_confirmed","0/1","the man, hot with desire, goes heedless after his pleasures and leaves his household in an uproar"]],"ch":"ki:4"},"ki:38":{"book":"kainnilai","num":38,"t":["கயலினம் பாயும் கழனி நல்லூர","நயமிலேன் எம்மனை இன்றொடு வாரல்","துயிலின் இளமுலையார் தோள்நயந்து வாழ்கின்","குயி............................... கொண்டு"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man of the good town of paddy fields where shoals of carp leap that she has no liking left for him and he is not to come to her house from today, since he lives desiring the shoulders of young-breasted women; the last line is lost.","n":"With line 4 lost, what he is to take (கொண்டு) or go to is gone; what survives is a door shut 'from today' (இன்றொடு), a dated break and not a passing sulk.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["oodal","parathai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 38","lb":"KI38","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கயலினம்","கயல்",1,"pira","scene","carp"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:4"},"ki:39":{"book":"kainnilai","num":39,"t":["முட்ட முதுநீர் அடைகரை மேய்ந்தெழுந்து","தொட்ட வரிவரால் பாயும் புனல்ஊரன்","கட்டலர் கண்ணிப் புதல்வனைக் கொண்டுஎம்மில்","சுட்டி அலைய வரும்"],"p":"","r":"The wife says the man of the river town, where the striped varaal fish feeds along the bank of the full old water and then leaps, comes to their house bringing his son in a garland of bound blossoms, pointing to the boy, only to torment them.","n":"The son is his pass to the door, and she sees it: the garlanded child is shown (சுட்டி) to win entry, and she calls the visit harassment (அலைய), not a homecoming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","makkal","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 39","lb":"KI39","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["vekuli","அலை"]],"k":[["வரிவரால்","வரால்",2,"pira","scene","murrel fish"],["கண்ணிப்","கண்ணி",3,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:4"},"ki:40":{"book":"kainnilai","num":40,"t":["தாரா இரியும் தகைவயல் ஊரனை","வாரான் எனினும் வரும்என்று - சேரி","புலப்படும் சொல்லும் இப்பூங்கொடி அன்னார்","கலப்படும் கூடுங்கொல் மற்று"],"p":"","r":"Of the man of the fair field town where the tharaa ducks scatter, the street declares openly that he will come even though he does not; will these women like flowering creepers really get to join him?","n":"The street (சேரி) is most likely the other women's quarter boasting of him; the speaker turns the boast into a question that doubts they will get him either, so his neglect is shared.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","alar","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 40","lb":"KI40","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாரா","தாரா",1,"pul","scene","duck"],["பூங்கொடி","பூங்கொடி",3,"maram","simile","flowering creeper"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:4"},"ki:41":{"book":"kainnilai","num":41,"t":["பொய்கைநல் லூரன் திறன்கிளப்ப என்னுடையை?","அஃதன்று எனினும் அறிந்தோம்யாம் - செய்தி","நெறியின் இனியசொல் நீர்வாய் மழலைச்","சிறுவன் எனக்குடைமை யால்"],"p":"","r":"The wife asks the bard what he gains by reciting the merits of the man of the good pond town: even without that she knows what he has done, and she has, as her own, a little son with a wet mouth whose babbling words are sweet.","n":"அறிந்தோம் யாம் செய்தி can mean that she already knows his doings, or that she learned them from the boy's prattle; either way the son, not the bard's praise, is what she holds as hers (எனக்குடைமை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","makkal","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 41","lb":"KI41","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["பொய்கைநல்","பொய்கை",1,"pira","scene","pond"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:4"},"ki:42":{"book":"kainnilai","num":42,"t":["நீத்தம் நீர்ஊரன் நிலைமையும் வண்ணமும்","யார்க்கு ரைத்திபாண? அதனால்யாம் என்செய்தும்?","கூத்தனாக் கொண்டு குறைநீர் உடையையேல்","ஆட்டுவித்து உண்ணினும் உண்"],"p":"","r":"The wife asks the bard to whom he is telling the state and nature of the man of the flood-water town, and what she could do about it: if he is in want, let him take the man as his dancer, make him dance, and live on that.","n":"The taunt reverses the roles: the bard, who dances for his meal, is told to make the husband the performer, as if the man already dances to others' tune.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 42","lb":"KI42","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நீத்தம்","நீத்தம்",1,"pira","scene","flood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:4"},"ki:43":{"book":"kainnilai","num":43,"t":["போதவிழ் தாமரைப் பூந்துறை ஊரனைத்","தாதவிழ் கோதைத் தகையிலார் தாம்புலப்பர்","ஏதின்மை சொல்லி இருப்பர் பிறர்மகளிர்","பேதமை தம்மேலே கொண்டு"],"p":"","r":"Women of pollen-shedding garlands who have no worth sulk at the man of the flower ford where lotus buds open, while other men's women stay silent and call him a stranger, taking the folly on themselves.","n":"The verse sets two answers to his straying side by side: the open sulking of the women without standing and the silent estrangement of the wife who blames herself; which group the speaker counts herself in is the hard part.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 43","lb":"KI43","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",1,"maram","scene","lotus"],["கோதைத்","கோதை",2,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:4"},"ki:44":{"book":"kainnilai","num":44,"t":["தன்துறை ஊரன் தடமென் பணைத்தோளாய்","வண்டூது கோதை வகைநாடிக் - கொண்டிருந்து","கோல வனமுலையும் புல்லினான் என்றெடுத்துச்","சாலவும் தூற்றும் அலர்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman of broad soft shoulders that the gossip picks out the man of the ford, saying he sought out a woman's bee-hummed garland, stayed with her and embraced her lovely breasts, and spreads it far and wide.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 44","lb":"KI44","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோதை","கோதை",2,"maram","ornament","garland"],["வண்டூது","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:4"},"ki:45":{"book":"kainnilai","num":45,"t":["மூத்தேம் இனிப்பாண முன்னாயின் நாம்இளையேம்","கார்த்தண் கலிவயல் ஊரான் கடிதுஎமக்குப்","பாத்தில் பயமொழி பண்பு பலகூறி","நீத்தல் அறிந்திலேம் இன்று"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the bard that they are old now, though young before, and that when the man of the cool rain-fed noisy fields hastened to them with many promising words of his goodness, they did not know he would one day leave them, as they know today.","n":"Age is set against the earlier courtship: what she failed to foresee in youth (நீத்தல் அறிந்திலேம்) is what her ageing now lets her see.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","ilamai","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 45","lb":"KI45","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","மூப்பு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:4"},"ki:46":{"book":"kainnilai","num":46,"t":["கயநீர்ப் பாய்ந்தோடும் காஞ்சிநல் ஊரன்","நயமே பலசொல்லி நாணினன் போன்றான்","பயமில் யாழ்ப்பாண பழுதாய கூறாது","எழுநீபோ நீடாது மற்று"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the useless lute-bard that the man of the good town of kaanchi trees, where water rushes into the ponds, spoke many pleasant things and seemed ashamed, so the bard should say nothing false and get up and go at once.","n":"நாணினன் போன்றான் says he only looked ashamed: she treats his contrition as an act already seen through, which leaves the bard nothing to add.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","inchol","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 46","lb":"KI46","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["காஞ்சிநல்","காஞ்சி",1,"maram","scene","kaanchi tree"],["யாழ்ப்பாண","யாழ்",3,"yaazh","scene","lute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:4"},"ki:47":{"book":"kainnilai","num":47,"t":["அரக்காம்பல் தாமரை அம்செங் கழுநீர்","ஒருக்கார்ந்த வல்லி ஒலித்தாரக் குத்துஞ்","செருக்கார் வளவயல்ஊரன் பொய்ப் பாண","இருக்கஎம் இல்லுள் வாரல்"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the lying bard of the man of the proud rich fields, where red water-lily, lotus, lovely red kazhuneer and massed valli creepers are thrust down amid the din, not to come into her house while she is there.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are hard to construe: whether the flowers are trampled, weeded out or pierced is unclear, so any hidden sense in the crushed flowers stays a guess and is not tagged.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","vaaimai","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 47","lb":"KI47","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அரக்காம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","scene","red water-lily"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","scene","lotus"],["கழுநீர்","கழுநீர்",1,"maram","scene","red lily"],["வல்லி","வல்லி",2,"maram","scene","creeper"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:4"},"ki:48":{"book":"kainnilai","num":48,"t":["கொக்கார் வளவயல்ஊரன் குளிர் சாந்தம்","மிக்க வனமுலை புல்லான் பொலிவுடைத்தா","தக்கயாழ்ப் பாணதளர் முலையாய் மூத்தமைந்தார்","உத்தரம் வேண்டா வரல்"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the bard of the fitting lute that the man of the rich crane-filled fields does not embrace the lovely breasts heavy with cool sandal, and that they, grown old with sagging breasts, want him not to come seeking an answer.","n":"Line 2 (பொலிவுடைத்தா) is unclear: it may be her irony on his 'splendid' conduct or the splendour of the breasts he passes over; the contrast of sandal-cool breasts and slack ones carries the poem either way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","yaakkai","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 48","lb":"KI48","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","மூப்பு"]],"k":[["கொக்கார்","கொக்கு",1,"pul","scene","crane"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",1,"pira","ornament","sandal paste"],["யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",3,"yaazh","scene","lute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:4"},"ki:49":{"book":"kainnilai","num":49,"t":["நாவாய் வழங்கு நனிதிரைத் தண்கடலுள்","ஓவா கலந்தார்க்கு ஒல்லென் இறாக்குப்பைப்","பாவாரம் சேர்ப்பதற்கு உரையாய் பரியாது","நோயான் நுணுகிய வாறு"],"p":"","r":"The heroine bids someone go and tell the lord of the cool sea, where ships ply over the great waves and heaps of prawns lie noisily piled, how she has wasted away with love-sickness, and not to spare him the telling.","n":"Line 3's பாவாரம் சேர்ப்பதற்கு is obscure; the sense needs சேர்ப்பற்கு, 'to the shore lord', as in 51 and 52, and ஓவா கலந்தார்க்கு (to the one who joined without ceasing) is read with it. The ships that never stop plying are set beside a man who has stopped coming.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["melivu","kaamanoy","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 49","lb":"KI49","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நாவாய்","நாவாய்",1,"seythi","scene","ship"],["இறாக்குப்பைப்","இறா குப்பை",2,"pira","scene","prawn heap"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:5"},"ki:50":{"book":"kainnilai","num":50,"t":["நெடுங்கடல் சேர்ப்ப நின்னோடு உரையேன்","ஒடுங்கு மடற்பெண்ணை அன்றிற்கும் சொல்லேன்","கடுஞ்சூளில் தான்கண்டு கானலுள் மேயும்","தடந்தாள் மடநாராய் கேள்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine says she will not speak to the lord of the long sea, nor even tell the anril on the palmyra of close-folded fronds, but bids the young broad-footed stork that feeds in the shore grove, which saw him swear his hard oath, to listen.","n":"She passes over the anril, the bird of paired love, which would only mirror her loss, and chooses the stork because it saw the oath: the bird is called as a witness, not a comforter. The poem ends on கேள், so what she tells it is left unsaid.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sool","kalaviyal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 50","lb":"KI50","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",2,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["அன்றிற்கும்","அன்றில்",2,"pul","scene","anril bird"],["மடநாராய்","நாரை",4,"pul","scene","stork"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:5"},"ki:51":{"book":"kainnilai","num":51,"t":["மணிநிற நெய்தல் மலர்புரையும் கண்ணாய்","அணிநல முண்டிறந்து...ம்மருளோ விட்ட","துணிமுந் நீர்ச் சேர்ப்பற்குத் தூதொடு வந்த","பணிமொழிப் புள்ளே பற"],"p":"","r":"Turning to the woman whose eyes are like the sapphire-blue neythal flower, the speaker tells the bird of humble words, which came as envoy for the lord of the clear sea who enjoyed her beauty and went his way, to fly off.","n":"The words தூதொடு வந்த பணிமொழி are the ones Natrinai 167 gives the shore lord's bard: the go-between refused at the door has become a bird. The gap in line 2 hides what the lord did after enjoying her beauty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["thoothu","nalam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 51","lb":"KI51","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","simile","blue water lily"],["புள்ளே","புள்",4,"pul","scene","bird"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:5"},"ki:52":{"book":"kainnilai","num":52,"t":["அன்னையும் இல்கடிந்தாள் யாங்குஇனியாம் என்செய்கம்","புன்னையங் கானலுள் புக்கருந்தும் - நின்னை","நினையான் துறந்த நெடுங்கழிச் சேர்ப்பற்கு","உரையேனோ பட்ட பழி"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the mother too has now kept the girl indoors and asks what they can do, and whether she should not tell the lord of the long backwater, who left without a thought for the one she addresses, the blame they have come to bear.","n":"இல்கடிந்தாள் is the mother's confinement of the girl; with the meetings ended, telling the man of the blame is the friend's way of pressing him to marry.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 52","lb":"KI52","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["புன்னையங்","புன்னை",2,"maram","scene","punnai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:5"},"ki:53":{"book":"kainnilai","num":53,"t":["அலவன் வழங்கும் அடும்பிமிர் எக்கர்","நிலவு நெடுங்கானல் நீடார் துறந்தார்","புலவுமீன் குப்பை கவரும் துறைவன்","கலவான்கொல் தோழி நமக்கு"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether he who left her in the long grove, where crabs move over the dune overrun by adumbu creeper, will not stay away long, and whether the lord of that shore, where heaps of reeking fish are carried off, will not come to be one with them.","n":"நீடார் can be 'will not delay' or 'did not stay'; read as the first, the poem is hope against the fact of his leaving, which is why it is waiting rather than lament.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 53","lb":"KI53","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அலவன்","அலவன்",1,"pira","scene","crab"],["அடும்பிமிர்","அடும்பு",1,"maram","scene","adumbu creeper"],["புலவுமீன்","மீன்",3,"pira","scene","fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:5"},"ki:54":{"book":"kainnilai","num":54,"t":["என்னையர் தந்த இறவுணங்கல் யாம்கடிந்து","புன்னையங் கானல் இருந்தேமாப் பொய்த்தெம்மைச்","சொன்னலம் கூறி நலனுண்ட சேர்ப்பனை","என்னைகொல் யாம்காணு மாறு"],"p":"","r":"The heroine recalls how she sat in the punnai grove keeping birds off the prawns her brothers had brought to dry, and asks how she can now see the shore lord who lied to her, spoke fine words and enjoyed her beauty.","n":"Near miss for ullurai: the guarded catch and the stolen beauty sit side by side, but no thief-bird is named, so only one pair (her kin's catch to her own beauty) can be drawn; in 56 the bird is named and the image holds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","nalam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 54","lb":"KI54","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["இறவுணங்கல்","இறவு",1,"pira","scene","dried prawn"],["புன்னையங்","புன்னை",2,"maram","scene","punnai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:5"},"ki:55":{"book":"kainnilai","num":55,"t":["கொக்கார் கொடுங்கழிக் கூடுநீர்த் தண்சேர்ப்பன்","நக்காங்கு அசதி தனியாடித் - தக்க","பொருகயல் கண்ணினாய் புல்லான் விடினே","இருகையும் நில்லா வளை"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl with eyes like fighting carp that the cool shore lord of the winding backwater, where herons gather at the meeting waters, laughed and played his teasing game by himself, and that if he leaves without embracing her, the bangles will not stay on either of her hands.","n":"அசதி தனியாடி: his play is a game he plays alone, light to him, while for her the bare chance of his leaving unembraced is enough to loosen the bangles.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["melivu","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 55","lb":"KI55","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொக்கார்","கொக்கு",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["பொருகயல்","கயல்",3,"pira","scene","carp"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:5"},"ki:56":{"book":"kainnilai","num":56,"t":["நுரைதரும் ஓதம் கடந்துஎமர் தந்த","கருங்கரை வன்மீன் கவரும்புள் ஓப்பின்","புகரில்லேம் யாமிருப்பப் பூங்கழிச் சேர்ப்பன்","நுகர்வனன் உண்டான் நலம்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that while she stayed blameless, driving off the birds that snatch the strong fish her kin had brought home across the foaming tide, the shore lord of the flowered backwater took his pleasure and consumed her beauty.","n":"She guarded the catch against the small thieves and lost the greater thing to the man; புகரில்லேம் insists the fault was not hers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kalaviyal","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 56","lb":"KI56","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வன்மீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","ullurai","fish"],["கவரும்புள்","புள்",2,"pul","ullurai","bird"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","கவரும்புள் வன்மீன்","not_confirmed","0/2","the shore lord and the heroine's beauty"],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ki:5"},"ki:57":{"book":"kainnilai","num":57,"t":["கொடுவாய்ப் புணரன்றில் கொய்மடல் பெண்ணைத்","தடவுக் கிளைபயிரும் தண்கடல் சேர்ப்பன்","நிலவுக் கொடுங்கழி நீந்திடும் முன்றில்","புலவுத் திரைபொருத போழ்து"],"p":"","r":"The lord of the cool sea, where the curve-beaked anril joined with its mate calls to its kin from the palmyra of cuttable fronds, is set at the hour when the reeking waves beat at the courtyard reached by swimming the moonlit winding backwater; the verse names the hour but not what happens in it.","n":"The four lines have no finite verb. Read as a night meeting, the man swims the backwater to the courtyard while the paired anril calls; the anril calling its mate could carry iraicchi, but with the stated sense unfixed the deletion test cannot be run, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 57","lb":"KI57","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புணரன்றில்","அன்றில்",1,"pul","scene","anril bird"],["பெண்ணைத்","பெண்ணை",1,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:5"},"ki:58":{"book":"kainnilai","num":58,"t":["சுறாஎறி குப்பை சுழலும் கழியுள்","இறாஎறி ஓதம் அலற இரைக்கும்","உறாஅநீர்ச் சேர்ப்பனை உள்ளி இருப்பின்","பொறாஅஎன் முன்கை வளை"],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that if she sits thinking of the lord of the unreachable water, where heaps of fish struck by sharks swirl in the backwater and the tide that flings up prawns roars, the bangles on her forearm will not hold.","n":"Every creature on his shore strikes or is struck (சுறா எறி, இறா எறி), and the tide roars; against that the loss is quiet: thinking of him alone is enough to loosen the bangles.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 58","lb":"KI58","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["சுறாஎறி","சுறா",1,"pira","scene","shark"],["இறாஎறி","இறா",2,"pira","scene","prawn"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:5"},"ki:59":{"book":"kainnilai","num":59,"t":["தாழை குருகுஈனும் தண்ணந் துறைவனை","மாழை மானோக்கின் மடமொழி - நூழை","நுழையும் மடமகன் யார்கொல் என்றுஅன்னை","புழையும் அடைத்தாள் கதவு"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the mother, asking who the young man is who slips through the narrow gap to the soft-spoken girl with the gaze of a young deer, the lord of the cool ford where the heron lays its eggs in the thaazhai, has blocked the gap and shut the door.","n":"நூழை and புழை are the small gaps of the fence by which he came; with the door barred the night meetings are over, and only marriage is left.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 59","lb":"KI59","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["தாழை","தாழை",1,"maram","scene","screw pine"],["குருகு","குருகு",1,"pul","iraicchi","heron"],["மானோக்கின்","மான்",2,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","குருகு","not_confirmed","0/1","the lord of a shore where even the heron settles to breed has yet to make a home with the girl, and the shut door says he must now do it"]],"ch":"ki:5"},"ki:60":{"book":"kainnilai","num":60,"t":["பொன்னம் பசலையும் தீர்ந்தது பூங்கொடி","தென்னவன் கொற்கைக் குருகுஇரிய - மன்னரை","ஓடுபுறம் கண்ட ஒண்தாரான் தேர்இதோ","கூடல் அணைய வரவு"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine, her flower-creeper, that her golden pallor is gone, for here is the chariot of the man of the bright garland, who has seen the backs of fleeing kings, coming near to join her and scattering the herons of the Pandya's Korkai.","n":"கூடல் is both 'union' and the Pandya's city, so the chariot comes at once to Koodal and to their meeting; the war epithet of the man who saw kings' backs turns the lover's return into a victor's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","punarchi","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kainnilai 60","lb":"KI60","poet":"புல்லங்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["குருகு","குருகு",2,"pul","scene","heron"]],"en":[["king","தென்னவன்",2],["town","கொற்கைக்",2],["town","கூடல்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"ki:5"},"kv:1":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":1,"t":["நாண்ஞாயி றுற்ற செருவிற்கு வீழ்ந்தவர்","வாண்மாய் குருதி களிறுழக்கத் - தாண்மாய்ந்து","முன்பசு லெல்லாங் குழம்பாகிப் பின்பகல்","துப்புத் துகளிற் கெழூஉம் புனனாடான்","தப்பியா ரட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the lord of the river country killed those who had wronged him, the blood of the fallen, deep enough to swallow swords, is churned by elephants until feet sink in it, lies as mud through the morning of the battle that met the rising sun, and by afternoon rises as dust red as coral.","n":"The poem measures the slaughter by a single day: the same blood is mud in the forenoon and coral-coloured dust after noon, so the field's horror is shown through time rather than numbers. Line 3's முன்பசுல் is a misprint; the printed variant reads முற்பகல்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 1","lb":"KV1","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["களிறுழக்கத்","களிறு",2,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:2":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":2,"t":["ஞாட்பினு ளெஞ்சிய ஞாலஞ்சேர் யானைக்கீழ்","போர்ப்பி லிடிமுரசி னூடுபோ மொண்குருதி","கார்ப்பெயல் பெய்தபிற் செங்குளக் கோட்டுக்கீழ்","நீர்த்தூம்பு நீருமிழ்வ போன்ற புனனாடன்","ஆர்த்தம ரட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the lord of the river country roared and killed in battle, the bright blood that runs under the elephants lying dead on the ground and through the thundering drums whose skins have burst looks like water spouting from the sluices under the bund of a red tank after the monsoon rain.","n":"The vehicle comes from the farmer's year, the full tank after the rains and its sluice, the same world that gives the genre its name of the threshing floor; the burst drum becomes the sluice pipe and the heaped elephants the bund.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 2","lb":"KV2","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["யானைக்கீழ்","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""],["லிடிமுரசி","முரசு",2,"parai","simile",""],["கார்ப்பெயல்","கார்ப் பெயல்",3,"pira","simile","monsoon rain"],["செங்குளக்","குளம்",3,"pira","simile","tank"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:3":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":3,"t":["ஒழுக்குங் குருதி யுழக்கித் தளர்வார்","இழுக்குங் களிற்றுக்கோ டூன்றி யெழுவர்","மழைக்குரன் மாமுரசின் மல்குநீர் நாடன்","பிழைத்தாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the lord of the water-rich land, whose great drum speaks like the rain cloud, killed those who erred against him, warriors who tire as they wade the streaming blood slip and rise again by leaning on the tusks of the fallen elephants.","n":"The dead elephant becomes a staff for the living: the tusk that killed is now what a slipping soldier leans on to stand. The printed variant in line 2 reads எழூஉம் for எழுவர்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 3","lb":"KV3","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["களிற்றுக்கோ","களிறு",2,"maa","scene",""],["மாமுரசின்","முரசு",3,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:4":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":4,"t":["உருவக் கடுந்தேர் முருக்கிமற் றத்தேர்ப்","பரிதி சுமந்தெழுந்த யானை - யிருவிசும்பிற்","செல்சுடர் சேர்ந்த மலைபோன்ற செங்கண்மால்","புல்லாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where Sengan killed those who would not yield, the elephants that smashed the handsome swift chariots and rose carrying those chariots' wheels on their tusks looked like mountains that the sun, moving across the wide sky, has come to rest upon.","n":"The word for the wheel, பரிதி, is also a word for the sun, so the simile of the sun on the mountain is already latent in the noun; the poet only has to make the elephant a hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 4","lb":"KV4","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[["king","செங்கண்மால்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:5":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":5,"t":["தெரிகணை யெஃகந் திறந்தவர் யெல்லாம்","குருதி படிந்துண்ட காகம் - உருவிழந்து","குக்கிற் புறத்த சிரல்வாய செங்கண்மால்","தப்பியா ரட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where Sengan killed those who had wronged him, the crows that plunged into and ate the blood of all the men opened by chosen arrows and spears lost their own colour, and now have the reddish back of the coucal and the red beak of the kingfisher.","n":"The black crow is turned into two other birds by the blood it eats; the change of colour is the whole measure of the slaughter, and the poem never shows a body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","saakkaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 5","lb":"KV5","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["காகம்","காகம்",2,"pul","scene",""],["குக்கிற்","குக்கில்",3,"pul","scene","coucal"],["சிரல்வாய","சிரல்",3,"pul","scene","kingfisher"]],"en":[["king","செங்கண்மால்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:6":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":6,"t":["நானாற் றிசையும் பிணம்பிறங்க யானை","யடுக்குபு வேற்றிக் கிடந்த - இடித்துரறி","யங்கண் விசும்பி னுருமெறித் தெங்கும்","பெருமலை தூறெறிந் தற்றே யருமணிப்","பூணேந் தெழின்மார் பியறிண்டேர்ச் செம்பியன் தெவ்","வேந்தரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the Chola of the strong chariot, whose handsome chest bears ornaments of rare gems, killed the hostile kings, elephants lie piled across one another with corpses heaped in every direction, as if thunder had roared from the wide sky and struck down the thickets of great mountains everywhere.","n":"The only verse of the chapter that names the victor by his line (செம்பியன், the Chola) and the defeated as kings (தெவ் வேந்தரை); the variant தூவெறிந்து in line 4 would make the thunder scatter rather than strike the thickets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","vendhan","pagai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 6","lb":"KV6","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[["king","செம்பியன்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:7":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":7,"t":["அஞ்சனக் குன்றேய்க்கும் யானை யமருழக்கி","இங்கு லிகக்குன்றே போற்றோன்றுஞ் - செங்கண்","வரிவரான் மீன்பிறழுங் காவிரி நாடன்","பொருநரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the lord of the Kaveri country, where red-eyed striped varal fish leap, killed his enemies, the elephants that looked like hills of collyrium before they churned the battle now appear like hills of vermilion.","n":"Two similes, one before and one after, tell the battle as a change of colour from black to red without describing a blow; the red-eyed fish of the king's river repeat the red in his own peaceful country.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 7","lb":"KV7","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","simile",""],["வரிவரான்","வரால்",3,"pira","scene","murrel fish"]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:8":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":8,"t":["யானைமேல் யானை நெரிதர வானாது","கண்ணேர் கடுங்கணை மெய்ம்மாய்ப்ப - எவ்வாயும்","எண்ணருங் குன்றிற் குரீஇயினம் போன்றவே","பண்ணா ரிடிமுரசிற் பாய்ப்புன னீர்நாடன்","நண்ணாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the lord of the land of rushing water, with his thundering tuned drum, killed those who would not come near him, elephants crushed upon elephants, their bodies hidden by unceasing fierce arrows aimed at the eye, looked on every side like countless hills covered with flocks of sparrows.","n":"The arrows standing out of the elephants' hides are the sparrows perched on a hill; the shafts, not the blood, carry the picture. The printed variant மெய்ம்மறைப்ப (line 2) makes the arrows cover the body rather than sink into it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 8","lb":"KV8","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானைமேல்","யானை",1,"maa","simile",""],["குரீஇயினம்","குரீஇ",3,"pul","simile","sparrow"],["ரிடிமுரசிற்","முரசு",4,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:9":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":9,"t":["மேலோரைக் கீழோர் குறுகிக் குறைத்திட்ட","காலார்சோ டற்ற கழற்கா லிருங்கடல்","ஊணில் சுறபிறழ்வ போன்ற புனனாடன்","நேராரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the lord of the river country killed those who stood against him, the anklet-wearing legs of the men above, cut off with their leg-guards by the men below who came in close, toss about like hungry sharks in the dark sea.","n":"மேலோர் and கீழோர் are most simply the riders on elephants and horses and the foot soldiers who cut at their legs from below; the words also carry rank, so the image is of the low cutting down the high. Line 3 is uncertain: சுற is a misprint for சுறா, and the variant reads நீலச் சுறா, blue sharks.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 9","lb":"KV9","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["சுறபிறழ்வ","சுறா",3,"pira","simile","shark"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:10":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":10,"t":["பல்கணை யெவ்வாயும் பாய்தலிற் செல்கலா","தொல்கி யுயங்குங் களிறெல்லாந் - தொல்சிறப்பிற்","செவ்வலங் குன்றம்போற் றோன்றும் புனனாடன்","தெவ்வரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the lord of the river country killed his foes, the elephants, pierced on every side by many arrows, cannot move and sink wearily where they stand, and look like hills of red earth of ancient renown.","n":"The elephants do not fall: they stand, unable to go, and so become hills; the stillness that elsewhere is courage is here exhaustion (ஒல்கி உயங்கும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 10","lb":"KV10","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["களிறெல்லாந்","களிறு",2,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:11":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":11,"t":["கழுமிய ஞாட்பினுண் மைந்திகந்தா ரிட்ட","ஒழிமுரச மொண்குருதி யாடித் - தொழின்மடிந்து","கண்காணா யானை யுதைப்ப விழுமென","மங்குன் மழையி னிதிரு மதிராப்போர்ச்","செங்கண்மா லட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where Sengan, unshaken in war, killed his enemies, the abandoned drums left behind by those whose strength failed in the thronging battle lie bathed in bright blood, and when the elephants, their work over and their eyes blinded, kick them, they boom like rain from dark clouds.","n":"The drum, the voice of a king's army, still sounds after its army is gone, but now it is struck by blind feet; the opening word கழுமிய (thronging) may echo the name of the battlefield, Kazhumalam, though the verse does not name it. The variant மைந்திழந்தார் (line 1) says the same: those who lost their strength.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 11","lb":"KV11","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["ஒழிமுரச","முரசு",2,"parai","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["king","செங்கண்மா",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:12":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":12,"t":["ஒவாக் கணைபாய வொல்கி யெழில்வேழந்","தீவாய்க் குருதி யிழிதலாற் செந்தலைப்","பூவலங் குன்றம் புயற்கேற்ற போன்றவெ","காவிரி நாடன் கடாஅய்க் கடிதாகக்","கூடாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the lord of the Kaveri country drove on swiftly and killed those who would not join him, the handsome elephants, sagging as arrows pierce them without pause, with blood running from wounds like mouths of fire, look like beautiful hills of red earth, red at the summit, taking the downpour of a storm cloud.","n":"Verse 10's red hill is here given rain: the blood running down the elephant is the storm water running down red earth, so the red of the hill is not only the elephant's colour but the colour the water carries.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 12","lb":"KV12","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["வேழந்","வேழம்",1,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:13":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":13,"t":["நிரைகதிர் நீளெஃக நீட்டி வயவர்","வரைபுரை யானைக்கை நூற - வரைமேல்","உருமெறி பாம்பிற் புரளுஞ் செருமொய்ம்பிற்","செஎய்பொரு தட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the red warrior of battle strength fought and killed, the fighters thrust their long spears of ranked gleam and cut the trunks of the hill-like elephants, and the trunks roll like snakes struck by thunder on a mountain.","n":"The two similes nest: the elephant is a hill, so its severed trunk is a snake that thunder has struck on that hill. செஎய் (line 4) is the king called by an epithet of red, or of Murugan; it is not a proper name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 13","lb":"KV13","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["யானைக்கை","யானை",2,"maa","simile",""],["பாம்பிற்","பாம்பு",3,"pira","simile","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:14":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":14,"t":["கவளங்கொள் யானையின் கைதுணிக்கப் பட்டுப்","பவளஞ் சொரிதரு பைபோற் றிவளொளிய","வொண்செங் குருதி யுமிழும் புனனாடன்","கொங்கரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the lord of the river country killed the Kongar, the trunks of the elephants that take their balls of food, cut off, spurt bright red blood of glittering light like a bag spilling coral.","n":"The only verse of the chapter to name the defeated: the Kongar, the people of the Kongu country. The trunk that a moment before was lifting food to the mouth now pours its own blood out like treasure from a bag.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","pagai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 14","lb":"KV14","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கவளங்கொள்","கவளம்",1,"unavu","gift_food","elephant's food ball"],["யானையின்","யானை",1,"maa","simile",""],["பவளஞ்","பவளம்",2,"pira","simile","coral"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:15":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":15,"t":["கொல்யானை பாயக் குடைமுருக்கி யெவ்வாயும்","புக்கவா யெல்லாம் பிணம்பிறங்கத் - தச்சன்","வினைபடு பள்ளிறிய் றோன்றும் செங்கட்","சினமால் பொருத களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the red-eyed, angry Sengan fought, wherever the killer elephants charged, smashing the royal umbrellas, corpses heaped up in every place they entered, so that the field looks like a carpenter's workshop.","n":"The carpenter's shed is the most domestic image in the chapter: cut timbers and shavings everywhere, the work of a craftsman, so the killing is made to look like trade. Line 3 is garbled in the source (பள்ளிறிய் றோன்றும், read as பள்ளியிற் றோன்றும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 15","lb":"KV15","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கொல்யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["king","சினமால்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:16":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":16,"t":["பரும வினமாக் கடவித் தெரிமறவர்","ஊக்கி யெடுத்த வரவத்தி னார்ப்பஞ்சாக்","குஞ்சரக் கும்பத்துப் பாய்வன குன்றிவரும்","வேங்கை யிரும்புலி போன்ற புனனாடன்","வேந்தரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the lord of the river country killed the kings, the harnessed horses, urged on by picked warriors and undaunted by the roar of the shouting, leap onto the frontal globes of the elephants like great tigers springing up a hill.","n":"ஆர்ப்பஞ்சா can belong to the horses (unafraid of the din) or to the elephants (unafraid of it, so the horses must climb them); either way the smaller animal, urged on (ஊக்கி), takes the larger like a tiger taking a hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","ookkam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 16","lb":"KV16","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["வினமாக்","மா",1,"maa","simile","horse"],["குஞ்சரக்","குஞ்சரம்",3,"maa","simile","elephant"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",4,"maa","simile","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:17":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":17,"t":["ஆர்ப்பெழுந்த ஞாட்பினு ளாளா ளெதிர்த்தோடித்","தாக்கி யெறிதா வீழ்தரு மொண்குருதி","கார்த்திகைச் சாற்றிற் கழிவிளக்கைப் போன்றனவே","ஆர்த்தம ரட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the king roared and killed in battle, in the fight where shouting rose and man ran against man, striking and hurling, the bright blood that falls is like the many lamps of the Karthigai festival.","n":"The festival of lamps, held in the dark month, gives the blood both its colour and its number: each falling drop a flame. The king is not named because the line that would name him is missing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 17","lb":"KV17","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:18":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":18,"t":["நளிந்த கடலுட் டிமிறிசை போலெங்கும்","விளிந்தார் பிணங்குருதி யீர்க்குந் - தெளிந்து","தடற்றிடங் கொள்வாட் டளையவிழுந் தார்ச்சே(ய்)","உடற்றியா ரட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the king, whose garland buds are opening and whose sword leaves its sheath, killed those who had angered him, the blood drags the corpses of the dead along on every side like boats moving on the crowded sea.","n":"The blood is now a sea and the dead its boats. Lines 3 and 4 are hard: தடற்றிடங்கொள் (variant தடற்றிலங்கொள்) and the editor's bracketed (ய்) in தார்ச்சே(ய்) leave the king's epithet uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","saakkaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 18","lb":"KV18","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["டிமிறிசை","திமில்",1,"seythi","simile","fishing boat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:19":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":19,"t":["இடைமருப்பின்விட்டெறிந்தவெஃகங்கான் மூழ்திக்","கடைமணி காண்வரத் தோற்றி - நடைமெலிந்து","முக்கோட்ட போன்ற களிறெல்லா நீர்நாடன்","புக்கம ரட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the lord of the water country entered battle and killed, the spears hurled between the tusks sank in up to the shaft and left their butt-ends showing, so that the elephants, slowed in their gait, looked as if they had three tusks.","n":"The wound makes the beast more fearsome-looking, a third tusk, at the very moment it weakens it (நடைமெலிந்து). கடைமணி is read here as the butt of the spear; the variant காழ் (shaft) for கால் supports this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 19","lb":"KV19","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிறெல்லா","களிறு",3,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:20":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":20,"t":["இரிசிறக ரீர்க்குப் பரப்பி யெருவை","குருதி பிணங்கவருந் தோற்றம் - அதிர்விலாச்","சீர்முழாப் பண்ணமைப்பான் போன்ற புனனாடன்","நேராரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"On the field where the lord of the river country killed those who stood against him, the vulture, spreading the quills of its ragged wings and tearing at the bloody corpses, looks like a musician tuning a fine drum that does not jar.","n":"The vulture stretching and tugging at a corpse's sinews becomes a drummer tightening the thongs of his drum: the field after battle is turned into the rehearsal before a performance, as if the poet's own art were being prepared there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","saakkaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 20","lb":"KV20","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யெருவை","எருவை",1,"pul","simile","vulture"],["சீர்முழாப்","முழா",3,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:1"},"kv:21":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":21,"t":["இணைவே லெழின்மருமத் திங்கப்புண் கூர்ந்து","கணையலைக் கொல்கிய யானை - துணையிலவாய்த்","தொல்வலி யாற்றித் துளங்கினவாய் மெல்ல","நிலங்கால் கவரு மலைபோன்ற செங்கட்","சினமால் பொருத களத்து"],"p":"","r":"Elephants with paired spears sunk in their chests and their wounds deepening, swaying under the arrows, with nothing to lean on and their old strength gone, stagger slowly like mountains whose footing the ground swallows, on the field where the red-eyed, wrathful Maal fought.","n":"The mountain does not fall; the ground takes its foot (நிலம் கால் கவரும்), so the image is of slow subsidence, not collapse: the elephant goes down by degrees. செங்கண் சினமால், 'the red-eyed wrathful Maal', is the king's epithet that recurs through the book.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 21","lb":"KV21","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[["king","சினமால்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:22":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":22,"t":["இருநிலஞ் சேர்ந்த குடைக்கீழ் வரிநுதல்","ஆடியல் யானைத் தடக்கை யொளிறுவாள்","ஓடா மறவர் துணிப்பத் துணிந்தவை","கோடுகொ ளொண்மதியை நக்கும்பரம் பொக்குமே","பாடா ரிடிமுரசிற் பாய்ப்புன னீர்நாடன்","கூடாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"Under umbrellas fallen to the ground, the trunks of the stately elephants with lined brows, cut through by the flashing swords of warriors who do not flee, lie across the umbrellas like a snake licking the bright crescent moon, on the field where the lord of the land of rushing water, with his thunderous drum, killed those who would not join him.","n":"The white umbrella is the moon and the cut trunk the snake that swallows it at an eclipse, so the fallen umbrella, the sign of a king, is shown eclipsed. The snake rests on an emendation: the line prints நக்கும்பரம் பொக்குமே, read here as நக்கும் பாம்பு ஒக்குமே.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 22","lb":"KV22","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானைத்","யானை",2,"maa","simile",""],["முரசிற்","முரசு",5,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["king","னீர்நாடன்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:23":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":23,"t":["ஏற்றி வயவ ரெறிய நுதல்பிளந்து","நெய்த்தோர்ப் புனலு ணிவந்தகளிற் றுடம்பு","செக்கர்கொள் வானிற் கருங்கொண்மூப் போன்றவே","கொற்றவேற் றானைக் கொடித்திண்டோர்ச் செம்பியன்","செற்றாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"Where the strong men hurled their spears up and split the foreheads, the elephants' bodies rising out of the stream of blood look like dark clouds in a red evening sky, on the field where Chempiyan, of the conquering spear army and the strong flagged chariots, killed his enemies.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 23","lb":"KV23","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிற்","களிறு",2,"maa","simile",""],["கருங்கொண்மூப்","கொண்மூ",3,"pira","simile","cloud"]],"en":[["king","செம்பியன்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:24":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":24,"t":["திண்டோண் மறவ ரெறியத் திசைதோறும்","பைந்தலை பாரிற் புரள்பவை - நன்கெனைத்தும்","பெண்ணையந் தோட்டம் பெருவளி புக்கேற்ற","கண்ணார் கமழ்தெரியற் காவிரி நீர்நாடன்","நண்ணாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"The fresh heads that strong-shouldered warriors struck off roll on the ground in every direction, much as fruit lies in a palmyra grove a great gale has passed through, on the field where the lord of the Kaveri's watered land, with his fragrant garland, killed those who would not come near.","n":"The heads are not said to be like fruit; the grove after the gale is set beside them, and the reader supplies the fallen palmyra nuts. Our text has no comparison marker in line 3 (புக்கேற்ற); tamilvu's புக்கற்றே would supply one (அற்று, 'like').","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","maram","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 24","lb":"KV24","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பெண்ணையந்","பெண்ணை",3,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["தெரியற்","தெரியல்",4,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[["king","நீர்நாடன்",4],["river","காவிரி",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:25":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":25,"t":["மலைகலங்கப் பாயு மலைபோ னிலைகொள்ளாக்","குஞ்சரம் பாயக் கொடி யெழுந்து பொங்குபு","வானந் துடைப்பன போன்ற புனனாடன்","மேவாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"Elephants that will not hold their ground charge like mountains that leap and set other mountains shaking, and as they charge the flags rise and surge as if to sweep the sky, on the field where the lord of the watered land killed those who did not love him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 25","lb":"KV25","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குஞ்சரம்","குஞ்சரம்",2,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[["king","புனனாடன்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:26":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":26,"t":["எவ்வாயு மோடி வயவர் துணித்திட்ட","கைவாயிற் கொண்டெழுந்த செஞ்செவிப் புன்சேவல்","ஐவாய் வயநாகங் கவ்வி விசும்பிவருஞ்","செவ்வா யுவணத்திற் றோன்றும் புனனாடன்","தெவ்வாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"A red-eared, dun male vulture rises with a hand that warriors running everywhere had cut off, and looks like the red-beaked Garuda climbing the sky with a five-hooded snake in its grip, on the field where the lord of the watered land killed his enemies.","n":"The five fingers of the hand make the five hoods of the snake (ஐவாய்), so the grisliest sight of the field is turned into the god's bird carrying its prey; the simile works by exact count, not by mood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","kolai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 26","lb":"KV26","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சேவல்","சேவல்",2,"pul","simile","male vulture"],["நாகங்","நாகம்",3,"pira","simile","cobra"],["யுவணத்திற்","உவணம்",4,"pul","simile","Garuda"]],"en":[["king","புனனாடன்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:27":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":27,"t":["செஞ்சேற்றுட் செல்யானை சீறி மிதித்தலால்","ஒண்செங் குருதிகள் தொக்கீண்டி நின்றவை","பூநீர் வியன்றமிடாப் போன்ற புனனாடன்","மேவாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"Blood trodden out by elephants that stamp in rage as they wade through red mire gathers and stands in pools like great vessels of flower-water, on the field where the lord of the watered land killed those who did not love him.","n":"The pools are the elephants' own footprints, filled; the vehicle is a festive thing, great jars of flower-scented water, so the field is made to look like preparations for a feast.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 27","lb":"KV27","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["king","புனனாடன்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:28":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":28,"t":["ஓடா மறவ ருருத்து மதஞ்செருக்கிப்","பீடுடை வாளார் பிறங்கிய ஞாட்பினுட்","கேடகத்தோ டற்ற தடக்கைகொண் டோ டி","இகலன்வாய்த் துற்றிய தோற்ற மயலார்க்குக்","கண்ணாடி காண்பாரிற் றோன்றும் புனனாடன்","நண்ணாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"In the press where warriors who do not flee, swelling with rage and fury, flash their glorious swords, a creature runs off with a great hand severed along with its shield and stuffs it in its mouth, and to the bewildered the sight looks like people gazing into a mirror, on the field where the lord of the watered land killed those who would not come near.","n":"The shield held before the face by a dead hand makes the mirror; the poet says the likeness holds only for those whose sight is confused (மயலார்க்கு), a rare admission that the simile is a trick of the eye.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","maram","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 28","lb":"KV28","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[],"en":[["king","புனனாடன்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:29":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":29,"t":["கடிகாவிற் காற்றுற் றெறிய வெடிபாட்டி","வீற்றுவீற் றோடு மயிலினம்போல் - நாற்றிசையும்","கேளி ரிழந்தா ரலறுபவே செங்கட்","சினமால் பொருத களத்து"],"p":"","r":"Like a flock of peacocks in a guarded grove that scatter each its own way when the wind strikes and a sudden crack startles them, those who have lost their kin cry out in all four directions on the field where the red-eyed, wrathful Maal fought.","n":"This is the one poem of the chapter whose subject is the living: the enemy's kin (கேளிர் இழந்தார்), seen as birds startled out of a guarded grove, still sit inside a poem whose last line credits the field to the victor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","sutram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 29","lb":"KV29","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மயிலினம்போல்","மயில்",2,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[["king","சினமால்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:30":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":30,"t":["மடங்க வெறிந்து மலையுருட்டு நீர்போல்","தடங்கொண்ட வொண்குருதி கொள்களி றீர்க்கு","மடங்கா மறமொய்ம்பிற் சினமால்","அடங்காரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"As a flood strikes and folds things over and rolls boulders down the hill, the bright blood pooled in the field drags along the killing elephants, on the field where the wrathful Maal of unbending valour killed those who would not submit.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 30","lb":"KV30","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["களி","களிறு",2,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[["king","சினமால்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:31":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":31,"t":["ஓடா மறவ ரெறிய நுதல்பிளந்த","கோடேந்து கொல்களிற்றுக் கும்பத் தெழிலோடை","மின்னுக் கொடியின் மிளிரும் புனனாடன்","ஒன்னாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"Where warriors who do not flee hurled and split the brow, the fine plate on the frontal globe of the tusked killer elephant flashes like a streak of lightning, on the field where the lord of the watered land killed those who were not his friends.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 31","lb":"KV31","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கொல்களிற்றுக்","களிறு",2,"maa","simile",""],["மின்னுக்","மின்னு",3,"pira","simile","lightning"]],"en":[["king","புனனாடன்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:32":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":32,"t":["மையின்மா மேனி நிலமென்னு நல்லவள்","செய்யது போர்த்தாள்போற் செவ்வந்தாள் - பொய்தீர்ந்த","பூந்தார் முரசிற் பொருபுன னீர்நாடன்","காய்ந்தாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"The good woman called Earth, of the fine body without a stain, has turned red as if she had wrapped herself in a red cloth, on the field where the lord of the battling watered land, whose drum is garlanded with flowers and never fails, killed those who raged against him.","n":"The earth is made a woman who has dressed for an occasion, so the blood is a garment she chose; the line goes no further, and the gentleness of நல்லவள் against the matter is the point.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 32","lb":"KV32","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முரசிற்","முரசு",3,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["king","னீர்நாடன்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:33":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":33,"t":["பொய்கை யுடைந்து புனல்பாய்ந்த வாயெல்லா","நெய்த லிடையிடை வாளை பிறழ்வனபோல்","ஐதிலங் கெஃகி னவிரொளிவா டாயினவே","கொய்சுவன் மாவிற் கொடித்திண்டோர் செம்பியன்","தெவ்வரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"Like vaalai fish leaping here and there among the water-lilies wherever the water rushes from a burst tank, the bright swords with their fine shining blades flash and spread, on the field where Chempiyan, of the horses with trimmed manes and the strong flagged chariots, killed his enemies.","n":"The broken tank is a picture of plenty in the Kaveri land, so the poet borrows the Chola country's most domestic sight, fish in the field channels, for the swords.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 33","lb":"KV33","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பொய்கை","பொய்கை",1,"pira","simile","tank"],["நெய்த","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","simile","water-lily"],["வாளை","வாளை",2,"pira","simile","vaalai fish"],["மாவிற்","மா",4,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["king","செம்பியன்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:34":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":34,"t":["இணரிய ஞாட்பினு ளேற்றெழுந்த மைந்தர்","சுடரிலங் கெஃக மெறியச் சோர்ந் துக்க","குடர்கொண்டு வாங்குங் குறுநரி கந்தில்","தொடரொடு கோணாய் புரையு மடர்பைம்பூட்","சேய்பொரு தட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"In the crowded clash, the young men who rose to meet the charge hurl their gleaming spears, and the small jackal tugging at the guts that slipped out and spilled looks like a hunting dog straining on its chain at a post, on the field where Chey of the heavy fresh ornaments fought and killed.","n":"The entrail is the chain: the jackal pulls against what holds it as a leashed dog pulls against its tether. சேய் names the king as the red, Murugan-like one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavazhi","kolai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 34","lb":"KV34","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["குறுநரி","நரி",3,"maa","simile","jackal"],["கோணாய்","கோள் நாய்",4,"maa","simile","hunting dog"]],"en":[["king","சேய்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:35":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":35,"t":["செவ்வரைச் சென்னி யரிமானோ டவ்வரை","ஒல்கி யுருமிற் குடைந்தற்றான் - மல்கிக்","கரைகொண் றிழிதரூஉம் காவிரி நாடன்","உரைசா லுடம்பிடி மூழ்க வரசோ(டு)","அரசுவா வீழ்ந்த களத்து"],"p":"","r":"As when a lion on the summit of a red mountain is broken by thunder together with that mountain, the famed lance plunged in and the enemy king fell with his royal elephant, on the field of the lord of the Kaveri land, whose river brims and comes down breaking its banks.","n":"The lion is the king and the mountain his elephant, so the simile keeps their unity in the fall: neither goes down alone. This is the only poem of the chapter whose last line names a fallen king rather than the victor's killing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","vendhan","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 35","lb":"KV35","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யரிமானோ","அரிமான்",1,"maa","simile","lion"],["யுருமிற்","உரும்",2,"pira","simile","thunder"],["அரசுவா","அரசுவா",5,"maa","simile","royal elephant"]],"en":[["king","காவிரி நாடன்",3],["river","காவிரி",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:36":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":36,"t":["ஓஓ உவம னுறழ்வின்றி யொத்ததே","காவிரி நாடன் கழுமலங் கொண்டநாள்","மாவுதைப்ப மாற்றார் குடையெலாங் கீழ்மேலா","ஆவுதை காளாம்பி போன்ற புனனாடன்","மேவாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"Oh, the likeness matches without a flaw: on the day the lord of the Kaveri land took Kalumalam, the enemies' umbrellas, kicked by the horses, all turned upside down like mushrooms kicked over by cows, on the field where the lord of the watered land killed those who did not love him.","n":"The poet praises his own simile before he gives it (உவமன் உறழ்வின்றி ஒத்ததே): horse for cow, umbrella for mushroom, the match is part for part. The umbrella, sign of a king's shelter, becomes a pasture weed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","vendhan","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 36","lb":"KV36","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["மாவுதைப்ப","மா",3,"maa","simile","horse"],["ஆவுதை","ஆ",4,"maa","simile","cow"],["காளாம்பி","காளாம்பி",4,"pira","simile","mushroom"]],"en":[["king","காவிரி நாடன்",2],["battle","கழுமலங்",2],["river","காவிரி",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:37":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":37,"t":["அரசர் பிணங்கான்ற நெய்த்தோர் முரசொடு","முத்துடைக் கோட்ட களிறீர்ப்ப - எத்திசையும்","பௌவம் புணரம்பி போன்ற புனனாடன்","தெவ்வரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"The blood that the kings' corpses poured out, dragging with it the drums and the elephants with pearl-bearing tusks, looks in every direction like boats meeting on the sea, on the field where the lord of the watered land killed his enemies.","n":"The drums, which spoke for the kings, float away on their blood; the pearls in the tusks make the sea of the simile literal at one point, since pearls come from the sea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","vendhan","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 37","lb":"KV37","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முரசொடு","முரசு",1,"parai","simile",""],["களிறீர்ப்ப","களிறு",2,"maa","simile",""],["பௌவம்","பௌவம்",3,"pira","simile","sea"],["புணரம்பி","அம்பி",3,"seythi","simile","boat"]],"en":[["king","புனனாடன்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:38":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":38,"t":["பருமப் பணையெருத்திற் பல்யானை புண்கூர்ந்(து)","உருமெறி பாம்பிற் புரளுஞ் - செருமொய்ம்பிற்","பொன்னார மார்பிற் புனைசுழற்காற் செம்பியன்","துன்னாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"Many elephants with harness on their thick necks, their wounds deepening, roll like snakes struck by thunder, on the field where Chempiyan, of mighty battle strength, the gold chain on his chest and the wrought anklet on his foot, killed those who would not come near.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 38","lb":"KV38","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","simile",""],["உருமெறி","உரும்",2,"pira","simile","thunder"],["பாம்பிற்","பாம்பு",2,"pira","simile","snake"]],"en":[["king","செம்பியன்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:39":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":39,"t":["மைந்துகால் யாத்து மயங்கிட ஞாட்பினுட்","புய்ந்துகால் போகிப் புலான்முகந்த வெண்குடை","பஞ்சிபெய் தாலமே போன்ற புனனாடன்","வஞ்சிக்கோ வட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"In the confused clash where strength was bound to strength, the white umbrella, torn from its staff and gone, scoops up flesh and looks like a tray heaped with cotton, on the field where the lord of the watered land killed the king of Vanji.","n":"This is the chapter's one poem that names the loser, வஞ்சிக்கோ, the Chera of Vanji. The umbrella that should shade a king now serves as a dish; line 1 (மைந்துகால் யாத்து) is obscure, and the reading of it here is a guess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","vendhan","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 39","lb":"KV39","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பஞ்சிபெய்","பஞ்சி",3,"maram","simile","cotton"]],"en":[["king","புனனாடன்",3],["king","வஞ்சிக்கோ",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:40":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":40,"t":["வெள்ளிவெண் ணாஞ்சிலான் ஞால முழுவனபோல்","எல்லாக் களிறு நிலஞ்சேர்ந்த - பல்வேற்","பணைமுழங்கு போர்த்தானைடச் செங்கட் சினமால்","கணைமாரி பெய்த களத்து"],"p":"","r":"As though the god with the silver-white plough were ploughing the world, all the elephants went down to the ground, on the field where the red-eyed, wrathful Maal, with his army of many spears and roaring drums, poured down a rain of arrows.","n":"Battle as ploughing is the old kalavazhi figure; here the ploughman is the white god with the plough, and the ploughshares are the falling elephants themselves, so the king's field is tilled on a cosmic scale.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 40","lb":"KV40","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ணாஞ்சிலான்","நாஞ்சிலான்",1,"deyvam","simile","Balarama"],["களிறு","களிறு",2,"maa","simile",""],["பணைமுழங்கு","பணை",3,"parai","scene","drum"]],"en":[["king","சினமால்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kv:41":{"book":"kalavazhi_narpathu","num":41,"t":["வேனிறத் திங்க வயவரா லேறுண்டு","கானிலங் கொள்ளாக் கலங்கிச் செவிசாய்த்து","மாநிலங் கூறு மறைகேட்ப போன்றவே","பாடா ரிடிமுரசிற் பாய்புன னீர்நாடன்","கூடாரை யட்ட களத்து"],"p":"","r":"Elephants pierced in the chest by the strong men's spears, unable to keep their footing, reel and lay their ears down as if listening to a secret the great earth is telling, on the field where the lord of the land of rushing water, with his thunderous drum, killed those who would not join him.","n":"மறை can be the Veda or a secret; either way the fallen elephant's ear on the ground is turned into attention, the earth speaking and the dying listening. Lines 4 and 5 repeat the close of 22.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 41","lb":"KV41","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முரசிற்","முரசு",4,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["king","னீர்நாடன்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"kv:2"},"kn:1":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":1,"t":["பொருகடல் வண்ணன் புனைமார்பிற் றார்போல்","திருவில் விலங்கூன்றித் தீம்பொழல் தாழ","வருதும் எனமொழிந்தார் வாரார்கொல் வானங்","கருவிருந் தாலிக்கும் போழ்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the man who promised 'we will come' when the rainbow lay across the sky like the garland on the chest of Mal, the god coloured like the warring sea, and sweet rain hung low, can hardly fail to come now that the sky, its clouds massed, roars and pours.","n":"The question வாரார்கொல் ('will he not come?') is reassurance put as a doubt, and the promise is quoted in his own word, வருதும். The mullai god Mal lends the rainbow his garland, so the season he swore by arrives with a sacred sign.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 1","lb":"KN1","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகட்குப் பருவங்காட்டி வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டி வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பொருகடல் வண்ணன்","மால்",1,"deyvam","simile","Mal, the sea-coloured god"],["றார்","தார்",1,"maram","simile","garland"],["திருவில்","திருவில்",2,"pira","simile","rainbow"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:2":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":2,"t":["கடுங்கதிர் நல்கூரக் கார்செல்வ மெய்த","நெடுங்காடு நேர்சினை யீனக் கொடுங்குழாய்","இன்னே வருவர் நமரென் றெழில்வானம்","மின்னு மவர்தூ துரைத்து"],"p":"","r":"As the fierce sun grows poor and the rain grows rich, and the long forest puts out shoots on every branch, the friend tells the heroine of the curved earrings that the lovely sky flashes its lightning, carrying his message: 'our man will come at once.'","n":"Sun and rain are cast as a poor man and a rich one, the very terms of the journey he made for wealth. The lightning is made his envoy (தூது), so the absent man's word reaches her through the sky.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["paruvam","thoothu","mazhai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 2","lb":"KN2","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகட்குப் பருவங்காட்டி வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டி வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நேர்சினை","சினை",2,"maram","scene","branch"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:3":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":3,"t":["வரிநிறப் பாதிரி வாட வளிபோழ்ந்","தயிர்மணற் றண்புறவி னாலி - புரள","உருமிடி வான மிழிய வெழுமே","நெருந லொருத்தி திறத்து"],"p":"","r":"Showing her own distress, the friend says that the striped paathiri is withering, the wind cuts through, hail rolls over the fine sand of the cool woodland and the thundering sky pours down and rises again, as if all of it, since yesterday, were aimed at one lone woman.","n":"ஒருத்தி, 'one woman', is the heroine seen from outside: the storm that should promise his return is felt as an assault on her alone. The colophon says the friend shows her own inability to bear it so that the heroine will bear hers; the comfort is left unspoken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","mazhai","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 3","lb":"KN3","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பருவங் கண்டழிந்த தலைமகள் ஆற்றல்வேண்டித் தோழி தனது ஆற்றாமை தோன்ற வுரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தனது ஆற்றாமை தோன்ற வுரைத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பாதிரி","பாதிரி",1,"maram","absent","trumpet-flower tree"],["னாலி","ஆலி",2,"pira","scene","hailstone"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:4":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":4,"t":["ஆடு மகளிரின் மஞ்ஞை யணிகொளக்","காடுங் கடுக்கை கவின்பெறப் பூத்தன","பாடுவண் டூதும் பருவம் பணைத்தோளி","வாடும் பசலை மருந்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine of bamboo-smooth shoulders that the peacocks put on their finery like dancing women, the konrai has bloomed and made the forest lovely, and this season, when singing bees hum, is the medicine for her wasting pallor.","n":"The friend does not say he has come; she says the season is the cure, because the season was his promise. The sign is made to stand in for the man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["paruvam","pasalai","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 4","lb":"KN4","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆடு மகளிரின்","ஆடு மகளிர்",1,"seythi","simile","dancing women"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",1,"pul","simile","peacock"],["கடுக்கை","கடுக்கை",2,"maram","scene","konrai"],["பாடுவண் டூதும்","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:5":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":5,"t":["இகழுநர் சொல்லஞ்சிச் சென்றார் வருதல்","பகழிபோ லுண்கண்ணாய் பொய்யன்மை யீண்டைப்","பவழஞ் சிதறி யவைபோலக் கோபந்","தவழுந் தகைய புறவு"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine whose eyes are like arrows that the return of the man who left fearing the words of those who scorn is no lie: here the woodland is such that red velvet-mites crawl over it like scattered coral.","n":"He went because of others' contempt, the contempt shown to the poor; the friend answers that talk with a proof that does not talk, the season's red insects on the ground.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","igal","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 5","lb":"KN5","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோபந்","இந்திரகோபம்",3,"pira","simile","velvet mite"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:6":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":6,"t":["தொடியிட வாற்றா தொலைந்ததோ ணோக்கி","வடுவிடைப் போழ்ந்தகன்ற கண்ணாய் வருந்தல்","கடிதிடி வான முரறு நெடுவிடைச்","சென்றாரை நீடன்மி னென்று"],"p":"","r":"Seeing her shoulders so wasted they cannot hold bangles, the friend tells the heroine, whose wide eyes are like a split tender mango, not to grieve: the sky thunders fiercely along the long road, telling those who went, 'do not delay.'","n":"The thunder speaks to the absent man, not to her: the friend makes the season his summons, so the comfort is that he too is being told. The variant நெறியிடை for நெடுவிடை does not change the sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["melivu","paruvam","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 6","lb":"KN6","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தொடியிட","தொடி",1,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:7":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":7,"t":["நச்சியார்க் கீதலு நண்ணார்த் தெறுதலுந்","தற்செய்வான் சென்றார்த் தரூஉந் தளரியலாய்","பொச்சாப் பிலாத புகழ்வேள்வித் தீப்போல","எச்சாரு மின்னு மழை"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the drooping heroine that the rain, flashing on every side like the fire of a famous sacrifice kept without lapse, will bring back the man who went to make himself able to give to those who seek him and to crush those who oppose him.","n":"His purpose is given as the two duties of a puram hero, giving and punishing (ஈதல், தெறுதல்). The sacrificial fire tended without lapse lends the rain the same steadiness: the season keeps its rite as he will keep his word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 7","lb":"KN7","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேள்வித் தீ","வேள்வித் தீ",3,"seythi","simile","sacrificial fire"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:8":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":8,"t":["மண்ணியன் ஞாலத்து மன்னும் புகழ்வேண்டிப்","பெண்ணிய னல்லாய் பிரிந்தார் வால்கூறும்","கண்ணிய லஞ்சனங் தோய்ந்தபோற் காயாவும்","நுண்ணுரும் பூழ்த்த புறவு"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine, good in all a woman's ways, that the woodland, where the kaayaa has opened its fine buds, blue as if dipped in the collyrium of an eye, announces the return of him who parted seeking fame that lasts in this world made of earth.","n":"மண் இயல் ஞாலத்து மன்னும் புகழ், lasting fame in a world made of earth, sets the permanent against the perishable. Here the friend makes his reason honour, not wealth, and lets the season answer it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 8","lb":"KN8","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காயாவும்","காயா",3,"maram","simile","kaayaa flower"],["லஞ்சனங்","அஞ்சனம்",3,"pira","simile","collyrium"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:9":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":9,"t":["உருவினை கண்மலர்போற் பூத்தன கார்க்கோற்","றெரிவனப் புற்றன தோன்றி - வரிவளை","முன்கை யிறப்பத் துறந்தார் வரல்கூறும்","இன்சொற் பலவு முரைத்து"],"p":"","r":"Flowers have bloomed like eyes, the glory lilies on their rain-fed stems have taken on their finest beauty, and, speaking many sweet words, they announce the return of the man who left her so that the striped bangles slip from her forearm.","n":"The flowers are given the friend's own task, sweet words of reassurance (இன்சொல்). Which flower line 1 names (உருவினை) is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","inchol","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 9","lb":"KN9","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தோன்றி","தோன்றி",2,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["வரிவளை","வளை",2,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:10":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":10,"t":["வானேறு வானத் துரற வயமுரண்","ஆனேற் றொருத்த லதனோ டெதிர்செறுப்பக்","கான்யாற் றொலியிற் கடுமான்றே ரென்றோழி","மேனி தளிர்ப்ப வரும்"],"p":"","r":"While the thunder roars in the sky and the strong, quarrelsome bull of the herd bellows back at it in anger, the swift-horsed chariot comes with the sound of a forest river, so that my friend's body will freshen like a new leaf.","n":"வானேறு and ஆனேறு pair the thunder, the sky's bull, with the bull of the herd, which challenges it. The chariot is heard before it is seen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 10","lb":"KN10","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஆனேற்","ஆனேறு",2,"maa","scene","bull"],["கான்யாற்","கான்யாறு",3,"pira","simile","forest stream"],["கடுமான்","கடுமான்",3,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:11":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":11,"t":["புணர்தரு செல்வந் தருபாக்குச் சென்றார்","வணரொலி யைம்பாலாய் வல்வருதல் கூறும்","அணர்த்தெழு பாம்பின் றலைபோற் புணர்கோடல்","பூங்குலை யீன்ற புறவு"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine of the curled, five-plaited hair that the woodland, where the kodal has put out clusters like the raised heads of snakes, announces the swift return of the man who went to bring the wealth that makes their union possible.","n":"புணர்தரு செல்வம், 'wealth that brings union', is the friend's gloss on why he left: the wealth was for their life together, not against it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","paruvam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 11","lb":"KN11","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோடல்","கோடல்",3,"maram","simile","glory lily"],["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",3,"pira","simile","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:12":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":12,"t":["மையெழி லுண்கண் மயிலன்ன சாயலாய்","ஐயந்தீர் காட்சி யவர்வருதல் திண்ணிதாம்","நெய்யணி குஞ்சரம் போல விருங்கொண்மூ","வைகலு மேரும் வலம்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine of kohl-dark lovely eyes and peacock-like grace that his coming, shown by a sign beyond doubt, is certain: every day the dark clouds, like elephants anointed with oil, rise on the right.","n":"ஐயந்தீர் காட்சி: the friend offers an omen as proof. Clouds rising on the right (வலம்) are a good sign, and they rise every day, so the sign is renewed daily.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 12","lb":"KN12","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மயிலன்ன","மயில்",1,"pul","simile","peacock"],["குஞ்சரம்","குஞ்சரம்",3,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:13":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":13,"t":["ஏந்தெழி லல்குலா யேமார்ந்த காதலர்","கூந்தல வனப்பிற் பெயறாழ - வேந்தர்","களிறெறி வாளரவம் போலக்கண் வௌவி","ஒளிறுபு மின்னு மழை"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine of the lovely raised waist that the rain hangs down as lovely as her hair, which her lover delights in, and the clouds flash lightning that dazzles the eye like the clashing swords of kings cutting down elephants.","n":"A battlefield is lent to the sky, kings' swords flashing among elephants, so the war that keeps the hero away is seen again in the weather that should bring him home. Whose hair line 2 means, and whether காதலர் goes with it, is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 13","lb":"KN13","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["களிறெறி","களிறு",3,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:14":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":14,"t":["செல்வந் தரவேண்டிச் சென்றநங் காதலர்","வல்லே வருத றெளிந்தாம் வயங்கிழாய்","முல்லை யிலங்கெயி றீன நறுந்தண்கார்","மெல்ல வினிய நகும்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine of shining jewels that they are sure their lover, who went to bring wealth, will come quickly: the fragrant cool rain-cloud laughs softly and sweetly as the mullai puts out its bright teeth.","n":"The mullai buds are teeth and the cloud laughs with them: the season smiles, and the friend reads the smile as the answer to her doubt. தெளிந்தாம், 'we are certain', is the friend's word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","selvam","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 14","lb":"KN14","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",3,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:15":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":15,"t":["திருந்திழாய் காதலர் தீர்குவ ரல்லர்","குருந்தின் குவியிண ருள்ளுறை யாகத்","திருந்தி னிளிவண்டு பாட விருந்தும்பி","இன்குழ லூதும் பொழுது"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine of well-made jewels that her lover is not one to abandon her: it is the season when, lodged within the clustered kurunthu blossoms, the bee sings in the ili mode and the large bee plays its sweet flute.","n":"உள்ளுறையாக here means 'lodging within' (the bee inside the flowers); it is the word the grammar uses for the hidden comparison, but nothing in this verse is hidden. The insects' lute-note and flute make the woodland a concert for the season of return.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","thelivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 15","lb":"KN15","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருந்தின்","குருந்து",2,"maram","scene","kurunthu"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",3,"pira","scene","large bee"],["குழ லூதும்","குழல்",4,"yaazh","scene","flute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:16":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":16,"t":["கருங்குயில் கையற மாமயி லாலப்","பெருங்கலி வான முரறும் - பெருந்தோள்","செயலை யிளந்தளி ரன்னநின் மேனிப்","பசலை பழங்கூண் கொள்"],"p":"","r":"As the black koel falls helpless, the great peacock dances and the loud sky thunders, the friend tells the heroine of broad shoulders that the pallor on her body, tender as young asoka shoots, will grow stale and pass.","n":"The koel, a bird of the spring, loses heart when the rains come while the peacock dances: one season's voice gives way to another's, as the pallor of waiting should give way to his return.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","paruvam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 16","lb":"KN16","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கருங்குயில்","குயில்",1,"pul","scene","koel"],["மாமயி லாலப்","மயில்",1,"pul","scene","peacock"],["செயலை","செயலை",3,"maram","simile","asoka"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:17":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":17,"t":["அறைக்க லிறுவரைமேற் பாம்பு சவட்டிப்","பறைக்குர லேறோடு பௌவம் பருகி","உறைத்திருள் கூர்ந்தன்று வானம் பிறைத்தகை","கொண்டன்று பேதை நுதல்"],"p":"","r":"On the high hill of rock ledges the thunder crushes the snakes; with thunder voiced like a drum the sky has drunk the sea and, pouring, has thickened the dark; and the simple girl's brow has taken on the beauty of the crescent moon.","n":"The last line turns from storm to face: the brow that pallor dimmed has its crescent shape and light again, because the rain promises him, so the friend reads the cure on the heroine rather than in the sky.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["paruvam","pasalai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 17","lb":"KN17","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",1,"pira","scene","snake"],["பறைக்குர","பறை",2,"parai","scene","drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:18":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":18,"t":["கல்பயில் கானங் கடந்தார் வரவாங்கே","நல்லிசை யேறொடு வான நடுநிற்பச்","செல்வர் மனம்போற் கவினீன்ற நல்கூர்ந்தார்","மேனிபோற் புல்லென்ற காடு"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that, to announce the return of those who crossed the stony forest, the thunder of good sound stands there in mid-sky, and the forest, which was as dull as a poor man's body, has put forth beauty like a rich man's mind.","n":"The forest has made the journey the man is making, from poverty to wealth. The two similes set the poor man's body against the rich man's mind: wealth shows inwardly as gladness, poverty outwardly as drabness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","selvam","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 18","lb":"KN18","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யேறொடு","ஏறு",2,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:19":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":19,"t":["நாஞ்சில் வலவ னிறம்போலப் பூஞ்சினைச்","செங்கான் மராஅந் தகைந்தன - பைங்கோற்","றொடிபொலி முன்கையாள் தோடுணையா வேண்டி","நெடுவிடைச் சென்றதென் னெஞ்சு"],"p":"","r":"The man, his work done, tells his charioteer that the red-stemmed maraa has blossomed on its flowered branches, white like the plough-bearing god, and his heart has already gone the long way ahead, wanting as its companion the woman whose forearm shines with green bangles.","n":"The heart sent ahead of the chariot is the returning hero's figure; தோடுணையா வேண்டி, 'wanting her as companion', makes the heart a traveller of its own on the road the chariot has yet to cover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","punarchi","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 19","lb":"KN19","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகள் பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நாஞ்சில் வலவ","நாஞ்சில் வலவன்",1,"deyvam","simile","Balarama, the plough-bearer"],["மராஅந்","மராஅம்",2,"maram","simile","kadamba"],["றொடி","தொடி",3,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:20":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":20,"t":["வீறுசால் வேந்தன் வினையு முடிந்தன","ஆறும் பதமினிய வாயின - ஏறோ","டருமணி நாக மனுங்கச் செருமன்னர்","சேனைபோற் செல்லு மழை"],"p":"","r":"The returning man says the glorious king's work is finished and the roads have turned pleasant to travel, while the rain-cloud moves on with its thunder like the army of warring kings, so that the gem-bearing serpents wilt.","n":"The army he has left is seen again in the sky: the cloud marches home like the troops of kings, as he does. The serpent that shrinks at thunder is the thunder's mark, not a sign of its own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","vendhan","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 20","lb":"KN20","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகள் பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஏறோ","ஏறு",2,"pira","scene","thunder"],["நாக","நாகம்",3,"pira","scene","serpent"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:1"},"kn:21":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":21,"t":["பொறிமாண் புனைதிண்டேர் போந்த வழியே","சிறுமுல்லைப் போதெல்லாஞ் செவ்வி - நறுநுதற்","செல்வ மழைந்தடங்கட் சின்மொழிப் பேதைவாய்","முள்ளெயி றேய்ப்ப வடிந்து"],"p":"","r":"The returning man tells his charioteer that all along the road the well-made, sturdy chariot has come, the small mullai buds stand in their prime, pointed like the sharp teeth in the mouth of the simple girl of fragrant brow, rich, cool wide eyes and few words.","n":"The usual simile runs from woman to flower (teeth like jasmine buds); here it runs from flower to woman, so every bud on the road home already shows him her mouth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","azhagu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 21","lb":"KN21","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகள் பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சிறுமுல்லைப்","முல்லை",2,"maram","simile","jasmine bud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:22":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":22,"t":["இளையரு மீர்ங்கட் டயர வுளையணிந்து","புல்லுண் கலிமாவும் பூட்டிய - நல்லார்","இளநலம் போலக் கவினி வளமுடையார்","ஆக்கம்போற் பூத்தன காடு"],"p":"","r":"The returning man says that with the young attendants making merry in the cool wet land and the plumed, grass-fed horses yoked, the woodland has bloomed, lovely as the young beauty of good women and rich as the prosperity of the wealthy.","n":"The two similes hold both ends of his journey in one forest: the beauty of the woman he is going back to and the wealth of those who prosper, the thing such journeys were made for.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 22","lb":"KN22","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகள் பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இளையரு","இளையர்",1,"seythi","scene","attendants"],["கலிமாவும்","கலிமா",2,"maa","scene","horse"],["புல்லுண்","புல்",2,"maram","gift_food","grass"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:23":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":23,"t":["கண்டிரண் முத்தங் கடுப்பப் புறவெல்லாந்","தண்டுளி யாலி புரளப் புயல்கான்று","கொண்டெழில் வானமுங் கொண்டன் றெவன் கொலோ","ஒண்டொடி யூடுநிலை"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the cloud has poured out so that cool drops and hailstones, like round shapely pearls, roll over all the woodland, and the sky has gathered its beauty: why then, girl of bright bangles, keep to your sulking?","n":"The friend treats the sulk as out of date: the season he named has arrived, so the grievance has lost its ground. The same closing line ends verse 38, where the colophon gives it the opposite force.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","oodal","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 23","lb":"KN23","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகட்குப் பருவங்காட்டி வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டி வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முத்தங்","முத்தம்",1,"pira","simile","pearl"],["யாலி","ஆலி",2,"pira","simile","hailstone"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:24":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":24,"t":["எல்லா வினையுங் கிடப்ப வெழுநெஞ்சே","கல்லோங்கு கானங் களிற்றின் மதநாறும்","பல்லிருங் கூந்தல் பணிநோனாள் கார்வானம்","மெல்லவுந் தோன்றும் பெயல்"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart to rise and go, leaving all the work lying, for the woman whose thick dark hair smells like the musth of the bull elephant in the rocky forest cannot bear it any longer, and the rain-cloud is beginning, gently, to show its rain.","n":"எல்லா வினையுங் கிடப்ப, 'let all the work lie', sits oddly with the colophon's வினைமுற்றிய (his work done): either the rest of the business is left to wait, or the rain has overtaken him before the task is finished.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["nenju","pirivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 24","lb":"KN24","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகள் நெஞ்சொடு சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","நெஞ்சொடு சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["களிற்றின்","களிறு",2,"maa","scene","bull elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:25":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":25,"t":["கருங்கால் வரகின் பொரிப்போ லரும்புவிழ்ந்","தீர்ந்தண் புறவிற் றெறுழிவீ மலர்ந்தன","சேர்ந்தன செய்குறி வாரா ரவரென்று","கூர்ந்த பசலை யவட்கு"],"p":"","r":"The friend, letting her own distress show, says that in the cool wet woodland the therukhi has opened its buds like puffed grains of black-stemmed millet, and at the thought that the signs he set have all come while he has not, the pallor has thickened on her friend.","n":"The friend speaks of the heroine in the third person (அவட்கு) and voices the worst herself, so that the heroine, hearing it, is left to be the one who bears up; the same half-line returns in verse 40 as the thought the sky cures.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 25","lb":"KN25","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பருவங்கண்டழிந்த தலைமகள் ஆற்றல்வேண்டித் தோழி தனது ஆற்றாமை தோன்ற வுரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தனது ஆற்றாமை தோன்ற"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வரகின்","வரகு",1,"unavu","simile","millet"],["றெறுழிவீ","தெறுழ்",2,"maram","simile","therukhi flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:26":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":26,"t":["நலமிகு கார்த்திகை நாட்டவ ரிட்ட","தலைநாள் விளக்கிற் றகையுடைய வாகிப்","புலமெலாம் பூத்தன தோன்றி சிலமொழி","தூதொடு வந்த மழை"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the thondri has blossomed over every field, lovely as the first-night lamps the people set out in the auspicious month of Karthigai, and the rain has come bearing a message of few words.","n":"The rain is made a messenger (தூது) of few words: he has sent no word of his own, so the season speaks for him; the variant மென்மொழி would make the message soft rather than brief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["paruvam","thoothu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 26","lb":"KN26","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகட்குப் பருவங்காட்டி வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டி வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["விளக்கிற்","விளக்கு",2,"pira","simile","festival lamp"],["தோன்றி","தோன்றி",3,"maram","simile","glory lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:27":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":27,"t":["முருகியம்போல் வான முழங்கி யிரங்கக்","குருகிலை பூத்தன கானம் - பிரிவெண்ணி","உள்ளா தகன்றாரென் றூடியாம் பாராட்டப்","பள்ளியுட் பாயும் பசப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The friend says the sky roars and rumbles like the music of Murugan's rite and the woodland has bloomed with kurukilai; if we sulk, dwelling on the parting and saying he left without a thought for us, pallor will spread over her in her bed.","n":"The friend says யாம், 'we', taking a share in the sulk in order to talk her out of it: a quarrel needs the man present, and turned on an absent man it feeds only the pallor. The rite of Murugan is hill-country (kurinji) music heard in the mullai thunder.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","pasalai","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 27","lb":"KN27","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"ஊடுதலாற் பசலைமிகும் எனத் தோழி தலைமகட்குக் கூற வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","ஊடுதலாற் பசலைமிகும்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முருகியம்","முருகியம்",1,"parai","simile","music of Murugan's rite"],["குருகிலை","குருகிலை",2,"maram","scene","a flowering plant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:28":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":28,"t":["இமிழிசை வானம் முழங்கக் குமிழின்பூப்","பொன்செய் குழையிற் றுணர் தூங்கத் தண்பதஞ்","செவ்வி யுடைய சுரநெஞ்சே காதலியூர்","கவ்வை யழுங்கச் செலற்கு"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart that with the sky booming and thundering and the kumizh flowers hanging in clusters like ear-pendants wrought of gold, the road through the cool ripe season is at its best for going to his beloved's town and quieting its gossip.","n":"கவ்வை is the talk his absence has stirred in her town; his return is meant to end the talk as well as her grief, so the gossip that belongs to secret love follows the married pair into separation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","alar","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 28","lb":"KN28","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகள் நெஞ்சொடு சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","நெஞ்சொடு சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குமிழின்பூப்","குமிழ்",1,"maram","simile","kumizh flower"],["குழையிற்","குழை",2,"pira","simile","ear-pendant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:29":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":29,"t":["பொங்கரு ஞாங்கர் மலர்ந்தன தங்காத்","தகைவண்டு பாண்முரலுங் கானம் - பகைகொண்ட","லெவ்வெத் திசைகளும் வந்தன்று சேறுநாஞ்","செவ்வி யுடைய கரம்"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart that the groves nearby have blossomed, the fine bees hum without rest like bards singing in the woodland, and the clouds, foes to the parted, have come from every quarter: let us go, for the road is at its best.","n":"The bees sing a bard's song, so the woodland performs the welcome a returning man's bards would; பகை, 'hostile', makes the cloud an enemy of those apart, whose arrival is a summons home.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nenju","paruvam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 29","lb":"KN29","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகள் நெஞ்சொடு சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","நெஞ்சொடு சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தகைவண்டு","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["பாண்முரலுங்","பாண்",2,"yaazh","scene","bard's song"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:30":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":30,"t":["வரைமல்க வானஞ் சிறப்ப வுறைபோழ்ந்","திருநிலந் தீம்பெய றாழ - விரைநாற","ஊதை யுளரு நறுந்தண்கா பேதை","பெருமட நம்மாட் டுரைத்து"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart that with the hills brimming, the sky at its height and sweet rain hanging low and breaking into drops over the wide earth, the cool fragrant grove, stirred by a wind that spreads its scent, speaks to us of the great innocence of our simple girl.","n":"The grove's scent recalls her, and the poem makes the grove the speaker: what reaches him is not her message but the land telling him of her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","nenju","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 30","lb":"KN30","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகள் நெஞ்சொடு சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","நெஞ்சொடு சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஊதை","ஊதை",3,"pira","scene","cold wind"],["நறுந்தண்கா","கா",3,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:31":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":31,"t":["கார்ச்சே ணிகந்த கரைமருங்கி னீர்ச்சேர்ந்","தெருமை யெழிலே றெறிபவர் சூடிச்","செருமிகு மள்ளரிற் செம்மாக்குஞ் செவ்வி","திருநுதற் கியாஞ்செய் குறி"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his charioteer that the season when the handsome bull-buffalo, going down to the water by the banks the rains have flooded far, struts with the creepers it has tossed on its horns like warriors proud in battle, is the sign he set for the woman of the fair brow.","n":"The warriors of the simile are the man's own company: the buffalo comes up from the flood like soldiers home from war, as he is; the buffalo of the watered fields is a marutham thing on a mullai road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","vaaimai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 31","lb":"KN31","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகள் பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தெருமை","எருமை ஏறு",2,"maa","simile","bull buffalo"],["பவர்","பவர்",2,"maram","ornament","creeper"],["மள்ளரிற்","மள்ளர்",3,"seythi","simile","warriors"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:32":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":32,"t":["கடாஅவுக பாகதேர் காரோடக் கண்டே","கெடாஅப் புகழ்வேட்கைச் செல்வர் மனம்போற்","பாடஅ மகிழ்வண்டு பாண்முரலுங் கானம்","பிடாஅப் பெருந்தகை நற்கு"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his charioteer to drive on, now that the rains are running, through the woodland where bees, glad as the hearts of wealthy men who crave undying fame, hum like bards on the pidavam: it is good for the noble one.","n":"A puram ideal, the rich man's pleasure in being sung for his gifts, is used to measure a bee's joy, so the woodland on the road home becomes a patron's court full of singers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","punarchi","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 32","lb":"KN32","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகள் பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மகிழ்வண்டு","வண்டு",3,"pira","simile","bee"],["பாண்முரலுங்","பாண்",3,"yaazh","simile","bard's song"],["பிடாஅப்","பிடவம்",4,"maram","scene","pidavam flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:33":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":33,"t":["கடனீர் முகந்த கமஞ்சூ லெழிலி","குடமலை யாகத்துக் கொள்ளப் பிறைக்கும்","இடமென வாங்கே குறிசெய்தேம் பேதை","மடமொழி யெவ்வங் கெட"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his charioteer that, to end the grief of his simple, soft-spoken girl, he fixed as his sign the time when the cloud, heavy as with young after drawing up the sea, would be taken into the breast of the western mountain.","n":"The promise is dated by the sky, not the calendar: the full cloud reaching the western hill is the appointed day, so the season itself becomes his word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["paruvam","vaaimai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 33","lb":"KN33","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகள் பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["லெழிலி","எழிலி",1,"pira","scene","rain-cloud"],["கடனீர்","கடல் நீர்",1,"pira","scene","sea water"]],"en":[["hill","குடமலை",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:34":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":34,"t":["விரிதிரை வெள்ளம் வெறுப்பப் பருகிப்","பெருவிறல் வானம் பெருவரை சேருங்","கருவணி காலங் குறித்தார் திருவணித்த","ஒண்ணுதல் மாதர் திறத்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend, letting her own distress show, says that he named, for the bright-browed woman adorned with beauty, the season when the mighty sky, having drunk its fill of the wide-waved sea, gathers dark on the great hills.","n":"She states only the fact, that this is the season he named, and leaves unsaid that he has not come; that silence is the distress the colophon says she lets show.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 34","lb":"KN34","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பருவங் கண்டழிந்த தலைமகள் ஆற்றல்வேண்டித் தோழி தனது ஆற்றாமை தோன்ற வுரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தனது ஆற்றாமை தோன்ற"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வெள்ளம்","வெள்ளம்",1,"pira","scene","sea flood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:35":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":35,"t":["சென்றநங் காதலர் சேணிகந்தா ரென்றெண்ணி","ஒன்றிய நோயோ டிடும்பை பலகூர","வென்றி முரசி னிரங்கி யெழில்வானம்","நின்று மிரங்கு மிவட்கு"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that as her friend, thinking that their beloved who went has passed far beyond, grows heavy with grief joined to pain, the lovely sky rumbles like a victorious war-drum and stays there rumbling, as if in pity for her.","n":"இரங்கும் is both 'rumbles' and 'grieves for': the thunder that ought to announce his return sounds instead like pity for her, and the drum of victory recalls the war that keeps him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","idumbai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 35","lb":"KN35","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பருவங் கண்டழிந்த தலைமகள் ஆற்றல்வேண்டித் தோழி தனது ஆற்றாமை தோன்ற வுரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தனது ஆற்றாமை தோன்ற"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முரசி","முரசு",3,"parai","simile","war-drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:36":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":36,"t":["சிரல்வாய் வனப்பின வாகி நிரலொப்ப","ஈர்ந்தண் தளவந் தகைந்தன - சீர்த்தக்க","செல்வ மழைமதர்க்கட் சின்மொழிப் பேதையூர்","நல்விருந் தாக நமக்கு"],"p":"","r":"The returning man tells his charioteer that the cool thalavam buds, lovely as kingfishers' beaks, have opened in rows, and the town of the simple girl with rich, cool, proud eyes and few words will be a good feast for us.","n":"விருந்து makes the husband a guest at his own home: after the long absence he will come to her town as to a feast. Line 3 repeats the epithets of verse 21.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","virundhu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 36","lb":"KN36","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றி மீளுந் தலைமகள் பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["சிரல்வாய்","சிரல்",1,"pul","scene","kingfisher"],["தளவந்","தளவம்",2,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:37":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":37,"t":["கருங்கடல் மேய்ந்த கமஞ்சூ லெழிலி","இருங்க லிறுவரை யேறி யியுர்க்கும்","பெரும்பதக் காலையும் வாரார்கொல் வேந்தன்","அருந்தொழில் வாய்த்த நமர்"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether, even in this season of great rains, when the cloud heavy as with young that grazed on the black sea climbs the great rocks of the steep hills, our man who took up the king's hard task will fail to come.","n":"வாரார்கொல் is reassurance put as a question. The king's hard task explains his absence and also vouches for him: a man fit for the king's work is one who keeps to an appointed time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","vendhan","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 37","lb":"KN37","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டித் தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["லெழிலி","எழிலி",1,"pira","scene","rain-cloud"],["கருங்கடல்","கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:38":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":38,"t":["புகர்முகம் பூழிப் புரள வுயர்நிலைய","வெஞ்சின் வேழம் பிடியோ டிசைந்தாடுந்","தண்பதக் காலையும் வாரா ரெவன்கொலோ","ஒண்டொடி யூடு நிலை"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that even in this cool season, when the fierce, tall bull elephant with its spotted face rolls in the dust and plays in accord with his mate, he has not come: why then, girl of bright bangles, this sulking?","n":"The closing line is verse 23's, but here, by the colophon, the friend concedes that he has broken his word and the question about sulking asks what good it does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","pirivu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 38","lb":"KN38","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவர் பொய்த்தாரெனக் கூறித் தோழி தலைவியை ஆற்றுவித்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தலைவர் பொய்த்தாரெனக் கூறித்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேழம்","வேழம்",2,"maa","iraicchi","bull elephant"],["பிடியோ","பிடி",2,"maa","iraicchi","cow elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வேழம்","confirmed","1/1","even the elephant keeps company with its mate in this season, so the man who stays away has broken his word and the grievance is just"]],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:39":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":39,"t":["அலவன்க ணேய்ப்ப வரும்பீன் றவிழ்ந்த","கருங்குர னொச்சிப் பசுந்தழை சூடி","இரும்புன மேர்க்கடி கொண்டார் பெருங்கௌவை","ஆகின்று நம்மூ ரவர்க்கு"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that now the men wearing the green leaves of the dark-clustered nochchi, whose buds have opened like crabs' eyes, have begun ploughing the wide dry fields, he has become the great talk of our town.","n":"கௌவை, the town's talk, is turned on him rather than on her: it is he who has become the byword for a broken promise. The crab is a shore creature borrowed as an image for a mullai bush.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["alar","paruvam","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 39","lb":"KN39","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவர் பொய்த்தாரெனக் கூறித் தோழி தலைவியை ஆற்றுவித்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தலைவர் பொய்த்தாரெனக் கூறித்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அலவன்க","அலவன்",1,"pira","simile","crab"],["னொச்சிப்","நொச்சி",2,"maram","ornament","nochchi leaves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"kn:40":{"book":"kar_narpathu","num":40,"t":["வந்தன செய்குறி வாரா ரவரென்று","நொந்த வொருத்திக்கு நோய்தீர் மருந்தாகி","இந்தின் கருவண்ணங் கொண்டன் றெழில்வானம்","நந்துமென் பேதை நுதல்"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that for the woman who grieved that the signs he set had come but he had not, the lovely sky has taken on the dark colour of the indu and become the medicine to end her sickness: the brow of my simple girl will regain its brightness.","n":"The sky does not bring him; it is itself the cure, because its dark colour proves the season and so his return. The first line repeats verse 25's despairing thought word for word, now as the thing the sky answers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["paruvam","marundhu","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kar Narpathu 40","lb":"KN40","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணங்கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பருவம் வந்தமையால் தலைவர் வருதல் ஒருதலையெனக் கூறித் தோழி தலைமகளை ஆற்றுவித்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தலைவர் வருதல் ஒருதலையெனக் கூறித்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இந்தின்","இந்து",3,"maram","simile","indu shrub"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kn:2"},"tn:1":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":1,"t":["நறைபடர் சாந்தம் அறஎறிந்து, நாளால்","உறைஎதிர்ந்து வித்தியஊழ் ஏனல் - பிறையெதிர்ந்த","தாமரைபோல் வாள்முகத்துத் தாழ்குழலீர்!- காணீரோ","ஏமரை போந்தன ஈண்டு"],"p":"","r":"The man asks the long-haired girls, whose bright faces are like lotuses facing the crescent moon, whether they have not seen an arrow-struck deer come this way into the millet field sown in good time with the first rain on a slope cleared of fragrant sandal.","n":"The question about the wounded deer is the conventional pretext of the hunter who comes to the field; the praise packed into the address (crescent brow over lotus face) shows that the real question is not about the deer. The plural address (தாழ்குழலீர்) takes in the heroine and her friend together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","azhagu","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 1","lb":"TN1","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",1,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",2,"unavu","scene","millet field"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",3,"maram","simile","lotus"],["ஏமரை","மரை",4,"maa","scene","arrow-struck deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:2":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":2,"t":["சுள்ளி சுனைநீலம் சோபா லிகைசெயலை","அள்ளி அளகத்தின் மேலாய்ந்து - தெள்ளி","இதணால் கடியடுங்கா ஈர்ங்கடா யானை","உதணால் கடிந்தான் உளன்"],"p":"","r":"The friend says there is a man who gathered sulli, pool lilies, sobhalika and asoka flowers, sorted them and set them on the girl's hair, and who with a blunt arrow drove off the wet-musth elephant that the shouts from the platform could not turn away.","n":"Two acts make the claim: he dressed her hair, which only a husband or one who means to be one does, and he saved her from the elephant. Said to the household, it is the friend's disclosure of the secret love (அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்), and the bare 'there is one' (உளன்) leaves the elders to ask who.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","aanmai","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 2","lb":"TN2","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சுள்ளி","சுள்ளி",1,"maram","ornament","sulli flower"],["சுனைநீலம்","நீலம்",1,"maram","ornament","blue water lily"],["செயலை","செயலை",1,"maram","ornament","asoka"],["இதணால்","இதண்",3,"seythi","scene","field platform"],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:3":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":3,"t":["சாந்தம் எறிந்துழுத சாரல் சிறுதினைச்","சாந்தம் எறிந்த இதண்மிசைச் - சாந்தம்","கமழக் கிளிகடியும் கார்மயில் அன்னாள்","இமிழக் கிளியழா ஆர்த்து"],"p":"","r":"On the slope where sandal trees were cut down and the ground ploughed for small millet, on the platform built of the cut sandal, the girl like a dark peacock, fragrant with sandal paste, scares off the parrots, and her shouts ring until the parrots cry out.","n":"The word சாந்தம் comes three times with three senses: the felled tree, the platform built from it, and the paste on her body, so the field's whole history is carried on her skin. Line 4 is hard: whether the parrots cry out or the girl's cry is meant is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 3","lb":"TN3","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",1,"maram","scene","sandal tree"],["சிறுதினைச்","தினை",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",2,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["கிளிகடியும்","கிளி",3,"pul","scene","parrot"],["கார்மயில்","மயில்",3,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:4":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":4,"t":["கோடா புகழ்மாறன் கூடல் அனையாளை","ஆடா அடகினும் காணேன்போர் - வாடாக்","கருங்கொல்வேல் மன்னர் கலம்புக்க கொல்லோ","மருங்குல்கொம் பன்னாள் மயிர்"],"p":"","r":"The man says he cannot see her, who is like the Koodal of Maran of unswerving fame, even among the greens of the field, and wonders whether the hair of her with a waist like a slender branch has gone into the vessels of kings whose dark killing spears never fail in battle.","n":"The kings of line 3 may be rival suitors who have come with gifts for her, so that her not being seen means she is being given elsewhere; the Pandya's city as the measure of her beauty brings puram fame into the field.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kalaviyal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 4","lb":"TN4","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அடகினும்","அடகு",2,"unavu","scene","edible greens"],["மருங்குல்கொம்","கொம்பு",4,"maram","simile","branch"]],"en":[["king","மாறன்",1],["town","கூடல்",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:5":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":5,"t":["வினைவிளையச் செல்வம் விளைவதுபோல் நீடாப்","பனைவிளைவு நாமெண்ணப் பாத்தித் - தினைவிளைய","மையார் தடங்கண் மயிலன்னாய்! தீத்தீண்டு","கையார் பிரிவித்தல் காண்!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the peacock-like girl of dark wide eyes that the millet in the plots has ripened before long, as wealth ripens when deeds ripen, and bids her see how this parts them as a hand jerks back from fire.","n":"Comparing the ripening millet to the ripening of past deeds into wealth makes the harvest a fate no one can hurry or delay: the end of the field-watch, and so of the meetings, arrives on its own schedule. Line 2 (பனைவிளைவு) and the fire image of line 4 are hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","oozh","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 5","lb":"TN5","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தினைவிளைய","தினை",2,"unavu","scene","millet"],["மயிலன்னாய்","மயில்",3,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:6":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":6,"t":["மானீல மாண்ட துகில்உமிழ்வ(து) ஒத்தருவி","மானீல மால்வரை நாட! கேள் - மாநீலம்","காயும்வேற் கண்ணாள் கனையிருளின் நீவர","ஆயுமோ மன்றநீ ஆய்"],"p":"","r":"The friend bids the lord of the great dark hill, where the waterfall looks like the hill spitting out a fine blue cloth, to listen and judge for himself whether she of spear-sharp eyes that put the blue lily to shame can be at ease while he comes through the thick dark.","n":"The friend puts the judgement on him (நீ ஆய்): the danger of the dark path is argued through her fear for him, not his own. The repeated மானீல / மாநீலம் plays the blue of the hill against the blue of the lily her eyes outdo. ஆயுமோ is read as 'will she be at ease'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 6","lb":"TN6","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஒத்தருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["மாநீலம்","நீலம்",2,"maram","scene","blue lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:7":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":7,"t":["கறிவளர்பூஞ் சாரல் கைந்நாகம் பார்த்து","நெறிவளர் நீள்வேங்கை கொட்கும் - முறிவளர்","நன்மலை நாட! இரவரின் வாழாளால்,","நன்மலை நாடன் மகள்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the good hill, on whose flowered pepper slopes the long tiger prowls the path watching the elephant, that if he comes by night the daughter of the lord of the good hill will not live.","n":"The same phrase நன்மலை நாட(ன்) names the lover in line 3 and the girl's father in line 4, setting the two men side by side as equals: the night visit endangers a match that could be made openly. The tiger lying in wait on the path carries the danger as a mood; it does no act of a person of the love, so it is scene, not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 7","lb":"TN7","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["கறிவளர்பூஞ்","கறி",1,"maram","scene","pepper vine"],["கைந்நாகம்","கைந்நாகம்",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["நீள்வேங்கை","வேங்கை",2,"maa","scene","tiger"],["முறிவளர்","முறி",2,"maram","scene","tender leaves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:8":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":8,"t":["அவட்காயின் ஐவனம் காவல் அமைந்த(து)","இவட்காயின் செந்தினைகார் ஏனல் - இவட்காயின்","எண்ணுளவால் ஐந்திரண்(டு) ஈத்தான்கொல் என்னாங்கொல்","கண்ணுளவால் காமன் கணை"],"p":"","r":"Looking at the girls at the fields, the man says that one guards the mountain rice and this one the red millet, and wonders whether the love god has given this one his five arrows twice over: what else could it be, when Kaman's arrows are in her eyes.","n":"The count is the joke: Kaman has five arrows, and she, with two eyes, seems to carry ten. Line 3 is obscure and the sense of 'five twice' is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 8","lb":"TN8","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["ஐவனம்","ஐவனம்",1,"unavu","scene","mountain rice"],["செந்தினைகார்","தினை",2,"unavu","scene","red millet"],["காமன்","காமன்",4,"deyvam","scene","love god"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:9":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":9,"t":["வஞ்சமே என்னும் வகைத்தாலோர் மாவினாய்த்","தஞ்சம் தமியனாய்ச் சென்றேன்என் - நெஞ்சை","நலங்கொண்டார் பூங்குழலாள் நன்றாயத்(து) அன்(று)என்","வலங்கொண்டாள் கொண்டாள் இடம்"],"p":"","r":"The man says he went alone and unprotected, asking after a beast in a way that was nothing but a ruse, and the girl of flowered hair who took his heart with her beauty, that day among her good companions, took his strength and took its place.","n":"The hero names his own pretext as deceit (வஞ்சமே), and the trickster is the one caught: he went to take and was taken. Line 4 (வலங்கொண்டாள் கொண்டாள் இடம்) may also be a play on right and left, she who went round him to the right took the left, his heart's side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","kalaviyal","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 9","lb":"TN9","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாவினாய்த்","மா",1,"maa","scene","beast (game)"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:10":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":10,"t":["கருவிரல் செம்முகம் வெண்பல்சூல் மந்தி","பருவிரலால் பைஞ்சுனைநீர் தூஉய்ப் - பெருவரைமேல்","தேன்வார்க்(கு) ஓக்கும் மலை நாட! வாரலோ","வான்தேவர் கொட்கும் வழி"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the hill, where the pregnant female monkey with black fingers, red face and white teeth scatters the green pool's water with her thick fingers and reaches for the honey dripping on the great rock, not to come by the path where the sky gods roam.","n":"The monkey's sprinkling of water and raising of hands toward the honey look like worship at a sacred hill, which prepares the closing danger of divine beings on the path. A pregnant creature craving honey may hint at the household he should give her, but it fails the act condition for iraicchi, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kuri","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 10","lb":"TN10","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["மந்தி","மந்தி",1,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["பைஞ்சுனைநீர்","சுனை",2,"pira","scene","pool water"],["தேன்வார்க்","தேன்",3,"unavu","scene","honey"],["வான்தேவர்","தேவர்",4,"deyvam","scene","sky gods"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:11":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":11,"t":["கரவில் வளமலைக் கல்லருவி நாட!","உரவில் வலியா ஒரு நீ - இரவின்","வழிகள்தாம் சால வரஅரிய வாரல்","இழிகடா யானை எதிர்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the rich, open hill of rocky falls that, strong as he is with his mighty bow and coming alone, the paths by night are very hard to travel, and he must not come where the musth-streaming elephant meets him.","n":"She grants his strength before refusing him: the bow does not answer the danger, because he comes alone (ஒரு நீ). கரவில் 'without hiding' may glance at the secrecy he keeps, set against his open hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 11","lb":"TN11","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["கல்லருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:12":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":12,"t":["வேலனார் போக மறிவிடுக்க வேரியும்","பாலனார்க்(கு) ஈக பழியிலாள் - பாலால்","கடும்புனலின் நீந்திக் கரைவைத்தாற்(கு) அல்லால்","நெடும்பனைபோல் தோள்நேராள் நின்று"],"p":"","r":"The friend says: let the diviner go, set the kid free, give the toddy to the children; by her nature the blameless girl will not offer her shoulders, round as the tall palm, to anyone but the man who swam the fierce flood and set her on the bank.","n":"The friend stops the rite by naming its true cause: the girl is not possessed but promised, by her own choice, to the man who saved her from the flood. The rescue gives the claim its warrant before the household. பனை is probably பணை (bamboo), the usual vehicle for shoulders.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 12","lb":"TN12","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேலனார்","வேலன்",1,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["மறிவிடுக்க","மறி",1,"maa","gift_food","kid"],["வேரியும்","வேரி",1,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["கடும்புனலின்","புனல்",3,"pira","scene","flood"],["நெடும்பனைபோல்","பனை",4,"maram","simile","palm (or bamboo)"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:13":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":13,"t":["ஒருவரைபோல் எங்கும் பல்வரையும் சூழ்ந்த","வருவரை யுள்ளதாம் சீறூர் - வருவரையுள்","ஐவாய நாதும் புறமெல்லாம் ஆயுங்கால்","கைவாய நாதும்சேர் காடு"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that their little village lies inside the hills that crowd round it on every side as if they were one hill, and that within those hills are five-hooded snakes and, all around, a forest where elephants gather.","n":"Read as 'snake' and 'elephant' (ஐவாய் நாகம், கைவாய் நாகம்), the two closing lines set a creature that strikes inside the hills against one that roams outside them, so every way in is guarded. The reading depends on emending நாதும் to நாகும்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","acham","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 13","lb":"TN13","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["ஐவாய","ஐவாய் நாகம்",3,"pira","scene","five-hooded snake"],["கைவாய","கைவாய் நாகம்",4,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:14":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":14,"t":["வருக்கை வளமலையுள் மாதரும் யானும்","இருக்கை இதண்மேலே மாகப் - பருக்கைக்","கடாஅமால் யானை கடிந்தானை அல்லால்","தொடாஅவால் என்தோழி தோள்"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that when the girl and she were sitting high on the platform in the rich hill of jackfruit and a huge musth elephant came, a man drove it off, and no one but him shall touch her friend's shoulders.","n":"The friend puts herself in the scene (மாதரும் யானும்), so she speaks as witness to the rescue: her testimony is what makes the claim binding on the household. பருக்கை in line 2 is uncertain; the sense does not turn on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","kalaviyal","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 14","lb":"TN14","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வருக்கை","வருக்கை",1,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["இதண்மேலே","இதண்",2,"seythi","scene","field platform"],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:15":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":15,"t":["வாடாத சான்றோர் வரவெதிர் கொண்டிராய்க்","கோடாது நீர்கொடுப்பின் அல்லது - கோடா","எழிலும் முலையும் இரண்டிற்கும் முந்நீர்ப்","பொழிலும் விலையாமோ போந்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl's kin that unless they receive the steadfast elders when they come and give her without wavering, not even the whole sea-girt world would be price enough for her unfailing beauty and her breasts.","n":"The argument turns the bride price inside out: since nothing could buy her, she can only be given, and the giving is the rite with the suitor's elders (சான்றோர்) received at the door. கோடா / கோடாது is played twice, the kin must not swerve, and her beauty does not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","saandror","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 15","lb":"TN15","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:16":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":16,"t":["நாள்நாகம் நாறும் நனைகுழலாள் நல்கித்தன்","பூண்ஆகம் நேர்வளவும் போகாது - பூண்ஆகம்","என்றேன் இரண்டாவ(து) உண்டோ மடல் மாமேல்","நின்றேண் மறுகிடையே நேர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The man says he will not go until she of the wet hair fragrant with fresh naagam flowers grants him her ornamented breast; he has said 'her ornamented breast': is there a second way? And he has taken his stand on the palmyra horse in the middle of the street.","n":"The repeated பூண் ஆகம் makes his demand a single, fixed word that he will not trade; 'is there a second?' closes off any other remedy, so mounting the madal in the street is the only one left.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 16","lb":"TN16","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நாள்நாகம்","நாகம்",1,"maram","ornament","naagam flower"],["மடல்","மடல்",3,"maram","scene","palmyra-stem horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:1"},"tn:17":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":17,"t":["அறி(கு)அவளை ஐய இடைம்மடவாய் ஆயச்","சிறிதவள்செல் வாள்இறுமென் றஞ்சிச் - சிறிதவள்","நல்கும்வாய் காணாது நைந்துருகி என்நெஞ்சம்","ஒல்கும்வாய் ஒல்கல் உறும்"],"p":"","r":"The man tells the friend that he knows the girl with the slender waist, fears it will snap if she walks even a little, and, finding no moment when she grants him even a little kindness, his heart melts and sways wherever she sways.","n":"The verse turns on ஒல்கு: she sways as she walks, and his heart sways with her, so his surrender copies her gait. Her fragility is praise, and the fear that her waist will break is his own helplessness projected onto her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nenju","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 17","lb":"TN17","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:18":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":18,"t":["என்னாங்கொல் ஈடில் இளவேங்கை நாளுரைப்பப்","பொன்னாம்போர் வேலவர் தாம்புரிந்த - தென்னே","மருவியா மாலை மலைநாடன் கேண்மை","இருவியாம் ஏனல் இனி"],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks what will become of it: the peerless young vengai, turning gold, has announced the day, the spear-bearing kin have settled on what they will do, and now the millet field will be stubble, and with it the bond with the hill lord.","n":"The vengai's flowering was the sign for the harvest, and once the millet is cut the girl no longer goes out to guard it; so the young tree 'announcing the day' is the clock that ends the secret meetings. மருவியா மாலை may be 'the evening not shared' or 'the garland not worn'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 18","lb":"TN18","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["இளவேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",4,"unavu","scene","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:19":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":19,"t":["பாலெத்த வெள்ளருவி பாய்ந்தாடிப் பல்பூப்பெய்","தாலொத்த ஐவனம் காப்பாள்கண் - வேலொத்(து)என்","நெஞ்சம்வாய்ப் புக்(கு)ஒழிவு காண்பானோ காண்கொடா","அஞ்சாயற் கேநோவல் யான்"],"p":"","r":"The man says that the eyes of the girl who, after leaping and bathing in a white fall like milk, guards the mountain rice that looks strewn with many flowers, entered his heart like a spear; will they ever let it go? He aches for the gentle one who will not let herself be seen.","n":"Three likenesses with the same marker build the scene from the outside in: the fall, the field, and then the eye, which ends inside him. The girl is busy guarding a field, yet it is he who is overrun.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 19","lb":"TN19","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["வெள்ளருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["பல்பூப்","பூ",1,"maram","simile","flowers"],["ஐவனம்","ஐவனம்",2,"unavu","simile","mountain rice"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:20":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":20,"t":["நாள்வேங்கை பொன்விளையும் நன்மலை நன்னாட!","கோள்வேங்கை போற்கொடியார் என்ஐயன்மார் - கோள்வேங்கை","அன்னையால் நீயும் அருந்தழையாம் ஏலாமைக்(கு)","என்னையோ நாளை எளிது"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the good hill, where the vengai yields gold on its day, that her brothers are cruel like killing tigers and the mother too is a tiger, so they cannot take his rare leaf-garment; what excuse will come easy tomorrow?","n":"The verse plays on வேங்கை, the gold-flowering kino tree of line 1 and the killing tiger of lines 2 and 3: the same word that gives the hill its beauty gives the family its menace. Refusing the leaf-garment is refusing the suit for now; 'tomorrow' hints that refusal cannot go on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","acham","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 20","lb":"TN20","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["நாள்வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["கோள்வேங்கை","வேங்கை",2,"maa","simile","tiger"],["அருந்தழை","தழை",3,"maram","gift_food","leaf garment"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:21":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":21,"t":["பொன்மெலியும் மேனியாள் பூஞ்சுணங்கு மென்முலைகள்","என்மெலிய வீங்கினவே பாவமென்று - என்மெலிவிற்(கு)","அண்கண்ணி வாடாமை யால்நல்ல என்(று)ஆற்றான்","உண்கண்ணி வாடாள் உடன்று"],"p":"","r":"The girl whose body puts gold to shame has soft breasts with flower-like spots that swelled as he wasted, the man complains, calling it a wrong; since she does not fade at his fading he cannot bear it, while the kohl-eyed girl, though vexed, does not fade.","n":"The symmetry is the point: her body grows as his shrinks, so her ripening looks to him like a theft from his own body. Who speaks lines 3 and 4 is unclear; read as the friend reporting his complaint, the last line turns it gently against the girl.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["melivu","kaamanoy","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 21","lb":"TN21","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:22":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":22,"t":["கொல்யானை வெண்மருப்பும் கொல்வல் புலியதளும்","நல்யானை நின்ஐயர் கூட்டுண்டு - செல்வர்தாம்","ஓரம்பி னான்எய்து போக்குவர்யான் போகாமல்","ஈரம்பி னால்எய்தாய் இன்று"],"p":"","r":"The man tells the girl that her rich brothers gather the white tusks of killing elephants and the skins of strong killing tigers, bringing each beast down with a single arrow, but she today has shot him with two arrows so that he cannot leave.","n":"One arrow against two: the brothers' skill is real, but she needs twice their shots to bring him down and still he does not go. ஈரம்பு can also be heard as 'a moist arrow', the wet bright eye.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 22","lb":"TN22","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொல்யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["வெண்மருப்பும்","மருப்பு",1,"maa","scene","tusk"],["புலியதளும்","புலி",1,"maa","scene","tiger skin"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:23":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":23,"t":["பெருமலை தாம்நாடித் தேன்துய்த்துப் பேணா(து)","அருமலை மாய்க்குமவர் தங்கை - திருமுலைக்கு","நாணழிந்து நல்ல நலனழிந்து நைந்துருகி","ஏண்அழிதற்(கு) யாமே இனம்"],"p":"","r":"The man says that for the lovely breasts of the sister of men who search the great hills, take their fill of honey and then carelessly ruin what was hard to reach, he is one fit to lose his shame, his good looks and his standing, melting away.","n":"The brothers take the honey and destroy what held it; the poem sets that beside the man's own ruin by their sister, so he feels himself used and spent the same way. அருமலை is hard: 'the hard-to-reach hill' or the hive on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","nalam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 23","lb":"TN23","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["தேன்துய்த்துப்","தேன்",1,"unavu","gift_food","honey"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:24":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":24,"t":["நறுந்தண் தகரம் வகுளம் இவற்றை","வெறும்புதல்போல் வேண்டாது வேண்டி எறிந்(து)உழுது","செந்தினை வித்துவார் தங்கை பிறர்நோய்க்கு","நொந்தினைய வல்லளோ நோக்கு"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man to consider whether the sister of men who, wanting only what they want, cut down fragrant cool thakaram and vakulam as if they were worthless bush and plough the ground to sow red millet could ever grieve and pine for another's pain.","n":"The friend turns the family's farming into a character sketch: people who fell fragrance for grain do not keep pity for an outsider's pain. Read as the man's own complaint, the verse would be a lover's bitterness rather than a refusal; the speaker is inferred.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","kudi","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 24","lb":"TN24","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தகரம்","தகரம்",1,"maram","scene","thakaram"],["வகுளம்","வகுளம்",1,"maram","scene","makizham"],["வெறும்புதல்போல்","புதல்",2,"maram","simile","bush"],["உழுது","உழவு",2,"seythi","scene","ploughing"],["செந்தினை","தினை",3,"unavu","scene","red millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:25":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":25,"t":["கொல்லியல் வேழும் குயவரி கோட்பிழைத்து","நல்லியல் தம்இனம் நாடுவபோல் - நல்லியல்","நாமவேல் கண்ணாள் நடுநடுப்ப வாராலோ","ஏமவேல் ஏந்திஇரா"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that the gentle girl with eyes like dread spears trembles, like an elephant of killing nature that has escaped the grip of the curve-striped tiger and goes looking for its own good herd; let him not come by night bearing his guarding spear.","n":"The simile gives the girl the elephant's state after danger, still shaking as it looks for safety: her fear outlasts each of his visits. His spear, ஏமவேல், is meant to protect, but it cannot protect her from fearing for him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","acham","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 25","lb":"TN25","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["வேழும்","வேழம்",1,"maa","simile","elephant"],["குயவரி","புலி",1,"maa","simile","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:26":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":26,"t":["கருங்கால் இளவேங்கை கான்றபூக் கள்மேல்","இருங்கால் வயவேங்கை ஏய்க்கும் - மருங்கால்","மழைவளரும் சாரல் இரவரின் வாழாள்","இழைவளரும் சாயல் இனி"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that on the cloud-fed slope the flowers the dark-trunked young vengai has dropped look like a big-pawed strong tiger, and if he comes by night she whose soft grace is set off by her jewels will not live.","n":"Again the two வேங்கை: the tree's spotted heap of flowers looks like the tiger of the same name, so even a false tiger is enough to kill the girl with fear for him. The danger is in her mind, not on the path, and that is the friend's argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 26","lb":"TN26","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["இளவேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","simile","kino tree"],["வயவேங்கை","வேங்கை",2,"maa","simile","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:27":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":27,"t":["பனிவரைநீள் வேங்கைப் பயமலைநன் நாட","இனிவரையாய் என்றெண்ணிச் சொல்வேன் - முனிவரையுள்","நின்றான் வலியாக நீவர யாய்கண்டாள்","ஒன்றாள்காப்(பு) ஈயும் உடன்று"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the good fruitful hill with tall vengai on its misty peaks that she speaks to urge him to marry now: when he came, relying on one who stood in the hill, mother saw him, and, displeased, she has angrily set a watch on the girl.","n":"The friend's 'now' (இனி) has a cause: the secret is out to the mother and the girl is kept in, so the only way left to meet her is marriage. முனிவரையுள் நின்றான் is obscure: a god of the hill whom he trusted, or a man who stood guard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 27","lb":"TN27","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene","kino tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:28":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":28,"t":["மேகம்தோய் சாந்தம் விசைதிமிசு காழ்அகில்","நாகம்தோய் நாகம்என இவற்றைப் - போக","எறிந்(து)உழுவார் தங்கை இருந்தடங்கண் கண்டும்","அறிந்துழல்வான் ஓ!இம் மலை?"],"p":"","r":"Having seen the great wide eyes of the sister of men who cut down cloud-touching sandal, thimisu, hard-cored agil and the sky-high naagam and plough the ground, would any man who knew wander about this hill, the friend asks.","n":"The list of precious trees felled without regret is a portrait of the brothers: what they do to sandal and agil they would do to a stranger. The question can also be heard as wonder at the man's daring, since he does wander the hill though he knows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kan","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 28","lb":"TN28","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",1,"maram","scene","sandal"],["திமிசு","திமிசு",1,"maram","scene","thimisu"],["அகில்","அகில்",1,"maram","scene","agil"],["நாகம்","நாகம்",2,"maram","scene","naagam tree"],["உழுவார்","உழவு",3,"seythi","scene","ploughmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:29":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":29,"t":["பலாஎழுந்த பால்வருக்கைப் பாத்தி அதன்நேர்","நிலாஎழுந்த வார்மணல் நீடிச் - சுலாஎழுந்து","கான்யாறு கால்சீத்த காந்தளம்பூந் தண்பொதும்பர்","தான்நாறத் தாழ்ந்த இடம்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker points out the place: by the bed of milky varukkai jackfruit trees a long stretch of sand white as risen moonlight, and where the forest stream, swirling up, has cut a channel, a cool thicket of glory lilies hangs low and fills it with scent.","n":"The verse is only a place, with no person named: read as the friend telling the man where to meet, every detail is a sign of shelter (low branches, scent, soft sand). The site's text lacks the first line, so this reading rests on our text alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 29","lb":"TN29","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பலா","பலா",1,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["வருக்கைப்","வருக்கை",1,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["கான்யாறு","கான்யாறு",3,"pira","scene","forest stream"],["காந்தளம்பூந்","காந்தள்",3,"maram","scene","glory lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:30":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":30,"t":["திங்களுள் வில்லெழுதித் தேராது வேல்விலக்கித்","தங்களுள் ளாள்என்னும் தாழ்வினால் - இங்கண்","புனங்காக்க வைத்தார்போல் பூங்குழலைப் போந்தென்","மனங்காக்க வைத்தார் மருண்டு"],"p":"","r":"The man says that her kin, drawing a bow upon the moon and holding back the spear without thought, and holding her cheaply as one of their own, set the flower-haired girl here to guard the millet field, and so, without knowing it, set her to guard his heart.","n":"The wit is in the double posting: the family sends her out to keep birds from the millet, and in doing so posts her over his heart, which she now holds. Line 1 is dense; the bow on the moon is most easily her brows on her face and the spear held back her eyes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","nenju","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 30","lb":"TN30","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பூங்குழலைப்","பூ",3,"maram","ornament","flowers in hair"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:31":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":31,"t":["தன்குறையி(து) என்னான் தழைகொணரும் தண்சிலம்பன்","நின்குறை என்னும் நினைப்பினனாய்ப் - பொன்குறையும்","நாள்வேங்கை நீழலுள் நண்ணான் எவன்கொலோ","கோள்வேங்கை யன்னான் குறிப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the lord of the cool slope brings leaves, never saying it is his own need but thinking only of hers; yet he does not come into the shade of the vengai whose gold is now thinning; what can he mean, this man like a killing tiger?","n":"The friend praises his tact (he brings the gift as if it were her need, not his) and in the same breath worries: the vengai's blossoms are falling, the season of meeting is passing, and he keeps away. Calling him a tiger is praise of his strength and a hint that he is hard to read.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 31","lb":"TN31","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தழைகொணரும்","தழை",1,"maram","gift_food","leaf garment"],["நாள்வேங்கை","வேங்கை",3,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["கோள்வேங்கை","வேங்கை",4,"maa","simile","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:2"},"tn:32":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":32,"t":["பானலம் தண்கழிப் பாடறிந்து தன்னைமார்","நூனல நுண்வலையால் நொண்டெடுத்த - கானல்","படுபுலால் காப்பாள் படைநெடுங்கண் நோக்கம்","கடிபொல்லா என்னையே காப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The man says that the girl set to guard the drying fish that her brothers, knowing the fishing grounds of the cool backwater of blue lilies, scooped up with nets of fine thread, turns on him the look of her long, weapon-like eyes: the guarding is cruel, and it is he, not the fish, whom she holds.","n":"The verb காப்பு plays on her task: she is set to guard the fish from birds, and the guard her eyes keep falls on the onlooker instead, so the shore chore becomes the first-sight scene. கடிபொல்லா is hard; 'the guarding is cruel' is the reading taken here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","anangu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 32","lb":"TN32","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பானலம்","பானல்",1,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["நுண்வலையால்","வலை",2,"seythi","scene","fishing net"],["படுபுலால்","புலால்",3,"unavu","scene","drying fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:33":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":33,"t":["பெருங்கடல் வெண்சங்கு காரணமாப் பேணா(து)","இருங்கடல் மூழ்குவார் தங்கை - இருங்கடலுள்","முத்தன்ன வெண்முறுவல் கண்டுருகி நைவார்க்கே","ஒத்தனம் யாமே உளம்"],"p":"","r":"The man says that seeing the white smile, like the pearls of the dark sea, of the sister of those who dive into the vast sea for the white conch without a thought for themselves, his heart has become like theirs, melting and wasting away.","n":"The brothers neglect their lives diving for the sea's white conch; the speaker, drawn by the sea's other white thing in her smile, says he has become one of those who melt and waste, so the kin's trade and the lover's ruin are set side by side without a marker.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 33","lb":"TN33","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வெண்சங்கு","சங்கு",1,"pira","scene","conch"],["மூழ்குவார்","மூழ்குவார்",2,"seythi","scene","divers"],["முத்தன்ன","முத்து",3,"pira","simile","pearl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:34":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":34,"t":["தாமரை தான்முகமாத் தண்அடையீர் மாநீலம்","காமர்கண் ஆகக் கழிதுயிற்றும் - காமருசீர்த்","தண் பரப்ப! பாயிருள் நீவரின்தாழ் கோதையாள்","கண்பரப்பக் காணீர் கசிந்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the cool expanse, whose backwater sleeps with the lotus for its face and the great blue lily among cool leaves for its lovely eyes, that if he comes in the spreading dark he will find the girl of the hanging garland melting in tears, her eyes stretched wide along the way.","n":"The backwater, given a lotus face and lily eyes, sleeps; the girl, whose own face and eyes those flowers stand for, does not. காணீர் can be read as 'you will see' or 'you will not see', which turns the line into a warning or a reproach.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 34","lb":"TN34","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","simile","lotus"],["மாநீலம்","நீலம்",1,"maram","simile","blue water lily"],["கோதையாள்","கோதை",3,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:35":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":35,"t":["புலால்அகற்றும் பூம்புன்னைப் பொங்கு நீர்ச்சேர்ப்ப!","நிலாவகற்றும் வெண்மணல்தண் கானல் - சுலா அகற்றிக்","கங்குல்நீ வாரல் பகல்வரின்மார்க் கவ்வையாம்","மங்குல்நீர் வெண்திரையின்மாட்டு"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore of swelling water, where the flowering punnai drives off the smell of fish and the white sand of the cool grove outshines the moon, not to come in the night, and warns that if he comes by day there will be talk by the white waves in the mist, so that neither way is left open to him.","n":"Shutting both the night tryst and the day tryst is the friend's way of leaving marriage as the only road; the poem does not say so, and the closing lines are obscure (சுலா அகற்றி, மார்க்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","alar","varaivu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 35","lb":"TN35","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பூம்புன்னைப்","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene","punnai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:36":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":36,"t":["முருகுவாய் முள்தாழை நீள்முகைபார்ப் பென்றே","குருகுவாய்ப் பெய்(து)இரை கொள்ளாது - உருகிமிக","இன்னா வெயில்சிற கால்மறைக்கும் சேர்ப்ப! நீ","மன்னா வரவு மற!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore, where a heron, taking the long bud of the fragrant thorny screwpine for its chick, puts food to it, and when it will not eat, melts and shades it with its wings from the cruel sun, to give up these coming-and-going visits.","n":"மன்னா வரவு, a coming that does not stay, is the point of the image: the bird gives unbroken care even to what only looks like its young, while the hero comes and goes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","anbu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 36","lb":"TN36","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முள்தாழை","தாழை",1,"maram","scene","screwpine"],["குருகு","குருகு",2,"pul","iraicchi","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","குருகு","confirmed","1/1","even a bird keeps steady, self-forgetting care for what it holds as its own, so the hero should stop coming and going and marry her"]],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:37":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":37,"t":["ஓதநீர் வேலி உரைகடியாப் பாக்கத்தார்","காதல்நீர் வாராமை கண்ணோக்கி - ஓதநீர்","அன்றறியும் ஆதலால் வாரா(து) அலர்ஒழிய","மன்றறியக் கொள்ளீர் வரைந்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hero that the people of the tide-fenced hamlet, who never hold back their talk, keep their eyes on his comings for love, and only the tide knew of that first day; so, rather than coming in secret, let him marry her for the common square to know, and the gossip will end.","n":"The tide appears twice, once as the fence of the gossiping hamlet and once as the only witness of the secret union; the friend sets the witness that cannot speak against the town that will not stop speaking, and asks for the square (மன்று) to become the witness instead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 37","lb":"TN37","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாக்கத்தார்","பாக்கத்தார்",1,"seythi","scene","hamlet folk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:38":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":38,"t":["மாக்கடல்சேர் வெண்மணல் தண்கானல் பாய்திரைசேர்","மாக்கடல்சேர் தண்பரப்பன் மார்(பு)அணங்கா - மாக்கடலே","என்போலத் துஞ்சாய் இதுசெய்தார் யார்உரையாய்","என்போலும் துன்பம் நினக்கு"],"p":"","r":"The girl, kept awake because the breast of the lord of the cool expanse has afflicted her, asks the great sea, which like her does not sleep, who has done this to it, and whether it too suffers a grief like hers.","n":"The addressee is the sea. The question 'who did this to you' answers itself in the first two lines, which name who did it to her: she reads her own cause into the sea's restlessness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","iravu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 38","lb":"TN38","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:39":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":39,"t":["தந்தார்க்கே ஆம்ஆல் தட மென்தோள் இன்னநாள்","வந்தார்க்கே ஆம்என்பார் வாய்காண்பாம் - வந்தார்க்கே","காவா இளமணல் தண்கழிக் கானல்வாய்ப்","பூவா இளஞாழல் போது"],"p":"","r":"The friend says the broad soft shoulders belong only to the one who gave himself to her, and she will see whether those who say they will go to whoever comes asking on such a day are speaking true; for the one who came, there is the young njaazhal of the cool backwater grove, unguarded and not yet in flower.","n":"The unguarded, unopened njaazhal bud, kept for the one who came, may stand for the girl already promised to her lover; it gives only one pair, so it is not tagged as ullurai. The verse reads like the friend's disclosure to the household when another suitor is spoken of, but the addressee is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 39","lb":"TN39","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இளஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",4,"maram","scene","njaazhal tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:40":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":40,"t":["தன்துணையோ(டு) ஆடும் அலவனையும் தான் நோக்கா","இன்துணையோ(டு) ஆட இயையுமோ? - இன்துணையோ(டு)","ஆடினாய் நீயாயின் அந்நோய்க்(கு)என் நொந்தென்று","போயினான் சென்றான் புரிந்து"],"p":"","r":"The girl says that, watching the crab play with its mate, she asked whether she might play with her own sweet companion; and he said, 'if you have played with your sweet companion, why grieve over that ache?', and went away, set on going.","n":"His reply turns her own word இன்துணை back on her: the pleasure is already had, so the ache is not his to answer for. Who speaks the second half is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 40","lb":"TN40","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["அலவனையும்","அலவன்",1,"pira","iraicchi","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","அலவனையும்","confirmed","1/1","even the crab keeps its partner at play beside it, while the man who should have stayed has gone"]],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:41":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":41,"t":["உருகுமால் உள்ளம் ஒருநாளும் அன்றால்","பெருகுமால் நம்அலர் பேணப் - பெருகா","ஒருங்குவால் மின்னோ(டு) உருமுடைத்தாய் பெய்வான்","நெருங்குவான் போல நெகிழ்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The girl says her heart melts, and not for one day only, while the talk about them swells, and she loosens like the cloud that draws close, gathered with lightning and thunder, and pours.","n":"The rain-cloud carries both halves of the first two lines: the heart that melts and the talk that swells (பெருகுமால்) gather together in one storm. பேண and பெருகா ஒருங்குவால் are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kaamanoy","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 41","lb":"TN41","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மின்னோ","மின்",3,"pira","simile","lightning"],["உருமுடைத்தாய்","உரும்",3,"pira","simile","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:42":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":42,"t":["கவளக் களிப்பியனமால் யானைசிற் றாளி","தவழத்தான் நில்லா ததுபோல் - பவளக்","கடிகை யிடைமுத்தம் காண்தொறும் நில்லா","தொடிகை யிடைமுத்தம் தொக்கு"],"p":"","r":"Like the great elephant, heavy with its mouthfuls, that cannot hold its ground when a young aali crawls near, her bangles will not stay on her arms each time she sees pearls set among the coral beads, and pearl-like tears gather in her hands.","n":"The elephant and the aali belong to the hills; the poet borrows them for a sea-shore girl. The line division is unsure: முத்தம் in line 4 may be the pearls of her ornament or her tears, and the speaker is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["melivu","kaamanoy","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 42","lb":"TN42","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","simile","elephant"],["சிற்றாளி","ஆளி",1,"maa","simile","aali"],["பவளக்","பவளம்",2,"pira","ornament","coral"],["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",3,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["தொடிகை","தொடி",4,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:43":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":43,"t":["கடற்கோ(டு) இருமருப்புக் கால்பாக னாக","அடற்கோட் டியானை திரையா - உடற்றிக்","கரைபாய்நீள் சேர்ப்ப! கனையிருள் வாரல்","வரைவாய்நீ யாகவே வா!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the long shore, where the waves become a fierce tusked elephant, with the sea's two horns for tusks and the wind for its mahout, and rage and leap upon the bank, not to come in the thick dark, but to come as one who will marry her.","n":"The danger of the night shore is drawn as a war elephant under a mahout; the wind drives the waves as the mahout drives the beast, so the image says the danger is steered, not random. கடற்கோடு, 'the sea's horns', is unclear: it may be the conch or the crests.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","iravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 43","lb":"TN43","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:44":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":44,"t":["கடும்புலால் புன்னை கடியும் துறைவ!","படும்புலால் புட்கடிவான் புக்க - தடம்புலாம்","தாழையா ஞாழல் ததைந்துயர்ந்த தாய்பொழில்","எழைமான் நோக்கி இடம்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the ford, where the punnai drives off the strong fish smell, that the place of the simple doe-eyed girl, who went in to chase the birds from the drying fish, is the broad grove grown thick and tall with screwpine and njaazhal.","n":"Naming the grove where the girl does her daily task is how the friend fixes a day meeting without saying so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 44","lb":"TN44","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene","punnai tree"],["படும்புலால்","புலால்",2,"unavu","scene","drying fish"],["புட்கடிவான்","புள்",2,"pul","scene","birds"],["தாழையா","தாழை",3,"maram","scene","screwpine"],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",3,"maram","scene","njaazhal tree"],["மான்","மான்",4,"maa","scene","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:45":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":45,"t":["தாழை தவழ்ந்துலாம் வெண்மணல் தண்கானல்","மாழை நுளையர் மடமகள் - ஏழை","இணைநாடில் இல்லா இருந்தடங்கண் கண்டும்","துணைநாடி னன்தோம் இலன்!"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that, having seen the large broad eyes, matchless though one searched every land, of the simple young daughter of the fisherfolk on the cool grove of white sand where the screwpine creeps, he sought her as his partner, and is without fault.","n":"துணை நாடினன் may be a plea for the hero: he wants her as a wife, not a passing love. The speaker and addressee are inferred, and மாழை and தோம் இலன் can be read otherwise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 45","lb":"TN45","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாழை","தாழை",1,"maram","scene","screwpine"],["நுளையர்","நுளையர்",2,"seythi","scene","fisherfolk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:46":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":46,"t":["தந்(து)ஆயல் வேண்டாஓர் நாட்கேட்டுத் தாழாது","வந்தால்நீ எய்துதல் வாயால்மற்(று) - எந்தாய்","மறிமகர வார்குழையாள் வாழாள்நீ வாரல்","எறிமகரம் கொட்கும் இரா"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hero that there is no need to bring gifts and search: if he asks for her one day and comes without delay, he will win her by word alone; but, my lord, the girl of the long makara earrings will not live if he comes in the night when the striking shark circles, so let him not come.","n":"The same word மகரம் is the ornament on her ear and the beast in the sea: what adorns her is what threatens him, and the friend's fear is for both. தந்து ஆயல் is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","iravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 46","lb":"TN46","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["மறிமகர","மகரம்",3,"pira","ornament","makara earring"],["எறிமகரம்","மகரம்",4,"pira","scene","shark"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:47":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":47,"t":["பண்ணாது பண்மேல்தே பாடும் கழிக்கானல்","எண்ணாது கண்டார்க்கே ஏரணங்கால் - எண்ணாது","சாவார்சான் றாண்மை சலித்திலா மற்றிவளைக்","காவார் கயிறுரீஇ விட்டார்"],"p":"","r":"The man says that in the backwater grove where bees sing tunes nobody set, she is a beauty that strikes like a goddess those who look without thinking, and her kin, whose courage never flags though they die without a thought, do not guard her: they have slipped the rope and let her loose.","n":"Her kin let her roam like a beast untied, which is a complaint about the danger she poses, not about her safety. Whether the 'courage that does not flag though dying' belongs to the kin or to the lovesick onlookers is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","azhagu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 47","lb":"TN47","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பண்மேல்தே","தேன்",1,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:3"},"tn:48":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":48,"t":["திரை மேற்போந்(து) எஞ்சிய தெள்கழிக் கானல்","விரைமேவும் பாக்கம் விளக்காக் - கரைமேல்","விடுவாய் பசும்புற இப்பிகால் முத்தம்","படுவாய் இருளகற்றும் பாத்து"],"p":"","r":"Where the waves have come up and drawn back from the clear backwater grove, the fragrant fishing village has for its lamp the pearls that open-mouthed, green-backed oysters cast on the shore, which drive off the dark as the sun goes down.","n":"The verse names no speaker or hearer; read beside 49, which turns the same pearl-light into a question to the shore lord, the lit village is a reason he cannot come unseen by night. The praise of the shore's riches is also a warning.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 48","lb":"TN48","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["திரை","திரை",1,"pira","scene","wave"],["இப்பிகால்","இப்பி",3,"pira","scene","pearl oyster"],["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",3,"pira","scene","pearl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:49":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":49,"t":["எங்கு வருதி இருங்கழித் தண்சேர்ப்ப!-","பொங்கு திரையுதைப்பப் போந்தெழிந்த - சங்கு","நரன்யியிர்த்த நித்திலம் நள்ளிருள்கால் சீக்கும்","வரன்றுயிர்த்த பாக்கத்து வந்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the cool, broad backwater where he thinks he will come, into a village where pearls breathed out by conches that the surging waves have kicked ashore sweep away even the midnight dark.","n":"The objection to the night visit is not the usual dark and its beasts but light: the shore is so rich that its pearls undo the midnight that should hide him. எங்கு வருதி is a refusal shaped as a question.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 49","lb":"TN49","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["திரையுதைப்பப்","திரை",2,"pira","scene","wave"],["சங்கு","சங்கு",2,"pira","scene","conch"],["நித்திலம்","நித்திலம்",3,"pira","scene","pearl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:50":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":50,"t":["திமில்களி றாகத் திரைபறையாப் பல்புள்","துயில்கெடத் தோன்றும் படையாத் - துயில்போல்","குறியா வரவொழிந்து கோலநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!","நெறியால்நீ கொள்வது நேர்"],"p":"","r":"With boats for elephants, waves for drums and the many birds that rise and break the sleep of the shore for an army, the lord of the lovely sea is asked to give up coming without a set tryst, like sleep, and to take her by the proper way.","n":"The two images pull against each other: the shore is arrayed like a royal army that marches in order, while he comes like sleep, by stealth and without a tryst. The friend asks him to match the order of his own shore (நெறியால் நீ கொள்வது).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kuri","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 50","lb":"TN50","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["திமில்களி","திமில்",1,"pira","simile","fishing boat"],["திரைபறையாப்","திரை",1,"pira","simile","wave"],["பல்புள்","புள்",1,"pul","simile","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:51":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":51,"t":["கடும்புலால் வெண்மணற் கானலுறு மீன்கண்","படும்புலால் பார்த்தும் பகர்தும் - அடும்பெலாம்","சாலிகை போல்வலை சாலம் பலவுணங்கும்","பாலிகை பூக்கும் பயின்று"],"p":"","r":"On the white sand of the grove that reeks of fish, we watch the catch and sell it, and over all the adumbu creepers nets like coats of mail lie drying while the flowers bloom thick about them.","n":"The speaker counts herself among the fish-sellers (பகர்தும், we sell): naming the smell and the trade is how the neythal friend reminds a well-born suitor of the distance between them, though the same chore can also mark the place where she may be met by day.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 51","lb":"TN51","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மீன்கண்","மீன்",1,"pira","scene","fish"],["பகர்தும்","பகர்",2,"seythi","scene","selling"],["அடும்பெலாம்","அடும்பு",2,"maram","scene","adumbu creeper"],["வலை","வலை",3,"seythi","simile","fishing net"],["பாலிகை","பாலிகை",4,"maram","scene","uncertain flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:52":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":52,"t":["திரைபாக னாகத் திமில்களி றாகக்","கரைசேர்ந்த கானல் படையா - விரையாது","வேந்து கிளர்ந்தன்ன வேலைநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப! நாள்","ஆய்ந்து வரைதல் அறம்"],"p":"","r":"Lord of the sea shore, where with the waves for mahouts, the boats for elephants and the grove on the bank for troops the scene stands like a king roused but unhurried: to choose a day and marry her is right.","n":"விரையாது carries the argument: a king does not rush to war but rises in order, and so the lord of such a shore should not rush to the grove at night but fix a day (நாள் ஆய்ந்து). Marriage is called அறம், a duty and not only a kindness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 52","lb":"TN52","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["திரைபாக","திரை",1,"pira","simile","wave"],["திமில்களி","திமில்",1,"pira","simile","fishing boat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:53":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":53,"t":["பாறு புரவியாப் பல்களிறு நீள்திமிலாத்தேறு","திரைபறையாப் புட்படையாத் - தேறாத","மன்கிளர்ந்த போலும் கடற்சேர்ப்ப! மற்றெமர்","முன்கிளர்ந்(து) எய்தல் முடி!"],"p":"","r":"Lord of the sea shore, where with ships for horses, long boats for many elephants, clear waves for drums and birds for troops the scene is like a restless king risen to war: make sure you come forward before our kin rise first.","n":"எமர் முன்கிளர்ந்து may mean that he should rise before our kin do (to give her elsewhere, or against him), or that he should come forward before our kin to ask for her; either way the restless king of the image is a pressure, not the calm one of 52.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 53","lb":"TN53","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாறு","பாறு",1,"pira","simile","ship"],["நீள்திமிலாத்தேறு","திமில்",1,"pira","simile","long boat"],["திரைபறையாப்","திரை",2,"pira","simile","wave"],["புட்படையாத்","புள்",2,"pul","simile","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:54":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":54,"t":["வாராய் வான்நீர்க் கழிக்கானல் நுண்மணல்மேல்","தேரின்மா காலாழும் தீமைத்தே - ஓரில்ஓர்","கோள்நாடல் வேண்டா குறியறிவார்க் கூஉய்க் கொண்டோர்","நாள் நாடி நல்குதல் நன்று"],"p":"","r":"You come, but on the fine sand of the backwater grove the legs of your chariot horses sink, which is a harm; on reflection you need seek no other plan: call those who read omens, choose a day and give yourself to her, which is good.","n":"The friend turns a fact of the shore (horses sink in the sand) into a reason for a public road to her: the secret way is literally unsound under his chariot, and the sound way is the diviners' fixed day. கொண்டோர் is hard to place; it may name the kin who take the bride.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 54","lb":"TN54","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தேரின்மா","மா",2,"maa","scene","horse"],["குறியறிவார்க்","குறியறிவார்",3,"seythi","scene","diviners"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:55":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":55,"t":["கண்பரப்பக் காணாய் கடும்பனி கால்வல்தேர்","மண்பரக்கும் மாயிருள் மேற்கொண்டு - மண்பரக்கும்","ஆறுநீர் வேலைநீ வாரல் வரின்ஆற்றாள்","ஏறுநீர் வேலை எதிர்"],"p":"","r":"Look about you: the cold dew is harsh, and the great dark spreads over the land; do not come in your swift chariot when the water floods the ways, for if you come she will not bear it, facing the rising tide.","n":"The danger is put in her, not in him: வரின் ஆற்றாள், if you come she will not endure it. The friend forbids the night visit by making the heroine's fear for him the cost of his coming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kalaviyal","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 55","lb":"TN55","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஏறுநீர்","ஏறு நீர்",4,"pira","scene","rising tide"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:56":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":56,"t":["கடற்கானல் சேர்ப்ப! கழியுலாஅய் நீண்ட","அடற்கானல் புன்னைதாழ்ந்(து) ஆற்ற - மடற்கானல்","அன்றில் அகவும் அணிநெடும் பெண்ணைத்(து)எம்","முன்றில் இளமணல்மேல் மொய்த்து"],"p":"","r":"Lord of the sea grove, where the punnai of the grove that stretches along the backwater bends low and shades, and the anril calls from the lovely tall palmyras of the frond-thick grove crowding the young sand of our front yard.","n":"The friend gives the shore lord the marks of their house (எம் முன்றில்): the drooping punnai and the palmyras where the anril calls. The anril, the bird that cannot bear parting from its mate, is the traditional sign of her nights; it only calls here and does nothing a lover does, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 56","lb":"TN56","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புன்னைதாழ்ந்","புன்னை",2,"maram","scene","punnai tree"],["அன்றில்","அன்றில்",3,"pul","scene","anril bird"],["பெண்ணைத்","பெண்ணை",3,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:57":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":57,"t":["வருதிரை தானுலாம் வார்மணல் கானல்","ஒருதிரை ஓடா வளமை - இருதிரை","முன்வீழுங் கானல் முழங்கு கடற்சேர்ப்ப!","என்வீழல் வேண்டா இனி"],"p":"","r":"Lord of the roaring sea, whose long-sanded grove the coming waves roam and whose plenty no single wave runs off from, where two waves at once fall before the grove: you need not desire me any more.","n":"The waves that come and fall, two at a time, may hint at a man whose comings are divided; the poem does not say so, and the image gives only one match of mood, so it is not tagged as ullurai. The last line is a refusal in the heroine's own voice (என்), not the friend's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 57","lb":"TN57","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வருதிரை","திரை",1,"pira","scene","wave"],["இருதிரை","திரை",2,"pira","scene","wave"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:58":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":58,"t":["மாயவனும் தம்முனும் போலே மறிகடலும்","கானலும்சேர் வெண்மணலும் காணாயோ - கானல்","இடையெலாம் ஞாழலும் தாழையும் ஆர்ந்த","புடையெலாம் புன்னை புகன்று?"],"p":"","r":"Do you not see the rolling sea and the white sand that lies against the grove, together like Mayavan and his elder brother, and the grove filled through its middle with njazhal and screw-pine and all round with punnai, flowering gladly?","n":"The dark god and his white elder brother give the shore its two colours, dark sea and white sand, joined as brothers are. Mayavan is the god of the mullai land, so the simile brings a mullai deity onto the neythal shore. காணாயோ invites the hearer into the grove, which reads as pointing out a meeting place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","azhagu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 58","lb":"TN58","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாயவனும்","மாயவன்",1,"deyvam","simile","Krishna"],["தம்முனும்","தம்முன்",1,"deyvam","simile","elder brother"],["மறிகடலும்","கடல்",1,"pira","simile","sea"],["வெண்மணலும்","மணல்",2,"pira","simile","sand"],["ஞாழலும்","ஞாழல்",3,"maram","scene","njazhal tree"],["தாழையும்","தாழை",3,"maram","scene","screw-pine"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",4,"maram","scene","punnai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:59":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":59,"t":["பகல்வரின் கவ்வை பலவாம் பரியாது","இரவரின் ஏதமும் அன்ன - புகஅரிய","தாழை துவளும் தரங்கநீர்ச் சேர்ப்பிற்றே","ஏழை நுளையர் இடம்"],"p":"","r":"If you come by day the gossip will be great, and if you come by night without care the dangers are as great; the home of the poor fisherfolk is on the wave-beaten shore where the screw-pine, hard to pass through, sways.","n":"Both trysts are refused at once, day for the gossip and night for the danger, which leaves only marriage open, though the friend never names it. ஏழை நுளையர், the poor fisherfolk, adds the modesty of class to the refusal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kuri","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 59","lb":"TN59","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாழை","தாழை",3,"maram","scene","screw-pine"],["நுளையர்","நுளையர்",4,"seythi","scene","fisher folk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:60":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":60,"t":["திரையலறிப் பேராத் தெழியாத் திரியாக்","கரையலவன் காலினாற் கானாக் - கரையருகே","நெய்தல் மலர்கொய்யும் நீள்நெடுங் கண்ணினாள்","மையல் நுளையர் மகள்"],"p":"","r":"Where the waves cry out and will not leave but shout and wander, and the shore crab marks the sand with its legs, the long-eyed girl plucking neythal flowers near the shore is the fisherman's daughter who has made me mad with love.","n":"The waves that cry, will not go and wander are the hero's own state set out before he names its cause; the closing word மையல் (madness of love) is placed on the girl, not on himself. She is identified by her people, நுளையர் மகள், which is all the lover knows of her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kalaviyal","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 60","lb":"TN60","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["திரையலறிப்","திரை",1,"pira","scene","wave"],["கரையலவன்","அலவன்",2,"pira","scene","crab"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"],["நுளையர்","நுளையர்",4,"seythi","scene","fisher folk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:61":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":61,"t":["அறி(கு)அரி(து) யார்க்கும் அரவ நீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!","நெறிதிரிவார் இன்மையால் இல்லை - முறிதிரிந்த","கண்டலந்தண் டில்லை கலந்து கழிசூழ்ந்த","மிண்டலந்தண் தாழை இணைந்து"],"p":"","r":"Lord of the sounding sea, it is hard for anyone to know of it, for no one strays along that path: there is nothing there but the backwater ringed by kandal with turning shoots and cool thillai mingled, and the dense cool screw-pine joined among them.","n":"Read as the friend naming a meeting place, the thicket of kandal, thillai and screw-pine is safe because no one walks there; the same words could be a warning that the way is pathless. இல்லை in line 2 has no stated subject.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 61","lb":"TN61","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கண்டலந்தண்","கண்டல்",3,"maram","scene","kandal mangrove"],["டில்லை","தில்லை",3,"maram","scene","thillai mangrove"],["தாழை","தாழை",4,"maram","scene","screw-pine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:62":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":62,"t":["வில்லார் விழவினும் வேலாழி சூழுலகில்","நல்லார் விழவகத்தும் நாம்காணேம் - நல்லாய்!","உவர்கத்(து) ஒரோஉதவிச் சேர்ப்பன்ஒப் பாரைச்","சுவர்கத்(து) உளராயின் சூழ்"],"p":"","r":"Neither at the bowmen's festivals nor at the festivals of fine people in this sea-girt world have we seen his like; good girl, if there are any in heaven to match the shore lord who once helped us in the salt land, consider it.","n":"The help (உதவி) is the founding act of the love, the once-only service in the salt pans; the praise ranks him above earth and sets heaven as the only place left to search, which makes a debt of gratitude into the measure of a lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","perumai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 62","lb":"TN62","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["வில்லார்","வில்லார்",1,"seythi","scene","bowmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:4"},"tn:63":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":63,"t":["எரிநிற நீள்பிண்டி இணரினம் எல்லாம்","வரிநிற நீள்வண்டர் பாடப் - புரிநிறநீள்","பொன்னணிந்த கோங்கம் புணர் முலையாய்! பூந்தொடித்தோள்","என்னணிந்த ஈடில் பசப்பு?"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the heroine, whose paired breasts are like kongam buds dressed in gold, why, now that striped bees sing over every fire-red cluster of the asoka, her shoulders with their flower bangles wear this matchless pallor.","n":"The question answers itself: the asoka in flower is the season he named, so her pallor marks that he has not come. என்னணிந்த makes the pallor something she has put on, set against the gold the kongam wears.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 63","lb":"TN63","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நீள்பிண்டி","பிண்டி",1,"maram","scene","asoka"],["வண்டர்","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bees"],["கோங்கம்","கோங்கம்",3,"maram","scene","kongu tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:64":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":64,"t":["பேணாய் இதன்திறத்(து) என்றாலும் பேணாதே","நாணாய நல்வளையாய் நாணிண்மை - காணாய்","எரிசிதறி விட்டன்ன ஈர்முருக்(கு) ஈடில்","பொரிசிதறி விட்டன்ன புன்கு"],"p":"","r":"Though she is told to keep herself in hand she will not, so the friend bids the girl of modest, fine bangles look at the shamelessness of the season: the moist murukku blazing as if fire had been scattered, the pungu white as if puffed rice had been strewn.","n":"நாணிண்மை is read as நாண் இன்மை; whether the shamelessness is the trees' open display or the girl's open grieving turns on that reading. Either way it is set against her own modesty in நாணாய நல்வளையாய்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","naanam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 64","lb":"TN64","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஈர்முருக்","முருக்கு",3,"maram","simile","flame of the forest"],["புன்கு","புன்கு",4,"maram","simile","pongam tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:65":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":65,"t":["தான்தாயாக் கோங்கம் தளர்ந்து முலைகொடுப்ப","ஈன்றாய்நீ பாவை இருங் குரவே! - ஈன்றாள்","மொழிகாட்டாய் ஆயினும் முள்ளெயிற்றாள் சென்ற","வழிகாட்டாய் ஈதென்று வந்து"],"p":"","r":"The mother searching for her eloped daughter speaks to the dark kuravam: you bore your doll-flowers while the kongam, sagging, gave them the breast as foster mother; though you will not speak to me as one mother to another, come and show me the way my sharp-toothed girl went.","n":"The kuravam's flower was called பாவை, doll, so the speaker sees a tree that bore a child and had another nurse it, which is the household's own arrangement of mother and foster mother; she asks the tree for ஈன்றாள் மொழி, the word a mother owes a mother. The addressee is the tree.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 65","lb":"TN65","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கோங்கம்","கோங்கம்",1,"maram","scene","kongu tree"],["பாவை","பாவை",2,"maram","scene","kuravam flower"],["குரவே","குரவம்",2,"maram","scene","kuravam tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:66":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":66,"t":["வல்வருங் காணாய் வயங்கி முருக்கெல்லாம்","செல்வர் சிறார்க்குப்பொற் கொல்லர்போல் - நல்ல","பவளக் கொழுந்தின்மேல் பொற்றாலி பாஅய்த்","திகழக்கான் றிட்டன தேர்ந்து!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine he will come soon: look, all the murukku trees, shining, have chosen and set gold talis over fine coral shoots, like goldsmiths making ornaments for rich men's children.","n":"The goldsmith simile makes the season a craftsman delivering on time (தேர்ந்து, choosing carefully), so the trees' punctuality stands surety for the man's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 66","lb":"TN66","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முருக்கெல்லாம்","முருக்கு",1,"maram","simile","flame of the forest"],["கொல்லர்போல்","பொற்கொல்லர்",2,"seythi","simile","goldsmiths"],["பவளக்","பவளம்",3,"pira","scene","coral"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:67":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":67,"t":["வெறுக்கைக்குச் சென்றார் விளங்கிழாய்! தோன்றார்","பொறுக்கஎன் றால்பொறுக்க லாமோ? - ஒறுப்பபோல்","பொன்னுள் உறுபவளம் போன்ற புணர்முருக்கம்","என்னுள் உறுநோய் பெரிது!"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that the man who went for wealth has not appeared; if she is told to bear it, can it be borne? The paired murukku blossoms, like coral set in gold, seem to punish her, and the sickness within her is great.","n":"வெறுக்கை, wealth, is the word the verse opens on, and நோய் the word it ends on: the trade of one for the other is the whole complaint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu","selvam","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 67","lb":"TN67","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["புணர்முருக்கம்","முருக்கு",3,"maram","simile","flame of the forest"],["உறுபவளம்","பவளம்",3,"pira","simile","coral"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:68":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":68,"t":["சென்றக்கால் செல்லும்வாய் என்னோ? இருஞ்சுரத்து","நின்றக்கால் நீடி ஒளிவிடா - நின்ற","இழைக்கமர்ந்த ஏஏர் இளமுலையாள் ஈடில்","குழைக்கமர்ந்த நோக்கின் குறிப்பு!"],"p":"","r":"The man asks his heart what way there is to go when, as he stands in the great wasteland, the meaning in the glance of the young girl who loves her jewels, her eyes reaching to her earrings, stays and will not let him go.","n":"A செலவழுங்குதல் poem: the journey is undone not by her words but by the குறிப்பு of a glance, which he reads only once he is already on the road. ஒளிவிடா (does not let go, or does not cease to shine) is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","pirivu","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 68","lb":"TN68","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:69":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":69,"t":["அத்தம் நெடிய அழற்கதிரோன் செம்பாகம்","அத்தமறைந் தான்இவ் அணியிழையோ(டு) - ஒத்த","தகையினால் எம்சீறூர்த் தங்கினிராய் நாளை","வகையினிராய்ச் சேறல் வனப்பு"],"p":"","r":"Villagers tell the eloping man that the road is long and the fiery sun has already sunk half his red disc behind the western hill, so it would be fitting for him to stay the night in their small village with this jewelled girl and go on tomorrow in proper order.","n":"அத்தம் is used twice in one line with two senses, the road and the hill of sunset, so a single word names both the danger and the hour; the offer is shelter for a couple who have left their kin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","virundhu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 69","lb":"TN69","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["kandor","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:70":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":70,"t":["நின்நோக்கம் கொண்டமான் தண்குரவ நீழல்காண்","பொன்நோக்கம் கொண்டபூங் கோங்கம்காண் - பொன் நோக்கம்","கொண்ட சுணங்கணி மென்முலைக் கொம்பன்னாய்!","வண்டல் அயர்மணல்மேல் வந்து!"],"p":"","r":"On the road the man tells the girl to see the deer with her look resting in the cool kuravam shade and the flowering kongam with the look of gold, and calls her, a slender branch with soft breasts decked in golden spots, to come and play at sand houses on the sand.","n":"வண்டல் அயர் is the girls' game of sand houses she has left at home; he offers her the wasteland as that playground, so the comfort is for the life she gave up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","anbu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 70","lb":"TN70","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொண்டமான்","மான்",1,"maa","scene","deer"],["தண்குரவ","குரவம்",1,"maram","scene","kuravam tree"],["கோங்கம்காண்","கோங்கம்",2,"maram","scene","kongu tree"],["கொம்பன்னாய்","கொம்பு",3,"maram","simile","branch"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:71":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":71,"t":["அஞ்சுடர்நள் வாண்முகத்(து) ஆயிழையும் மாநிலா","வெஞ்சுடர்நீள் வேலானும் போதரக் கண்டு - அஞ்சி","ஒருசுடரும் இன்றி உலகுபா ழாக","இருசுடரும் போந்தனஎன் றார்"],"p":"","r":"Seeing the jewelled girl, her face bright as the midnight moon, and the man of the long spear, hot-bright as the sun, walking along together, onlookers cried out in fear that both lights had come down and the world, left with neither, would lie desolate.","n":"The hyperbole puts sun and moon on one road, which the sky never allows; the onlookers' fear is praise. மாநிலா in line 2 is hard to place and is read with the man's sun-image only loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 71","lb":"TN71","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["kandor","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:72":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":72,"t":["முகந்தா மரைமுறுவல் ஆம்பல்கண் நீலம்","இகந்தார் விரல்காந்தள் என்றென்று - உகந்தியைந்த","மாழைமா வண்டிற்காம் நீழல் வருந்தாதே","ஏழைதான் செல்லும் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"Taking her face for a lotus, her smile for an ambal, her eyes for blue lilies and her fingers for kaanthal, the bees gather over her in delight and make a shade, so the simple girl goes on her way pleasantly, without suffering.","n":"The bees' mistake turns her beauty into shelter: the four flowers belong to the watered fields and the hills, not the waste, so she carries a cooler land with her and is shaded by it. The speaker is not named; onlookers reassuring the foster mother fits the closing line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 72","lb":"TN72","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["kandor","sevili",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தா மரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","scene","lotus"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","scene","white water-lily"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",1,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"],["விரல்காந்தள்","காந்தள்",2,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["வண்டிற்காம்","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bees"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:73":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":73,"t":["செவ்வாய்க் கரியகண் சீரினால் கேளாதும்","கவ்வையால் காணாதும் ஆற்றாதும் - அவ் வாயம்","தார்த்தத்தை வாய்மொழியும் தண்கயத்து நீலமும்","ஓர்த்தொழிந்தாள் என்பேதை ஊர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The mother laments that her simple girl, with red mouth and dark eyes, has gone off and given up the garlanded parrot of her playmates, whose speech was like hers, and the blue lily of the cool pond, which was like her eyes, unable to bear the town's gossip.","n":"The parrot and the lily are the things her mouth and eyes were praised by; the mother counts it a loss that the girl no longer needs her likenesses. The run of negatives in lines 1 and 2 (கேளாதும், காணாதும், ஆற்றாதும்) is read only loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","alar","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 73","lb":"TN73","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தார்த்தத்தை","தத்தை",3,"pul","scene","parrot"],["நீலமும்","நீலம்",3,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:74":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":74,"t":["புன்புறவே! சேவலோ(டு) ஊடல் பொருளன்றால்","அன்புறவே உடையார் ஆயினும் - வன்புற்று","அதுகாண் அகன்ற வழிநோக்கிப் பொன்போர்த்து","இதுகாண்என் வண்ணம் இனி!"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells a small dove not to sulk with its mate, since sulking is no matter even between those bound in love: he hardened and left, and she bids it look at the road he went by and at her own colour, now turned to gold from watching it.","n":"The warning may carry a confession: her own sulking came before his going. The dove pair is addressed and the moral is spoken aloud, so it is neither ullurai (the tenor is stated) nor iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 74","lb":"TN74","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புன்புறவே","புறா",1,"pul","scene","dove"],["சேவலோ","சேவல்",1,"pul","scene","male dove"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:75":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":75,"t":["எரிந்து சுடும்இரவி ஈடில் கதிரான்","விரிந்து விடுகூந்தல் வெஃகாப் - புரிந்து","விடுகயிற்றின் மாசுணம் வீயும்நீள் அத்தம்","அடுதிறலான் பின் சென்ற ஆறு"],"p":"","r":"The mother says the road her girl took behind the man of slaying strength is the long wasteland where the sun burns with matchless rays, not sparing her loosened hair, and where the python lies dying, limp as a twisted rope let go.","n":"The heat kills even the python that lies in wait for travellers, so the mother's fear passes from the beast to the sun. Line 2 (விரிந்து விடுகூந்தல் வெஃகா) is a repaired line whose reading differs from the web witness, and its sense is guessed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","acham","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 75","lb":"TN75","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மாசுணம்","மாசுணம்",3,"pira","scene","python"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:76":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":76,"t":["நெஞ்சம் நினைப்பினும் நெற்பொரியும் நீளத்தம்","அஞ்சல் எனஆற்றின் அஞ்சிற்றால் - அஞ்சப்","புடைநெடும் காதுறப் போழ்ந்தகன்று நீண்ட","படைநெடுங்கண் கொண்ட பனி"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that, on the long waste where paddy would pop into puffed rice at the mere thought of it, she was afraid of the road even when told not to fear, and tears filled her long spear-like eyes, slit wide to her ears.","n":"The hyperbole (grain pops at a thought) puts the heat in the mind before the body; whose fear அஞ்சிற்று names, and whether the speaker is the friend, is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","pirivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 76","lb":"TN76","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெற்பொரியும்","நெல்",1,"unavu","scene","paddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:77":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":77,"t":["வந்தால்தான் செல்லாமோ வாரிடையாய்! வார்கதிரால்","வெந்தாற்போல் தோன்றும்நீள் வேய்அத்தம் - தந்தார்","தகரக் குழல்புரளத் தாழ்துகில்கை யேந்தி","மகரக் குழைமறித்த நோக்கு?"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her slender-waisted friend whether, when he comes, she cannot go with him across the long bamboo waste that looks burnt by the sun's long rays, and recalls the look given as she set out, her fragrant hair tumbling, her trailing robe gathered in her hand, her makara earring turned back.","n":"Who gave the look of lines 3 and 4 (தந்தார்) and to whom is not clear; the reading takes it as the picture of a girl setting out with her lover.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 77","lb":"TN77","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேய்அத்தம்","வேய்",2,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["தகரக்","தகரம்",3,"maram","ornament","fragrant tagara"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:78":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":78,"t":["ஒருகை, இருமருப்பின் மும்மதமால் யானை","பருகுநீர் பைஞ்சுனையில் காணா(து) - அருகல்","வழிவிலங்கி வீழும் வரைஅத்தம் சென்றார்","அழிவிலர் ஆக அவர்!"],"p":"","r":"The heroine prays that the man who went into the hill waste, where even the great elephant with one trunk, two tusks and three musth streams finds no water to drink in the green spring, turns off the path and falls, may come to no harm.","n":"The counting conceit (one, two, three) builds the elephant up only to fell it, so the wish அழிவிலர் ஆக asks for more than the land allows. Not iraicchi: the elephant is alone and does nothing a lover does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 78","lb":"TN78","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["பைஞ்சுனையில்","சுனை",2,"pira","absent","spring"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:79":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":79,"t":["சென்றார் வருதல் செறிதொடி! சேய்த்தன்றால்","நின்றார்சொல் தேறாதாய்! நீடின்றி - வென்றார்","எடுத்த கொடியின் இலங்கருவி தோன்றும்","கடுத்த மலைநாடு காண்!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the tight-bangled heroine, who will not trust those who stand by her, that the return of the man who went is not far off: let her see, without delay, the hill country where the shining waterfall shows like the banner raised by victors.","n":"The victors' banner makes the waterfall announce his success before he arrives; the falls run full only when the rains he promised to return by have come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 79","lb":"TN79","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இலங்கருவி","அருவி",3,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:80":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":80,"t":["உருவேற் கண்ணாய்! ஒரு கால்தேர்ச் செல்வன்","வெருவிவீந்து உக்கநீள் அத்தம் - வருவர்","சிறந்து பொருள்தருவான் சேட்சென்றார் இன்றே","இறந்துகண் ஆடும் இடம்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine of spear-fine eyes that the man who went far to bring wealth will come today in honour across the long waste where things have shrivelled and dropped in dread of the lord of the one-wheeled chariot, for this is the hour her left eye throbs.","n":"இடம் closes the verse in two senses, place and the left side: the throbbing of a woman's left eye is the good omen, so the poem ends on the sign, not the road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 80","lb":"TN80","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:81":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":81,"t":["கொன்றாய்! குருந்தாய்! கொடி முல்லாய்! வாடினீர்","நின்றேன் அறிந்தேன் நெடுங்கண்ணாள் - சென்றாளுக்(கு)","என்னுரைத்தீர்க்(கு) என்னுரைத்தாட்(கு) என்னுரைத்தீர்க்(கு) என்னுரைத்தாள்","மின்னுரைத்த பூண்மிளிர விட்டு?"],"p":"","r":"The mother of the girl who has gone speaks to the konrai, the kuruntham and the mullai creeper: you have withered; I stand here and I know; what did you say to the long-eyed girl as she went, and what did she say to you, leaving with her lightning-bright ornaments glittering?","n":"The stammered question of line 3 (என்னுரைத்தீர்க்கு என்னுரைத்தாட்கு...) puts the grief into the syntax. The withered mullai plants may be read as having withered at the girl's going.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 81","lb":"TN81","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கொன்றாய்","கொன்றை",1,"maram","absent","konrai"],["குருந்தாய்","குருந்து",1,"maram","absent","kuruntham"],["முல்லாய்","முல்லை",1,"maram","absent","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:82":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":82,"t":["ஆண்கட னாம்ஆற்றை ஆயுங்கால் ஆடவர்க்குப்","பூண்கடனாப் போற்றிப் புரிந்தமையால் - பூண்கடனாச்","செய்பொருட்குச் செல்வரால் சின்மொழி! நீசிறிது","நைபொருட்கண் செல்லாமை நன்று"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the soft-spoken heroine that men, weighing the way that is a man's duty, have taken it up and cherished it as a duty they must bear, and so will go to make wealth; it is good that she not go even a little into wasting grief.","n":"கடன் is said three times (ஆண்கடன், பூண்கடன் twice): the comfort is an argument from obligation, which casts the heroine's grief as a lapse from her own duty to bear it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","selvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 82","lb":"TN82","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:83":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":83,"t":["செல்பவோ சிந்தனையும் ஆகாதே நெஞ்செரியும்","வெல்பவோ சென்றார் வினைமுடிய - நல்லாய்","இதடி கரையும்கல் மாபோலத் தோன்றுச்","சிதடி கரையும் திரிந்து"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks whether he would really go: even the thought is unbearable and her heart burns; will he who went win and finish his work? She bids her friend hear the cicadas that wander and shrill like a doe crying at a beast of stone.","n":"The simile of lines 3 and 4 is obscure (இதடி, கல் மா); the reading follows the plain words, a doe calling at something that will never answer, which fits a voice crying to an absent man.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 83","lb":"TN83","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இதடி","இதடி",3,"maa","simile","doe"],["சிதடி","சிதடி",4,"pira","scene","cicada"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:84":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":84,"t":["கள்ளியங் காட்ட கடமா இரிந்தோடத்","தள்ளியும் செல்பவோ தம்முடையார் - கொள்ளும்","பொருளில ராயினும் பொங்கெனப்போந்(து) எய்யும்","அருளில் மறவர் அதர்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks whether men with someone of their own would thrust her aside and go by the track of graceless warriors, who rush out and shoot travellers even when they carry no wealth to take, in the cactus scrub where the wild bison flees.","n":"அருளில் sets the robbers' lack of grace beside the man's own: he goes for wealth by a road where others kill those who have none, and the parallel is left for the hearer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","arul","kolai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 84","lb":"TN84","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["கள்ளியங்","கள்ளி",1,"maram","scene","cactus"],["கடமா","கடமா",1,"maa","scene","gaur"],["மறவர்","மறவர்",4,"seythi","scene","bowmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:85":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":85,"t":["பொருள் பொருள் என்றார்சொல் பொன்போலப் போற்றி","அருள்பொருள் ஆகாமை யாக - அருளான்","வளமை கொணரும் வகையினால் மற்றோர்","இளமை கொணர இசை"],"p":"","r":"The man who keeps saying 'wealth, wealth' has treasured those words like gold until grace counts for nothing; since he has a way to bring wealth back, let him agree to bring back youth as well.","n":"The taunt rests on what it leaves unsaid: wealth can be fetched again, youth cannot, so his own reasoning about wealth refutes his going.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","selvam","arul","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 85","lb":"TN85","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:86":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":86,"t":["ஒல்வார் உளரேல் உரையாய் ஒழியாது","செல்வார்என் றாய்நீ சிறந்தாயே - செல்லாது","அசைந்தொழிந்த யானை பசியால்ஆள் பார்த்து","மிசைந்தொழியும் அத்தம் விரைந்து"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend, who said he would surely go, to name anyone who could bear it, and calls her excellent indeed, when he would hurry across the waste where a worn-out elephant that can go no farther eyes men in its hunger and eats.","n":"சிறந்தாயே is irony. ஆள் பார்த்து மிசைந்தொழியும் is read literally, though an elephant eating men is odd; the sense may be that it waits by the path for travellers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 86","lb":"TN86","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:87":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":87,"t":["ஒன்றானும் நா(ம்)மொழிய லாமோ செலவுதான்","பின்றாது பேணும் புகழான்பின் - பின்றா","வெலற்கரிதாம் வில்வலான் வேல்விடலை பாங்காச்","செலற்(கு) அரிதாச் சேய சுரம்"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether they can say even one word against the going of the man who guards his fame and never turns back: the far waste is hard to cross even as companion to that unconquerable young spearman, skilled with the bow.","n":"The repeated பின்றா (not turning back) is made his virtue and the reason no one can hold him; whether the heroine is being told she could not go with him is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","pirivu","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 87","lb":"TN87","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:88":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":88,"t":["அல்லாத என்னையும் தீரமற்(று) ஐயன்மார்","பொல்லாத தென்பது நீபொருந்தாய் - எல்லார்க்கும்","வல்லி ஒழியின் வகைமைநீள் வாட்கண்ணாய்","புல்லி ஒழிவான் புலந்து"],"p":"","r":"Speaking of her elder brothers' disapproval, which the heroine of long sword-like eyes will not accept, the friend seems to warn her that if she holds back now he will embrace her once and go away offended.","n":"The first two lines are hard to construe; the reading rests on ஐயன்மார் (elder brothers), வல்லி ஒழியின் (if you fail to be firm) and புல்லி ஒழிவான் புலந்து (he will embrace and leave, sulking).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 88","lb":"TN88","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:89":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":89,"t":["நண்ணிநீர் சென்மின் நமர்அவர் ஆபவேல்","எண்ணிய எண்ணம் எளிதரா - எண்ணிய","வெஞ்சுடர் அன்னானை யான்கண்டேன் கண்டாளாம்","தண்சுடர் அன்னாளைத் தான்"],"p":"","r":"Travellers answer the searching mother: go on close behind them, for if they are your people your purpose will not come easily; I saw the man like the hot sun, and she saw the girl like the cool moon.","n":"The sighting is divided by sex: the man saw the man and his woman companion saw the girl, a propriety of the road; sun and moon make the couple a matched pair. Line 2's எண்ணிய எண்ணம் எளிதரா is read only tentatively.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 89","lb":"TN89","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["kandor","sevili",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:90":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":90,"t":["வேறாக நின்னை வினவுனேன் தெய்வத்தால்","கூறாயோ கூறுங் குணத்தினனாய் - வேறாக","என்மனைக்(கு) ஏறக்கொணருமோ வெல்வளையைத்","தன்மனைக்கே உய்க்குமோ தான்"],"p":"","r":"The mother asks the diviner apart, by his god, to tell her, since telling is his gift: will the youth bring the girl of victorious bangles first up to her house, or take her straight to his own?","n":"வேறாக, said twice, frames the question as private: the mother asks apart because the answer (his house, not hers) would shame her before others. The addressee is the diviner.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","velan","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 90","lb":"TN90","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:91":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":91,"t":["கள்ளிசார் காரோமை நாரில்பூ நீள்முருங்கை","நற்றியவேய் வாழ்பவர் நண்ணுபவோ - புள்ளிப்","பருந்து கழுகொடு வம்பலர்ப் பார்த்தாண்(டு)","இருந்துறங்க வீயும் இடம்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks whether men who mean to live would go near a place of cactus, dark omai, long murungai with fibreless flowers and bamboo, where the spotted kite and the vulture watch for travellers and sit there sleeping till they die.","n":"The birds' patience is the horror: they sit and doze because the travellers will die without their help. நற்றிய and நாரில்பூ are uncertain words, and வாழ்பவர் may mean those who wish to live or those who live well.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","acham","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 91","lb":"TN91","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["கள்ளிசார்","கள்ளி",1,"maram","scene","cactus"],["காரோமை","ஓமை",1,"maram","scene","omai tree"],["நீள்முருங்கை","முருங்கை",1,"maram","scene","drumstick tree"],["நற்றியவேய்","வேய்",2,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பருந்து","பருந்து",3,"pul","scene","kite"],["கழுகொடு","கழுகு",3,"pul","scene","vulture"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:92":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":92,"t":["செல்பவோ தம்மடைந்தார் சீரழியச் சிள்துவன்றிக்","கொல்பபோல் கூப்பிடும் வெங்கதிரோன் - மல்கிப்","பொடிவெந்து பொங்கிமேல் வான்சுடும் கீழால்","அடிவெந்து கண்சுடும் ஆறு"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks whether a man would go, ruining the well-being of those who depend on him, by the road where cicadas crowd and shrill as if to kill under the hot sun, the dust swells and burns, the sky scorches from above and from below the feet burn and the eyes smart.","n":"தம்மடைந்தார் (those who have come to him for refuge) makes the wife a dependant, so his going is a breach of the protector's duty, not only of love; the heat is mapped above, below and in the eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","arul","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 92","lb":"TN92","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சிள்துவன்றிக்","சிள்",1,"pira","simile","cicada"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:5"},"tn:93":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":93,"t":["கருங்கடல் மாந்திய வெண்தலைக் கொண்மூ","இருங்கடல்மா கொன்றான்வேல் மின்னிப் - பெருங்கடல்","தன்போல் முழங்கித் தளவம் குருந்தனைய","என்கொல்யான் ஆற்றும் வகை"],"p":"","r":"The white-topped cloud that drank the dark sea flashes lightning like the spear of the god who killed the beast in the great sea and roars as the great sea roars, while the thalavam and the kurunthu flower together; the woman asks how she is to bear it.","n":"The cloud drank the sea and now roars like it: the sea comes back as sound. Murugan, god of the hills, is borrowed only for his spear's flash, so the mullai scene carries a kurinji god (mixed). குருந்தனைய may be read குருந்து அணைய, the thalavam creeper embracing the kurunthu, which would set joined plants against her loneliness; read as அனைய ('such'), it is a list of the season's flowers, so the plants stay scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 93","lb":"TN93","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கொண்மூ","கொண்மூ",1,"pira","scene","rain-cloud"],["கொன்றான்","கடல் மா கொன்றான்",2,"deyvam","simile","Murugan"],["தளவம்","தளவம்",3,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"],["குருந்தனைய","குருந்து",3,"maram","scene","kurunthu tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:94":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":94,"t":["பகல்பருகிப் பல்கதிர் ஞாயிறுகல் சேர","இகல்கருதித் திங்கள் இருளைப் - பகல்வர","வெண்ணிலாக் காலும் மருள்மாலை வேய்த்தோளாய்","உள் நிலாது என்ஆவி யூர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"As the many-rayed sun, having drunk up the day, reaches the hill and the moon, as if picking a quarrel, pours white moonlight to bring day back over the dark, the woman tells her bamboo-shouldered friend that in this bewildering evening her life will not stay within her but rides away.","n":"The sky is a battle (இகல்) between sun and moon in which neither side is hers; the moon's false day is no comfort. ஊர்ந்து makes her life a thing that rides off. மருள்மாலை is the Kural's word for the same hour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 94","lb":"TN94","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:95":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":95,"t":["மேல்நோக்கி வெங்கதிரோன் மத்தியநீர் கீழ் நோக்கிக்","கால்நோக்கம் கொண்டழகாக் காண்மடவாய் - மானோக்கி","போதாரி வண்டெலாம் நெட்டெழுத்தின் மேல்புரிய","சாதாரி நின்றறையுஞ் சார்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The speaker bids the simple, doe-eyed woman look: the hot sun has turned upward, the gathered water comes down, the wind has taken on beauty, and the bees that fill every flower hum the saadhaari mode on long-drawn notes.","n":"The verse plays on looking: the sun looks up (மேல்நோக்கி), the water looks down (கீழ் நோக்கி), the wind takes on a look (நோக்கம்), and the woman addressed is herself 'doe-look' (மானோக்கி). நெட்டெழுத்தின் மேல் puts the bees' hum on long syllables, as a singer holds a note.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 95","lb":"TN95","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["போதாரி","போது",3,"maram","scene","blossom"],["வண்டெலாம்","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bees"],["சாதாரி","சாதாரி",4,"yaazh","scene","melodic mode"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:96":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":96,"t":["இருள்பரந்(து) ஆழியான் தன்னிறம்போல் தம்முன்","அருள்பரந்த ஆய்நிறம் போன்றும் - மருள்பரந்த","பால்போலும் வெண்ணிலவும் பையர அல்குலாய்","வேல்போலும் வீழ் துணைஇ லார்க்கு"],"p":"","r":"The dark spreads like the colour of the discus-bearer and the moonlight like the pale colour of his gracious elder brother, white as milk and bewildering; the speaker tells the woman of the serpent-hood waist that it falls like a spear on those who have lost their partner.","n":"Both halves of the night are coloured by the mullai god and his brother, the dark one and the white one, so the land's own divine pair makes the night; the night of the two brothers is a spear to one who has no partner.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 96","lb":"TN96","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஆழியான்","ஆழியான்",1,"deyvam","simile","Maayon"],["பால்போலும்","பால்",3,"unavu","simile","milk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:97":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":97,"t":["பாழிபோல் மாயவன்தன் பற்றார் களிற்றெறிந்த","வாழிபோல் ஞாயிறு கல்சேரத் - தோழி","மான்மாலை தம்முன் நிறம்போல் மதிமுளைப்ப","யான்மாலை ஆற்றேன் நினைந்து"],"p":"","r":"As the sun reaches the hill like the discus Maayavan hurled at his foes' elephant, and the moon sprouts in the dusky evening with the colour of his elder brother, the woman tells her friend she cannot bear the evening as she thinks of him.","n":"வாழி in line 2 is read as ஆழி, the discus, with a glide after எறிந்த; பாழிபோல் in line 1 (like a stronghold, or like a field of war) is not fixed. The setting sun is a weapon still red from killing, the evening a battle that ends in her defeat.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 97","lb":"TN97","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மாயவன்தன்","மாயவன்",1,"deyvam","simile","Maayon"],["களிற்றெறிந்த","களிறு",1,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:98":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":98,"t":["வீயும் வியப்புறவின் வீழ்துளியால் மாக்கடுக்கை","நீயும் பிறரொடும்காண் நீடாதே - ஆயும்","கழலாகிப் பொன்வட்டாய்த் தாராய் மடலாய்க்","குழலாகிக் கோல்சுரியாய்க் கூர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"With the drops falling over the wondrous scrubland the great konrai bursts into flower, and the speaker bids her friend look at it at once, with the others too: its blossoms sharpen into anklet, golden coin, garland, sheath, curl and twisted rod.","n":"The list of ornaments is a timeline: the konrai passes from bud to full cluster through six shapes, and to watch it is to watch the season he named ripen. Calling on others as witnesses makes the flowering evidence, not scenery.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 98","lb":"TN98","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["மாக்கடுக்கை","கடுக்கை",1,"maram","scene","konrai"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:99":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":99,"t":["பொன்வாளால் காடில் கருவரை போர்த்தாலும்","என்வாளா என்றி இலங்கெயிற்றாய் - என் வாள்போல்","வாள்இழந்த கண்தோள் வனம்பிழந்த மெல்விரலும்","நாள்இழந்த எண்மிக்கு நைந்து"],"p":"","r":"Though golden blades of lightning cloak the dark hill of the forest, you say 'idly', bright-toothed friend; yet, like my own lustre, my eyes have lost their light, my shoulders their beauty, and my soft fingers are worn out with counting the days that have gone.","n":"The verse turns on வாள்: the golden blades over the hill, the friend's வாளா ('for nothing'), and வாள் as lustre, which her eyes have lost. Counting days on the fingers until they wear thin is the Kural's image, here put in an answer to the friend.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 99","lb":"TN99","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:100":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":100,"t":["பண்(டு)இயையச் சொல்லிய சொற்பழுதால் மாக்கடல்","கண்(டு)இயைய மாந்திக்கால் வீழ்த்(து) இருண்(டு) - எண்திசையும்","கார்தோன்றக் காதலர் தேர்தோன்றா தாகவே","பீர்தோன்றி நீர்தோன்றும் கண்"],"p":"","r":"Because the word he once spoke to win her has proved false, the cloud has seen the great sea, drunk its fill, let down its rain and darkened; the rains appear in all eight quarters but the lover's chariot does not, so pallor appears, and tears appear in her eyes.","n":"Four 'appearings' carry the poem: the rains appear, the chariot does not, the pallor appears, the tears appear. The blame is laid on his word (சொல்பழுதால்), as if the broken promise had itself called up the rain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","vaaimai","paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 100","lb":"TN100","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:101":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":101,"t":["வண்டினம் வௌவாத ஆம்பலும் வாரிதழான்","வண்டினம் வாய்வீழா மாலையும் - வண்டினம்","ஆராத பூந்தார் அணிதேரான் தான்போத","வாராத நாளே வரும்"],"p":"","r":"The water-lily the bees do not plunder, the evening in which the swarms do not drop into the mouths of the long-petalled flowers: since the man of the decorated chariot, whose flower garland the bees never tire of, went away, only the days on which he does not come keep coming.","n":"Bees run through all three lines: they leave the lily and the evening flowers alone but never tire of his garland. One may hear in it her untouched beauty against his crowded garland, but only one pair can be named, so the bees stay scene (a near miss on the ullurai test). The last line makes the calendar itself the injury: every day that comes is one on which he does not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 101","lb":"TN101","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",1,"pira","scene","bees"],["ஆம்பலும்","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","scene","water-lily"],["பூந்தார்","பூந்தார்",3,"maram","ornament","flower garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:102":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":102,"t":["மான்எங்கும் தம்பிணையோ(டு) ஆட மறிஉகள","வான்எங்கும் வாய்த்து வளம்கொடுப்பக் - கான்எங்கும்","தேனிறுத்த வண்டோடு தீதா எனத்தேராது","யானிறுத்தேன் ஆவி இதற்கு"],"p":"","r":"With deer everywhere playing with their does and fawns leaping, the sky everywhere giving its plenty and bees all over the forest that have stored their honey, the woman says that she, who did not judge his going a harm, has paid her life for this season.","n":"எங்கும் is repeated three times (deer, sky, forest): the plenty is everywhere except in her house. தீதா எனத்தேராது turns the reproach on herself: she did not see his leaving as the harm it was.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 102","lb":"TN102","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மான்எங்கும்","மான்",1,"maa","iraicchi","deer"],["பிணையோ","பிணை",1,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["மறிஉகள","மறி",1,"maa","scene","fawn"],["வண்டோடு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மான்எங்கும்","confirmed","1/1","even the beasts of the scrub keep their mates by them in this season, while the man who should be with her is away"]],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:103":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":103,"t":["ஒருவந்தம் அன்றால் உறைமுதிரா நீரால்","கருமம்தான் கண்டழிவு கொல்லோ - பருவந்தான்","பட்டின்றே என்றி பணைத் தோளாய்! கண்ணீரால்","அட்டினேன் ஆவி அதற்கு"],"p":"","r":"To the friend who says it is not certain, that this water of unripened rain may mean his task has met ruin and that the season has not truly come, the woman answers that she has already boiled her life away in tears over it.","n":"The friend's comfort is the old one, that this is not the true season; the woman does not argue the weather but answers with what the waiting has already cost (அட்டினேன் ஆவி, 'I have cooked my life').","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 103","lb":"TN103","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:104":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":104,"t":["ஐந்துருவின் வில்லெழுத நாற்றிசைக்கும் முந்நீரை","இந்துருவின் மாந்தி இருங்கொண்மூ - முந்துருவின்","ஒன்றாய் உருமுடைத்தாய் பெய்வான்போல் பூக்கென்று","கொன்றாய்கொன் றாய்என் குழைத்து"],"p":"","r":"As the five-coloured bow is drawn and the great cloud, having drunk the sea of the four quarters, gathers into one with thunder as if about to pour, the woman tells the konrai that, by flowering at that word, it has killed her and wilted her.","n":"கொன்றாய்கொன் றாய் is a pun: 'O konrai' and 'you have killed'. The tree is blamed for believing the cloud's show, and its flowering kills her because it announces a season in which he has not come. இந்துருவின் in line 2 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","uyir","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 104","lb":"TN104","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["இருங்கொண்மூ","கொண்மூ",2,"pira","scene","rain-cloud"],["கொன்றாய்","கொன்றை",4,"maram","scene","konrai"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:105":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":105,"t":["எல்லை தருவான் கதிர் பருகி யீன்றகார்","கொல்லைதரு வான்கொடிகள் ஏறுவகாண் - முல்லை","பெருந்தண் தளவொடுதம் கேளிரைப்போல் காணாய்","குருந்(து)அங்(கு) ஒடுங்கழுத்தம் கொண்டு"],"p":"","r":"The speaker bids the listener see how the rain-cloud, born after drinking the rays of the sun that sets the day's bound, makes the creepers climb in the fields, and how the mullai, with the great cool thalavam, clings close about the kurunthu like kinsfolk.","n":"The simile runs backward: plants are likened to kin, not people to plants. Their embrace is the subject of the whole poem, so it fails the deletion test and is not tagged iraicchi. The last line (ஒடுங்கழுத்தம்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 105","lb":"TN105","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொடிகள்","கொடி",2,"maram","scene","creepers"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","simile","jasmine"],["தளவொடு","தளவம்",3,"maram","simile","wild jasmine"],["குருந்","குருந்து",4,"maram","simile","kurunthu tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:106":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":106,"t":["என்னரே ஏற்ற துணைப்பிரிந்தார் ஆற்றென்பார்","அன்னரே யாவர் அவரவர்க்கு - முன்னரே","வந்(து)ஆரம் தேங்கா வருமுல்லை, சேர்தீந்தேன்","கந்தாரம் பாடுங் களித்து"],"p":"","r":"Who are they, those who tell a woman parted from her chosen partner to bear it? They speak so only for themselves; for already, before he comes, the bees that settle on the unceasing mullai sing the gandhaaram mode in their joy.","n":"The bees' gladness is the evidence against the counsel: the season of joy is already here, before him (முன்னரே). Line 3 (வந்(து)ஆரம் தேங்கா) is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","porai","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 106","lb":"TN106","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",3,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["தீந்தேன்","தேன்",3,"pira","scene","honey-bee"],["கந்தாரம்","கந்தாரம்",4,"yaazh","scene","melodic mode"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:107":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":107,"t":["கருவுற்ற காயாக் கணமயிலென்று றஞ்சி","உருமுஉற்ற பூங்கோடல் ஓடி - உருமுற்ற","ஐந்தலை நாகம் புரையும் மணிக்கார்தான்","எந்தலையே வந்த(து) இனி"],"p":"","r":"Fearing the budding kaayaa to be a flock of peacocks, the thunder-struck glory lily has shrunk away, and the gem-dark rain-cloud, like a five-headed cobra in the thunder, has now come down upon my head.","n":"A chain of fears: the glory lily, shaped like a snake's hood, fears the kaayaa it takes for peacocks; the cloud is itself a snake in the thunder; and the woman is the one it falls on. The lily's fear carries a mood but is not an act of a person of the situation, so it is not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","acham","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 107","lb":"TN107","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["காயாக்","காயா",1,"maram","simile","kaayaa bush"],["கணமயிலென்று","மயில்",1,"pul","simile","peacock"],["பூங்கோடல்","கோடல்",2,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["நாகம்","நாகம்",3,"pira","simile","cobra"],["உருமுஉற்ற","உருமு",2,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:108":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":108,"t":["கண்ணுள வாயின் முலையல்லை காணலாம்","எண்ணுள வாயின் இறவாவால் - எண்ணுளவா","அன்றொழிய நோய்மொழிச்சார் வாகா(து) உருமுடை வான்","ஒன்றொழிய நோய்செய்த வாறு"],"p":"","r":"Very obscurely, the woman seems to say that the thundering sky, if it had eyes, could see her, and if it had thought, she would not be dying; lacking both, it has made her sick in the one way it could.","n":"The verse runs on two conditionals (கண்ணுள வாயின், எண்ணுள வாயின்) and seems to charge the sky with blindness and thoughtlessness rather than cruelty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 108","lb":"TN108","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:6"},"tn:109":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":109,"t":["என்போல் இகுளை! இருங்கடல் மாந்தியகார்","பொன்போல்தார் கொன்றை புரிந்தன - பொன்போல்","துணைபிரிந்து வாழ்கின்றார் தோன்றுவர் தோன்றார்,","இணைபிரிந்து வாழ்வர் இனி"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend, a woman in her own plight, that the clouds which drank the dark sea have come and the konrai has hung out its gold-like strings; the man who left his partner, precious as gold, will not appear, and from now on the two will live apart.","n":"தோன்றுவர் தோன்றார் sets 'he will appear' against 'he will not' in one breath, so line 3 may be her doubt swinging both ways rather than a verdict; என்போல் இகுளை makes the friend another woman left alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 109","lb":"TN109","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["இருங்கடல்","கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["கார்","கார்",1,"pira","scene","rain-cloud"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",2,"maram","simile","konrai"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:110":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":110,"t":["பெரியார் பெருமை பெரிதே இடர்க்காண்","அரியார் எளியரென்(று) ஆற்றாப் - பரிவாய்த்","தலையழுங்க தண்தளவம் தாம்நகக்கண்(டு) ஆற்றா","மலையழுத சால மருண்டு"],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that the greatness of the great is great indeed: when the cool wild jasmine laughed in bloom at her as she grieved with bowed head, the hills, lofty and hard to reach, were moved to pity for one so low and wept in streams, deeply troubled.","n":"Nature splits into mockers and mourners: the small flower laughs, the great hills weep, and that is the 'greatness of the great' of line 1. Line 2 (அரியார் எளியரென்று) is hard; it may instead say that those hard to reach (the hills) came down to her level.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","perumai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 110","lb":"TN110","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தண்தளவம்","தளவம்",3,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:111":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":111,"t":["கானம் கடியரங்காக் கைம்மறிப்பக் கோடலார்","வானம் விளிப்பவண்(டு) யாழாக - வேனல்","வளரா மயிலாட வாட்கண்ணாய்! சொல்லாய்","உளராகி உய்யும் வகை"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that the forest has become a festival stage, the glory lilies beckoning like hands, the full sky calling, the bees playing the lute and the peacock that starved through the summer dancing, and asks her to say how one is to stay alive through it.","n":"The whole festival is staged for a return that has not happened, so its gaiety is the threat: உளராகி உய்யும் வகை asks not how to be happy but how to survive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 111","lb":"TN111","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கோடலார்","கோடல்",1,"maram","scene","white glory lily"],["வானம்","வானம்",2,"pira","scene","sky"],["வண்(டு)","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["மயிலாட","மயில்",3,"pul","scene","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:112":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":112,"t":["தேரோன் மலைமறையத் தீங்குழல் வெய்தாக","வாரான் விடுவானோ வாட்கண்ணாய்! - காராய்","குருந்தோடு முல்லை குலைத்தனகாண் நாமும்","விருந்தோடு நிற்றல் விதி"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine that as the sun goes behind the hill and the sweet flute burns in the ear, the man will not leave his word unkept: the kurundham and the mullai have flowered together in the rains, and it is their part now to stand ready to welcome him as a guest.","n":"விதி may be fate as much as duty, so 'our lot is to stand with the guest-feast' can be weary (they wait, ready, once more) rather than confident. The kurundham joined by the mullai is a pair the season shows while the couple are apart, but the poem draws nothing from it, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","virundhu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 112","lb":"TN112","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தீங்குழல்","குழல்",1,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["குருந்தோடு","குருந்து",3,"maram","scene","kurundham"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",3,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:113":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":113,"t":["பறியோலை மேலொடு கீழா இடையர்","பிறியோலை பேர்த்து விளியாக் - கதிப்ப","நரியுளையும் யாமத்தும் தோன்றாரால் அன்னாய்!","விரியுளைமான் தேர்மேல்கொண் டார்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that even at midnight, when the herdsmen, turning their leaf baskets upside down, call out and drive the herd and the jackal howls, the man who mounted the chariot of spread-maned horses has not appeared.","n":"The herdsmen settle and call at night, the jackal is awake, and only the man with the fast chariot, who could most easily come, does not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 113","lb":"TN113","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இடையர்","இடையர்",1,"seythi","scene","herdsmen"],["நரியுளையும்","நரி",3,"maa","scene","jackal"],["விரியுளைமான்","மான்",4,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:114":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":114,"t":["பாத்துப் படுகடல் மாந்திய பல்கொண்மூக்","காத்துக் கனைதுளி சிந்தாமைப் - பூத்துக்","குருந்தே! -பருவங் குறித்துவளை நைந்து","வருந்தேயென் றாய்நீ வரைந்து"],"p":"","r":"The heroine speaks to the kurundham: before the many clouds that drank the deep sea could let fall their heavy drops, it blossomed, marked out the season, and so decreed that she should grieve with her bangles slipping.","n":"She blames a tree for the timetable the man set: the blossom is the sign he named, so its coming before him turns his promise into her sentence. The first line (பாத்து, காத்து) is hard, and if வருந்தே is read as a prohibition the tree tells her instead not to grieve. The addressee is the tree.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 114","lb":"TN114","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["படுகடல்","கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["பல்கொண்மூ","கொண்மூ",1,"pira","scene","clouds"],["குருந்தே","குருந்து",3,"maram","scene","kurundham"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:115":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":115,"t":["படுந்தடங்கண் பல்பணைபோல் வான்முழங்க மேலும்","கொடுந்தடங்கண் கூற்றுமின் ஆக - நெடுந்தடங்கண்","நீர்நின்ற நோக்கின் நெடும்பணை மென்தோளாட்(கு)த்","தேர் நின்ற(து) என்னாய் திரிந்து"],"p":"","r":"With the sky roaring like many wide-faced drums and the lightning above like Death with cruel wide eyes, the speaker asks why, for the girl whose long wide eyes stand full of tears and whose soft shoulders are like long bamboo, the chariot has stopped and turned away.","n":"தடங்கண் comes three times: the drum's broad face, Death's eye in the lightning, and her own eye full of tears, so the storm's eyes are set against hers; பணை is drum in line 1 and bamboo in line 3. The speaker is not stated; the third person for the girl suggests the friend speaking to the man or his charioteer.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 115","lb":"TN115","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["பல்பணைபோல்","பணை",1,"parai","simile","drum"],["மின்","மின்",2,"pira","simile","lightning"],["நெடும்பணை","பணை",3,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:116":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":116,"t":["குருந்தே! கொடிமுல்லாய்! கொன்றாய்! தளவே!","முருந்தேய் எயிறொடுதார் பூப்பித்து - இருந்தே,","அரும்(பு)ஈர் முலையாள் அணிகுழல்தாழ் வேய்த்தோள்","பெரும்பீர் பசப்பித்தீர் பேர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend reproaches the kurundham, the mullai creeper, the konrai and the wild jasmine: by staying here and putting out flowers and buds white as peacock-quill teeth, they have spread a great pallor, like the ridge-gourd flower, over the bamboo shoulders of the girl with budding breasts and decked, falling hair.","n":"கொன்றாய் is the vocative of konrai and also 'you have killed', so of the four flowers hailed, one is hailed as a killer. இருந்தே, 'staying', sets the plants that stay against the man who went. The addressees are the flowers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 116","lb":"TN116","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குருந்தே","குருந்து",1,"maram","scene","kurundham"],["கொடிமுல்லாய்","முல்லை",1,"maram","scene","jasmine creeper"],["கொன்றாய்","கொன்றை",1,"maram","scene","konrai"],["தளவே","தளவம்",1,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"],["முருந்தேய்","முருந்து",2,"pira","simile","peacock quill"],["வேய்த்தோள்","வேய்",3,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["பெரும்பீர்","பீர்",4,"maram","simile","ridge-gourd flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:117":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":117,"t":["கதநாகம் புற்றிடையக் காரேறு சீற","மதநாகம் மாறு முழங்கப் - புதல்நாகம்","பொன்பயந்த வெள்ளி புறமாகப் பூங்கோதாய்!","என்பசந்த மென்தோள் இனி"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her flower-garlanded friend that now, when the angry cobra shrinks into its anthill as the rain's thunder rages, the rutting elephant trumpets back at it, and the naagam in the thicket bears its gold beside silver, her soft shoulders have turned pale.","n":"நாகம் is cobra, elephant and the naagam tree in three successive half-lines: the creatures answer the thunder by hiding or by roaring back, while she can only pale. The gold and silver of line 3 (flowers, or the colours of the tree) are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","paruvam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 117","lb":"TN117","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கதநாகம்","நாகம்",1,"pira","scene","cobra"],["காரேறு","கார் ஏறு",1,"pira","scene","thunder"],["மதநாகம்","நாகம்",2,"maa","scene","elephant"],["புதல்நாகம்","நாகம்",2,"maram","scene","naagam tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:118":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":118,"t":["கார்தோன்றிப் பூவுற்ற காந்தள் முகைவிளக்குப்","பீர்தோன்றித் தூண்டுவாள் மெல் விரல்போல் - நீர் தோன்றித்","தன்பருவம் செய்தது கானம் தடங்கண்ணாய்!","என்பருவம் அன்(று)என்றி இன்று"],"p":"","r":"The heroine answers her friend: the rains have appeared, the glory-lily buds have opened like the soft finger of a woman, pale herself, trimming a lamp, water has come and the forest has kept its season, so how can she say today that it is not the season.","n":"The friend's consolation, that this rain is not the season he named, is quoted only to be refused. The pallor (பீர்) is put on the lamp-trimmer's finger inside the simile, so the heroine's own paling slips into the flower.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 118","lb":"TN118","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",1,"maram","simile","glory lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:119":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":119,"t":["உகவும்கள் அன்றென்பார் ஊரார் அதனைத்","தகவு தகவனென்(று) ஓரேன் - தகவேகொல்","வண்துடுப்பாயப் பாம்பாய் விரலாய் வளைமுறியாய்","வெண்குடையாம் தண்கோடல் வீந்து"],"p":"","r":"The heroine says the townsfolk talk and she cannot settle whether what they say is fitting; is it fitting, she asks, that the cool white kodal has passed through all its shapes, a ladle, a snake's hood, a finger, a broken bangle, a white parasol, and has withered.","n":"The flower's life is a calendar: from bud to wilting, the whole season the man named has passed while she watched. The first line (உகவும்கள் அன்று) is obscure, so what the townsfolk say is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","alar","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 119","lb":"TN119","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாம்பாய்","பாம்பு",3,"pira","scene","snake"],["தண்கோடல்","கோடல்",4,"maram","scene","white glory lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:120":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":120,"t":["பீடிலார் என்பார்கள் காணார்கொல் வெங்கதிரால்","கோடெலாம் பொன்னாய்க் கொழுங்கடுக்கைக் கோடெலாம்,","அத்தம் கதிரோன் மறைவதன்முன் வண்டொடுதேன்","துத்தம் அறையும் தொடர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks whether those who say she has lost her dignity cannot see that the hot sun has turned every branch of the lush konrai to gold, and that before the sun sets on the hill the bees and honey-bees hum the thuttham note without pause.","n":"பீடிலார் may be aimed at her (she has lost her composure) or at the man (he has broken his word); on either reading the bloom and the evening hum are her defence, the season's evidence that excuses grief.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","naanam","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 120","lb":"TN120","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொழுங்கடுக்கைக்","கடுக்கை",2,"maram","scene","konrai"],["வண்டொடுதேன்","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bees"],["துத்தம்","துத்தம்",4,"yaazh","scene","musical note"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:121":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":121,"t":["ஒருத்தியான் ஒன்றல பல்பகை என்னை","விருத்தியாக் கொண்டன வேறாப் - பொருத்தில்","மடல்அன்றில் மாலை படுவசி ஆம்பல்","கடலன்றிக் காரூர் கறுத்து"],"p":"","r":"The heroine says she is one woman, and not one but many foes have taken her for their living, each apart: the anril on the palmyra frond, the evening, the falling rain, the ambal flute, the sea, and, beyond the sea, the dark cloud that rides in blackening.","n":"The list of foes runs across landscapes, the anril and the sea from the shore, the evening, the flute and the cloud from the pasture, so the separation draws enemies from every land. ஆம்பல் may be the evening flute tune or the water lily, and பொருத்தில் is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 121","lb":"TN121","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மடல்அன்றில்","அன்றில்",3,"pul","scene","anril bird"],["படுவசி","வசி",3,"pira","scene","rain"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",3,"yaazh","scene","ambal flute"],["கடலன்றிக்","கடல்",4,"pira","scene","sea"],["காரூர்","கார்",4,"pira","scene","rain-cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:122":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":122,"t":["கானம் தலைசெயக் காப்பார் குழல்தோன்ற,","ஏனம் இடந்த மணிஎதிரே - வானம்","நகுவதுபோல் மின்ஆட நாண்இல்என் ஆவி","புகுவது போலும் உடைந்து"],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that as the forest flourishes and the herders' flutes sound, while lightning plays over the gems the boar has dug up as though the sky were laughing, her shameless life seems to break and slip away.","n":"She calls her own life நாண் இல், shameless, because it clings on after the season he named has come; the boar turning up gems is a hill image set in the pasture forest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","paruvam","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 122","lb":"TN122","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குழல்தோன்ற","குழல்",1,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["ஏனம்","ஏனம்",2,"maa","scene","boar"],["மின்ஆட","மின்",3,"pira","simile","lightning"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:123":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":123,"t":["இம்மையால் செய்ததை இம்மையே ஆம்போலும்","உம்மையே ஆமென்பார் ஓரார்காண் - நம்மை","எளியர் எனநலிந்த ஈர்ங்குழலார் ஏடி","தெளியச் சுடப்பட்ட வாறு"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that what is done in this life seems to bear its fruit in this life, and those who say it comes in the next do not understand: see how the women with moist hair, who tormented the two of them thinking them weak, have now plainly been burned.","n":"Filed under mullai, the poem has no rain, flower or evening; its matter is the rival women. If 'burned' means their own lovers are now away and the rains scorch them as they scorched her, it stays mullai waiting. Either way a doctrine of deeds and their fruit is turned into a wife's satisfaction.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","parathai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 123","lb":"TN123","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:7"},"tn:124":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":124,"t":["செவ்வழியாழ்ப் பாண்மகனே! சீரார்தேர் கையினால்","இவ்வகை ஈர்த்துய்ப்பான் தோன்றாமுன் - இவ்வழியே","ஆடினான் ஆய்வய லூரன்மற்(று) எங்கையர்தோள்","கூடினான் பின் பெரிது கூர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the bard of the sevvazhi lute that, before his little son could even appear drawing his fine toy chariot along this way by hand, the lord of the town of fine fields had bathed in this stream, embraced the shoulders of her 'younger sisters', and grown keener for them after.","n":"The son with his toy chariot is used as a clock: the man went to the other women 'before the boy appears', so the claim the child should make on him came too late. எங்கையர், 'my younger sisters', is the wife's barbed name for the other women.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 124","lb":"TN124","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["செவ்வழியாழ்ப்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene",""],["பாண்மகனே","பாண்மகன்",1,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:125":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":125,"t":["மாக்கோல்யாழ்ப் பாண்மகனே! மண்யானைப் பாகனார்","தூக்கோல் துடியோடு தோன்றாமுன் - தூக்கோல்","தொடியுடையார் சேரிக்குத் தோன்றுமோ சொல்லாய்","கடியுடையேன் வாயில் கடந்து"],"p":"","r":"The wife asks the bard of the great-stemmed lute to tell her whether, before the little mahout of a clay elephant comes out with his drum on its hanging stick, the man will pass her guarded door and go off to the quarter of the women with hanging bangles.","n":"Like 124, the poem sets the man's going against the boy at play: the child is a mahout of a clay elephant, his drum a toy, a parody of the royal procession the father might be leading instead.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","makkal","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 125","lb":"TN125","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாக்கோல்யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene",""],["பாண்மகனே","பாண்மகன்",1,"seythi","scene","bard"],["துடியோடு","துடி",2,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:126":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":126,"t":["விளரியாழ்ப் பாண்மகனே! வேண்டா அழையேல்","முளரி மொழியா(து) உளரிக் - கிளரிநீ","பூங்கண் வயலூரன் புத்தில் புகுவதன்முன்","ஆங்கண் அறிய உரை"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the bard of the vilari lute not to call for her in vain; instead, stirring his strings, he should go and say it over there, where it will be heard, before the lord of the flowering fields enters his new house.","n":"விளரி is the mode of lament: she names the bard by the tune of grief even as she sends him back to the house of pleasure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 126","lb":"TN126","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["விளரியாழ்ப்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene",""],["பாண்மகனே","பாண்மகன்",1,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:127":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":127,"t":["மென்கண் கலிவய லூரன்தன் மெய்ம்மையை","எங்கட்(கு) உரையாது எழுந்துபோய் - இங்கண்","குலம்காரம் என்(று(அணுகான் கூடும்கூர்த்(து) அன்றே","அலங்கார நல்லார்க்(கு) அறை"],"p":"","r":"The wife says the bard rose and left without telling them the truth about the lord of the town of soft, noisy fields, who does not come near this house, pleading his family's standing, and bids him go announce it instead to the adorned women he joins so eagerly.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","vaaimai","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 127","lb":"TN127","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:128":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":128,"t":["செந்தா மரைப்பூ உற நிமிர்ந்த செந்நெல்லின்","பைந்தார்ப் புனல்வாய்ப்பாய்ந்(து) ஆடுவாள் - அந்தார்","வயந்தகம்போல் தோன்றும் வயலூரன் கேண்மை","நயந்தகன்(று) ஆற்றாமை நன்று"],"p":"","r":"Of the lord of the fields where red paddy stands up to touch the red lotus and looks like the forehead jewel of a woman who leaps into the stream to bathe, the speaker says that to love his friendship and then hold apart and bear the pain is the better course.","n":"The vehicle of the simile is the ornament of a woman bathing in the stream, so the other woman (punal aadal was the man's sport with her) is set inside the description of his own fields. Close in frame to 129.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","pirivu","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 128","lb":"TN128","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["செந்தா மரைப்பூ","தாமரை",1,"maram","simile",""],["செந்நெல்லின்","செந்நெல்",1,"maram","simile",""],["புனல்வாய்ப்பாய்ந்(து)","புனல்",2,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:129":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":129,"t":["வாடாத தாமரைமேல் செந்நெற் கதிர்வணக்கம்","ஆடா அரங்கினுள் ஆடுவாள் - ஈடாய","புல்லகம் ஏய்க்கும் புகழ்வயல் ஊரன்தன்","நல்லகம் சேராமை நன்று"],"p":"","r":"The lord of the famed fields, where ears of red paddy bowing over unwithering lotuses look like the brow ornament of a dancer dancing in an undanced hall: better not to enter his good house.","n":"As in 128 the comparison borrows a dancing woman's ornament for the field, so the man's landscape is already furnished with the women of his straying; the speaker answers it by refusing his 'good house' (நல்லகம்), an ironic name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 129","lb":"TN129","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாமரைமேல்","தாமரை",1,"maram","simile",""],["செந்நெற்","செந்நெல்",1,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:130":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":130,"t":["இசையுரைக்கும் என்செய் திரம்நின் றவரை","வசையுரைப்பச் சால வழத்தீர் - பசைபொறை","மெய்ம்மருட்(டு) ஒல்லா மிகுபுனல் ஊரன்தன்","பொய்ம்மருட்டுப் பெற்ற பொழுது"],"p":"","r":"The wife says that the bards who sing the man's praise turned to blaming those who stood by her at the very time she took for truth the false coaxing of the lord of the full-watered town, who has no true tenderness.","n":"The pair மெய்ம்மருட்டு / பொய்ம்மருட்டு sets a true beguiling, which the man is not capable of, against the false one she received.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 130","lb":"TN130","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["மிகுபுனல்","புனல்",3,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:131":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":131,"t":["மடங்(கு)இறவு போலும்யாழ்ப் பண்பிலாப் பாண!","தொடங்குறவு சொல்துணிக்க வேண்டா - முடங்கிறவு","பூட்டுற்ற வில்ஏய்க்கும் பூம்பொய்கை யூரன்பொய்","கேட்டுற்ற கீழ்நாள் கிளர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the bard, whose lute is curved like a bent prawn and who has no good nature, that he need not fashion words to open a reconciliation: she was roused for good on the day she heard the lies of the lord of the flowered pond, where bent prawns look like strung bows.","n":"The two similes are chained: the bard's lute is like the prawn, and the prawn of the man's pond is like a strung bow, so the instrument that comes to make peace is lined up with a weapon drawn. The bard's lute and the man's lie (பொய், inside பொய்கை) meet in one line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","oodal","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 131","lb":"TN131","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மடங்(கு)இறவு","இறவு",1,"pira","simile","prawn"],["முடங்கிறவு","இறவு",2,"pira","simile","prawn"],["யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:132":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":132,"t":["எங்கை யரில்உள்ளா னேபாண! நீபிறர்","மங்கை யரில் என்று மயங்கினாய் - மங்கையரில்","என்னா(து) இறவா(து) இவணின் இகந்தேகல்","பின்னாரில் அந்தி முடிவு"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the bard he was confused in thinking the man was among other women when he is among her own 'younger sisters', and that, going from here without a word, each of his evenings ends among the newer women.","n":"The bard's excuse is turned against him: whether the women are 'others' or her 'sisters', the man is with them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 132","lb":"TN132","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:133":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":133,"t":["பாலையாழ்ப் பாண்மகனே! பண்டுநின் நாயகற்கு","மாலையாழ் ஓதி வருடாயோ? - காலையாழ்","செய்யும் இடமறியாய் சேர்ந்தாநின் பொய்ம்மொழிக்கு","நையும் இடமறிந்து நாடு"],"p":"","r":"The wife asks the bard of the paalai lute whether he does not, as before, play the evening mode to soothe his master; he does not know where the morning mode should be played, and since he has bound himself to lies, let him find out the place where she wastes away.","n":"The modes are named by their hours (paalai, evening, morning): the bard plays the evening to the man at the other house and does not know the morning, the hour of the wife's house, so his music itself keeps the man's timetable.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","vanjam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 133","lb":"TN133","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாலையாழ்ப்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene",""],["பாண்மகனே","பாண்மகன்",1,"seythi","scene","bard"],["மாலையாழ்","யாழ்",2,"yaazh","scene",""],["காலையாழ்","யாழ்",2,"yaazh","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:134":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":134,"t":["கிழமை பெரியோர்க்குக் கேடின்மை கொல்லோ","பழமை பயன்நோக்கிக் கொல்லோ- கிழமை","குடிநாய்கர் தாம்பல பெற்றாரில் கேளா","அடியேன் பெற்றா அருள்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker wonders whether it is because the bond of the great does not decay, or because they look to the worth of old intimacy, that she, a humble servant who never asked, received the grace that the many the master of the house has taken did not.","n":"The two words of the Kural's chapter on old friendship, கிழமை and பழமை, are set as alternatives: was his return a matter of character or of looking to old use? The question (கொல்லோ ... கொல்லோ) leaves it open, and அடியேன் may be humble or bitter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","kizhamai","parathai","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 134","lb":"TN134","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:135":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":135,"t":["என்கேட்டி ஏழாய்! இருநிலத்தும் வானத்தும்","முன்கேட்டும் கண்டும் முடிவறியேன் - பின்கேட்டு","அணியிகவா நிற்க அவன்அணங்கு மாதர்","பணியிகவான் சாலப் பணிந்து"],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells her friend that in all she has heard and seen on earth and in heaven she never knew such an end: she heard later that, while her own ornaments slipped away, he bowed low before the woman who torments him, never failing in his service.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","anangu","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 135","lb":"TN135","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:136":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":136,"t":["எங்கை இயல்பின் எழுவல் யாழ்ப் பண்மகனே!","தங்கையும் வாழும் அறியாமல் - இங்கண்","உளர உளர உவன்ஓடிச் சால","வளர வளர்ந்த வகை"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the bard she will rise and go the way her 'sisters' do, since the man, not knowing that his own young one is living here, ran there distractedly, and his straying has grown and grown.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 136","lb":"TN136","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene",""],["பண்மகனே","பண்மகன்",1,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:137":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":137,"t":["கருங்கோட்டுச் செங்கண் எருமை கழனி","இருங்கோட்டு மென்கரும்பு சாடி - அருங்கோட்டால்","ஆம்பல் மயக்கி அணிவளை ஆர்ந்(து) அழகாத்","தாம்பல் அசையினவாய் தாழ்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"A black-horned, red-eyed buffalo of the fields tramples the soft, thick-jointed sugarcane, tangles the water lilies with its fierce horns, and, having fed its fill, sinks down with its tethers swaying.","n":"Near miss for ullurai: the buffalo trampling the cane and tangling the lilies maps readily onto the straying man (two pairs can be named), but neither the poem nor the colophon states the situation, so condition 4 fails and the buffalo stays scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 137","lb":"TN137","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","scene",""],["மென்கரும்பு","கரும்பு",2,"maram","scene",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",3,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:138":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":138,"t":["கன்றுள்ளிச் சோர்ந்தபால் காலொற்றித் தாமரைப்பூ","வன்றுள்ளி அன்னத்தை ஆர்த்துவான் - சென்றுள்ளி","வந்(து)ஐ,ஆ என்னும் வகையிற்றே மற்றிவன்","தந்தையார் தம்மூர்த் தகை"],"p":"","r":"In the town of this boy's father, the milk a buffalo lets fall as she thinks of her calf, pressed out by her feet, splashes hard on the lotus and sets the swan crying, so that whoever goes there and looks comes back exclaiming in wonder.","n":"The man is named only as 'this boy's father', and the town's richness is a mother's milk running for an absent calf.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","ninaivu","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 138","lb":"TN138","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கன்றுள்ளிச்","கன்று",1,"maa","iraicchi","calf"],["தாமரைப்பூ","தாமரை",1,"maram","scene",""],["அன்னத்தை","அன்னம்",2,"pul","scene","swan"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கன்றுள்ளிச்","not_confirmed","0/2","even the cattle of his own town yearn toward their young, yet the father of this boy stays away from him"]],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:139":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":139,"t":["மருதோடு காஞ்சி அமர்ந்துயர்ந்த நீழல்","எருதோடு உழல்கின்றார் ஓதை - குருகோடு","தாராத்தோ(று) ஆய்ந்தெடுப்பும் தண்ணம் கழனித்தே","ஊராத்தே ரான்தந்தை ஊர்"],"p":"","r":"In the shade where marutham and kaanji trees rise together, with the din of men toiling behind their oxen and herons picking among the cool fields, lies the town of the father of the boy whose chariot does not run.","n":"ஊராத்தேரான், 'the one with the chariot that does not run', names the child by his toy, and the man only through the child: the town is his father's, not her husband's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","uzhavu","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 139","lb":"TN139","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மருதோடு","மருது",1,"maram","scene",""],["காஞ்சி","காஞ்சி",1,"maram","scene",""],["எருதோடு","எருது",2,"maa","scene",""],["உழல்கின்றார்","உழவர்",2,"seythi","scene","ploughmen"],["குருகோடு","குருகு",2,"pul","scene","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:8"},"tn:140":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":140,"t":["மண்ணார் குலைவாழை உள்தொடுத்த தேன்நமதென்(று)","உண்ணாப்பூந் தாமரைப் பூவுள்ளும் - கண்ணார்","வயலூரன் வண்ணம் அறிந்து தொடுப்பாள்","மயல் ஊ ரரவர் மகள்"],"p":"","r":"The bee that will not drink even in the lotus flower, holding that the honey laid up inside the earth-rooted banana cluster is its own, has its likeness in the lord of the eye-filling field town, and the daughter of that bewitched town, knowing his nature, strings her garland to suit it.","n":"A hidden comparison is near: the bee that scorns the lotus for honey it calls its own is like the lord who leaves the wife for another house. It is left untagged because the pairs cannot be fixed on this text: who 'strings' (தொடுப்பாள்) and what, and who the 'daughter of the bewitched town' is, turn on a broken last line. தேன் is read as the bee, as often in this book.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 140","lb":"TN140","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குலைவாழை","குலை வாழை",1,"maram","scene","banana cluster"],["தேன்","தேன்",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",2,"maram","scene","lotus"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:141":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":141,"t":["அணிக்குரல்மேல் நல்லாரோ(டு) ஆடினேன் என்ன","மணிக்குரல்மேல் மாதராள் ஊடி - மணிச்சிரல்","பாட்டை இருந்தயரும் பாய்நீர்க் கழனித்தே","யாட்டை இருந்துறையும் ஊர்"],"p":"","r":"When he said he had sported in the water with fair women of lovely voice, the lady with a voice like a bell sulked; such is the town of fields fed by running water where the blue kingfisher sits and wearies by the path.","n":"He reports the sulk with his own boast in it (ஆடினேன், 'I sported'), so the offence and the account of it come from one mouth. The two voices are matched, அணிக்குரல் for the other women and மணிக்குரல் for the wife.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 141","lb":"TN141","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மணிச்சிரல்","மணிச் சிரல்",2,"pul","scene","kingfisher"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:142":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":142,"t":["தண்கயத்துத் தாமரைநீள் சேவலைத் தாழ்பெடை","புண்கயத் துள்ளும் வயலூர! - வண்கயம்","போலும்நின் மார்பு புளிவேட்கைத்(து) ஒன்(று)இவள்","மாலும்மா றாநோய் மருந்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the field town, where in the cool pond the stooping hen keeps to her tall cock among the lotus, that his chest is like a rich pool, and that the wife, with a single craving like a pregnant woman's for sour things, wants it as the only cure for the longing that will not leave her.","n":"The hen keeping to her mate could be read as iraicchi (as she clings, so the wife wants only him), and the deletion test would pass; it is left as scene because the verb of line 2 (புண்கயத்துள்ளும்) is doubtful, so the act cannot be fixed. The pond of line 1 returns in line 2 as the simile for his chest (வண்கயம்), so the scene and the comparison share one word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","marundhu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 142","lb":"TN142","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["தாமரைநீள்","தாமரை",1,"maram","scene","lotus"],["சேவலைத்","சேவல்",1,"pul","scene","cock bird"],["பெடை","பெடை",1,"pul","scene","hen bird"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:143":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":143,"t":["நல்வயல் ஊரன் நறுஞ்சாந்(து) அணிஅகலம்","புல்லிப் புடைபெயரா மாத்திரைக்கண் - புல்லியார்","கூட்டு முதலுறையும் கோழி துயிலெடுப்ப","பாட்டு முரலுமாம் பண்"],"p":"","r":"No sooner had she embraced the sandal-smeared chest of the lord of the good field town, before she had even turned on her side, than the cock that lives at the foot of the poor folk's huts woke the sleepers and the dawn tune began to hum.","n":"The night is measured in a single gesture: புடைபெயரா மாத்திரைக்கண், 'in the time it takes not even to turn aside'. That the cock lives among the huts of the poor (புல்லியார் கூட்டு) sets the lovers' brief night against the town's working day that the bird begins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","iravu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 143","lb":"TN143","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோழி","கோழி",3,"pul","scene","cock"],["நறுஞ்சாந்(து)","சாந்து",1,"pira","ornament","sandal paste"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:144":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":144,"t":["அரத்தம் உடீஇ, அணிபழுப்பப் பூசிச்","சிரத்தையால் செங்கழுநீர் சூடிப் - பரத்தை","நினைநோக்கிக் கூறினும் நீமொழியல் என்று","மனைநோக்கி மாண விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The courtesan, wrapped in red cloth, smeared with paste that yellows her jewels and wearing red water-lilies with care, sends him off towards his house in style, coaching him: 'even if she looks at you and speaks, do not answer'.","n":"Read with பரத்தை as the subject of all three participles and of விடும், the verse turns the wife's complaint into a report of the rival's stage-management: she dresses him off, even scripts his silence. Read otherwise (the warning as the wife's), the sense changes, so the verse is marked uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 144","lb":"TN144","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["செங்கழுநீர்","செங்கழுநீர்",2,"maram","ornament","red water-lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:145":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":145,"t":["பாட்டார வம்பண் அரவம் பணியாத","கோட்டரவம் இன்னிவை தாங்குழுமக் - கோட்டரவம்","மந்திரம் கொண்டோங்கல் என்ன மகச்சுமந்து","இந்திரன்போல் வந்தான் இடத்து"],"p":"","r":"With the noise of songs, of tunes and of the unbending horns all crowding together, rising like a hooded serpent that rears at a charm, he came carrying his son to our place like Indra.","n":"கோட்டரவம் is used twice with two senses: in line 2 the sound of horns (கோடு, horn), in line 3 the serpent with a curved hood. Indra is marutham's god (porul 1.5), so the simile places the husband's return in his own landscape. The pomp may be mocking: he needs a procession and a child to get through the door.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","oodal","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 145","lb":"TN145","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["இந்திரன்போல்","இந்திரன்",4,"deyvam","simile","Indra"],["கோட்டரவம்","கோட்டு அரவம்",2,"pira","simile","hooded serpent"],["பண்","பண்",1,"yaazh","scene","tune"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:146":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":146,"t":["மண்கிடந்த வையகத்தோர் மற்றுப் பெரியராய்","எண்கிடந்த நாளான் இகழ்ந்தொழுகப் - பெண்கிடந்த","தன்மை யழியத் தரள மூலையினாள்","மென்மைசெய் திட்டாள் மிக"],"p":"","r":"Though the great of this earth-bound world went on slighting her for days past counting, she of the pearl-strung breasts, letting go the reserve that is a woman's nature, made herself wholly gentle.","n":"The verse repeats கிடந்த three times (மண்கிடந்த, எண்கிடந்த, பெண்கிடந்த): the earth that lies there, the days that lie uncounted, the nature that lies in a woman, and only the last is undone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nirai","naanam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 146","lb":"TN146","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தரள","தரளம்",3,"pira","ornament","pearl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:147":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":147,"t":["செங்கண் கருங்கோட்(டு) எருமை சிறுகனையால்","அங்கண் கழனிப் பழனம்பாய்ந்(து) - அங்கண்","குவளையம் பூவொடு செங்கயல்மீன் சூடி","தவளையும்மேற் கொண்டு வரும்"],"p":"","r":"The red-eyed, black-horned buffalo, with a little lowing, plunges into the field pond of the lovely fields and comes back crowned with blue lilies and red kayal fish, carrying frogs on its back as well.","n":"This is the classic marutham ullurai vehicle (the buffalo that raids the pond and comes home wearing its flowers, as the husband comes home with other women's marks), but nothing in the verse or its colophon states the situation, so condition 4 fails and the buffalo is scene. The frog riding home on its back is a comic touch no Sangam buffalo has.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 147","lb":"TN147","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["குவளையம்","குவளை",3,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["செங்கயல்மீன்","கயல் மீன்",3,"pira","scene","carp"],["தவளையும்மேற்","தவளை",4,"pira","scene","frog"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:148":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":148,"t":["இருள்நடந்த(து) அன்ன இருங் கோட்(டு) எருமை","மருள்நடந்த மாப்பழனம் மாந்திப் - பொருள்நடந்த","கற்பேரும் கோட்டால் கனைத்துதன் கன்றுள்ளி","நெற்போர்வு சூடி வரும்"],"p":"","r":"The dark-horned buffalo, like darkness walking, having grazed the great field pond where all is confusion, with horns that could shift stones, lows remembering its calf and comes home wearing a cover of paddy straw.","n":"Unlike verse 147, this buffalo's raid ends with a return, and the cause given is the calf (தன் கன்றுள்ளி). Read as iraicchi it would say the husband should come home for his son, but the verse has no other sense to survive the deletion test, so the buffalo is the subject, not a carrier.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","ninaivu","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 148","lb":"TN148","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","simile","buffalo"],["கன்று","கன்று",3,"maa","scene","calf"],["நெற்போர்வு","நெல் போர்வு",4,"maram","ornament","paddy straw"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:149":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":149,"t":["புண்கிடந்த புள்மனுநுன் நீத்தொழுகி வாழினும்","பெண்கிடந்த தன்மை பிறி(து)அரோ - பண்கிடந்து","செய்யாத மாத்திரையே செங்கயல்போல் கண்ணினாள்","நையாது தான்நாணும் ஆறு"],"p":"","r":"Even if you leave her and live apart, a woman's nature is something else: see how she of eyes like red kayal fish, not wasting away, only feels shame, before even a tune has been played.","n":"Line 2 repeats the phrase of verse 146, பெண்கிடந்த தன்மை, and here the woman's nature holds where there it gave way: shame (நாணும்) instead of pining (நையாது).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","nirai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 149","lb":"TN149","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["செங்கயல்போல்","கயல்",3,"pira","simile","carp"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:150":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":150,"t":["கண்ணுங்கால் என்கொல் கலவையாழ்ப் பாண்மகனே!","எண்ணுங்கால் மற்(று(இன்(று) இவளடுநேர் - எண்ணின்","கடல் வட்டத்(து) இல்லையால் கல் பெயர் சேராள்","அடல் வட்டத்(து) ஆர்உளரேல் ஆம்"],"p":"","r":"O bard of the lute of mingled strings, what of it when one looks closely? Reckoned up, there is none today in the sea-ringed world equal to her; if there were any in the circle of the mighty, so be it.","n":"Turning the bard away by praising the wife's worth rather than blaming the husband leaves the bard nothing to argue with. Line 3's கல் பெயர் சேராள் is obscure in our text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nalam","karpu","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 150","lb":"TN150","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கலவையாழ்ப்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["பாண்மகனே","பாணன்",1,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:151":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":151,"t":["சேறாடுங் கிண்கிணிக்கால் செம்பொன்செய் பட்டத்து","நீறாடும் ஆயதிவன் இல்முனா - வேறாய","மங்கையரின் ஆடுமோ மாக்கோல்யாழ்ப் பாண்மகனே!","எங்கையரின் ஆடலாம் இன்று"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the bard of the great-stemmed lute that her son, with anklet bells on his mud-splashed feet and a red-gold plate on his brow, plays in the dust before this house and will not play as other women's do; today his father may go and play with 'our younger sisters'.","n":"எங்கையர், 'our younger sisters', is the wife's ironic courtesy for her rivals. The verb ஆடு runs through the verse in three senses: the son plays in mud and dust, the other women 'play', and the husband may go and sport.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 151","lb":"TN151","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["கிண்கிணிக்கால்","கிண்கிணி",1,"pira","ornament","anklet bells"],["பட்டத்து","பட்டம்",1,"pira","ornament","forehead plate"],["மாக்கோல்யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",3,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["பாண்மகனே","பாணன்",3,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:152":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":152,"t":["முலையாலும் பூணாலும் முன்கண்தாம் சேர்ந்த","இலையாலும் இட்ட குறியை - உலையாது","நீர்சிதைக்கும் வாய்ப்புதல்வன் நிற்கும் உனைமுலைப்பால்","தார்சிதைக்கும் வேண்டா தழூம்"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her husband not to embrace her: the marks printed on his chest by another woman's breasts, jewels and leaf-paintings would be wiped away by their son's wet mouth, and her breast milk would spoil his garland.","n":"The refusal is phrased as care for his finery: what the son's mouth and her milk would 'spoil' is the evidence of the other woman (இட்ட குறி), so her concern is the accusation. உலையாது, 'without fail', makes the spoiling certain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 152","lb":"TN152","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["இலையாலும்","இலை",2,"maram","ornament","leaf-pattern"],["பூணாலும்","பூண்",1,"pira","ornament","jewel"],["தார்சிதைக்கும்","தார்",4,"maram","ornament","garland"],["முலைப்பால்","முலைப் பால்",3,"unavu","scene","breast milk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:153":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":153,"t":["துனிபுலவி ஊடலின் நோக்கு(எ)ன் தொடர்ந்த","கனிகலவி காதலினும் காணேன் -முனிஅகலின்","நாணா நடுங்கும் நளிவய லூரனைக்","காணாஎப் போதுமே கண்"],"p":"","r":"Neither in the look of sulk, quarrel and pique she kept up, nor in the love of the ripe union that followed, has she seen him; and if he goes off angry her eyes tremble without shame: her eyes never once see the lord of the dense field town.","n":"The three grades of the quarrel are named in a row (துனி, புலவி, ஊடல்) and then union is called 'ripe' (கனி), the vocabulary the Kural uses in its chapter on புலவி. The paradox is that every stage of the cycle hides him from her eyes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kan","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu 153","lb":"TN153","poet":"கணிமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"tn:payiram":{"book":"thinaimalai_nootraimpathu","num":154,"t":["முனிந்தார் முனி(வு) ஒழியச் செய்யுட்கண் முத்துக்","கனிந்தார் களவியல் கொள்கைக் - கணிந்தார்","இணைமாலை யீடிலா இன்தமிழால் யாத்த","திணைமாலை கைவரத் தேர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The prefatory verse says that the poet, ripe in the doctrine of secret love, bound the Thinaimalai, a garland of paired landscapes, in matchless sweet Tamil, so that the displeasure of the critical might cease and the book come easily to the hand of those who study it.","n":"கணிந்தார் in line 2 seems to play on the poet's name, Kanimethaviyar, and the book is offered as a garland (மாலை) twice over: இணைமாலை and திணைமாலை.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","kalaviyal","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimalai Nootraimpathu payiram","lb":"TNpayiram","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"tn:9"},"ti:1":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":1,"t":["புகழ்மிகு சாந்தெறிந்து புல்லெரி யூட்டிப்","புகைகொடுக்கப் பெற்ற புலவோர் - துகள்பொழியும்","வானுயர் வெற்ப! இரவின் வரல்வேண்டா","யானை யுடைய கரம்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the sky-high hill, where sandal burnt with a grass fire sends up its smoke as an offering and dust rains down, that he must not come by night, since the dark ways hold elephants.","n":"The opening may make the hill a place of worship: புலவோர் 'who are given smoke' reads most easily as the celestials receiving burnt sandal, so the hill he comes from is sacred and fragrant, and the warning that closes the poem turns that grandeur into danger. The last word கரம் is hard; 'hidden ways' or 'trunks' are both possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 1","lb":"TI1","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["சாந்தெறிந்து","சாந்து",1,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["புல்லெரி","புல்",1,"maram","scene","grass"],["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:1"},"ti:2":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":2,"t":["கணமுகை கையெனக் காந்தள் கவின","மணமுகை யென்றெண்ணி மந்திகொண் டாடும்","விறன்மாலை நாட! வரிஅரிதாங் கொல்லோ","புனமும் அடங்கின காப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the mighty hill, where the kaanthal's clustered buds bloom like hands and the female monkey, taking them for fragrant buds, plays with them, whether his coming will now be hard, since the guarding of the millet fields has ended.","n":"The end of the field-watch is the end of the girl's licensed days outdoors, so the question about his coming is a push toward marriage. The monkey's delight in the hand-like buds is an image of the hill's ease set against the closing of their meetings; its exact sense (மணமுகை, fragrant bud or wedding bud) is open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 2","lb":"TI2","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",1,"maram","simile","glory lily"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",2,"maa","scene","female monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ti:1"},"ti:3":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":3,"t":["ஓங்கல் இறுவரைமேல் காந்தள் கடிகவினப்","பாம்பென ஓடி உரும்இடிப்பக் கண்டிரங்கும்","பூங்குன்ற நாடன் புணர்ந்தஅந் நாள்போலா","ஈங்கு நெகிழ்ந்த வளை"],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that the bangles that slipped here are not as they were on the day she joined the lord of the flowered hill, where on the high steep slope the kaanthal blooms and thunder, taking it for a snake, rushes and strikes and roars.","n":"Thunder that kills snakes is a stock hill image; here it falls on a flower that only looks like a snake's hood, so the violence lands on something harmless. Set beside the loosened bangles it reads as the hurt that has come to her though she did no wrong, but there is no act of a person in it and it stays scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["melivu","kalaviyal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 3","lb":"TI3","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",1,"maram","simile","glory lily"],["பாம்பென","பாம்பு",2,"pira","simile","snake"],["உரும்இடிப்பக்","உரும்",2,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:1"},"ti:4":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":4,"t":["ஏனல் இடத்திட்ட ஈர்மணிகொண்(டு) எல்லிடைக்","கானவர் மக்கள் கனலெனக் கைகாய்த்தும்","வானுயர் வெற்பன் வருவான்கொல் என்தோழி","மேனி பசப்புக் கெட"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether the lord of the sky-high hill will come, so that the pallor of her body may leave, in his hill where the hunters' children pick up the wet gems lying in the millet field and warm their hands at them by night as if at embers.","n":"Gems so common that children use them as a fire make the hill rich beyond need, which sharpens the one thing she lacks: his coming, the only cure she names for her pallor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 4","lb":"TI4","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["ஈர்மணிகொண்(டு)","மணி",1,"pira","simile","gem"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",2,"seythi","scene","hill hunters"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:1"},"ti:5":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":5,"t":["விரைகமழ் சாரல் விளைபுனம் காப்பார்","வரையிடை வாரல்மின் ஐய! உரைகடியர்","வில்லினர் வேலர் விரைந்துசெல் அம்பினர்","கல்லிடை வாழ்நர் எமர்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man not to come among the hills, for those who guard the ripening fields on the fragrant slope are her kin, harsh of word, with bows, spears and swift arrows, living among the rocks.","n":"The threat is built as a list of weapons, and the last word, எமர் 'our people', is held back to the end, so the danger is named as kinship: the men he would face are the girl's own family, whose consent marriage would need.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 5","lb":"TI5","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காப்பார்","காப்பார்",1,"seythi","scene","field guards"],["வில்லினர்","வில்லினர்",3,"seythi","scene","bowmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:1"},"ti:6":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":6,"t":["யானை உழலும் மணிகிளர் நீள்வரைக்","கானக வாழ்க்கைக் குறவர் மகளிரேம்","ஏனலுள் ஐய! வரவுமற்(று) என்னைகொல்","காணினும் காய்வர் எமர்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that they are daughters of the hill folk who live in the forest of the long gem-bright hill where elephants roam, and asks why he comes into the millet field, since their kin would rage even to see him.","n":"The friend's 'we' names the two girls by their tribe, so the refusal speaks in the voice of the family; it is the same self-naming the friend uses in verse 8 to turn aside his pretext.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 6","lb":"TI6","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",2,"seythi","scene","hill folk"],["ஏனலுள்","ஏனல்",3,"unavu","scene","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:1"},"ti:7":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":7,"t":["யாழும் குழலும் முழவும் இயைந்தன","வீழும் அருவி விறன்மலை நன்னாட!","மாழைமான் நோக்கியும் ஆற்றாள் இரவரின்","ஊரறி கௌவை தரும்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the good land of the mighty hill, whose falling cascades sound as lute, flute and drum together, that the girl with a young deer's glance cannot bear his absence, but that if he comes by night it will bring gossip the whole town knows.","n":"The waterfall as an orchestra makes the hill a stage, and the warning follows from it: what happens on a stage is heard, and the night visit will become the town's talk. The girl's distress is set against the danger, which leaves marriage as the only way out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","iravu","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 7","lb":"TI7","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யாழும்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["குழலும்","குழல்",1,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["முழவும்","முழவு",1,"parai","scene","drum"],["மாழைமான்","மான்",3,"maa","scene","young deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:1"},"ti:8":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":8,"t":["வேங்கை மலர வெறிகமழ் தண்சிலம்பின்","வாங்கமை மென்தோள் குறவர் மகளிரேம்","சோர்ந்து குருதி ஒழுகமற்(று) இப்புறம்","போந்த(து)இல் ஐய! களிறு"],"p":"","r":"The friend answers the man who asks after a wounded elephant: we are girls of the hill folk with soft shoulders like bending bamboo, on this cool slope fragrant with blooming vengai, and no tusker came this way, sir, flagging and streaming blood.","n":"His question is the hunter's pretext for speaking to the girls, and the friend answers it literally, which is a way of showing she sees through it; she also names who they are, the hill folk's daughters, as in verse 6.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 8","lb":"TI8","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["வாங்கமை","அமை",2,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",2,"seythi","scene","hill folk"],["களிறு","களிறு",4,"maa","scene","tusker"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:1"},"ti:9":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":9,"t":["பிணிநிறம் தீர்ந்து பெரும்பணைத்தோள் வீங்க","மணிமலை நாடன் வருவான்கொல் தோழ!","கணிநிறை வேங்கை மலர்ந்துவண்டு ஆர்க்கும்","அணிநிற மாலைப் பொழுது"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether the lord of the gem hill will come, so that her sickly colour leaves and her broad bamboo shoulders fill out again, in this lovely evening when the vengai, the tree that tells the season, is in full flower and bees hum.","n":"கணி, 'the reckoner', is a name of the vengai, whose flowering marks the season of the millet harvest and of weddings; so the evening she describes is a sign that the time for his coming to marry her is here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","maalai","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 9","lb":"TI9","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",3,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:1"},"ti:10":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":10,"t":["பலவின் பழம்பெற்ற பைங்கட் கடுவன்","எலஎன்(று) இணைபயிரும் ஏகல்சூழ் வெற்பன்","புலவுங் கொல் தோழி! புணர்வறிந்(து) அன்னை","செலவுங் கடிந்தாள் புனத்து"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether the lord of the rock-ringed hill, where the green-eyed male monkey that has got a jackfruit calls its mate with 'ela!', will be vexed, now that mother, learning of their union, has forbidden her to go to the millet field.","n":"The confinement (இற்செறிப்பு) ends the field meetings; the question whether he will be vexed is put so that he, or the friend for him, will see that only marriage can bring them together again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 10","lb":"TI10","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பலவின்","பலா",1,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",1,"maa","iraicchi","male monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கடுவன்","confirmed","1/1","the hill lord, who has the means, should likewise call her to him openly, now that the field where they met is closed to her"]],"ch":"ti:1"},"ti:11":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":11,"t":["கழுநீர் மலர்க்கண்ணாய்! கௌவையோ நிற்கப்","பொருள்நீரார் காதலர் பொய்த்தனர் நீத்தார்","அழிநீர் வாகி அரித்தெழுந்து தோன்றி","வழிநீர் அறுத்த சுரம்"],"p":"","r":"Gossip aside, the speaker tells the woman with eyes like washed blue lilies that her lover, whose nature is set on wealth, broke his word and left for a wasteland where the waters have perished, the heat rises shimmering, and the water along the way is cut off.","n":"பொய்த்தனர் and நீத்தார் stand side by side: the lie and the leaving are one act. The water named three times (அழிநீர, வழிநீர் and the lily of கழுநீர்) sets her watered eyes against the land that has lost its water.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","vaaimai","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 11","lb":"TI11","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கழுநீர்","கழுநீர்",1,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"],["வழிநீர்","நீர்",4,"pira","absent","water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:2"},"ti:12":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":12,"t":["முரிபரல வாகி முரணழிந்து தோன்றி","எரிபரந்த கானம் இயைபொருட்குப் போவீர்!","அரிபரந்த வுண்கண்ணாள் ஆற்றாமை நும்மின்","தெரிவார்யார் தேரும் இடத்து"],"p":"","r":"To the man setting out for the wealth he hopes to gain across a forest where fire has spread and the land, strewn with broken pebbles, has lost its nature, the speaker says that when it comes to weighing it, no one can know better than he the helplessness of the woman whose kohl-dark eyes are streaked with red.","n":"The closing question flatters and accuses at once: he, her intimate, knows her grief best, so if he goes he goes knowing. The ruined land (முரண் அழிந்து, its strength or nature destroyed) foreshadows what his going will do to her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 12","lb":"TI12","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முரிபரல","பரல்",1,"pira","scene","pebbles"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:2"},"ti:13":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":13,"t":["ஓங்கு குருந்தோ(டு) அரும்பீன்று பாங்கர்","மராஅ மலர்ந்தன தோன்றி விராஅய்க்","கலந்தனர் சென்றார் வலந்தசொல் எல்லாம்","பொலந்தொடீஇ பொய்த்த குயில்"],"p":"","r":"The tall kurunthu has budded, beside it the maraa has bloomed, and glory lilies have come out among them; now, O you with golden bangles, the koel has turned into a lie every word he swore when he joined us and went.","n":"The season of the flowers is the season he named, so the koel's call is the proof that his oath failed; the poem blames the bird for making the words false, a displacement that spares the man's name. Read the other way, the koel's call is itself false, a season come too soon, and the line consoles; the verse is marked uncertain for that reason.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 13","lb":"TI13","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["குருந்தோடு","குருந்து",1,"maram","scene",""],["மராஅ","மரா",2,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["தோன்றி","தோன்றி",2,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["குயில்","குயில்",4,"pul","scene","koel"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:2"},"ti:14":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":14,"t":["புன்னை பொரிமலரும் பூந்தண் பொழிலெல்லாம்","செங்கண் குயில்அக வும்போழ்து கண்டும்","பொருள்நசை உள்ளம் துரப்பத் துறந்தார்","வருநசை பார்க்கும்என் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"","r":"Even after seeing the season when the red-eyed koel calls through every cool flowering grove where the punnai opens its puffed-rice blossoms, he left her, driven by his heart's craving for wealth; yet her heart keeps watching for his coming.","n":"Two cravings are set in the same word: his நசை for wealth that drove him off and her heart's நசை for his return. கண்டும், 'even seeing', makes his leaving a choice made against the season of love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","nenju","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 14","lb":"TI14","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene",""],["குயில்","குயில்",2,"pul","scene","koel"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:2"},"ti:15":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":15,"t":["சிறுபுன் புறவொடு சிற்றெழால் சீறும்","நெறியரு நீள்சுரத்(து) அல்குவர்கொல் தோழி!","முறிஎழில் மேனி பசப்ப அருள்ஒழிந்(து)","ஆர்பொருள் வேட்கை அவர்"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether the man, who gave up grace for the craving of hard-won wealth while her body, lovely as tender leaves, grows pale, is now halting somewhere on the long wasteland of hard paths, where the little hawk rages at the small dull dove.","n":"Reproach and worry share one sentence: he abandoned grace (அருள் ஒழிந்து), yet she wonders where he lodges tonight. The hawk at the dove is the road's violence; it could be read as his harshness to the meek, but no act of a lover is in it and the deletion of line 1 takes nothing she says, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","arul","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 15","lb":"TI15","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புறவொடு","புறவு",1,"pul","scene","dove"],["சிற்றெழால்","எழால்",1,"pul","scene","small hawk"],["முறிஎழில்","முறி",3,"maram","scene","tender leaf"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:2"},"ti:16":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":16,"t":["கருங்கால் மராஅம் நுணாவோ(டு) அலர","இருஞ்சிறை வண்டினம் பாலை முரல","அரும்பிய முள்ளெயிற்(று) அஞ்சொல் மடவாய்","விரும்புநாம் செல்லும் இடம்"],"p":"","r":"The man tells the shy girl with sweet speech and newly budded sharp teeth that the place they are going to is one to be wished for, now that the dark-trunked maraa blooms together with the nuna and swarms of broad-winged bees hum the paalai melody.","n":"The bees hum பாலை, the music named for the very land the lovers must cross, so the man turns the dreaded landscape into a song to lead her on. The verse never names the road's hardship; its comfort is all in what it leaves out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","inbam","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 16","lb":"TI16","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மராஅம்","மரா",1,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["நுணாவோடு","நுணா",1,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bees"],["பாலை","பாலை",2,"yaazh","scene","paalai mode"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:2"},"ti:17":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":17,"t":["கல்லதர் வாயில் கருந்துடி கள்பம்பும்","வில்லுழுது வாழ்நர் குறும்புள்ளும் போவர்கொல்","எல்வனை மென்தோள் நெகிழப் பொருள்நசைஇ","நல்கா துறந்த நமர்"],"p":"","r":"The woman wonders whether our man, who left without grace for love of wealth while her soft shoulders with their bright bangles grew slack, will pass even through the hamlets of those who live by ploughing with the bow, where at the mouth of the stony track the black thudi drum sounds and toddy flows.","n":"வில்லுழுது வாழ்நர், 'those who live by ploughing with the bow', is the bandit's livelihood named as a farmer's, which makes the threat sound settled and ordinary. நமர், 'our man', keeps him hers even in the sentence that calls him graceless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","acham","melivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 17","lb":"TI17","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கருந்துடி","துடி",1,"parai","scene",""],["கள்","கள்",1,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["வில்லுழுது வாழ்நர்","வில்லுழவர்",2,"seythi","scene","bowmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:2"},"ti:18":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":18,"t":["கதிர்சுடக் கண்ணுடைந்து முத்தம் சொரியும்","வெதிர்பிணங்கும் சோலை வியன்கானம் செல்வார்க்(கு)","எதிர்வன போல்இலவே எவ்வளையோ கொன்னே","உதிர்வன போல உள"],"p":"","r":"For the man going to the wide forest where tangled bamboo, its nodes split by the scorching sun, sheds pearls, the speaker's bangles do not look like ones that will meet his return; every one of them seems ready to fall, for nothing.","n":"The bamboo sheds its pearls in the heat as her bangles are about to fall from her thinning arms: the land drops its treasure on the road he takes for wealth while she loses her ornaments uselessly (கொன்னே). Line 3 is hard to construe, so the verse is marked uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 18","lb":"TI18","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வெதிர்பிணங்கும்","வெதிர்",2,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",1,"pira","scene","bamboo pearl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:2"},"ti:19":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":19,"t":["கலையொடு மான்இரங்கும் கல்லதர் அத்தம்","நிலைஅஞ்சி நீள்சுரத்(து) அல்குவர்கொல் தோழி!","முலையொடு சோர்கின்ற பொன்வண்ணம் அன்னோ","வளையொடு சோரும்என் தோள்"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether he, afraid of the stony desert track where the doe cries out with the stag, will halt somewhere in the long wasteland; alas, the gold colour that wilts down over her breasts, and her shoulders, slip away together with her bangles.","n":"Three things droop together, the pallor with the breasts and the shoulders with the bangles, matching the pair of deer that suffer together on his road: in the waste even the beasts keep each other company, while he and she wilt apart.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","melivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 19","lb":"TI19","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கலையொடு மான்","கலை, மான்",1,"maa","iraicchi","stag and doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கலையொடு மான்","not_confirmed","0/1","even the deer of the waste keep together in distress, while he faces the road alone and she wilts alone at home"]],"ch":"ti:2"},"ti:20":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":20,"t":["ஏற்றிய வில்லின் எயினர் கடுஞ்சுரம்","பாற்றினம் சேரப் படுநிழல் கண்டஞ்சிக்","கூற்றின வல்வில் விடலையோ(டு) என்மகள்","ஆற்றுங்கொல் ஐய நடந்து"],"p":"","r":"The mother asks a passing man whether her daughter can bear to walk, beside the young man with a strong bow fierce as Death, across the harsh wasteland of the Eyinar with their strung bows, where flocks of vultures gather and she will shrink in fear from the shadows they cast.","n":"The youth she went with is himself likened to Death (கூற்றின), so the mother finds no safety even in the girl's protector; the question is whether the girl can bear it (ஆற்றுங்கொல்), not whether she will come back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","acham","kootru","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 20","lb":"TI20","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["எயினர்","எயினர்",1,"seythi","scene","hunters of the waste"],["பாற்றினம்","பாறு",2,"pul","scene","vultures"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:2"},"ti:21":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":21,"t":["அஞ்சனக் காயா மலரக் குருகிலை","ஒண்டொடி நல்லார் முறுவல் கவின்கொளத்","தண்கழற் கோடல் துடுப்(பு)ஈனக் காதலர்","வந்தார் திகழ்நின் தோள்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine that her lover has come and her shoulders shine: the collyrium-dark kaayaa has bloomed, the kurukilai buds have taken the beauty of the smile of women with bright bangles, and the cool kodal has put out its ladle-shaped spathes.","n":"Unlike the verses around it, this one reports the arrival as done (வந்தார், past), so the season's flowers are no longer a promise but the frame of the return; her shining shoulders are the pallor's undoing, said without naming the pallor. The women's smile is set inside the flower, so the woodland already smiles the smile the heroine will now smile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","paruvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 21","lb":"TI21","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["காயா","காயா",1,"maram","scene","kaayaa flower"],["குருகிலை","குருகு இலை",1,"maram","scene","kurukilai buds"],["கோடல்","கோடல்",3,"maram","scene","glory lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:3"},"ti:22":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":22,"t":["மென்முலைமேல் ஊர்ந்த பசலைமற்(று) என்னாங்கொல்","நன்னுதல் மாதராய்! ஈதோ நமர்வருவர்","பல்நிற முல்லை அரும்பப் பருவஞ்செய்(து)","இன்னிறம் கொண்ட(து)இக் கார்"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the heroine of the fine brow what will become now of the pallor that crept over her soft breasts, for here, she says, our man will come: this rain-cloud has made the season, with mullai of many colours in bud, and taken on its sweet colour.","n":"The pallor is spoken of as a creeping thing (ஊர்ந்த) whose fate is now in question, so the friend's 'what will become of it' is a playful threat against the pallor rather than a question to the girl. The cloud is said to 'make' the season (பருவஞ்செய்து), as if keeping the man's appointment for him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","paruvam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 22","lb":"TI22","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",3,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:3"},"ti:23":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":23,"t":["சென்றார் வருவர் செறிதொடீஇ! காரிஃதோ","வெஞ்சின வேந்தர் முரசின் இடித்துரறித்","தண்கடல் நீத்தம் பருகித் தலைசிறந்து","இன்றையில் நாளை மிகும்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine of close-set bangles that he who went will come: here is the rain, thundering and roaring like the drums of fiercely angry kings, having drunk the flood of the cool sea and come to its height, and tomorrow it will be greater than today.","n":"'Tomorrow more than today' turns the friend's proof into a rising scale: each day's heavier rain is a nearer return. The kings' drum keeps the reason for his absence, the king's war or work, inside the sound that promises his coming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 23","lb":"TI23","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முரசின்","முரசு",2,"parai","simile","war drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:3"},"ti:24":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":24,"t":["செஞ்சுணங்கின் மென்முலையாய்! சேர்பசலை தீர்இஃதோ","வஞ்சினம் சொல்லி வலித்தார் வருகுறியால்","வெஞ்சினம் பொங்கி இடித்(து)உரறிக் கார்வானம்","தண்பெயல் கான்ற புறவு"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine, whose soft breasts bear red beauty spots, to let the pallor that has settled on her go: here is the woodland where the rain-sky, swelling with fierce anger, thundering and roaring, has poured its cool rain as the sign of the return of him who swore his oath and held to his resolve.","n":"Two kinds of சினம் face each other: his வஞ்சினம், the sworn vow he made when he left, and the sky's வெஞ்சினம், fierce anger, so the storm reads as the vow made audible. The sign (குறி) is his, not the sky's: the rain is the token he named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","vanjinam","paruvam","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 24","lb":"TI24","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:3"},"ti:25":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":25,"t":["கருவியல் கார்மழை கால்கலந்(து) ஏத்த","உருகு மடமான் பிணையோ(டு) உகளும்","உருவ முலையாய்! நம் காதலர் இன்னே","வருவர் வலிக்கும் போது"],"p":"","r":"As the massed dark rain-clouds, mingling with the wind, rise up, and the tender young buck leaps with his doe, the friend tells the heroine of lovely breasts that our lover will come right now, at the time he resolved on.","n":"Strike line 2 and the friend's word stands: the rains have come and he will come now, at the time he set. The buck with his doe is the one thing in the verse that acts as a lover should, which is why it can comfort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","punarchi","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 25","lb":"TI25","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மடமான்","மடம் மான்",2,"maa","iraicchi","young buck"],["பிணையோ","பிணை",2,"maa","iraicchi","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மடமான்","confirmed","2/2","the creatures keep their mates beside them in this season, so the man who fixed this time will be beside her now"]],"ch":"ti:3"},"ti:26":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":26,"t":["இருங்கடல் மாந்திய ஏர்கொள் எழிலி","சுருங்கொடி முல்லை கவின முழங்கிப்","பெரும்பெயல் தாழப் பெயர்குறி செய்தார்","பொருந்த நமக்குரைத்த போழ்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend says this is the time he told us of, fittingly, when he made the sign of his return: the beautiful cloud that drank the dark sea has thundered so that the curling mullai creeper grows lovely, and the great rain hangs low.","n":"பொருந்த, 'fittingly', says the season and his word now match: the friend's proof is the agreement of the two, not the rain alone. The poem names the moment of his speaking (நமக்குரைத்த போழ்து), so the friend counts herself among those he promised.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","kuri","vaaimai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 26","lb":"TI26","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:3"},"ti:27":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":27,"t":["ஆயர் இனம்பெயர்த்(து) ஆம்பல் அடைதாய்","பாய முழங்கிப் படுகடலுள் நீர்முகந்து","மாயிரு ஞாலம் இருள்கூர் மருண்மாலை","செயவர் செய்த குறி"],"p":"","r":"The friend points to the bewildering evening, when the herdsmen move their herds home, the cloud, having scooped water from the deep sea, thunders to spread far, and the vast dark world fills with darkness, as the sign that he who went on his work set for his return.","n":"The mullai evening, which elsewhere torments the waiting woman, is here turned by the friend into his appointed token. The verse gives no vocative, so who speaks is inferred from the run of the decade.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kuri","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 27","lb":"TI27","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆயர்","ஆயர்",1,"seythi","scene","herdsmen"],["இனம்","இனம்",1,"maa","scene","herd"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",1,"yaazh","scene","ambal flute tune"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:3"},"ti:28":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":28,"t":["அதிர்குரல் ஏறோ(டு) அலைகடல் மாந்தி","முதிர்மணி நாகம் அனுங்க முழங்கிக்","கதிர்மறை மாலை கனைபெயல் தாழப்","பிதிரும் முலைமேல் கணங்கு"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that, as the cloud with its thunder of shaking voice drinks the wave-tossed sea and roars so that the serpent with its ripe gem cowers, and in the evening that hides the sun the heavy rain hangs low, the beauty spots will spread over her breasts.","n":"The spots (சுணங்கு) on a woman's breasts are a sign of happiness and union, the opposite of the pallor of the other verses; the friend predicts the body's change from waiting to joy. The gem-bearing serpent that shrinks at thunder is a stock belief of the rains.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","azhagu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 28","lb":"TI28","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நாகம்","நாகம்",2,"pira","scene","serpent"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:3"},"ti:29":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":29,"t":["கோடல்அம் கூர்முகை கோளரா நேர்கருதக்","காடெலாம் கார்செய்து முல்லை அரும்(பு)ஈன","ஆறெலாம் நுண்ணறல் வார் அணியிழாய்!","போதராய் காண்பாம் புறவு"],"p":"","r":"The friend calls the heroine of fine jewels to come out and see the woodland with her: the rain has made the whole forest its own, the sharp buds of the lovely kodal look like snakes ready to strike, the mullai has put out buds, and fine sand flows down every stream.","n":"The proof is no longer reported but shared: 'come, let us see' (போதராய் காண்பாம்) invites the heroine out of the house into the season itself. The same words could be the returned man's to his wife; the run of the decade favours the friend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","azhagu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 29","lb":"TI29","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["கோடல்அம்","கோடல்",1,"maram","simile","glory lily"],["கோளரா","கோள் அரா",1,"pira","simile","striking snake"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:3"},"ti:30":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":30,"t":["அருளி அதிரக் குருகிலை பூப்பத்","தெரிஆ இனம்நிறை தீம்பால் பிலிற்ற","வரிவனைத் தோளி! வருவார் நமர்கொல்","பெரிய மலர்ந்த(து)இக் கார்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine of the striped-bangled arm that this rain has bloomed in full, the cloud thundering in grace, the kurukilai flowering and the chosen herds of cows oozing sweet milk; will our man not come?","n":"'Will our man come?' (வருவார் நமர்கொல்) is a question whose answer the whole season has given; the cows streaming milk for their calves are the plainest picture of a return home, though the verse draws no match, so they stay scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 30","lb":"TI30","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருகிலை","குருகு இலை",1,"maram","scene","kurukilai flowers"],["இனம்நிறை","இனம்",2,"maa","scene","cattle herd"],["தீம்பால்","பால்",2,"unavu","scene","milk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:3"},"ti:31":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":31,"t":["பழனம் படிந்த படுகோட்(டு) எருமை","கழனி வினைஞர்க்(கு) எதிர்ந்த பறைகேட்(டு)","உரனிழிந்(து) ஓடும் ஒலிபுனல் ஊரன்","கிழமை யுடையன்என் தோட்டு"],"p":"","r":"The lord of the town of loud waters, where the bent-horned buffalo wallowing in the field-pond hears the drum beaten against the field workers and runs off with its strength gone, still holds, the speaker says, his rightful bond (கிழமை) with her.","n":"The buffalo that bolts at the field drum invites a reading of the man startled out of his pleasures, but the poem gives only that one match and never states the situation it would point to, so it stays scene; the last word, தோட்டு, does not construe cleanly, and whether the claim of கிழமை is a concession or a bitter irony turns on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 31","lb":"TI31","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பழனம்","பழனம்",1,"pira","scene","field-pond"],["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["வினைஞர்க்","வினைஞர்",2,"seythi","scene","field workers"],["பறைகேட்","பறை",2,"parai","scene","drum"],["ஒலிபுனல்","புனல்",3,"pira","scene","running water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:4"},"ti:32":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":32,"t":["கணைக்கால் நெடுமருது கான்ற நறுந்தா(து)","இணைக்கால் நீலத்(து) இதழ்மேல் சொரியும்","பணைத்தாள் கதிர்ச்செந்நெல் பாய்வயல் ஊரன்","இணைத்தான் எமக்குமோர் நோய்"],"p":"","r":"The lord of the town of spreading fields of thick-stalked red paddy in ear, where the tall marutham tree with its arrow-straight trunk sheds fragrant pollen on the petals of the blue lily, has fastened a sickness on us too.","n":"எமக்கும், 'on us too', is the barb: the sickness he gives is not hers alone but shared with the women he goes to. The tree that scatters its pollen on whatever lily lies under it reads easily as the man spending his love freely, but only that one correspondence is clear, so it is a near miss for ullurai (condition 3) and stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 32","lb":"TI32","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["நெடுமருது","மருது",1,"maram","scene","marutham tree"],["நீலத்","நீலம்",2,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"],["கதிர்ச்செந்நெல்","செந்நெல்",3,"unavu","scene","red paddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ti:4"},"ti:33":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":33,"t":["கடையாயார் நட்பேபோல் காஞ்சிநல் ஊர!","உடைய இளநலம் உண்டாய் - கடைய","கதிர்முலை ஆகத்துக் கண்ணன்னார் சேரி","எதிர்நலம் ஏற்றுநின் றாய்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the good town of kaanji trees that, like the friendship of the base, he has consumed the young beauty that was his own, and now at the last stands taking in the fresh beauty of the street of bright-breasted women he holds dear as his eyes.","n":"The simile is from the ethics of friendship, not from the fields: a friend who eats while there is something to eat and leaves when it is gone. உடைய, 'your own', makes the wife's youth his property, which makes spending it and turning away a loss to himself as much as to her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","natpu","nalam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 33","lb":"TI33","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காஞ்சி","காஞ்சி",1,"maram","scene","kaanji tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:4"},"ti:34":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":34,"t":["செந்நெல் விளைவய லூரன் சிலபகல்","தன்னலம் என்அலார்க்(கு) ஈயான் எழுபாண!","பாரித்த அல்குல் பணைத்தோளார் சேரியுள்","வாரிக்குப் புக்குநின் றாய்"],"p":"","r":"The woman says the lord of the town of fields where red paddy ripens would not, for some days, give his love to any but her; so get up, bard: you have gone into the street of the broad-hipped, bamboo-shouldered women and stand there for your own gain.","n":"The bard is shown not as the husband's go-between to the wife but as one who frequents the other women's street for what it brings him (வாரி). Who speaks is not certain: the other woman could say 'he gives himself to none but me', and the sense of வாரிக்கு is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 34","lb":"TI34","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["செந்நெல்","செந்நெல்",1,"unavu","scene","red paddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:4"},"ti:35":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":35,"t":["வேனிற் பருவத்(து) எதிர்மலரேல் தூதும்","கூனிவண்(டு) அன்ன குளிர்வயல் நல்லூரன்","மாணிழை நல்லார் இளநலம் உண்டவர்","மேனி ஒழிய விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The man of the good town of cool fields is like the bent bee that hums over freshly opened flowers in the hot season: he enjoys the young beauty of well-jewelled women and leaves them with nothing but their bodies.","n":"மேனி ஒழிய, 'only the body left', is the point of the simile: the bee takes the honey and the flower stands, emptied. The verse speaks of the women in general (நல்லார்), so the wife counts herself among many.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","nalam","ilamai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 35","lb":"TI35","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கூனிவண்டு","வண்டு",2,"pira","simile","bee"],["எதிர்மலரேல்","மலர்",1,"maram","simile","fresh flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:4"},"ti:36":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":36,"t":["செந்தா மரைலருஞ் செய்வயல் நல்லூர!","நொந்தான்மற்(று) உன்னைச் செயப்படுவ(து) என்னுண்டாம்","தந்தாயும் நீயே தரவந்த நன்னலம்","கொண்டாயும் நீஆயக் கால்"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the good town of tilled fields where red lotus blooms: if she is hurt, what can anyone do to you, when you are the one who gave and you are the one who took back the fine beauty that came with your giving?","n":"தந்தாயும் நீயே ... கொண்டாயும் நீ: her beauty bloomed from his love, so the one who caused it is the one who has ruined it, and there is no one else to complain to. The question is a reproach that leaves him no answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","oodal","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 36","lb":"TI36","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["செந்தா மரை","செந்தாமரை",1,"maram","scene","red lotus"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:4"},"ti:37":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":37,"t":["பல்காலும் வந்து பயின்றுரையல் பாண! கேள்","நெல்சேர் வயவல லூரன் புணர்ந்தநாள்","எல்வளைய மென்தோளேம் எங்கையர் தம்போல","நல்லஅருள் நாட்டம்இ லேம்"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the bard not to come again and again pressing his words: even on the days the lord of the town of paddy fields was joined with us, we of the soft shoulders and bright bangles never had the gracious regard of his eyes that 'our younger sisters' have.","n":"எங்கையர், 'our younger sisters', is the polite family word turned to irony for the women he goes to; நாட்டம், his eyes' regard, is what she says she never had, which makes the bard's assurances of his love pointless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","parathai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 37","lb":"TI37","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெல்சேர்","நெல்",2,"unavu","scene","paddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:4"},"ti:38":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":38,"t":["நல்வயல் ஊரன் நலமுரைத்தும் நீபாண!","சொல்லிற் பயின்றுரைக்க வேண்டா - ஒழிதிநீ","எல்லுநன் முல்லைத்தார் சேர்ந்த இருங்கூந்தல்","சொல்லுமவர் வண்ணம் சோர்வு"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the bard that though he speaks of the goodness of the man of the town of good fields, he need not go on repeating it in practised words; let him stop: the dark hair against which his fine jasmine garland lay tells, by its loosened state, what that man is like.","n":"She sets a sign against the bard's speech: the disordered hair answers his praise without argument. Whose hair it is, the other woman's or her own, the Tamil leaves open, and எல்லு is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 38","lb":"TI38","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முல்லைத்தார்","முல்லை",3,"maram","ornament","jasmine garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:4"},"ti:39":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":39,"t":["கருங்கயத்(து) ஆங்கண கழுமிய நீலம்","பெரும்புற வாளைப் பெடைகதூஉம் ஊரன்","விரும்புநாள் போலான் வயின்நலம் உண்டான்","கரும்பின்கோ(து) ஆயினேம் யாம்"],"p":"","r":"The lord of the town where, in the dark pond, the big-backed vaalai fish bites at the full-blown blue lily, no longer as on the days he desired us, has eaten up our beauty, and we have become the chewed refuse of sugarcane.","n":"The closing image is the bitterest in the decade: not a flower left standing but the cane-pith spat out after the juice is gone. பெடை may make the biter the female fish, which would point the bite at the other woman rather than the man; the ullurai is tagged on the reading that the fish is the man.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nalam","oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 39","lb":"TI39","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["கருங்கயத்","கயம்",1,"pira","scene","pond"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",1,"maram","ullurai","blue water-lily"],["வாளைப்","வாளை",2,"pira","ullurai","vaalai fish"],["கரும்பின்கோ","கரும்பு",4,"maram","scene","sugarcane pith, the vehicle of the closing metaphor"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","வாளைப் நீலம்","not_confirmed","0/1","the husband who has eaten up his wife's beauty"],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ti:4"},"ti:40":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":40,"t":["ஆம்பல் அணித்தழை ஆரம் துயல்வரும்","தீம்புனல் ஊரன் மகளிவள் ஆய்ந்தநறும்","தேமலர் நீலம் பிணையல் செறிமலர்த்","தாமரை தன்ஐயர் பூ"],"p":"","r":"This girl, on whom a leaf-skirt of water-lily and a necklace sway, is the daughter of the lord of the town of sweet waters; her garland is of chosen, fragrant, honeyed blue lilies, and the close-petalled lotus is the flower of her brothers.","n":"The flowers are badges: the lily garland is hers, the lotus her brothers'. Naming her father and her brothers' flower tells a suitor she is of a guarded house, which in a love poem reads as a push to seek her openly in marriage; who speaks to whom is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 40","lb":"TI40","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","ornament","white water-lily leaf-skirt"],["தீம்புனல்","புனல்",2,"pira","scene","running water"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",3,"maram","ornament","blue water-lily garland"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",4,"maram","ornament","lotus, the brothers' flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:4"},"ti:41":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":41,"t":["நெய்தல் படப்பை நிறைகழித் தண்சேர்ப்பன்","கைதைசூழ் கானலுள் கண்டநாள் போலானான்","செய்த குறியும்பொய் யாயின ஆயிழையாய்!","ஐயகொல் ஆன்றார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that the lord of the cool shore, with its neythal gardens and brimming backwaters, is no longer as he was the day she first saw him in the screw-pine-ringed seaside grove; the trysts he set have proved false, and she asks whether the bond of the noble is really so frail.","n":"ஐயகொல் ஆன்றார் தொடர்பு turns a maxim into a reproach: she does not say he lied, but asks whether the bond of the worthy (ஆன்றார்) is so slight, which quietly puts him outside that class. The first sight in the grove (கண்டநாள்) is the measure he has fallen from.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","natpu","kalaviyal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 41","lb":"TI41","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"],["கைதைசூழ்","கைதை",2,"maram","scene","screw-pine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:5"},"ti:42":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":42,"t":["முத்தம் அரும்பும் முடத்தாள் முதுபுன்னை","தத்தும் திரைதயங்கும் தண்ணங் கடற்சேர்ப்ப!","சித்திரப் பூங்கொடி அன்னாட்(கு) அருள்ஈயாய்","வித்தகப் பைம்பூணின் மார்பு"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the cool sea shore, where the old bent-trunked punnai puts out buds like pearls and the leaping waves heave, to grant his grace to the girl who is like a painted flowering creeper: his chest with its fine green ornaments.","n":"What the friend asks him to give is his chest (மார்பு), so arul here is not pity but the embrace and the claim it makes; the plea for grace is a plea for him to take her openly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","punarchi","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 42","lb":"TI42","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முதுபுன்னை","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene","punnai tree"],["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",1,"pira","simile","pearl"],["பூங்கொடி","பூங்கொடி",3,"maram","simile","flowering creeper"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:5"},"ti:43":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":43,"t":["எறிசுறா நீள்கடல் ஓதம் உலாவ","நெறியிறாக் கொட்கும் நிமிர்கழிச் சேர்ப்பன்","அறிவுஅறா இன்சொல் அணியிழையாய்! நின்னில்","செறிவுஅறா செய்த குறி"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine, girl of fine jewels whose sweet speech never parts from good sense, that the trysts set by the lord of the long backwater, where the tide of the shark-tossed sea moves and prawns wheel along their path, never lose their closeness with her.","n":"நின்னில் செறிவுஅறா செய்த குறி can also mean that the marks of their meetings never leave her body, so that the friend has guessed the secret (an opening of the friend's discovery) rather than reassured her. The striking shark beside the wheeling prawns invites a hidden reading, but it gives only a mood, not two pairs, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 43","lb":"TI43","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எறிசுறா","சுறா",1,"pira","scene","shark"],["நெறியிறாக்","இறா",2,"pira","scene","prawn"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:5"},"ti:44":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":44,"t":["இளமீன் இருங்கழி ஓதம் உலாவ","மணிநீர் பரக்கும் துறைவ! தகுமோ","குணநீர்மை குன்றாக் கொடியன்னாள் பக்கம்","நினைநீர்மை இல்லா ஒழிவு"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the ford, where the tide with its young fish moves over the dark backwater and gem-clear water spreads, whether it is fitting for him to stay away from the side of the creeper-like girl whose good nature never fails, without even the grace of thinking of her.","n":"The verse plays on நீர் three ways: the gem-clear water (மணிநீர்), her unfailing nature (குணநீர்மை) and his lack of the nature of remembering (நினைநீர்மை). Her constancy is set against his forgetting in the same word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 44","lb":"TI44","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இளமீன்","மீன்",1,"pira","scene","fish"],["கொடியன்னாள்","கொடி",3,"maram","simile","creeper"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:5"},"ti:45":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":45,"t":["கடல்கொழித்(து) ஈட்ட கதிர்மணி முத்தம்","படமணி அல்குல் பரதர் மகளிர்","தொடலைசேர்த்(து) ஆடும் துறைவ! என்தோழி!","உடலுள் உறுநோய் உரைத்து"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the ford, where fishermen's daughters with jewelled girdles on hood-shaped hips string into garlands the bright pearls the sea has washed up and heaped, and play, of the sickness that has entered her friend's body.","n":"The playing girls who make ornaments of what the sea gives freely stand against the friend's girl, who is ill; the contrast is left unstated. The last line has no finite verb (உரைத்து), so the friend's message is cut off at 'telling of her sickness'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 45","lb":"TI45","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",1,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["பரதர்","பரதர்",2,"seythi","scene","fisherfolk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:5"},"ti:46":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":46,"t":["முருகியல் கானல் அகன்கரை யாங்கண்","குருதினம் ஆர்க்கும் கொடுங்கழிச் சேர்ப்ப","மருவி வரலுற வேண்டும்என் தோழி","உருவழி உன்நோய் கெட"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the curving backwater, where flocks of herons cry on the wide shore of the fragrant seaside grove, that he must come to join her for good, so that the sickness he caused, which wastes her beauty, may end.","n":"மருவி வரல் asks for a coming that stays, not another visit, so the plea is for marriage; and உன்நோய், 'your sickness', lays the girl's illness at his door.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kaamanoy","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 46","lb":"TI46","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருதினம்","குருகு இனம்",2,"pul","scene","flock of herons"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:5"},"ti:47":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":47,"t":["அணிபூங் கழிக்கானல் அற்றைநாள் போலான","மணியெழில் மேனி மலர்பசப்(பு) ஊரத்","துணிகடல் சேர்ப்பன் துறந்தான்கொல் தோழி!","தணியும்எள் மென்தோள் வளை"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether the lord of the shore of the surging sea has abandoned her, now that she is not as she was that day in the lovely flowered grove by the backwater: pallor spreads like flowers over her gem-bright body and the bangles slip down her soft shoulders.","n":"அற்றைநாள் போலான, 'not as on that day', is the same measure as in verse 41, but here it is her own body, not his conduct, that has changed; the question துறந்தான்கொல் she cannot answer reads itself on her skin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 47","lb":"TI47","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வளை","வளை",4,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:5"},"ti:48":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":48,"t":["கறங்கு மணிநெடுந்தேர் கண்வாள் அறுப்பப்","பிறங்கு மணல்மேல் அலவன் பரப்ப","வறங்கூர் கடுங்கதிர் வல்விரைந்து நீங்க","நிறங்கூரும் மாலை வரும்"],"p":"","r":"As the tall chariot with ringing bells cuts its way, crabs scatter over the gleaming sand and the fierce sun that parches hurries away, the evening, deepening in colour, comes on.","n":"The speaker is not named, and the chariot can be the man's coming at dusk (a hope) or the sun's own car leaving (a dread of evening). கண்வாள் அறுப்ப is obscure: the wheel's blade-like rim cutting the sand is the likeliest sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 48","lb":"TI48","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அலவன்","அலவன்",2,"pira","scene","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:5"},"ti:49":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":49,"t":["மயில்கொல் மடவாள்கொல் மாநீர்த் திரையுள்","பயில்வதோர் தெய்வம்கொல் கேளீர்! குயில்பயிரும்","கன்னி இளஞாழல் பூம்பொழில் நோக்கிய","கண்ணின் வருந்தும்என் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"","r":"The man, struck at first sight, asks his companions whether she was a peacock, a young woman, or a goddess dwelling in the waves of the great sea, for his heart aches from the eyes that looked at him by the flowering grove of young njazhal where the koel calls.","n":"This is the first-sight doubt (ஐயம்) of kalavu, and the shore supplies its goddess: not the hill's anangu but one who lives in the waves. The pain is placed in her look (நோக்கிய கண்ணின்), not her beauty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kan","nenju","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 49","lb":"TI49","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["மயில்கொல்","மயில்",1,"pul","scene","peacock"],["தெய்வம்கொல்","தெய்வம்",2,"deyvam","scene","sea goddess"],["குயில்பயிரும்","குயில்",2,"pul","scene","koel"],["இளஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",3,"maram","scene","njazhal tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ti:5"},"ti:50":{"book":"thinaimozhi_aimpathu","num":50,"t":["பவளமும் முத்தும் பளிங்கும் விரைஇப்","புகழக் கொணர்ந்து புற(வு) அடுக்கும் முன்றில்","தவழ்திரைச் சேர்ப்பன் வருவான்கொல் தோழி","திகழும் திருஅமர் மார்பு"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether the lord of the shore of creeping waves, his bright chest where Tiru dwells, will come to their front yard, where coral, pearls and crystal, mixed together, are brought as if in praise and heaped up at the edge.","n":"Who heaps the gems is not said (the waves, or the fisherfolk), and புகழக் கொணர்ந்து makes it an offering. A reader may hear in the treasure piled at her door the bride-wealth she hopes he will bring, but the act is not tied to him, so it is left as scene rather than iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 50","lb":"TI50","poet":"கண்ணன் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பவளமும்","பவளம்",1,"pira","scene","coral"],["முத்தும்","முத்து",1,"pira","scene","pearl"],["பளிங்கும்","பளிங்கு",1,"pira","scene","crystal"],["திருஅமர்","திரு",4,"deyvam","scene","Tiru, goddess of fortune"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ti:5"}},"links":[{"a":"aa:1","b":"kn:12","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 12: the dark clouds, like oiled elephants, rise on the right every day (ஏரும் வலம்), a sign beyond doubt. Ainthinai Aimpathu 1 ends on the same omen, வலனேருங் கார், after giving the cloud the colour of Mal, the spear of Murugan and the garland of Shiva.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rightward rise is a fixed reassurance in the mullai venba, and holding the two together shows the Ainthinai poet strengthening a plain omen by assembling three gods' emblems around it, where Kar Narpathu leans on its daily regularity."},{"a":"aa:1","b":"kn:1","type":"same_image","note":"Kar Narpathu 1 makes the rainbow the garland on the chest of sea-coloured Mal; Ainthinai Aimpathu 1 gives Mal the cloud's dark body and gives the garland to Shiva as the konrai.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both opening verses of the mullai collections divine the storm, but they share it out differently, so the god of the mullai was a ready frame that each poet filled by choosing which part of the sky belongs to which god."},{"a":"aa:2","b":"kn:4","type":"tension","note":"Kar Narpathu 4: the friend calls the season, with peacocks and konrai, the medicine for the heroine's wasting pallor. Ainthinai Aimpathu 2: with the peacock calling and the rain cloud low, every sight of the sky turns the heroine's shoulders yellow. On a plain reading the same season cures the pallor and causes it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set side by side the friend's argument and the heroine's experience disagree about the same sky, which shows that the season-as-proof consolation works only from outside, while to the waiting woman the season itself is the illness."},{"a":"aa:3","b":"nt:69","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 69: if the evening came like this in the land where he went for work, he could not hold to his resolve. Ainthinai Aimpathu 3: is there not this rain in the land where our man went?","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows a single argument of the waiting wife, that the season must reach him too and would bring him home, carried from evening in the long poem to the rain in the venba, where its failure becomes the sharper doubt whether the rain is in his land at all."},{"a":"aa:6","b":"k:1224","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1224: when the lover is absent, evening comes like enemies to a killing ground. Ainthinai Aimpathu 6: evening, sweet with the bee at the jasmine for those who have company, comes and saws those who have no one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both women make evening a killer, but the venba adds that the same hour is sweet to the companioned, so it locates the couplet's hostility in the absence and not in evening itself, which the couplet's 'when the lover is absent' only implies."},{"a":"aa:7","b":"k:1228","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1228: the herdsman's flute, envoy of the burning evening, is a weapon that kills. Ainthinai Aimpathu 7: at red evening the herdsman's fine little flute, played in joy once the cattle have fed, is to her like a spear thrown.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet states that the flute is a deadly weapon, and the venba supplies the scene that explains it: the herdsman plays for joy at his own day's return, so the weapon is someone else's homecoming."},{"a":"aa:7","b":"a:74","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 74: the heroine could hold firm on her friend's comfort if the hard-mouthed small flute (வல்வாய் சிறுகுழல்) of the unlettered herdsmen did not torment her in the evening. Ainthinai Aimpathu 7 calls the same small flute fine (சீர்சால் சிறுகுழல்) and its player glad, yet it strikes her like a spear.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One heroine blames the flute's harsh mouth and the other concedes its sweetness, so together they show the evening flute's pain moving from the instrument to the hearer, which is why the venba needs the simile of the spear."},{"a":"aa:8","b":"kn:18","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 18: the forest, dull like the body of a poor man, has put forth beauty like a rich man's mind. Ainthinai Aimpathu 8: the valli, dull like the body of the parted, has grown lovely like the body of the united.","grade":"sound","better":"same_image","te":"Both measure the revived plant by a human state, but Kar Narpathu uses poverty and wealth, the motive of his journey, and Ainthinai uses parting and union, its outcome, so the same simile can point either to why he left or to what she awaits."},{"a":"aa:9","b":"kn:14","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 14: the friend is sure he will come quickly, since the cool rain cloud laughs softly as the jasmine shows its bright teeth. Ainthinai Aimpathu 9: he will come; the jasmine has opened its buds, having heard the secret of the new rain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In both the friend's proof is the budding jasmine, but one reads it as the season's laughter and the other as the season's whispered secret already received, so the same flower serves as a public sign in one and as private knowledge in the other."},{"a":"aa:10","b":"nt:142","type":"qualifies","note":"Natrinai 142: returning, the man tells his charioteer of the young woman of mullai-fitting chastity who rejoices when a guest comes even at night. Ainthinai Aimpathu 10: returning, he tells the charioteer to hurry to see her who stands with the bolt of her chastity drawn shut, hand to cheek.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The same speaker on the same road imagines the wife's chastity as an open door to guests in one poem and as a shut bolt in the other, so the pair shows karpu as both the household's hospitality and its closing against the world while the husband is away.","gr":3},{"a":"aa:11","b":"nt:259","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 259: the friendship made with the hill lord in the millet field, chasing parrots and bathing in the falls by the vengai slope, now looks hard to keep because the millet heads have ripened dry. Ainthinai Aimpathu 11: the few days in the vengai grove of the millet field have gone, sweet because no one gave orders.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Natrinai gives the crop's ripening as the reason the meetings end and Ainthinai names what made them possible, the absence of anyone to command the girls, so together they show the secret love as lent by the harvest calendar and taken back by it."},{"a":"aa:12","b":"a:38","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 38 the friend imagines the hill man finding the swing, the pool and the reaped field empty, and regrets that she forgot to tell him 'the high hill crowned with that white waterfall is our village'. Ainthinai Aimpathu 12 is that telling: the guarding is over, and this village by the clean waterfall is our place.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The longer poem shows the cost of not telling him where the house is when the field closes, and the quatrain makes the telling itself, so the pair shows pointing out the village by its waterfall as the friend's expected move at harvest, the step that turns field meetings toward the house.","gr":3},{"a":"aa:12","b":"nt:213","type":"develops","note":"In Natrinai 213 the man asks the girls which is the small rock-fenced village near the roaring falls, and whether the guarding of the millet is theirs too. In Ainthinai Aimpathu 12 the friend says their guarding of the millet is over and this village by the waterfall is their place.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The man's question and the friend's answer use the same two markers, the waterfall village and the guarding of the millet, which shows the exchange as a set dialogue of kurinji courtship in which the friend's answer comes only when the guarding has ended."},{"a":"aa:14","b":"a:388","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 388: a young hunter with a hound comes to the millet platform asking whether a wounded tusker has passed through their field. Ainthinai Aimpathu 14: he came like those who ask after a beast, but there is something else he means to ask.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru stages the hunter's question in full while Ainthinai compresses it into a simile, 'like those who ask after a beast', which shows that the asking-after-game approach was a recognised pretext the girls themselves could name and see through."},{"a":"aa:15","b":"nt:44","type":"same_image","note":"Natrinai 44: the man remembers the girl after she bathed in the falls with her companions, her large eyes reddened by the beating water. Ainthinai Aimpathu 15: the friend says the girl's red-lined eyes reddened because they bathed in the leaping falls after plucking vengai.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The eyes reddened by the waterfall are a cherished sight for the lover in Natrinai and an explanation offered to others in Ainthinai, so the same bath serves both the love and its concealment, which is why it is safe for the friend to name."},{"a":"aa:16","b":"a:308","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 308: a tusker worn from fighting a tiger, its long wound unwashed, marks the hill of a man who comes by night; the friend asks him to come by day. Ainthinai Aimpathu 16: an elephant that escaped the tiger shrinks at every footfall on the path, and the girl cannot sleep remembering that path.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In Akananuru the tiger-wounded elephant only marks the hill while the friend argues for day visits, whereas Ainthinai carries the beast's after-fear onto the path itself and into the girl's sleepless eyes, showing how the same hill emblem can move from scenery to the measure of a lover's dread."},{"a":"aa:17","b":"nt:151","type":"qualifies","note":"Natrinai 151: even if the girl's forehead pales and her shoulders grow thin, may the hill lord not come by night. Ainthinai Aimpathu 17: because he comes without fail, the girl, afraid for him, loses her bloom and wastes away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Natrinai offers her wasting as the lesser harm to be borne so that he stays away at night, but Ainthinai shows the night visits themselves causing the wasting through fear, so neither his coming nor his staying away spares her and only marriage can."},{"a":"aa:18","b":"nt:306","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 306: the millet is being reaped and the hill girl sent home; how will he come now to see her? Ainthinai Aimpathu 18: not even the parrots come to the millet field now; do not forget your bond with her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Natrinai states the practical loss at harvest, no place left to meet, and Ainthinai names the risk behind it, that like the parrots he may stop coming once the field is empty, so the pair shows the end of the guarding feared as a test of his memory as well as of access."},{"a":"aa:19","b":"nt:104","type":"develops","note":"In Natrinai 104 the girl asks whether anyone but her thinks of the narrow midnight path he takes while thunder strikes and floods block the way. In Ainthinai Aimpathu 19 the friend, picturing him swimming the falls at midnight, says her heart asks what will become of the girl.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The girl fears the night path for the man, and the friend fears it for the girl who would not survive his harm, so the pair shows the danger of the night meeting passing through two hearts, with the friend's fear one step removed and therefore usable as an argument for marriage."},{"a":"aa:20","b":"a:388","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 388 the old velan says 'my lord's seizure brought this sickness; I know the cure', and the girl mocks him. In Ainthinai Aimpathu 20 the velan, not knowing that the hill man took her nature, says a spirit has seized her and sheds the kid's blood.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Akananuru lets the velan claim knowledge of the cure and Ainthinai calls him plainly the one who does not know, so together they show the veri rite built around the diviner's ignorance, with the girl's secret as the one thing his craft cannot read."},{"a":"aa:21","b":"a:146","type":"develops","note":"Both wives turn away the bard over the husband's வாய்மொழி: Akananuru 146 pities the woman who will trust that deceiver's word as she once did; Ainthinai Aimpathu 21 says the bard who asks her to trust it is hawking needles in the smiths' street.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 146 shows the wife as a past victim of the man's word, and 21 shows why that history makes the bard's pleading absurd: she is the one market where his word cannot be sold."},{"a":"aa:21","b":"nt:200","type":"qualifies","note":"Natrinai 200 has the friend warn the town's women against the bard's lie-wrapped crooked words; Ainthinai Aimpathu 21 treats his pleading not as dangerous but as pointless.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two refusals name two faults in the go-between, falsehood that endangers others and futility before the wife who already knows, so the refusal of the bard had both a public and a private argument."},{"a":"aa:22","b":"a:56","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 56 the wife tells the bard who blundered into her house 'இம்மனை அன்று; அஃது உம்மனை'; in Ainthinai Aimpathu 22 she tells him 'நெறிஅதுகாண் எங்கையர் இற்கு'.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same retort appears as a told comic incident in Akananuru and as a direct taunt in the four-line venba, which shows that pointing the bard to the other house was a fixed refusal move the later poet could use bare, without the story.","gr":3},{"a":"aa:23","b":"k:785","type":"develops","note":"Kural 785: shared feeling alone, without meeting or acquaintance, gives the claim of friendship. Ainthinai Aimpathu 23: the bond of those who know needs no bard to plead for the man's grace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The wife turns the Kural's rule about friendship into an argument against intercession in marriage: if feeling alone makes the bond, then the presence of a go-between is itself proof that the feeling is gone."},{"a":"aa:24","b":"ku:376","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kuruntokai 376 the man says his beloved is cool like Pothiyil sandal in summer and warm like a lotus heart in the cold; in Ainthinai Aimpathu 24 the wife says her shoulders were once sandal paste to him and are now hotter than caustic.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The lover's praise of a body that tempers every season is answered by the wife's claim that the same body's heat follows his conduct, not the weather, so the courtship image is shown to depend on fidelity."},{"a":"aa:25","b":"kl:81","type":"develops","note":"In Kalittokai 81 the son's prattle is praised as 'நோய் நாம் தணிக்கும் மருந்து', the medicine that eases our illness; in Ainthinai Aimpathu 25 the pain of longing for the man's chest is met by the son's மழலைவாய் கட்டுரை.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make the child's prattle the cure for the wife's love-sickness in a marutham quarrel, and Kalittokai's longer scene shows what the venba compresses: the boy repeats words learned in the street, so his chatter can carry the father back into the house.","gr":2},{"a":"aa:25","b":"k:66","type":"develops","note":"Kural 66: only those who have not heard their children's prattle (மழலைச்சொல்) call the flute and lute sweet. Ainthinai Aimpathu 25: the son's மழலைவாய் talk eases the pain of longing for the husband.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural ranks prattle above music as a pleasure; the venba gives it a harder task, standing in for an absent husband's embrace, so the general praise of children is tested in the one place it might fail."},{"a":"aa:26","b":"a:66","type":"tension","note":"In Akananuru 66 the husband, dressed for a new wedding, stops his chariot when his son runs to him and gives the wedding up; in Ainthinai Aimpathu 26, as the drums of his new wedding sound, the little one who goes after his chariot comes to grief. On the reading of இவன் as the son, the appeal that succeeds in one fails in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Akananuru 66 presents the son as the force that turns a husband back from another marriage, and 26 shows the same scene with the opposite outcome, so the son's claim emerges as a hope the wives hold rather than a rule the poems guarantee."},{"a":"aa:28","b":"a:66","type":"tension","note":"Akananuru 66: seeing the son run to him, the man cries 'நெடுந்தேர் தாங்குமதி, வலவ!' and climbs down. Ainthinai Aimpathu 28: the son stands yearning, the man sees him and drives the chariot on without a look.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Set side by side the two chariots mark the test of a straying husband as whether he stops for his child, and 28 makes the wife's hardening a verdict on his failing that test rather than on his other women."},{"a":"aa:27","b":"k:1309","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1309: sulking, like water in the shade, is sweet only with those who love. Ainthinai Aimpathu 27: the faded wife asks whether she is still one the man would sulk at.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's rule that a quarrel belongs inside love lets the wife read the man's absence of pulavi as the absence of love, so the venba shows the rule used as evidence by someone on the losing side."},{"a":"aa:29","b":"k:1308","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1308: what use is suffering when there is no lover who knows we suffer? Ainthinai Aimpathu 29: she would refuse his embrace, but he has no thought of her refusal, so she will serve him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both say a protest needs a lover who registers it, and the venba draws the practical end the couplet stops short of: with no such lover, the wife abandons oodal and goes back to service."},{"a":"aa:30","b":"k:1330","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1330: sulking is love's pleasure, and its pleasure is to embrace after it. Ainthinai Aimpathu 30: even while sulking, union with the man's fragrant body is sweet, like the south wind in the cold season.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural places the embrace after the sulk, while the wife finds it sweet during the sulk itself, so the venba collapses the Kural's sequence and shows desire overriding the quarrel instead of crowning it."},{"a":"aa:31","b":"a:99","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 99: the man leading the eloping girl bids her admire a forest strewn with murukku buds like the gripping claws of the striped tiger (கோள்உகிர் அன்ன). Aimpathu 31 uses the same simile, murukku buds like a tiger's blood-dyed claws, for the spring that breaks the waiting woman's heart.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Aimpathu 31 says outright that this season gives joy only to those who are together (எதிருநர்க்கு இன்பம்), and Akananuru 99 is that joy spoken by a man walking beside his girl, so the pair shows one flowering read as delight or as wound according to whether the lovers are together."},{"a":"aa:32","b":"ku:37","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 37: the friend tells the heroine that the road he took is a loving one, where the tusker strips the yaa branches to ease his mate's hunger. Aimpathu 32: on his road the tusker pours the little water of a rock pool over his heat-worn mate's head.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Kuruntokai 37 states the friend's conclusion itself, that the road is loving (அன்பின), while the Aimpathu gives only the tusker's act and leaves the inference to the heroine, so the pair shows the same comfort working first as a stated argument and then as an image she must read."},{"a":"aa:33","b":"a:49","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 49: the foster mother remembers seeing that the girl who loved her parrot, ball and play-seeds (கிளியும், பந்தும், கழங்கும்) was not as before, without guessing why. Aimpathu 33: the girl no longer thinks of doll, ball, parrot and playmates, and the mother asks whether she will go after her lover.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In both the neglected playthings are the sign that the girl has passed out of childhood into love, but Akananuru 49 reads the sign too late and dwells on the mother's missed chance, while the venba reads it and turns at once to the burning road, so the pair shows the same evidence serving regret or foreboding."},{"a":"aa:33","b":"a:369","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 369: after the daughter's elopement her parrot will not drink its milk, her playmates do not play and her doll gets no offering. Aimpathu 33: the girl herself has stopped thinking of doll, ball, parrot and playmates.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Aimpathu 33 says the girl has forgotten her toys and companions, and Akananuru 369 says those toys and companions still mourn her, so together they show the mother's grief as a forgetting that runs one way: the house keeps faith with a child who has already left it in mind."},{"a":"aa:34","b":"k:1151","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1151: if you are not leaving, tell me; if you mean to come back soon, tell it to those who will still be alive. Aimpathu 34: the friend asks the man whether he will cross many wastes of killing robbers, leaving the woman of sword-bright eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both are said to the man at the point of departure to stop him, but the Kural argues from the woman's death and the Aimpathu from the man's own danger among robbers, so the pair shows the two levers the dissuading speaker can pull against the journey, her life and his."},{"a":"aa:35","b":"a:53","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 53: the woman says the man who loves wealth more than her crossed a waste where a red dog rests with its starving mate in the sweet shade of a lettered hero-stone (எழுத்துடை நடுகல் இன்நிழல்). Aimpathu 35: on the road of the man bent on wealth a ghoul gorged on the fat of the slain sleeps in a hero-stone's wide shade.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In both the hero-stone's shade is the only rest on the wealth-seeker's road, but Akananuru shelters a hungry pair there and the Aimpathu a sated ghoul alone, so the pair shows the stone's shade turned from a picture of shared hardship into one of the road's appetite for men, which is the woman's fear."},{"a":"aa:36","b":"a:395","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 395: the woman says the man crossed a forest where a fork-horned stag, chasing the mirage, strays off the path and calls for its grazing doe in a failing voice. Aimpathu 36: an elephant pair takes the white mirage for water, runs to it together and falls with tangled legs.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The two poems take the same chase after a mirage to opposite ends: in Akananuru it parts the stag from its mate and gives the abandoned woman her own cry, in the Aimpathu the pair are fooled and fall together, so the image can either mirror her loneliness or shame the man who leaves when even deceived beasts do not part."},{"a":"aa:37","b":"n:398","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 398: the girl answers the friend who asks whether she can walk tomorrow behind her man through the hard forest: one who gets a great horse learns to ride it at once. Aimpathu 37: the mother asks whether the girl who faltered at play can keep pace behind her lover on the stony path. The mother doubts the feet that the girl says love will make able.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same question, can her feet go with him, gets opposite answers: the mother measures the girl by her weakness at childhood play, the girl by the man she has gained, so across the two books the pair stages the quarrel of the elopement between the generation that remembers and the one that goes."},{"a":"aa:37","b":"a:153","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 153: the foster mother says the girl who grew hot and tired even running after a ball with her friends has gone into the burning summer forest, and asks whether her tender feet can manage it (வல்லுநள் கொல்லோ). Aimpathu 37 asks the same with the same verb (வல்லவோ), recalling her faltering at play by the ford.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The verb வல் and the evidence from play recur in both, which shows the mother's doubt as a set argument of the form: she judges the girl's strength for the road only by her weakness at games, a measure that the girl's going has already overturned.","gr":2},{"a":"aa:38","b":"kl:11","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 11: the woman recalls that the man, calling the forest cruel, told her the tusker there lets his mate drink the little muddy water first and the stag gives its own shade to the doe. Aimpathu 38: the stag, judging the pool's little water too scant, feigns drinking so the doe drinks.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kalittokai puts the beasts' care in the man's own report, so the woman reasons that one who saw and told it will not let her wither; Aimpathu 38 adds the stag's pretence, a deceit in the service of love, and sets it on the road his heart has already taken, so the same proof is offered from the friend's side and made finer.","gr":2},{"a":"aa:38","b":"a:371","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 371: a twist-horned stag whose mate was shot will not drink the little water of the pool (சில்நீர் உண்ணாது) and lies stricken; the man crossing alone sees it and thinks of his love's eyes. Aimpathu 38: the stag feigns drinking the pool's little water so the doe may drink.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two stags at a pool of scant water show the paalai poets making the stag's not drinking the sign of love both while the mate lives and after she dies, and Akananuru 371 bears out the friend's hope in 38, since there the traveller does see such a stag and his thoughts turn home.","gr":3},{"a":"aa:39","b":"a:5","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 5: the man tells his heart he gave up the journey when his wife, without a word, showed her grief in her face and her sigh over their son. Aimpathu 39: the man calls his heart foolish, for when they stir to leave for wealth she sobs and sighs; how will she bear it once they are gone?","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Akananuru 5 shows the journey given up on the sight of her mute grief, and Aimpathu 39 shows the man reasoning from her sobs at the setting out to the harder grief to come, so the pair gives the decision against wealth both as what he saw and as what he foresees."},{"a":"aa:40","b":"a:167","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 167: the man asks his heart whether, after tonight with his wife in the tall mansion, they will lie tomorrow sleepless in the ruined hall of a deserted village. Aimpathu 40: after tonight on the shoulders of her of the cool garland, where will they lie tomorrow, alone beyond the hill paths?","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 167 answers its own question with a ruined lodging described in full, while Aimpathu 40 leaves the place unnamed (என்கொலே சேக்கும் இடம்), so the pair shows the same tonight-and-tomorrow argument against the journey working either by the horror of a described shelter or by the bare want of any."},{"a":"aa:41","b":"a:50","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 50: the hidden-tearful girl says the anril on the palmyra by the house cannot sleep at night if parted even once from its mate. Ainthinai Aimpathu 41: the anril cries though its mate has come and rests beside it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The anril's wakefulness is a fixed proof of sleepless separation, and the venba reverses its case: the long poem argues from a parted bird by likeness, the four-line poem from a joined bird by greater reason, so the same bird can carry both the analogy and the a fortiori."},{"a":"aa:42","b":"a:170","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 170 the woman begs the crab to carry her word to the shore lord, since grove, backwater and punnai will not speak; in Ainthinai Aimpathu 42 she begs the crab not to trample his chariot's tracks.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Taken together they show why the crab is the neythal heroine's confidant: it alone moves where the lover moved, so she can ask it either to carry her word to him or to spare the one trace of him she still has.","gr":2},{"a":"aa:44","b":"nt:378","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 378: the bond began when the shore lord came and broke up their little striped sand-house and spoke soft words. Ainthinai Aimpathu 44: the weeping girl tells her mother that the sea came and wrecked her sand-house.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Natrinai 378 shows that the wrecked sand-house is the very scene where the secret love began, which turns the girl's excuse in Ainthinai Aimpathu 44 into a coded confession: the 'sea' that broke her play is the man of the sea."},{"a":"aa:45","b":"ku:25","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 25: no one but the thief was there, and only a heron watching for aaral fish, so if he lies she has no recourse. Ainthinai Aimpathu 45: will the herons of the grove let his oath go? On a plain reading one heron is no witness and the other herons are.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same bird stands at the secret oath in both, but Kuruntokai's heron, intent on its fish, proves that a kalavu vow has no witness, while the venba's herons are made guarantors, so the pair shows the heroine's two answers to the oathless union: despair, or a trust that invents its witnesses."},{"a":"aa:46","b":"nt:283","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 283 and Ainthinai Aimpathu 46 both have the friend ask the shore lord, beside girls decking their sand-houses (with neythal flowers, with pearls), whether it is fitting (தகுமோ, தகுவதோ) that the girl's beauty should waste.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend's reproach has a fixed form, the girls' sand-house play set against the heroine's wasting beauty with the question 'is it fitting?', and Natrinai adds the ground the venba leaves implicit: his word was held truer than the sun, so the wasting is a breach of his truth."},{"a":"aa:47","b":"a:80","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 80 the friend tells the shore lord that they spend the day scaring birds from the fish their father brought, and bids him come by day; in Ainthinai Aimpathu 47 the hero sees the girl scaring birds from the fish her brothers brought and finds her a tormenting goddess.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Guarding the drying catch is the neythal girl's appointed work and so the one place she is seen by day: the friend can offer it as a meeting place, while the hero's first sight of her there already places her among the kinsmen whose catch she guards, which is why the sight brings fear."},{"a":"aa:48","b":"n:203","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 203: the great never say they will not support those who have come to depend on them (தற்சேர்ந் தவரை), as a branch bears its own fruit. Ainthinai Aimpathu 48: can those who do not hold to the distress of those who have come to depend on them (தற்சேர்ந்தார்) carry great renown?","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared word தற்சேர்ந்தார் shows the friend borrowing the maxim of supporting one's dependents and turning it on the lover: the girl in secret love is made a dependent with a kinsman's claim, and his renown is staked on marrying her."},{"a":"aa:50","b":"a:4","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 4 the returning man has tied his chariot's bell-tongues so as not to startle the paired birds sleeping in the flowering boughs; in Ainthinai Aimpathu 50 the waiting girl takes the noise of birds for his horses' bells.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two poems make the chariot bell the sound by which each lover's attention is measured: the man who has come home to his wife silences it out of care for mated birds, while the girl still in secret love hears it in every bird."},{"a":"ae:3","b":"nt:57","type":"same_image","note":"Natrinai 57: while the herd of wild cows sleeps with their calves, a female monkey creeps up, squeezes the swollen udder and wrings the milk into her young one's hands. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 3: the monkey strokes the udder and the wild cow, glad as with her calf, lets the milk flow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together they show one scene turned from theft to gift: the older poem's monkey steals while the cows sleep, fitting a friend who warns of the girl's ruin, while the later venba makes the cow consent, fitting a girl who says she cannot part, so the speaker's case decides whether the milk is stolen or given."},{"a":"ae:4","b":"k:782","type":"develops","note":"Kural 782: the friendship (கேண்மை) of the wise waxes like the crescent moon, that of fools wanes. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 4: the friendship of the worthy (சான்றவர் கேண்மை) stands firm and grows into strength, so the mountain lord's will too.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The venba lifts the Kural's maxim about friendship with the worthy into a girl's mouth as her proof that her lover will not fail, which shows the ethics books' rule of friendship being used, within the akam frame, to judge a lover's character before marriage."},{"a":"ae:5","b":"k:1102","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 1102: the man says the jewelled woman (அணியிழை) is herself the medicine for the sickness she causes. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 5: the friend tells the man there is no medicine but him for the bright-jewelled girl (வயங்கிழை).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same paradox of the lover as the only cure runs in both directions, the woman for the man in the Kural, the man for the woman here, and the change of speaker turns the Kural's private wonder into the friend's demand that he give the cure."},{"a":"ae:10","b":"a:318","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 318: the friend tells the man who comes alone at night by a path made fearful by elephants, snakes and tigers not to come from today, and to blow his horn on reaching home so they know he is safe. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 10: the girl asks her friend to tell the lord of the snake-haunted groves not to come by night, to keep a life safe.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The long poem spells out what the four lines only hint, that refusing the night meeting is an act of care for the man's life on a dangerous path, and the venba moves the plea into the girl's own wish (குறையொன்று உடையேன்), showing the same refusal felt from inside."},{"a":"ae:12","b":"a:242","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 242: the mother, not knowing of the man who chased parrots from the millet with them, calls the ignorant velan, and before the kid's life is given he should come and embrace the girl. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 12: parrots raid the millet, the girl has embraced him fully, and the velan has resolved to cut the kid.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both set the embrace against the kid's sacrifice, but the older poem makes the embrace the remedy that must come before the rite, while the venba gives both as done, which leaves the velan preparing a useless sacrifice for a cure already taken."},{"a":"ae:14","b":"k:1233","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1233: the shoulders that swelled on the day of union now announce loudly that he has gone. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 14: because he is said to be good, her shoulders have forgotten to waste even if he leaves.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"On a plain reading the two make incompatible claims about the same sign: the Kural takes wasting shoulders as the body's certain witness to separation, while the venba says trust in the man's goodness can stop the body from giving that witness at all.","gr":3},{"a":"ae:15","b":"aa:5","type":"same_image","note":"Both close on 'each time I see the sky' and the eye: Aimpathu 5 says drops mingle and fall from the eye (துளிகலந்து வீழ்தருங் கண்); Ezhupathu 15 says the eye tosses with water as the sky tosses with clouds (நீரோ(டு) அலமரும் கண்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared closing formula could match eye and sky in two different ways, by substance in Aimpathu (tears are the rain's drops) and by motion in Ezhupathu (the eye tosses as the sky tosses), so the formula fixed the pairing but left the basis of the likeness to the poet."},{"a":"ae:17","b":"k:1224","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1224: with the lover absent, evening comes like enemies to a killing ground. Ezhupathu 17: as kodal buds and thunder greet the rains, the little evening comes like killers so that those with no one to embrace them tremble.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural's image of evening as executioner, spoken by one woman of one absent man, appears in the Ezhupathu tied to the rainy season and widened to a class (those with no one to hold them), which shows the image was a mullai convention of the promised-return season before or beside its use as a private complaint.","gr":3},{"a":"ae:17","b":"aa:6","type":"develops","note":"Aimpathu 6: the bee humming at the jasmine is sweet to those who have someone to lean on, but the dark evening comes and saws at us who have no one. Ezhupathu 17: the evening comes like killers upon those with no one to embrace them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Aimpathu 6 states both halves of the rule, sweet to the partnered and cutting to the lone, while Ezhupathu 17 states only the second, so together they show that the evening's cruelty was located in the hearer's state, not in the hour, and the later poem could leave the happy half unsaid."},{"a":"ae:21","b":"k:1226","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1226: she did not know, while her husband had not yet gone, that evening brings sickness. Ezhupathu 21: before he returns, the evening comes with the rains to torment her, and no one lives in such folly as she.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the woman's ignorance at the time of parting as the cause of her suffering, while the Ezhupathu's மடம் is a folly she still lives in now, knowing the evening, so the pairing separates ignorance that excuses from folly that the speaker mocks in herself."},{"a":"ae:22","b":"k:1228","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1228: the herdsman's flute is an envoy of the burning evening and a weapon that kills. Ezhupathu 22: herdsmen blow konrai flutes, the herd goes in, evening comes as she watches, and the sky thunders and draws its bow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the herdsman's flute the evening's envoy and weapon, and Ezhupathu 22, which names no pain at all, gives the same evening a second weapon, the rainbow drawn as a bow, so the reader of the Kural sees that the evening's arming was a scene the venba poets could show without comment."},{"a":"ae:22","b":"aa:7","type":"qualifies","note":"Aimpathu 7: after the cattle have fed, the herdsman's glad flute at the red evening is like a spear thrown at her. Ezhupathu 22: the herdsmen's konrai flutes and the herd entering the fold are only watched, with no feeling stated.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Aimpathu 7 states the wound the herdsman's dusk flute gives, and Ezhupathu 22 relies on it unstated, so read together they show that by these short anthologies the homecoming herd and flute were enough on their own to signal the waiting wife's pain."},{"a":"ae:30","b":"a:35","type":"develops","note":"Both mothers lament a daughter gone with a man through the bowmen's wasteland; Akananuru 35 prays to the god that the stranger may adorn her and lull her on his chest, while Ainthinai Ezhupathu 30 can only wonder whether the girl pines or walks on behind him with reddened feet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they give the mother's lament two stages, first not knowing whether the girl regrets her going and then accepting it and praying for the man's care, so the venba catches the moment the Akananuru poem has already passed."},{"a":"ae:31","b":"a:91","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 91 the elephant that shrinks with its hungry mate in drought lets the friend reassure the heroine after the man has gone; in Ainthinai Ezhupathu 31 the elephant sleeping with its mate in the burning forest comes when the man only speaks of going, and her tears answer it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same iraicchi of the elephant pair keeping together in the heat serves to comfort after the parting and to dissuade before it, which shows the image carries the lovers' bond itself and the speaker turns it to whatever the moment needs."},{"a":"ae:32","b":"a:53","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 53: past the lettered hero-stone of men felled by the archers' fine arrows, the heroine says his love is for wealth more than for her (பொருளே காதலர் காதல்). Ainthinai Ezhupathu 32 keeps the lettered stones, the archers' fine arrows and the claim that his love's longing has become hard-won wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The venba compresses Akananuru 53's scene and accusation into four lines but drops the friend's defence that he goes from a giving heart, putting his 'fitting labour' in its place, so the later poem shows which part of the older argument had become formula and which part it chose to change."},{"a":"ae:33","b":"a:35","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 35's road is held by bowmen of வில் ஏர் வாழ்க்கை, 'the life whose plough is the bow', and their cairns; Ainthinai Ezhupathu 33's road by வில்லுழுது உண்பார், 'those who eat by ploughing with the bow', among the cairns.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together they show the bow-as-plough figure for the wasteland robbers passing intact from the Sangam anthology into the later venba, while its use shifts from a mother's fear for a daughter on the road to a wife's reproach that the man can see the road's dangers but not her."},{"a":"ae:34","b":"k:1247","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1247 the woman in separation tells her heart to give up either desire or shame, since she cannot bear both; in Ainthinai Ezhupathu 34 she foresees her heart throwing off shame and going with her life after him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet poses the choice between desire and shame as a demand on the heart, and the venba shows the heart's answer and its cost, since choosing desire there means the life leaving the body with it."},{"a":"ae:40","b":"a:297","type":"same_image","note":"In Akananuru 297 the man on his way for wealth sees a wildcat's fluffy kitten following its mother like stars round the moon; in Ainthinai Ezhupathu 40 the heroine hears that on his road the spotted wildcat watches for prey for its kitten.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Each lover sees the wildcat with its young on the wealth road from his or her own side, the man as the household he leaves behind and the woman as the provision he goes to win, so the creature gathers both halves of the paalai argument about leaving for wealth.","gr":3},{"a":"ae:41","b":"k:1203","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1203: a sneeze that seems to come and then dies away makes her think he seems about to think of her and does not. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 41: sneezes come one after another, so he is thinking of her. On the plain reading the same sign gives opposite verdicts.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both women read the sneeze by the same folk belief, but the Kural uses its failure for a wry doubt while the venba piles it up as proof, which shows the belief was firm enough to support either despair or hope depending on what the body did.","gr":4},{"a":"ae:42","b":"a:289","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 289 the man on his journey imagines her praying 'speak good' each time the lizard chirps; in Ainthinai Ezhupathu 42 the lizard chirps at the favourable side, together with a throbbing eye and good dreams.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The man's imagined picture of her waiting on the lizard and her own report that the lizard has spoken well are the two ends of one practice, which shows that the omen was a shared language the separated lovers each expected the other to be reading."},{"a":"ae:44","b":"kl:85","type":"develops","note":"In Kalittokai 85 the mother shares out her milk in mockery to the 'other mothers' who wept after trusting the father's deceiving oath (மாயச் சூள்); in Ainthinai Ezhupathu 44 a woman says she believed his words long ago, not knowing them for a false oath (பொய்ச்சூள்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the false oath is the wound the wife shares with her rivals: Kalittokai's mother pities the other women it deceived, and the venba's speaker finds herself among them, so the oath binds all the man's women in one injury."},{"a":"ae:45","b":"kl:79","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 79: the wife tells the husband not to take up their son, whose hands will pull the pearl chain and garland by which the other women marked him (அணி சிதைப்பான்). Ainthinai Ezhupathu 45: he comes with the son on his chest, the boy's pearl ornaments crushing his frontlet (பட்டஞ் சிதைப்ப).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Kalittokai spells out what the four-line poem only shows: the son's hands spoiling the father's finery are spoiling the other women's tokens, so the venba's ironic 'he is resourceful' means the child he brings as a shield is also the one who exposes him.","gr":2},{"a":"ae:47","b":"nt:380","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 380: refusing the bard, the friend says they smell of ghee, smoke and new birth, holding the son with milk at the breast, and are no match for the man of the bright-jewelled women's street. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 47: the wife says the son is suckled and she is old, and sends the bard to the street of the budding-breasted women.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that the nursing mother's body is a set argument in refusing the go-between: the friend in Natrinai offers it as humble unfitness, the wife in the venba turns it into scorn by sending the bard to dance for his food, so the same motherhood can be pleaded as modesty or thrown as contempt."},{"a":"ae:49","b":"a:46","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 46: the friend refusing the husband entry describes his town's red-eyed buffalo breaking its tether in the night, scattering fish and grazing the lotus. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 49: the red-eyed buffalo breaks its wooden clog, eats the field crop and rests in the shade, and the bard is told to carry this to the flower-like women.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same hidden comparison is aimed at different hearers: in Akananuru 46 it shames the husband at the door, in the venba it is sent through the bard to the other women as a warning, which shows the buffalo ullurai was a portable accusation that could be addressed to either party of the triangle."},{"a":"ae:49","b":"nt:330","type":"qualifies","note":"Natrinai 330: the male buffalo plunges into the pond and rests from the day's labour in the sweet shade of the marutham (இன் நிழல் வதியும்), and the friend blames the courtesans' mean hearts. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 49: the buffalo breaks its clog, eats the crop and then rests in the shade (நிழல்வதியும்).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The resting buffalo is innocent after labour in Natrinai and guilty after plunder in the venba, and the blame follows suit, falling on the courtesans in one and on the husband in the other, so what the beast did before the shade decides whom the poem accuses."},{"a":"ae:51","b":"a:66","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 66: the husband, dressed to wed another, is drawn home by the little son who runs to his chariot, and the wife says the old saying about sons has proved true. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 51: the wife sends the bard away, saying her little son with gold bangles is her companion.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The son works on both sides of the marutham quarrel: in Akananuru 66 he pulls the straying father back into the house, in the venba the wife holds him up as companion enough to refuse the father's go-between, so the child is at once the husband's way in and the wife's reason to keep him out."},{"a":"ae:52","b":"k:773","type":"tension","note":"Kural 773 calls generous help to a fallen foe (ஊராண்மை) the cutting edge of valour. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 52: the friend mocks the husband for treating his going to the other street as ஊராண்மை, manliness before the town.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural makes ஊராண்மை a virtue of restraint toward the weak, while the friend uses the same word for the husband's public swagger in neglecting his wife, so on a plain reading the two give the word opposite contents, and the venba's sarcasm depends on the higher sense the Kural fixes."},{"a":"ae:54","b":"k:572","type":"develops","note":"Kural 572: the world's ways rest on கண்ணோட்டம், and those without it are a burden on the earth. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 54: the husband who resolves to withhold கண்ணோட்டம் while taking the wife's devoted service is told the blame falls on him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states kindly regard as a duty of public life; the venba brings it inside the marriage and makes its withdrawal a husband's blame, which shows the ethical term was already at work in the language of domestic quarrel."},{"a":"ae:55","b":"a:146","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 146: refusing the bard, the wife asks who the woman will be who now trusts the deceiving philanderer and will weep and pale as she did. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 55: the wife tells the other women directly not to embrace him to love's end or pine for him, but to give up their longing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 146 only pities the next woman from a distance, while the venba turns to the rivals and counsels them, so together they show the wife's grievance passing into a warning to the women who will suffer as she did."},{"a":"ae:56","b":"ai:65","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 65: the wife tells the husband not to embrace her body that bore their son, since it would spoil his chest. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 56: with the son trampling him, he praises her slackened breast, though he presses the budding breasts of a girl elsewhere.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In both the body that bore the son is the ground of the wife's sulking: in Ainkurunuru she withholds it as unfit for his groomed chest, in the venba she sees his praise of it as a cover for his taste for younger bodies, so the mother's body is what she bars him from and what he flatters to come back."},{"a":"ae:50","b":"kl:85","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 85 says the father uses the lying bard as a fishhook to bind the hearts of the women he wants. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 50 tells the bard to go on without pause to the street of the gold-like women.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kalittokai names the bard as the husband's lure for other women, which explains the venba's order to keep going to that street: the wife is sending the go-between back to his real work, so refusing the bard and accusing him are one act."},{"a":"ae:59","b":"aa:50","type":"develops","note":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 50: the heroine says she rose at what she took for his horses' bells and turned back on hearing it was birds. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 59 tells the same mistake of her in the third person, with the same words புள்ளரவம் கேட்டுப் பெயர்ந்.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two venbas share a stock scene, and the second adds what the first leaves out: in the heroine's own telling she turns back from a melting heart, in the onlooker's telling from fear of the hamlet's talk, so the formula carried either the private grief or the public danger of kalavu waiting."},{"a":"ae:60","b":"nt:335","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 335: the sea's sound never stops and the tide comes and goes, and with the anril and the lute the night cannot be survived. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 60: the sea, like her, does not sleep as the tide shifts, because the shore lord took her beauty and left.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In the Natrinai the restless sea is one of the things that keep the heroine awake, while the venba makes the same tide sleepless for her own reason, so the pair shows the neythal sea turning from a cause of sleeplessness into a fellow sufferer.","gr":2},{"a":"ae:62","b":"ku:25","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 25: no one was there but the thief, only a heron watching for aaral fish on the day he took her. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 62: does the heron of the grove not know his cruelty, though it saw him go and turn graceless?","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai's heron proves that the secret union had no witness worth the name, and the venba goes one step on, upbraiding that same silent witness for not knowing what it saw, so the heron of kalavu moves from absent proof to complicit bystander."},{"a":"ae:62","b":"aa:45","type":"tension","note":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 45: will the herons of the grove let his oath go unpunished? Ainthinai Ezhupathu 62: the heron of the grove saw his cruelty and does not know it. On a plain reading one makes the herons guarantors of his oath and the other finds the heron a useless witness.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Held together, the two shore venbas show the heroine's trust in the grove's herons as witnesses of a secret oath collapsing once he has gone: the birds she counted on to hold him are the birds she then reproaches for seeing nothing."},{"a":"ae:63","b":"ku:236","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 236: the friend tells the shore lord to go if he must, but first give back the beauty he consumed (நீ உண்டவென் னலனே). Ainthinai Ezhupathu 63: the heroine would accost him herself and say 'give back the colour' (வண்ணந்தா).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The demand to give back consumed beauty belongs to the friend in Kuruntokai, but the venba puts it in the heroine's own mouth and doubts that only looking at him would suffice, so the pair shows the reproach moving from the go-between to the injured woman herself."},{"a":"ae:63","b":"ae:65","type":"develops","note":"Both poems end on the same demand, வண்ணம் தா ... தொடுத்து: in 63 the heroine and her friend would accost the shore lord themselves; in 65 she sends the paired anril to say it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Two poems of one sequence ending on the same words show the demand for her colour passing from a plan to face him to a message given to a bird, which tells us how far her confidence in meeting him has fallen."},{"a":"ae:64","b":"aa:42","type":"develops","note":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 42: the heroine begs the crab not to blur the tracks of his chariot so she can gaze at the path he took. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 64: she tells the stork that it knows the path by which he went.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In both venbas the path of the departed shore lord is known only to the shore's creatures, the crab who can erase it and the stork who has seen it, so the pair shows the neythal heroine's knowledge of his leaving lodged entirely in what the animals of the beach can keep or destroy."},{"a":"ae:65","b":"a:170","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 170: since grove, backwater and punnai will not speak, the woman begs the crab to carry her word to the shore lord, at midnight when the sea-crow dreams beside its mate. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 65: she bids the anril, joined with its mate, to tell him 'give back the colour'.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The long poem shows why a creature is her messenger, since the land itself is mute, while the venba chooses a messenger whose own paired life is the reproach, so together they show the creature-messenger used first for its reach and then for its example."},{"a":"ae:66","b":"k:1157","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1157: do not the bangles slipping from my wrist spread the news that the shore lord has left me? Ainthinai Ezhupathu 66: will the thief-hearted shore lord walk off by another path while her good bangles slip?","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet makes the slipping bangle a herald announcing his departure, while the venba places it at the moment of his going and calls his heart a thief's, so the pair qualifies the Kural's sign: the bangle that tells the town of his leaving also convicts him of planned deceit."},{"a":"ae:68","b":"a:380","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 380: the shore lord, wooing, showed the girls a crab carrying a naaval fruit to its mate in its burrow and said 'this is a fine way'. Ainthinai Ezhupathu 68: the friend tells the shore lord of the crab with its mate on the dune, and that her friend has paled.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In the Akananuru the hero himself offers the crab that cares for its mate as a sign of how he will behave, and in the venba the friend returns the same crab to him as a reproach while the girl pales, so the pair shows a courting pledge turned into the evidence against the man who gave it."},{"a":"ki:1","b":"k:1182","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1182 has pallor ride the woman's body proud that 'he gave it'; Kainnilai 1 has her body waste pale as if saying 'the forest lord joined with her'.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set in the guarded millet field of the secret love, the Kural's private conceit of pallor naming its giver becomes a public danger, since a body that names the lover undoes the secrecy that kalavu depends on."},{"a":"ki:3","b":"ku:342","type":"same_image","note":"Both set a hill creature that has handled ripe fruit against snares; in Kuruntokai the hunter sets nets on every tree after the fruit is already touched, in Kainnilai the monkey tastes the fruit and is caught.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same fruit-and-snare economy of the hill serves two speakers opposite ways: the friend uses the late snares to tell the man the girl is now guarded too late, while the girl uses the caught monkey to say she herself is the one bound by the sweetness she tasted."},{"a":"ki:4","b":"ku:42","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 42's friend asks the hero whether their bond will wane even if desire ends; Kainnilai 4's friend asks the girl whether he is one to join and then forget.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The one doubt about the man's constancy is put by the friend as a challenge to him in Kuruntokai and as a comfort to the girl in Kainnilai, so the pair shows the friend's double office of pressing the hero and calming the heroine with the same question.","gr":2},{"a":"ki:6","b":"k:1249","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1249's woman asks her heart whom it goes seeking when he is within it; Kainnilai 6's girl lets her heart go unspoken to his jewelled chest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The heart's going out to the lover is chided as foolish in the Kural's separation chapter but is a silent secret gift in Kainnilai's secret stage, so the same movement of the heart is judged by whether he is still near."},{"a":"ki:7","b":"k:1157","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1157 the slipping bangles proclaim that the lord of the shore has left; in Kainnilai 7 they slip at the mere telling of the hill lord's friendship.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the bangles as a sign without a scene, and Kainnilai supplies the scene that explains them: a monkey that snatches the fruit and bolts, so the bangles fall because the 'friendship' was a theft and the leaving was already in it."},{"a":"ki:10","b":"a:298","type":"develops","note":"Both have the hill lord of the vengai coming at midnight with spear in hand; Kainnilai 10 is the girl's eager forecast, Akananuru 298 her words when he has come, sweet yet unable to speak her love for fear of the mother.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together the two give the before and after of the night tryst: the quatrain's sleepless certainty that he will come turns, once he is there, into fear of the household and a plea for marriage, so the midnight visit is never the end the girl longs for."},{"a":"ki:13","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1151: the friend (or the woman) tells the man that his promise of a quick return should be told to those still alive then. Kainnilai 13: told of his going by the long wasteland, her eyes went soft with tears.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both answer the man's announcement of departure, but the Kural puts the threat to her life into argued words while the Kainnilai lets the friend report only the eyes giving way on hearing, so the pair shows the same reply could be made as a sharp argument or as a bare bodily fact laid before him."},{"a":"ki:18","b":"a:387","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 387: on the road, a lizard's small click by the hero-stones makes even lords with elephants halt and turn back. Kainnilai 18: at home, the truthful lizard chirps as if it has seen the traveller's return.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together they show that the lizard's voice was trusted at both ends of the journey with opposite force, as a warning that turns travellers back on the road and as a promise of return to those at home, so the omen's sense depended on which way the hearer faced."},{"a":"ki:18","b":"a:351","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 351: the returning man imagines the lizard that chirps whenever she thinks of him sounding at her ear as medicine for her aching shoulders. Kainnilai 18: the friend tells her the truthful lizard has chirped, seeing the traveller come.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The man in the Akananuru counts on the lizard to announce him and the friend in the Kainnilai reports that it has, so the pair gives both halves of one belief, that the traveller's approach reaches home through the lizard before he does."},{"a":"ki:19","b":"k:1265","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1265: the pallor of her shoulders will leave only after she has seen her husband with her eyes. Kainnilai 19: the friend says the brightness returning to her forehead shows he is coming, before he has come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On the Kural's reading the body recovers only from the sight of him and news cannot cure it, while the Kainnilai friend reads a recovery already begun as a sign of his return, so the two disagree on whether the body answers his presence or foreknows it."},{"a":"ki:21","b":"k:1249","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1249: the woman asks her heart whom it goes out looking for, since the beloved is already within it. Kainnilai 21: the woman says her heart has gone after the man, swimming the long way through the scorched forest.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Both women speak of a heart that leaves to follow the man, but the Kural calls that going needless because he lives inside her, while the Kainnilai takes the heart's long swim as real, so the pair sets an inward answer to absence against the older picture of the heart travelling the road."},{"a":"ki:22","b":"ki:21","type":"develops","note":"Kainnilai 21: the woman says her heart swims the long way after him (நீந்து). Kainnilai 22: the friend says he will swim back over the old clefts (நீந்திப் பெயர்ந்தவர்) and they will soon see him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence, the friend answers the woman's image with her own verb turned round, the heart swimming outward becoming the man swimming home, which shows how the friend's comfort in this book works by reversing the heroine's words rather than contradicting them."},{"a":"ki:22","b":"a:29","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 29: the returned man tells his wife that only his body stayed in the burning waste for the work, while his foolish heart was with her. Kainnilai 22: the friend asks whether she thinks the heart of the man who crossed the robber forest has parted from her.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The friend's question in the Kainnilai presumes exactly what the man asserts on his return in the Akananuru, that the body went and the heart stayed, so the pair shows the body-heart split was a shared assurance used by the comforter before and by the man after the journey."},{"a":"ki:24","b":"nt:84","type":"tension","note":"Natrinai 84: the woman reports that he will go (they say) by the hard waste where the mirage lures the deer, and excuses him as one who cannot live a home life unable to relieve beggars. Kainnilai 24: the woman reports that the hard waste where the deer runs after the mirage is, they say, the road of the loveless one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same reported road with the same mirage-chasing deer ends in opposite verdicts on the man, a duty that excuses him in the Natrinai and a want of love that condemns him in the Kainnilai, so the landscape fixed the scene while the poet chose the moral."},{"a":"ki:25","b":"kn:21","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 21: the returning man says the mullai buds along the road stand sharp like the teeth of his simple girl (முள்ளெயிறு ஏய்ப்ப). Kainnilai 25: the friend shows the heroine the mullai putting out sharp teeth (கூர்எயிறு ஈன) as proof he will come.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"One bud is read at both ends of the waiting: on the road the man sees her teeth in it, at home the friend offers it to her as his promise kept, so the mullai's teeth tie the two absent lovers to a single sight."},{"a":"ki:26","b":"kn:26","type":"qualifies","note":"Kar Narpathu 26: the thondri blooms over every field like the first-night lamps of Karthigai, and the rain comes as messenger. Kainnilai 26: the blood-red thonri buds are so like fire that a bird shies from them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both friends read the red thonri as flame, but one sees festival lamps and the other a fire that frightens, so the same sign of return can carry welcome or alarm, and the Kainnilai friend lets a trace of fear into her comfort."},{"a":"ki:27","b":"k:1182","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1182: the woman says pallor rides over her body (மேனிமேல் ஊரும் பசப்பு), claiming it is his gift. Kainnilai 27: at the rainy evening, thinking of his coming, pallor spreads over her body (ஊர்தரு மேனி பசப்பு).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet says only that pallor rides her body by his right; the Kainnilai fragment gives it an hour and a cause, the rain-season evening and the thought of his coming, so expectation, not only absence, is what sets the pallor moving."},{"a":"ki:29","b":"a:23","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 23: in the rains the stag embraces his doe (பிணை ... தழீஇ), and the woman asks if this is the time her man meant. Kainnilai 29: kurunthu blooms, the stag embraces his doe and the rain-cloud thunders; the rest is lost.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Akananuru poem shows the stag's embrace being weighed as the promised season's clock, which tells a reader of the broken Kainnilai verse what such an embrace was there to measure, even though its own ending is gone."},{"a":"ki:30","b":"a:14","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 14: the wife asks what will become of them by morning if he does not remember even in the evening when the cattle crowd into the common (மன்றுநிறை புகுதரும் மாலையும்). Kainnilai 30: the hour when the cowherds with konrai pipes enter the common (மன்றம் புகுதரும் போழ்து).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Akananuru wife names the homecoming to the common as the hour by which a man who remembers would be back, which explains why the Kainnilai poet could mark the waiting by that hour alone: the village's return is the measure of his not returning."},{"a":"ki:33","b":"kn:22","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 22: the returning man says the plumed, spirited horses are yoked (உளையணிந்து ... கலிமாவும் பூட்டிய). Kainnilai 33: the friend says he will come yoking his fine spirited plumed horses (உளையார் கலிநன் மாப்பூட்டி வருவார்).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The friend's promise uses nearly the words with which the hero himself reports setting out, so the comfort at home is shown to be an accurate picture of what is happening on the road, and the formula passes between the friend's and the hero's mouths."},{"a":"ki:34","b":"kn:33","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 33: the man says he set as his sign the time when the cloud that drew up the sea (கடனீர் முகந்த) would gather on the western hill. Kainnilai 34: as the cloud that scooped the sea pours (கடல்முகந்து கார்பொழிய), the lover has come.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two verses close the loop of the mullai promise: one poet's hero names the sea-drinking cloud as his appointed sign, and the other poet has the lover arrive exactly as that cloud breaks."},{"a":"ki:35","b":"a:74","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 74: the heroine says she could bear the friend's comfort but for the harsh small pipe the unlettered cowherds blow (கல்லாக் கோவலர் ஊதும் வல்வாய் சிறுகுழல்) at evening. Kainnilai 35: the herders who do not know (தெரிவிலர்) blow the sweet pipe (தீங்குழல்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both poets excuse the herders by their ignorance, but one calls the pipe harsh and the other sweet, which shows that the cruelty is not in the sound itself but in the hour and the ear that hears it."},{"a":"ki:35","b":"k:1228","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1228: the herdsman's flute is the messenger of the burning evening and a weapon that kills (கொல்லும் படை). Kainnilai 35: the herders, heedless, blow the sweet pipe at that hour.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the flute a weapon; the Kainnilai adds that those who wield it do not know they are wounding anyone, so the evening's weapon kills without a killer who intends it.","gr":2},{"a":"ki:37","b":"ae:49","type":"develops","note":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 49: the wife tells the bard to carry to the other women the man's description as lord of the town where the red-eyed buffalo breaks its clog, sinks into the pond and gorges on the crop. Kainnilai 37: the rutting buffalo plunges into the pond before the wife tells the bard to stop lying.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 49 tells the bard to deliver the buffalo as the man's own portrait, which shows that in Kainnilai 37 the same plunging buffalo set before the bard is a character sketch of the husband and not just the setting of his town.","gr":2},{"a":"ki:37","b":"ai:43","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 43: the woman tells the man his bard is a bigger liar than he is and swears many oaths. Kainnilai 37: the wife tells the bard to stop pouring out lies before her.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru 43 ranks the bard's lying above the man's, and Kainnilai 37 lets her silence the bard directly while the man is only named, so together they show the wife's anger moving onto the go-between as the safer target."},{"a":"ki:38","b":"k:1311","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1311: the woman refuses the chest of the man she calls a philanderer, because all women feed on him with their eyes. Kainnilai 38: the wife bars him from her house from today because he lives desiring young women's shoulders.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's refusal rests on a witty pretext, that other women merely look at him, while the venba's rests on his actual desire for other women, so the pair shows the Kural turning a real grievance of the marutham poems into the fine point of a lovers' quarrel."},{"a":"ki:39","b":"nt:250","type":"tension","note":"Natrinai 250: the man tells the bard how, coming near to hug his toddling son, his wife took it otherwise and shied away. Kainnilai 39: the wife says he comes bringing the garlanded son, pointing to him, to torment her. On the man's telling the son is a real tenderness she misreads; on hers it is a tactic.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Read together the two poems give both sides of the same approach through the child, the husband insisting his affection was misjudged and the wife calling it harassment, so neither poem's account can be taken as the plain fact of the scene."},{"a":"ki:39","b":"ae:50","type":"agrees","note":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 50: the wife calls it folly that he seeks entry with the son as his backing. Kainnilai 39: he comes with the garlanded son, pointing to him, to trouble her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 50 names the son as the man's 'witness' (துணைச்சான்றோன்) and Kainnilai 39 shows the boy dressed in a garland and pointed out, so the pair shows the child being staged as evidence of the marriage the husband has broken."},{"a":"ki:41","b":"ae:51","type":"develops","note":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 51: speaking of the man of the good pond town is my grief; get up and go, bard; I have my little son as companion. Kainnilai 41 opens with the same line (பொய்கைநல் லூரன் திறன்கிளப்ப) and ends with the little son as what she owns.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two venbas share their first line and their closing son, but where Ainthinai Ezhupathu 51 calls the man's praise her grief (எவ்வம்), Kainnilai 41 says she already knows his doings, so the reworking turns the wife's pain into a cool dismissal while keeping the son as her answer.","gr":2},{"a":"ki:41","b":"kl:81","type":"qualifies","note":"Kalittokai 81: the wife asks the toddling son to repeat the words the 'mothers' of the big street taught him, and the husband blames the bard. Kainnilai 41: she knows his doings, having the sweet babbling son as her own.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Kalittokai 81 shows a son's prattle betraying where his father has taken him, which supports reading Kainnilai 41's 'we know' as knowledge gained from the boy's babble rather than only as a mother's comfort."},{"a":"ki:42","b":"ae:47","type":"develops","note":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 47: the wife tells the greasy-mouthed bard to go to the street of young women and eat there even if he must dance for it (கூத்தாடி உண்ணினும் உண்). Kainnilai 42: if he is in want, let the bard take the man as his dancer, make him dance and eat even so (ஆட்டுவித்து உண்ணினும் உண்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The closing formula is the same, but Kainnilai moves the dancing from the bard to the husband, so the pair shows the later poet sharpening the taunt into a picture of the man as a performer worked by others."},{"a":"ki:42","b":"ki:46","type":"develops","note":"Kainnilai 42: the bard may make the man his dancer. Kainnilai 46: the man spoke pleasantly and only seemed ashamed.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Verse 46 says his shame was a show and verse 42 makes him a dancer, so the decade presents the husband's contrition as performance from both sides, his own acting and the bard's staging."},{"a":"ki:45","b":"ae:47","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 47: the son has been fed and we are old now (மூத்தேம் இனியாம்); go to the young women's street. Kainnilai 45: we are old now, bard (மூத்தேம் இனிப்பாண), young before, and we did not know he would leave.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 47 offers age as the reason he goes to younger women, while Kainnilai 45 sets the same words against his old promises, so the pair shows the formula of the ageing wife used once to explain his straying and once to expose a betrayal she could not foresee."},{"a":"ki:45","b":"ki:48","type":"develops","note":"Kainnilai 45: we are old now and did not know he would leave. Kainnilai 48: grown old with sagging breasts, we want no answer from him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In 45 her age is the vantage from which she sees his promises were false, and in 48 it becomes the ground of her refusal, so the decade moves age from grief to a reason for closing the door."},{"a":"ki:46","b":"tn:131","type":"qualifies","note":"Thinaimalai 131: the wife tells the graceless bard he need not fashion words to begin a reconciliation; she turned the day she heard the man's lies. Kainnilai 46: the man spoke many pleasant things and seemed ashamed; useless bard, say nothing false and go.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Thinaimalai 131 dates her refusal to the lies she heard, while Kainnilai 46 adds that the man had already acted out his shame, so the pair shows the bard's plea coming after the husband's own performance has failed."},{"a":"ki:47","b":"ku:127","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 127: because the man's one bard proved a liar, every bard looks like a thief to the women he left. Kainnilai 47: the wife calls the bard a liar (பொய்ப் பாண) and bars him from her house.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai 127 states the general effect of one lying go-between and Kainnilai 47 shows its consequence in a single house, the door shut to the bard himself, so the pair shows distrust of the messenger hardening into exclusion."},{"a":"ki:48","b":"nt:380","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 380: the friend refuses the bard, saying our breasts ooze milk, we smell of new birth and are no match for the man of the jewelled women's street. Kainnilai 48: the man does not embrace the sandal-cool breasts; we have grown old with sagging breasts; come for no answer.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Natrinai 380 makes motherhood the wife's disqualification and Kainnilai 48 makes age, so the pair shows the wife's body at two stages of marriage offered in the same mocking way as the reason the man has gone elsewhere."},{"a":"ki:50","b":"ku:25","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 25: no one was there but the thief and a heron watching for aaral fish on the day he took her, so what can she do if he lies. Kainnilai 50: she will tell neither him nor the anril, only the stork of the grove that saw his hard oath.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The heron that in Kuruntokai only proves how alone the heroine was becomes, in the venba, the one hearer she trusts with the broken oath, so the bird witness of kalavu moves from a sign of her helplessness to the only court left to her."},{"a":"ki:50","b":"ae:62","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainthinai Ezhupathu 62: does the heron of the grove not know his cruelty, though it saw him turn graceless? Kainnilai 50: the stork of the grove, which saw his harsh oath, is the one she bids listen.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The two shore venbas value the same bird witness in opposite ways, reproached for seeing and not knowing once the oath is broken, confided in because it saw while she still hopes the oath can be held, so the witness's worth follows the stage of her trust."},{"a":"ki:51","b":"nt:167","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 167: the friend refuses the bard who came with the shore lord's message (துறைவன் தூதொடும் வந்த ... பணி மொழி). Kainnilai 51 uses the same words for a bird (தூதொடு வந்த பணிமொழிப் புள்ளே) and tells it to fly off.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The formula that names the bard refused at the door is given in the venba to a bird, showing the short form compressing the social scene of வாயில் மறுத்தல் into a creature of the shore while keeping the old wording.","gr":2},{"a":"ki:52","b":"a:20","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 20: the friend, with the man listening unseen, says that on the town women's talk of a chariot on the dunes the mother has forbidden their shore play (அன்னை கடிகொண்டனளே). Kainnilai 52: the mother has kept the girl in (அன்னையும் இல்கடிந்தாள்), and the speaker asks whether to tell the man of the blame.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The long poem lets the man overhear the mother's ban, while the venba weighs telling him outright, so the pair shows the ban as the friend's lever for marriage, worked by indirection in one and nearly by open message in the other.","gr":2},{"a":"ki:54","b":"ku:320","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 320: on the dune of drying fish and prawns she has not even one day's laughter with the shore lord to be blamed for, yet the town gossips. Kainnilai 54: on the same drying-ground he lied to her and enjoyed her beauty. On a plain reading one heroine denies what the other confesses.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"The prawn-drying ground serves one heroine as the scene of an innocence the town will not credit and another as the scene of her seduction, so the stock place of neythal kalavu could carry either a defence before the town or an accusation of the lover.","gr":4},{"a":"ki:56","b":"a:20","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 20: the friend recalls chasing birds from her father's drying fish (எந்தை தந்த கொழுமீன் உணங்கல் படுபுள் ஓப்பி) among the shore pastimes now forbidden. Kainnilai 56: while driving the birds from the fish her kin brought (எமர் தந்த ... வன்மீன் கவரும்புள் ஓப்பின்), the shore lord took her beauty.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same chore, keeping birds off the family's catch, is innocent play in the Akananuru list and the very hour of the theft in the venba, which explains why the mother's ban in the long poem falls on just those shore tasks."},{"a":"ki:59","b":"nt:138","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 138: the lord of the cool ford where the white heron lays its eggs (வெண் குருகு ஈனும் தண்ணம் துறைவன்) is now known to the old town. Kainnilai 59: the lord of the cool ford where the heron lays its eggs in the thaazhai (குருகுஈனும் தண்ணந் துறைவனை) is now known to the mother, who shuts the door.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epithet of the nesting heron frames in both poems the moment the secret becomes known, to the town in one and the mother in the other, which supports reading the breeding heron as a call for the man to set up house now that kalavu is over."},{"a":"ki:59","b":"a:315","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 315: the mother who saw the girl grow and suspected, yet did not shut her in (செறியேன்), mourns her elopement. Kainnilai 59: the mother, seeing the young man come through the gap, at once blocks it and shuts the door.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The confinement one mother blames herself for omitting is done at first sight by the other, and the friend reports it as the pressure that forces marriage, so the pair shows the mother's guard as the hinge between the two endings of kalavu, marriage and flight."},{"a":"ki:55","b":"k:1157","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1157: do the bangles slipping from her wrist not already proclaim that the shore lord has left? Kainnilai 55: if the shore lord leaves without embracing her, the bangles will not stay on either hand.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The venba states as a condition what the couplet packs into a past tense, so it supports reading the Kural's 'has left' as anticipatory: the bangles slip at the mere prospect of the shore lord going."},{"a":"ki:58","b":"k:1262","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1262: if she forgets him today her beauty will leave and her bangles slip. Kainnilai 58: if she sits thinking of him, her bangles will not hold. On a plain reading forgetting ruins her in one and remembering in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes memory the thing that keeps her body whole and the venba makes memory the thing that wastes it, so together they show the bangle as a sign the two traditions could hang on either side of remembering the absent man."},{"a":"ki:60","b":"ai:200","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 200: the lord of the golden chariot has come, her bangles fit again and they can laugh at the pallor. Kainnilai 60: her golden pallor is gone, for here is the chariot of the man who saw the backs of fleeing kings, nearing Koodal.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both mark the end of pallor by the chariot's arrival, but the venba arms the returning lover with a victor's epithet and the Pandya's Korkai and Koodal, showing a late akam poem borrowing puram glory to make homecoming a triumph."},{"a":"kv:2","b":"pn:369","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 369 (Paranar, thurai மறக்களவழி) builds the whole battle as a season of rain and tillage, elephants as clouds and arrows as rain on a wet field (ஈரச் செறுவயின்), ending in a request for elephants; Kalavazhi Narpathu 2 gives one farming image, blood running through burst drums like a tank's sluices after the monsoon (கார்ப்பெயல் பெய்தபின்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show how the kalavazhi theme changed form: Paranar's single sustained allegory of the farm year, made to earn a gift, becomes in Poigaiyar one quick water-and-field simile per stanza with no request, so the farming root of the genre's name survives only as a store of images."},{"a":"kv:8","b":"pn:19","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 19 (lines 8 to 9) likens arrows lodged in a wounded elephant to a flock of sparrows settled on a hill (குன்றத்து இறுத்த குரீஇஇனம் போல); Kalavazhi Narpathu 8 says elephants hidden by arrows look like countless hills with flocks of sparrows (எண்ணருங் குன்றிற் குரீஇயினம்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The near-identical image shows a stock battle simile passing from a Pandya victory poem to a Chola one, and the frames show it carries no fixed feeling: in Purananuru 19 it leads into mothers weeping for sons and Death fleeing in shame, while in Poigaiyar it is pure spectacle of the victor's field.","gr":3},{"a":"kv:10","b":"k:597","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 597 holds up the elephant that plants its feet under a sheaf of arrows (புதையம்பிற் பட்டுப்பா டூன்றுங் களிறு) as the model of a mind that does not give way; Kalavazhi Narpathu 10 shows elephants pierced everywhere by arrows that cannot move and sink wearily where they stand (செல்கலா தொல்கி யுயங்கும்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Seen from the battlefield, the Kural's emblem of steadfastness is ambiguous: the same arrow-struck elephant that stands its ground is, for the victor's poet, a beast too spent to move, so the maxim's courage depends on reading stillness as resolve rather than as defeat."},{"a":"kv:11","b":"pn:62","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 62 describes a battle in which both kings die, their drums are abandoned (முரசு ஒழிந்தனவே) and no one is left to claim the field (களங்கொளற்கு உரியோர் இன்றி); Kalavazhi Narpathu 11 describes the abandoned drums (ஒழிமுரசம்) of those whose strength failed, kicked by blinded elephants, on the field Sengan won.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The abandoned drum is the same sign in both, but whose drum it is decides what it means: when only the loser's drum is dropped it proves a victor and the field becomes his song, and when both drums fall, as in Purananuru 62, the field has no owner and the poet can only ask what the war was for."},{"a":"kv:14","b":"pn:373","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 373 (Kovur Kizhar, thurai மறக்களவழி) praises the Chola Killivalavan who made the Kongu turn their backs (கொங்கு புறம் பெற்ற) and ends among vultures on the fearful field, asking a gift; Kalavazhi Narpathu 14 names the Kongar as the enemy the lord of the river country killed (கொங்கரை அட்ட களத்து).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show that a Chola victory over the Kongu was already sung as a field-of-battle poem in the Purananuru, with the bard asking for his share; Poigaiyar keeps the enemy and the field but lets the stanza end on the field itself, turning a petition into a celebration complete without the bard's request."},{"a":"kv:16","b":"k:599","type":"develops","note":"Kural 599, in the chapter on energy (ஊக்கமுடைமை), says the elephant, for all its bulk and sharp tusks, fears a tiger's attack; Kalavazhi Narpathu 16 shows horses, urged on (ஊக்கி) by picked warriors, leaping onto the elephants' brows like tigers springing up a hill.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The battle verse turns the Kural's animal fable into tactics: the tiger's assault on the elephant is what cavalry does to war elephants, so the couplet's claim that drive overcomes size had a familiar picture on the field, and Poigaiyar's simile names the driving force with the Kural's own word.","gr":2},{"a":"kv:36","b":"a:44","type":"same_image","note":"Kalavazhi 36 sings the day the Kaveri lord took Kalumalam (கழுமலங் கொண்டநாள்), the enemy umbrellas overturned like mushrooms; Akananuru 44, a hero hurrying home after his king's war, names the Chola Senni who took Kalumalam and captured Kanaiyan (கணையன் அகப்படக் கழுமலம் தந்த) and likens the heroine to that king's rich town Kudavayil.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kalavazhi leaves the loser at Kalumalam unnamed and shows only the carnage, while the akam poet names the captive Kanaiyan and spends the victory as a mark of the Chola's prosperity, so together they show one battle remembered both as a field of dead and as a byword for a well-kept land.","gr":2},{"a":"kv:39","b":"pn:74","type":"tension","note":"Kalavazhi 39 ends on the field where the Chola killed the Vanji king (வஞ்சிக்கோ), his white umbrella torn down and full of flesh; Purananuru 74 is a Chera king's own poem from captivity, held like a chained dog (தொடர்ப்படு ஞமலியின்) and begging water. The two are tied by the tradition that the Chera defeated in Kalavazhi was Kanaikkal Irumporai, the poet of Purananuru 74; neither text in our corpus states it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The victor's song counts the Chera's defeat as the end of the matter, but the defeated king's poem says that being taken alive and fed by strangers is worse than the sword, so on that reading the field the Kalavazhi praises produced a fate its own frame cannot see."},{"a":"kv:40","b":"pn:19","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 19 has the cut-trunked elephants rolling on the ground like ploughs (நாஞ்சில் ஒப்ப நிலமிசைப் புரள) in a field where old mothers weep for their sons; Kalavazhi 40 has all the elephants fall as if the god of the silver plough (வெள்ளிவெண் ணாஞ்சிலான்) were ploughing the world.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Purananuru simile sits beside the mothers' grief, while the Kalavazhi lifts the same plough image to a god's tillage and drops the mourners, showing how the battlefield-as-ploughing figure grew into a frame of pure praise once the kalavazhi became a genre of its own."},{"a":"kv:29","b":"pn:25","type":"tension","note":"Purananuru 25 praises Chezhiyan because his spear stopped short when he saw the enemy's widows beat their breasts and cut their hair; Kalavazhi 29 shows the enemy's bereaved kin wailing in all four directions and closes on the Chola's field.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both poets put the defeated side's mourners in a victory poem, but one makes the king's restraint before them the glory and the other makes their wailing part of the spectacle of the field, so the pair shows two incompatible uses of the same grief in royal praise."},{"a":"kv:37","b":"pn:62","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 62 describes a field where both kings fell, the umbrellas shook, the famous drums were lost and no one was left to claim the field (களங்கொளற்கு உரியோர் இன்றி); Kalavazhi 37 has the kings' corpses pour out blood that carries off the drums and the elephants, and the field belongs to the lord of the watered land.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same lost drums and dead kings make a kalavazhi only because one king survives to own the field; Purananuru 62 shows the case where nobody does, and the poet must turn the praise to the heavenly guests, which marks the victor as the genre's precondition."},{"a":"kv:35","b":"k:773","type":"tension","note":"Kural 773 says fierceness is great manhood, but help given when misfortune befalls the foe is its cutting edge; Kalavazhi 35 ends with the enemy king falling with his royal elephant, struck like a lion on a thunder-struck mountain, and goes no further.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural locates the height of valour in the moment after the foe falls, and the kalavazhi, built to stop at the field of killing, has no place for that moment, so the couplet names exactly what the battle-song leaves out."},{"a":"kv:23","b":"k:774","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 774 praises the warrior who sends his spear off in an elephant and laughs as he plucks another from his own body; in Kalavazhi 23 the strong men (வயவர்) hurl and split the elephants' foreheads, and the field is Chempiyan's.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural keeps the spear-thrower's own pride and wound in view, while the battle-song shows the same feat only through the speared elephants and credits it to the king, so the pair shows the warrior's deed moving from his ethic into his lord's fame."},{"a":"kn:1","b":"pp:5:1-23","type":"same_image","note":"Mullaippattu 1-6 likens the rain-cloud rising from the sea to Mal, who grew to measure the world; Kar Narpathu 1 likens the rainbow to the garland on the chest of Mal, coloured like the warring sea.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both mullai poems open their rains with Mal, the god of the mullai land, so the land's god is the first image of its season; the later venba moves him from the rising cloud to the rainbow, making the sky his adorned chest rather than his growing body.","gr":4},{"a":"kn:1","b":"a:85","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 85: the friend tells the heroine not to weep, since the rain now spreading is the season he named when he assured them 'வருதும் யாம்'. Kar Narpathu 1 quotes the same promise, வருதும், and asks 'will he not come?'","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The promise is the same word in both, which shows the friend's argument as a fixed form, quote his 'we shall come' and point to the rain, with the older poem supplying the peacock calling its mate as the proof the four-line verse leaves out.","gr":2},{"a":"kn:2","b":"pp:5:1-23","type":"develops","note":"In Mullaippattu the old women seeking an omen hear a herd girl tell the calves 'இன்னே வருகுவர் தாயர்' and take it as a sign; in Kar Narpathu 2 the lightning speaks his message, 'இன்னே வருவர் நமர்'.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same formula of reassurance, 'they will come at once', is an overheard chance word in the idyll's omen rite and the sky's own message in the venba, so the later poem turns the ritual of listening for a sign into the season speaking for the absent man.","gr":4},{"a":"kn:4","b":"a:4","type":"develops","note":"Both carry the same thurai, the friend showing the season to reassure the heroine, with the konrai in bloom; Akananuru 4 ends with his chariot in sight, Kar Narpathu 4 with the season as medicine for her pallor.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set together they give two endings to one speech: the long poem can produce the man himself on the horizon, while the four-line venba has to stop at the season and so makes the season itself the cure.","gr":3},{"a":"kn:4","b":"k:1265","type":"tension","note":"Kar Narpathu 4: the season is the medicine for her pallor. Kural 1265: the pallor of her soft shoulders will leave only after she has seen her husband with full eyes; on a plain reading the two disagree about what cures pasalai.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The friend claims the sign of his return heals the pallor and the Kural's woman holds that only the sight of him will, so the couplet reads as the heroine's answer to the whole method of the Kar Narpathu's friend.","gr":4},{"a":"kn:5","b":"a:111","type":"qualifies","note":"Kar Narpathu 5: he left fearing the words of those who scorn. Akananuru 111: the friend says he will come back, ashamed of the mocking words of hostile men (ஏஎச்சொல் நாணி).","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Taken together, other men's contempt both sends the hero away for wealth and, in the friend's argument, drives him home again, so shame works on him at both ends of the journey.","gr":4},{"a":"kn:7","b":"k:1263","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1263: she lives on, longing for the return of the man who went desiring victory with his resolve as his only companion. Kar Narpathu 7: the friend says he went to give to seekers and crush foes, and the rain will bring him back.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet's woman treats his heroic aim as the rival that left her behind, while the friend names the same aim, giving and conquest, as a reason to trust he will return in the rains: one motive is a grievance in the Kural and a guarantee in the venba.","gr":7},{"a":"kn:8","b":"kn:7","type":"develops","note":"Verse 7 gives the purpose of his journey as giving to seekers and destroying foes; verse 8 gives it as lasting fame in this earthly world.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence, the friend's account of his motive moves from duties toward others to fame for himself while her proof stays the same rain-season sign, so the series varies the cause of absence and holds the cure fixed.","gr":4},{"a":"kn:9","b":"a:74","type":"qualifies","note":"Kar Narpathu 9: the flowers speak many sweet words announcing his return. Akananuru 74: the heroine tells the friend she could hold firm on her many sweet words of reassurance (வன்புறை இன்சொல் நன்பல) were it not for the herders' flute at evening.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Akananuru 74 gives the heroine's answer that the Kar Narpathu never records: sweet words of comfort hold her only until the evening flute, so the reassurance the venbas repeat is shown elsewhere to fail at its own hour.","gr":6},{"a":"kn:10","b":"a:134","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 134: the returning hero tells the charioteer that if she hears the sound of the swift-horsed chariot (கடுமான் தேர்ஒலி) there may be union at midnight. Kar Narpathu 10: the friend hears the swift-horsed chariot coming like a forest stream and says the heroine will bloom.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The chariot's sound is the hinge of the reunion from both sides: the hero hopes she will hear it and the friend reports that it is heard and what it does to the heroine's body, in the same phrase கடுமான் தேர்.","gr":2},{"a":"kn:11","b":"a:154","type":"same_image","note":"Both liken the opening kodal cluster to a snake's raised hood in the rain-fed woodland; in Akananuru 154 the returning man sees it from the chariot, in Kar Narpathu 11 the friend points to it as the sign of his quick return.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same flower-image is seen by the man on the road home and offered by the friend at home as a promise, so the shared stock of mullai images lets the two halves of a separation read the same sign in the same season.","gr":3},{"a":"kn:12","b":"pp:5:1-23","type":"same_image","note":"Kar Narpathu 12: dark clouds like oiled elephants rise on the right every day, a sight beyond doubt that he will come. Mullaippattu 4-5: the cloud drinks the cold sea and rises rightward (வலனேர்பு), just before the old women go out to seek an omen.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The idyll sets the rightward-rising cloud beside a separate omen rite, while the venba makes the cloud itself the omen 'beyond doubt', which shows the rising on the right read as a good sign for the absent man's return.","gr":2},{"a":"kn:19","b":"a:9","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 9 ends with the returning man asking whether his heart, which went ahead longing for her soft shoulders, has reached her; Kar Narpathu 19 has the man say his heart has gone the long road wanting her as companion.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both returning heroes speak of a heart that outruns the chariot, so the figure belongs to the set speech of return; the long poem follows the heart into the house and to her touch, while the venba stops at its setting out.","gr":3},{"a":"kn:20","b":"a:44","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 44: the king has finished his work, the enemy has paid tribute, and the man bids the charioteer drive ahead to sleep on his wife's breast. Kar Narpathu 20: the glorious king's work is done, the roads are pleasant, and the rain moves like an army.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make the king's finished war the condition of the husband's return, and the pair shows the venba compressing the long poem's catalogue of kings and battles into one simile in which the rain-cloud marches like the armies he leaves.","gr":2},{"a":"kn:20","b":"k:1268","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1268: let the king fight and win; let us join the house and keep the evening feast. Kar Narpathu 20: the king's work is finished, so the roads home are sweet.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet is the wish spoken while the war goes on and the venba is the same man once it is granted, so read together they give the before and after of one situation, the hero held by the king's war until it ends.","gr":4},{"a":"kn:21","b":"ku:240","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 240: the waiting woman sees mullai buds like a wild cat's teeth (வெருக்குப்பல் லுருவின் முல்லை) crowding the bush as the north wind comes and his hill sinks at evening. Kar Narpathu 21: the returning man sees the mullai buds pointed like the teeth of his girl.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same bud-and-teeth simile becomes a predator's teeth for the waiting woman and the beloved's teeth for the returning man, so the mullai bud's meaning in these poems is set by which side of the separation is looking, not by the flower."},{"a":"kn:23","b":"kn:38","type":"qualifies","note":"Both close with the same line, ஒண்டொடி யூடுநிலை. In 23 the friend shows the season to reassure; in 38, by the colophon, she concedes that he has lied.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One closing question about sulking serves first to dismiss the heroine's grievance (the season has come, so he will) and then to grant it (the season has come and he has not), so the series uses a formula line whose force is fixed only by the lines before it."},{"a":"kn:24","b":"a:47","type":"tension","note":"Akananuru 47: if we have finished the work here, rise quickly, heart (வினைஇவண் முடித்தனம் ஆயின்). Kar Narpathu 24: rise, heart, leaving all the work lying (எல்லா வினையுங் கிடப்ப). On a plain reading they disagree on whether the task must be done before he turns home.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The older poem holds the man's return behind the finishing of his task, while the venba's man drops the task once the rain and the memory of her hair reach him, so the same address to the heart sets duty and love in two different orders."},{"a":"kn:26","b":"a:235","type":"tension","note":"Both see the thonri open like a lamp in the rains: in Akananuru 235 (சுடர்கொள் அகலின்) the heroine asks whether he has forgotten them; in Kar Narpathu 26 (கார்த்திகை ... விளக்கிற்) the friend says the rain has come with his message.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The lamp-like thonri is evidence that he has forgotten for the heroine and evidence that he has sent word for the friend, so the one sign of the promised season can be read against him or for him depending on who speaks.","gr":2},{"a":"kn:27","b":"k:1187","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1187: she turned aside a little from his embrace and the pallor was on her thick enough to scoop up. Kar Narpathu 27: if we sulk that he left without a thought for us, pallor spreads over her in bed (பள்ளியுட் பாயும் பசப்பு).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's hyperbole, that the least turning away from him in bed brings pallor, becomes in the friend's mouth a practical warning against sulking at an absent man, so the venba shows the Kural's figure used as an argument for bearing up."},{"a":"kn:32","b":"k:231","type":"develops","note":"Kural 231: to give and live so that renown arises; there is no other gain for a life. Kar Narpathu 32: bees glad as the hearts of wealthy men who crave undying fame (கெடாஅப் புகழ்வேட்கைச் செல்வர் மனம்போற்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states the craving for renown through giving as the one gain of life, and the venba treats that craving as so familiar that a returning lover can measure a bee's gladness by it, which shows the puram ideal already turned into common stock for akam imagery."},{"a":"kn:36","b":"ai:447","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 447: may the king's work be released, and then we shall see her bright brow, when the bee opens the red thalavu bud shaped like a kingfisher's beak (சிரல்வாய்ச் செம்முகை). Kar Narpathu 36: the man returning from the work sees the thalavam buds, lovely as kingfishers' beaks (சிரல்வாய் வனப்பின), opened in rows.","grade":"sound","better":"same_image","te":"The same thalavu bud is the hour hoped for in the man's prayer while the king's work holds him and the sight on the road once he is released, so read together the bud marks the two ends of a single wait."},{"a":"kn:37","b":"a:304","type":"qualifies","note":"Kar Narpathu 37: will our man, who took up the king's hard work, not come even in the season of great rains? Akananuru 304: in the king's war camp as the first rains fall, the man fears she will think him unkind, not knowing his pain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend's confidence rests on the season alone, and the Akananuru shows that season from inside the king's camp, where the man is still held and can only dread her reproach, so the king's work she counts to his credit is the very thing keeping him away."},{"a":"kn:38","b":"a:85","type":"tension","note":"Akananuru 85: the friend says the cool rains, when the elephant feeds his young mate and calf with bamboo shoots, are the season he promised, so do not weep. Kar Narpathu 38: the bull elephant plays with his mate in the cool season, yet he has not come, and the colophon says the friend tells her he lied.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The elephant pair in the rains is the friend's proof of his promise in one poem and her proof of his breach in the other, so the creatures' act carries the same sense in both while the verdict on the man turns on whether he has come."},{"a":"kn:39","b":"nt:267","type":"same_image","note":"Natrinai 267: crabs of the sand dune with eyes like the nochchi's dark buds (நொச்சி மா அரும்பு அன்ன கண்ண). Kar Narpathu 39: nochchi buds opened like crabs' eyes (அலவன்க ணேய்ப்ப).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The simile runs in opposite directions: the neythal poem makes its shore crab the subject and borrows the nochchi as image, while the mullai venba makes its nochchi the subject and borrows the crab, so each poet takes the tenor from the poem's own land."},{"a":"kn:40","b":"kn:25","type":"develops","note":"Both turn on the same words, செய்குறி வாரா ரவரென்று: in 25 the friend says that thought thickened the heroine's pallor; in 40 she says the darkening sky has become the medicine for the woman who grieved at it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The series states one despairing thought twice, once as the cause of pallor in the friend's confession of her own distress and once as the sickness the sky now cures, so her speeches of despair and of comfort rest on the same premise and differ only in how they read the sky."},{"a":"tn:1","b":"ti:8","type":"develops","note":"Thinaimalai 1 gives the man's question to the girls about a wounded beast (ஏமரை); Thinaimozhi 8 gives the friend's answer that no wounded tusker came this way.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Put together the two short books make one exchange, and the question's lavish praise of the girls' faces next to the friend's flat denial shows each side treating the wounded animal as a cover: he courts through it, and she refuses through it."},{"a":"tn:9","b":"tn:1","type":"develops","note":"In 1 the man asks after the arrow-struck deer; in 9 he tells his companion that the asking after a beast was a ruse (வஞ்சமே) and that he was the one taken.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The book places the pretext and the confession apart, so 9 tells the reader how to read 1: the hunter's question was never about game, and the hunter who set out to deceive came back captured."},{"a":"tn:9","b":"aa:14","type":"develops","note":"Ainthinai 14 has the friend say the man came 'like those who ask after a beast' (மாவினவு வார்போல) with something else to ask; Thinaimalai 9 has the man himself call his asking after a beast deceit.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The ruse is seen through from both sides, the friend naming it as a likeness and the hero confessing it as a trick, which shows the beast question as a convention both parties knew and played along with rather than a deception that worked.","gr":4},{"a":"tn:12","b":"a:388","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 388 the girl, in private, mocks the diviner whose god is said to have caused her sickness; in Thinaimalai 12 the friend tells the household to send the velan away, free the kid and recognise the man who saved her from the flood.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The long poem stops at the girl's private scorn for the rite, and the short one shows the next move, so the pair shows mockery of the velan turning into the friend's disclosure to the family, where dismissing the diviner and naming the rescuer are one act.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:12","b":"tn:14","type":"develops","note":"12 says the girl will give her shoulders only to the man who swam the flood and set her on the bank; 14 says no one but the man who drove off the elephant shall touch her friend's shoulders. Both use the same 'except him, no one' formula.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same exclusive formula is fitted to two different rescues, flood and elephant, which shows that the rescue is an interchangeable warrant and that what the friend really presents to the household is the girl's choice, already made."},{"a":"tn:2","b":"tn:14","type":"develops","note":"2 tells of the man who dressed the girl's hair with flowers and drove off the musth elephant from the platform; 14 tells of the elephant driven off and concludes that no one but him shall touch her shoulders.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in order, 2 lays out the evidence (the flowers in her hair, the rescue) and 14 draws the verdict, so the disclosure to the household is built like a case: first the facts, then the right those facts give him.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:16","b":"k:1131","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1131 says that for those who suffer in love there is no safeguard and strength but the palmyra horse; in Thinaimalai 16 the man stands on the madal in the street and will not leave until she grants him her breast.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet names the madal as the lover's only strength in general terms, and the quatrain shows what that strength is in use: a public siege in the street with a single stated condition and no second way, so the 'safeguard' is pressure on her household, not comfort for him.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:8","b":"k:1085","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1085 has the man at first sight find in her look Death, an eye and a doe; Thinaimalai 8 has the man at the fields find Kaman's arrows, five twice over, in the girl's two eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both men read a killing power in the first look, but the Kural names it Death while the field poem gives it to the love god and counts the arrows, which shows the same wound drawn once as mortal danger and once as a wound meant to be healed in union."},{"a":"tn:15","b":"nt:300","type":"qualifies","note":"In Natrinai 300 a suitor sets a great chariot at the gate as the price of the girl's bangles (சிறு வளை விலை); in Thinaimalai 15 the friend says no price, not the sea-girt world, could buy the girl, and she must be given to the elders who come for her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Natrinai shows a bride price actually offered, and Thinaimalai answers the practice: by declaring her beyond any price the friend moves the marriage from purchase to gift, so the only claim the household should honour is the formal coming of the suitor's elders.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:7","b":"a:18","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 18 says that if the man suffers harm on the night ford even once, her friend will not live after it, and asks him to come by day; Thinaimalai 7 says that if he comes by night the daughter of the hill lord will not live.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Thinaimalai's flat 'she will not live' is explained by Akananuru 18: her life hangs on his safety on the path, so forbidding the night visit protects her through him, and the long poem's offer of a daytime meeting shows what the short ban leaves for him to infer."},{"a":"tn:5","b":"ti:2","type":"develops","note":"Thinaimozhi 2 says the guarding of the millet fields has ended and asks whether his coming will now be hard; Thinaimalai 5 says the millet has ripened like the fruit of past deeds and that this parts them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Thinaimozhi gives the harvest as a fact of the field, and Thinaimalai frames the same harvest as the ripening of deeds into their fruit, which turns the end of the meetings from a farming date into something like fate, and so adds weight to the push for marriage."},{"a":"tn:17","b":"k:1115","type":"develops","note":"Both men fear the girl's waist will break: the Kural through the flowers' stems, Thinaimalai 17 if she walks even a little.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural keeps the snapping waist a joke of praise, a mock funeral drum, while Thinaimalai 17 lets the same fear spill into the man's own body, whose heart sways wherever she sways, so the conceit of her fragility becomes a measure of his surrender.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:18","b":"a:132","type":"develops","note":"Thinaimalai 18 fears that the harvested millet ends the bond; in Akananuru 132 the millet ears are already cut and the friend tells the man to take the girl away.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Thinaimalai 18 stops at the fear that the stubble field ends the meetings, and Akananuru 132 shows what the friend does with that fear, turning it into a demand to take the girl away, so the harvest is the point at which kalavu must become marriage or flight."},{"a":"tn:22","b":"k:1085","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's man asks whether her look is Death; the man of Thinaimalai 22 says her two eyes shot him as her brothers shoot beasts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the killing look in the abstract, and Thinaimalai 22 grounds it in her people's trade, measuring her two eyes against her brothers' single killing arrow, so the deadly glance of the couplet turns out to be a hunter's weapon of the hills.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:22","b":"nt:13","type":"same_image","note":"Natrinai 13 likens the girl's red-lined eyes to the arrows millet-watchers pull out of a felled beast; Thinaimalai 22 has her eyes as two arrows that have felled the man.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend of Natrinai 13 gives the eyes the hunters' bloodied arrows, and the man of Thinaimalai 22 draws the consequence that he is the beast they brought down, so the two poems together show the eye-as-arrow belonging to a hunting household's daily kill."},{"a":"tn:24","b":"aa:18","type":"qualifies","note":"Both use the formula of the kin who cut and plough (எறிந்து உழுது, எறிந்தெமர் தாம்உழுத); in Ainthinai Aimpathu 18 it is the field they cleared, in Thinaimalai 24 a sign of their hardness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In Ainthinai Aimpathu 18 clearing and ploughing is just the kin's labour whose harvest ends the meetings, but Thinaimalai 24 reads the same labour as a temperament, men who fell fragrance for grain, so the friend can turn the hill family's farming into a reason the girl will not pity the man."},{"a":"tn:25","b":"aa:16","type":"same_image","note":"Both show an elephant that has escaped the curve-striped tiger; in Ainthinai Aimpathu 16 it lies hurt on the hero's night path, in Thinaimalai 25 it is the likeness of the trembling girl.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Ainthinai Aimpathu 16 puts the escaped elephant on the man's path as a danger the sleepless girl imagines, and Thinaimalai 25 makes the escaped elephant the girl herself, still shaking after the danger, so one stock scene can stand either for the night road or for the fear it leaves in her."},{"a":"tn:26","b":"a:228","type":"same_image","note":"In both the fallen vengai flowers look like a tiger: in Akananuru 228 a she-elephant bolts from them on the man's moonlit way home, in Thinaimalai 26 the girl will not survive his night visit.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 228 shows the vengai's false tiger frightening a beast on the path, and Thinaimalai 26 transfers that fright to the waiting girl, which shows that the friend's case against the night visit rests on fear of a likeness rather than on any real tiger.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:27","b":"ti:10","type":"develops","note":"In Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 10 the girl tells her friend that mother, learning of the union, has barred her from the field; in Thinaimalai 27 the friend tells the man that mother saw him and keeps the girl guarded.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same discovery by the mother is grief in the girl's mouth, who wonders whether he will be vexed, and a lever in the friend's, who uses it to tell him to marry now, so the confinement that ends kalavu is also the friend's strongest argument for marriage."},{"a":"tn:28","b":"ti:5","type":"qualifies","note":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 5 warns the man off because the kin carry bows, spears and swift arrows; Thinaimalai 28 warns him through the precious trees the kin cut down to plough.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 5 states the threat as weapons, and Thinaimalai 28 leaves weapons out and lets the felled sandal and agil show what the brothers would do to an intruder, so the threat can be carried by how a family treats its own hill rather than by its arms."},{"a":"tn:29","b":"a:92","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 92 forbids the night and promises the man a day meeting by a cool fall where red glory lilies open; Thinaimalai 29 describes such a place by a forest stream in a glory-lily thicket.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 92 gives the day tryst as the friend's offer in exchange for the dangerous night, and Thinaimalai 29 gives only the place, which shows the daytime meeting ground was a fixed set (stream, glory lilies, low shade) a four-line verse could call up without the bargain around it."},{"a":"tn:31","b":"tn:20","type":"qualifies","note":"Both turn on the leaf-garment and the pun on வேங்கை (kino tree and tiger); in 20 the tigers are the girl's brothers and mother and the gift is refused, in 31 the tiger is the man himself and his gift is praised.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Read together, the friend moves the tiger from the girl's family to her lover as she moves from turning the leaf-garment away to pressing the girl toward him, so the same pun marks which side of the courtship holds the danger at each stage."},{"a":"tn:32","b":"k:1085","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1085 finds death, an eye and a doe in the young woman's look; Thinaimalai 32 calls the fish-guard's long eyes a weapon (படைநெடுங்கண்) whose guarding falls on the man.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet lists in the abstract what a first look can be, and the poem supplies the scene that makes 'death' concrete: a girl posted to guard the drying fish whose watch is turned on the man who looks, so the Kural's killing glance is a guard's weapon."},{"a":"tn:32","b":"tn:44","type":"develops","note":"32 is the man's first sight of the girl guarding the drying fish; 44 has the friend name the grove of that same task as the girl's place.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together the two verses show the fish-drying watch doing for the neythal girl what the millet watch does in kurinji: it is where the man first sees her and, later, the daytime meeting place the friend appoints."},{"a":"tn:44","b":"a:80","type":"agrees","note":"Akananuru 80: the friend says they spend the day driving birds from their father's drying fish and bids him come by day to the punnai grove; Thinaimalai 44 names the grove where the girl went to drive birds from the drying fish (புட்கடிவான் புக்க).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The long poem spells out the trade behind the short one: naming the grove where the girl chases birds from the fish is the friend's way of replacing the dangerous night swim with a day meeting, which Thinaimalai 44 implies but never states."},{"a":"tn:35","b":"a:80","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 80 forbids the night swim and offers a day meeting in the punnai grove; Thinaimalai 35 forbids the night and warns that a day visit brings gossip.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside Akananuru 80, where the day meeting is the friend's remedy for the night's danger, Thinaimalai 35 shows a later stage in which the day too is closed by gossip, so that the only road left is marriage."},{"a":"tn:36","b":"ai:151","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a shore bird that takes something else for a heron's chick: in Ainkurunuru 151 a stork treads a neythal flower going to see it; in Thinaimalai 36 a heron feeds a screwpine bud and shades it with its wings.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same mistake of a bird taking a flower for a chick can carry opposite senses: in Ainkurunuru the error bruises the flower, which still gives off scent, like the heroine who stays tender to the man who hurt her, while in Thinaimalai it becomes devoted care held up to shame a man whose visits do not stay."},{"a":"tn:37","b":"ku:25","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 25: no one but the man was there, only a heron watching for fish, on the day he took her; Thinaimalai 37: the tide knew it that day (ஓதநீர் அன்றறியும்), so marry her before the common square.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai's girl fears that a union witnessed only by a shore creature leaves her helpless if he lies, and Thinaimalai's friend answers that fear: a witness that cannot speak is why the union must be made public in the square."},{"a":"tn:38","b":"ku:163","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 163 asks the sea who has afflicted it (யாரணங் குற்றனை) since she hears it even at midnight; Thinaimalai 38 asks the sleepless sea who did this to it, 'like me'.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai leaves the girl's own state unsaid behind her question to the sea, while Thinaimalai 38 names it, the breast of the shore lord and a sleeplessness 'like mine', so the pair shows the later poet making explicit the projection the older poem left to the hearer."},{"a":"tn:40","b":"a:380","type":"same_image","note":"In Akananuru 380 the man shows the girls a crab carrying a naaval fruit to its mate and says 'this is a fine way'; in Thinaimalai 40 the girl watches a crab playing with its mate after the man has left.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The crab with its mate is the shore lover's own emblem of devotion, used by the man in Akananuru 380 to court the girl; Thinaimalai 40 shows the emblem turned against him, watched by the girl alone after he dismissed her ache and went."},{"a":"tn:41","b":"k:1147","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1147: the sickness grows with the town's gossip as manure and the mother's words as water; Thinaimalai 41: the heart melts day after day while the talk swells, like a storm cloud pouring.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the gossip the food of the love-sickness, and Thinaimalai 41 makes the two one weather, the swelling talk and the melting heart pouring together like a cloud, which shows the girl suffering the growth the couplet describes as a cause."},{"a":"tn:46","b":"nt:287","type":"qualifies","note":"In Natrinai 287 the girl says he came unafraid of the dread crocodile of the backwater; in Thinaimalai 46 the friend bars him from the night when the striking shark circles.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The night beast of the backwater counts for the girl in Natrinai 287 as the proof of his love and for the friend in Thinaimalai 46 as the reason to stop him and ask for marriage, so the same danger is read by each speaker to serve her own aim."},{"a":"tn:47","b":"k:1081","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1081: the man cannot tell whether she is a goddess (அணங்கு), a peahen or a woman; Thinaimalai 47: she is a beauty that strikes like a goddess (ஏரணங்கு) those who look without thinking.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's man hesitates over what she is, while the Thinaimalai speaker has settled it and turns to blame, saying her kin have let such a danger loose unguarded, so the pair shows the first-sight wonder of the couplet hardening into a lover's complaint."},{"a":"tn:48","b":"tn:49","type":"develops","note":"48 describes the pearl-lit village at nightfall with no hearer; 49 addresses the shore lord with the same pearls dispelling the midnight dark and asks where he will come.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Read together, the bare description of 48 gains its speech act from 49: the pearl-light that seems praise of the shore's riches is the friend's ground for refusing the night tryst."},{"a":"tn:49","b":"a:80","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 80: the friend says he has come by night across the dark backwater of crocodiles and sharks and asks him to come by day to the grove where they guard drying fish. Thinaimalai 49: no dark at all to cross, since the pearls sweep away the midnight.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two friends refuse the same night visit for opposite reasons, the older one because the dark hides beasts and the venba because the shore has no dark to hide him, so the convention needs only the refusal and lets the reason be turned inside out."},{"a":"tn:50","b":"tn:52","type":"develops","note":"50 sets the hero's stealthy coming, like sleep and without a tryst, against the proper way; 52, with the same shore-army image, names the proper way: choose a day and marry.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The vague நெறியால் of 50 is spelled out in 52 as நாள் ஆய்ந்து வரைதல், so the sequence moves from forbidding the secret visit to prescribing the rite, with the marshalled shore as the model of order in both."},{"a":"tn:52","b":"tn:53","type":"qualifies","note":"52: the shore is like a king who rises without haste, so fix a day. 53: the shore is like a king who has risen and will not settle, so come before our kin move.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"One image of the shore as a royal army is given opposite tempos, unhurried and restless, to argue first for deliberation and then for urgency, which shows the poet using the image to set the pace of the plea rather than to carry a fixed sense.","gr":3},{"a":"tn:52","b":"pn:42","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 42: the Chola's army roars like the sea. Thinaimalai 52: the sea, with waves for mahouts and boats for elephants, is like a king's force rising.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The puram simile is run backwards in the neythal venba: where the war poem makes an army sound like the sea, the love poem makes the sea a mustered army, so that the shore lord is held to a king's measured conduct and the wedding becomes his orderly campaign."},{"a":"tn:51","b":"nt:45","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 45: the friend tells the rich man's son that they are fishermen's daughters who drive birds from drying shark and smell of fish (புலவு நாறுதும்), so their life is no match for his. Thinaimalai 51: we watch and sell the fish on the sand that reeks of it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The venba keeps only the smell and the trade and drops the explicit 'go away, we are not your equals' of the Natrinai, which shows how far the later poet could rely on the reader supplying the refusal from the fish-smell alone."},{"a":"tn:51","b":"a:80","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 80: the friend tells the shore lord that they guard their father's drying fish from birds in the punnai grove and asks him to come there by day. Thinaimalai 51: we watch and sell the fish in the grove.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same chore that in Natrinai 45 marks the girls as beneath the suitor is in Akananuru 80 the cover for a daytime meeting, so the venba's bare picture of fish-watching could be either a rebuff or an invitation, which is why its sense stays open."},{"a":"tn:54","b":"a:86","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 86: the husband recalls the wedding held at a bright dawn when the moon stood free of the evil planet on a faultless day. Thinaimalai 54: the friend tells the hero to call the diviners and choose a day.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The diviners' day that the friend demands in the venba is the day the older poem remembers by the moon's freedom from the planet, so the pair shows the omen-reading as the hinge that turns the secret tryst into the public rite."},{"a":"tn:56","b":"nt:303","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 303: the heroine lies sleepless at each cry of the anril joined with its mate in the palmyra of the common ground. Thinaimalai 56: the friend tells the shore lord that the anril calls from the tall palmyras of their front yard.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Natrinai 303 shows what the anril's cry does to the heroine at midnight, so when the friend in the venba points the hero to the anril in their own yard she is telling him, without saying so, what the girl hears each night he is away."},{"a":"tn:58","b":"kl:124","type":"same_image","note":"Kalittokai 124: the white waves ring the white dune like the blue garment worn by the milk-white elder brother of the god who measured the world. Thinaimalai 58: the dark sea and the white sand lie together like Mayavan and his elder brother.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both neythal poets read the shore's dark and white through the two divine brothers, but Kalittokai puts both colours on the one white brother in his blue cloth, while the venba splits them between the two brothers, so the sea and the sand become kin rather than body and garment."},{"a":"tn:59","b":"ku:217","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 217: the friend tells the hill lord that meeting by day is possible while they guard the millet, but she fears his coming by night. Thinaimalai 59: by day the gossip is great, and by night the dangers are as great.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kuruntokai friend still leaves the day open and fears only the night, while the venba's friend closes both, so the pair shows the second step of the convention, where barring day and night together leaves marriage as the only way."},{"a":"tn:60","b":"k:1081","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1081: on first seeing her the man cannot tell whether she is a goddess, a peahen or a woman, and his heart reels (மாலும்). Thinaimalai 60: the hero names the long-eyed girl plucking neythal as the fisherman's daughter who has made him mad (மையல்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's lover reels because he cannot tell what she is, while the venba's lover knows exactly who she is, a fisherman's daughter at the shore, and is mad all the same, so the scene shows the bewilderment surviving the answer to the Kural's question."},{"a":"tn:62","b":"k:101","type":"develops","note":"Kural 101: for help given unasked, earth and heaven together are hardly a return. Thinaimalai 62: no one on this sea-girt earth matches the shore lord who once helped in the salt land; seek his equal in heaven.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The venba takes the Kural's measure of unrequited help, earth and heaven, and applies it to the helper himself: the lover's one service makes him without equal in either world, so the ethical rule of gratitude becomes the friend's argument for the man."},{"a":"tn:64","b":"kl:33","type":"same_image","note":"Kalittokai 33: in spring the silk-cotton flowers like fire and the pungu sheds blossom like puffed rice (எரி உரு உறழ இலவம், பொரி உரு உறழப் புன்கு), and the heroine feels the season mock the lonely. Thinaimalai 64 gives the murukku as scattered fire and the pungu as scattered puffed rice.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pungu as puffed rice is fixed across both poems while the fire moves from silk-cotton to murukku, and the same paired images serve the heroine's complaint in Kalittokai and the friend's rebuke of open grieving in the venba, so the spring catalogue was a shared stock that either speaker could turn."},{"a":"tn:73","b":"a:369","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 369: the foster mother grieves that since the girl left, the parrot will not drink its milk, her playmates do not play and the doll gets no offering. Thinaimalai 73: the mother says the girl has given up her playmates' parrot and the pond's blue lily.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they give both sides of the break: in the Akananuru the home's things go without the girl, in the venba the girl has already let go of the things her beauty was measured by, so the mother's grief covers both what the house lost and what the daughter chose."},{"a":"tn:78","b":"kl:11","type":"qualifies","note":"Kalittokai 11: the heroine reasons that the man will return because he told her the burning forest holds a tusker that lets its mate drink the little muddy water first, a dove that fans its mate and a stag that shades its doe. Thinaimalai 78: an elephant alone finds no water in the spring and falls.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same waterless road gives comfort in Kalittokai 11 and fear in the venba, and the difference is whether the poet gives the elephant a mate: the paired beast turns drought into proof of tenderness, the lone beast only into a measure of danger."},{"a":"tn:80","b":"kl:11","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 11 closes its argument that the man will return with the omens at home: the lizard chirps on the good side and her lovely eye throbs on the left (உண் கண்ணும் ஆடுமால், இடனே). Thinaimalai 80 ends on the same left-eye omen, with the same closing word இடம்.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Kalittokai 11 reaches the throbbing left eye only after a long proof from the man's own words, while the venba rests the promise of return on the omen alone, which shows the left-eye sign was a recognised closing proof that could carry a whole short poem.","gr":4},{"a":"tn:82","b":"ku:135","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 135: the friend tells the heroine not to cry: the man himself said work is life to men and men are life to women of the house, so he will put off his going. Thinaimalai 82: the friend says a man's duty makes him go for wealth, so she should not grieve. On the plain reading the two friends draw opposite conclusions from the same premise.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Both friends begin from the maxim that work belongs to men, but the Kuruntokai adds that men are women's life and concludes he will stay, while the venba stops at the man's duty and concludes he will go, so the outcome turns entirely on whether the maxim's second half is said.","gr":3},{"a":"tn:82","b":"pn:312","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 312 assigns each person a கடன்: bearing the son is the mother's, making him worthy the father's, and killing elephants in battle the young man's. Thinaimalai 82 calls going to make wealth the man's கடன், repeating the word three times.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The venba carries the heroic vocabulary of duty from the battlefield to the trade road: what Purananuru 312 makes the young man's duty in war, the later akam friend makes his duty in wealth-making, and uses it to silence the wife's grief."},{"a":"tn:84","b":"a:375","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 375: the heroine says she would not weep if she knew he passed unharmed the waste where raw youths kill even travellers without ornaments to test their arrows. Thinaimalai 84: the same robbers who shoot travellers with no wealth are the heroine's reason he should not go at all.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The robbers who kill the penniless serve the Akananuru heroine as a fear for a man already on the road and the venba's heroine as an argument against his setting out, so one road-danger supports both the anxiety after departure and the plea before it."},{"a":"tn:85","b":"kl:15","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 15 ends a long plea with the argument that wealth can be made any day, but asks whether it can bring back youth once it has passed (வளமையோ வைகலும் செயல் ஆகும் ... இளமையும் தருவதோ). Thinaimalai 85 builds its whole verse on the same contrast of வளமை and இளமை.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The venba condenses into four lines the closing move of a long Kalittokai paalai, keeping even the rhyme of வளமை and இளமை, which shows that the retort had become a fixed argument of the separation poem that needed no road scene to carry it."},{"a":"tn:85","b":"n:11","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 11: knowing grey hair will come, the wise renounce in childhood, while those who delight in fleeting youth rise later on a staff. Thinaimalai 85: because youth cannot be brought back, the man should stay with his wife rather than chase wealth. On a plain reading the same fact counsels withdrawal in one and staying for love in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"From the one premise that youth does not return, the Naladiyar argues for leaving pleasure early and the paalai friend for keeping it while it lasts, so the impermanence of youth was an argument that served the householder's love as readily as the renouncer's flight."},{"a":"tn:86","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1151: if you are not leaving, tell me; tell your quick return to those who will still be alive. Thinaimalai 86: told that he will surely go, the heroine says to name anyone who could bear it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both meet the news of his going by sending the message elsewhere, to whoever can survive it, and the venba supplies the starving elephant's waste that the couplet leaves out, so the Kural's bare retort is shown to be the stock reply of the paalai heroine to the announcement of departure."},{"a":"tn:89","b":"kl:9","type":"qualifies","note":"Kalittokai 9: the mother asks travelling brahmins whether they saw her daughter and the youth; they say they did, and argue that as sandal, pearl and music serve others than their source, so the daughter belongs to the man she followed. Thinaimalai 89: travellers say they saw the man like the sun and the girl like the moon.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The venba keeps Kalittokai 9's frame of the mother questioning travellers and their 'we saw them' but drops the brahmins' argument that a daughter belongs to her husband, so the consolation shrinks to an image of a well-matched pair, and the mother is left with a report rather than a reason."},{"a":"tn:89","b":"ku:315","type":"same_image","note":"Kuruntokai 315: the heroine says the hill man is like the sun (ஞாயிறு அனையன்) and her shoulders like the nerunji flower. Thinaimalai 89: the man is like the hot sun and the girl like the cool moon.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the Kuruntokai the man as sun makes the woman a nerunji that turns after him, while in the venba she is the moon, a second light of her own, so the same sun-image yields dependence in one poem and parity in the other.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:90","b":"a:195","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 195: the mother of the eloped girl, after picturing the other mother preparing her house for the couple, asks the diviner whether the youth will bring the girl first to her house or take her to his own (எம்மனை முந்துறத் தருமோ? தன்மனை உய்க்குமோ?). Thinaimalai 90 asks the same question in nearly the same words.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The venba keeps the Akananuru mother's question almost word for word while dropping the rival mother's preparations and her own years of nursing, which shows that by the later anthology the question to the diviner had become a fixed turn of the eloped girl's mother that could stand without its reasons."},{"a":"tn:94","b":"k:1230","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1230: thinking of the man whose nature is wealth, in this bewildering evening (மருள்மாலை) my life that would not die is dying. Thinaimalai 94: in the bewildering evening (மருள்மாலை), when the moon pours a false day, my life does not stay within but rides away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The venba takes the couplet's own phrase for the hour and adds the cause the couplet leaves out, a moon that fakes daylight, so the bewilderment of the evening becomes a trick of the sky rather than only a state of her mind."},{"a":"tn:96","b":"k:1221","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1221: you are not evening but வேலை, the time that eats the life of the once-wed. Thinaimalai 96: the dark like Maayon's colour and the milk-white moonlight are like a spear (வேல்) to those without a partner.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's pun on வேலை (hour, and the sense that kills) is made literal in the venba as a spear, and it moves from the evening to the moonlit night, showing a later poet turning the couplet's wordplay into a simile."},{"a":"tn:96","b":"kn:19","type":"same_image","note":"Kar Narpathu 19: the returning man sees the maraa blossom white like the plough-bearing god's colour and his heart runs ahead. Thinaimalai 96: the moonlight is like the pale colour of the discus-bearer's elder brother and falls like a spear on the parted.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Balarama's whiteness is a stock likeness in the Kilkanakku mullai poems, but it serves the hurrying man as the colour of the flowers that call him home and the waiting woman as the colour of a moonlight that wounds her, so the same divine colour signs joy or pain by who sees it.","gr":4},{"a":"tn:97","b":"k:1224","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1224: when the lover is away, evening comes like enemies coming to a killing ground. Thinaimalai 97: the sun sets like the discus Maayavan hurled at his foes' elephant, and she cannot bear the evening.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make evening a killing, but the couplet gives the killers no face while the venba names them through the land's own god, so the waiting woman of the mullai sees her evening through the war myth of the god of her own landscape."},{"a":"tn:98","b":"ku:148","type":"same_image","note":"Kuruntokai 148: konrai buds like the gold coins of rich children's anklets bloom with the kurunthu; if you say this is not the rainy season, is it a dream? Thinaimalai 98: the konrai blooms into anklet, golden coin, garland and more; look at it now, you and the others.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai uses the anklet-coin likeness to make the konrai unanswerable evidence that the season has come, and the venba expands that single likeness into a run of six ornament shapes and calls witnesses, so the flowering becomes a proof displayed to an audience."},{"a":"tn:99","b":"k:1261","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1261: my eyes have lost their lustre (வாளற்று) and my fingers are worn from counting the days since he left. Thinaimalai 99: like my lustre, my eyes have lost their light (வாள் இழந்த), my shoulders their beauty, my soft fingers worn with counting the days.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The venba keeps the couplet's two proofs of waiting, dim eyes and worn fingers, but sets them as a retort to a friend who called the grief idle (வாளா), so the Kural's private lament is turned into evidence in an argument.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:100","b":"kn:25","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 25: the flowers have opened; at the thought that the signs he set have come and he has not, the pallor has thickened on her. Thinaimalai 100: the rains appear in all quarters, his chariot does not, pallor appears and tears appear.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kar Narpathu's friend reports the pallor from outside as a result of a broken sign, while the Thinaimalai woman lays it out as one sequence of appearings that begins with his false word, so the same symptom is shown first as a diagnosis and then as an accusation."},{"a":"tn:100","b":"k:1185","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1185: look there, my lover goes; look here, the pallor spreads on my body. Thinaimalai 100: the rains appear, the lover's chariot does not, the pallor appears.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet times the pallor to his leaving and the venba times it to his failure to return, so the two together show pallor at both ends of the absence, first as its shadow and then as its proof.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:101","b":"k:1269","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1269: for those who long for the day of the far one's return, one day passes like seven. Thinaimalai 101: since the man of the decorated chariot went, only the days on which he does not come keep coming.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet stretches each day of waiting while the venba empties the calendar of the one day she waits for, so together they show waiting as both a lengthening of days and an endless supply of the wrong ones."},{"a":"tn:102","b":"kn:38","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 38: even in this cool season, when the bull elephant plays with his mate, he has not come; why sulk? Thinaimalai 102: the deer everywhere play with their does, and I, not judging his going a harm, have paid my life for it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In Kar Narpathu the friend uses the playing pair against the absent man and against the woman's sulk, while in Thinaimalai the woman reads the playing deer against herself, so the same sign of paired beasts can be turned to blame him or to blame her own consent."},{"a":"tn:103","b":"ku:148","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 148: if you say this is not the rainy season (காரன்று என்றி), is this a dream? Thinaimalai 103: you say the season has not come (பருவந்தான் பட்டின்றே என்றி); with tears I have cooked my life for it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both women quote the friend's denial of the season with the same என்றி, but the Kuruntokai woman answers with the evidence of the flowers and the Thinaimalai woman with the cost to her life, so the reply moves from disputing the fact to declaring the dispute too late."},{"a":"tn:103","b":"ku:382","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 382: the friend says the rain is unseasonable (வம்பு); if this were the true season, would he not come? Thinaimalai 103: to the friend who says the rain is unripe and the season not yet come, the woman says she has already wasted her life in tears.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai gives the friend's consolation in full and lets it stand, and the Thinaimalai venba shows it being received and failing, so the pair shows the stock argument of the false season from both the side that offers it and the side it does not reach."},{"a":"tn:104","b":"ku:66","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 66: the friend says the konrai are foolish (மடவ) to take the stray rain for the season and bloom. Thinaimalai 104: the woman says the konrai, blooming at the cloud's word, has killed her (கொன்றாய்).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"On the friend's reading the konrai's early flowering is a harmless mistake that proves nothing, and on the woman's it is a killing, so the same tree judged foolish in the consolation is judged guilty by the one it was meant to console."},{"a":"tn:106","b":"ku:290","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 290: do those who tell me to bear my love not know what it is, or are they so strong? Thinaimalai 106: who are they who tell the parted to bear it? They are so only for themselves, for the bees already sing in the mullai.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai answers the counsel to endure by doubting the counsellors' knowledge, while the venba adds the season's evidence against it, the bees already singing in joy, so the refusal to bear up gains in the later poem a reason drawn from the landscape."},{"a":"tn:107","b":"kn:11","type":"same_image","note":"Kar Narpathu 11: the kodal puts out clusters like the raised heads of snakes, and the scrub announces his quick return. Thinaimalai 107: the kodal, fearing the kaayaa as peacocks, shrinks, and the cloud like a five-headed snake falls on my head.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kar Narpathu uses the snake-headed kodal as a happy sign of his return, and Thinaimalai keeps the snake likeness but lets it run on into the logic of snake and peacock, so the same image that comforted the waiting woman becomes a fable of fear."},{"a":"tn:107","b":"ku:183","type":"same_image","note":"Kuruntokai 183: the great flowering branch of the kaayaa shows like a peacock's neck in the poor forest. Thinaimalai 107: the kodal fears the budding kaayaa as a flock of peacocks.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The kaayaa-peacock likeness that Kuruntokai offers as the poet's own comparison is in Thinaimalai mistaken by a flower in the scene, so the later poet makes a stock simile into an error committed inside the landscape."},{"a":"tn:109","b":"nt:246","type":"tension","note":"In Natrinai 246 the friend sees the golden konrai and the rumbling sky and says this is the season he named, so he will come; in 109 the heroine sees the same konrai under the sea-fed clouds and says he will not appear.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same golden konrai is read as proof of return by the friend and as proof of desertion by the heroine, which shows that the flower settles nothing by itself: it only fixes the day by which his failure becomes certain."},{"a":"tn:110","b":"k:1222","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1222 the parted woman pities the dim evening as a sufferer like herself; in 110 the hills weep in pity for her while the wild jasmine laughs.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural lets the woman lend her grief to one hour of the day, while the quatrain divides the whole landscape into those who mock her and those who mourn with her, so the pathetic projection becomes a judgment on which things are great."},{"a":"tn:111","b":"kn:4","type":"tension","note":"Kar Narpathu 4: peacocks like dancing women, the konrai in bloom and humming bees make this season the medicine for her pallor, says the friend; 111: the forest as a stage, the bees as lute and the peacock dancing make the heroine ask how to survive.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set side by side, the two show that the rainy-season pageant had two fixed readings, the friend's cure and the heroine's ordeal, and that the dancing peacock and humming bees are the same evidence in both: what differs is whether one trusts the promise."},{"a":"tn:111","b":"a:82","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 82 makes the hill a stage with bamboo flute, waterfall drum, bee lute and peacock dancer, ending in the heroine alone with tear-filled eyes; 111 rebuilds the stage in the rain-forest with the glory lily's hands, the sky's call and the bee lute.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Moved from the kurinji hill to the mullai forest, the nature-theatre keeps its function of tormenting the lone woman but changes its occasion, from the lover seen by many in secret love to the season of a husband's promised return.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:112","b":"k:1228","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1228: the herdsman's flute, herald of the burning evening, kills like a weapon; 112: at sunset the sweet flute turns hot, and the friend answers that he will not fail and they must stand ready to receive him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural stops at the killing flute, while the quatrain gives the friend's reply to that very hour, turning the evening's heat into the household's readiness for a guest, so the flute is shown to be a test of trust as well as a pain."},{"a":"tn:114","b":"ku:66","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 66: the konrai is foolish, blooming on a stray rain before the season he named; 114: the kurundham blooms before the clouds have even shed their drops, and the heroine says it has fixed the season and decreed her grief.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The premature bloom that the Kuruntokai friend waves away as a plant's mistake is, to the heroine of 114, a verdict, which shows that the consolation of untimely rain only works on someone who does not take the flower as the sign itself.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:116","b":"kn:21","type":"qualifies","note":"Kar Narpathu 21: the returning man sees the small mullai buds pointed like the girl's teeth; 116: the friend tells the mullai and its companions that their tooth-white buds have made the girl pale.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The bud that looks like her teeth is a pleasure on the road home and a wound in the waiting house, so the image's sense depends on whether the man is coming: the same likeness that recalls her smile to him drains her colour while he is away."},{"a":"tn:117","b":"kn:10","type":"qualifies","note":"Kar Narpathu 10: the thunder roars and the herd bull bellows back at it, and the chariot comes so her body will freshen; 117: the thunder rages, the rutting elephant trumpets back and the cobra hides, and her shoulders pale.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In both the beast answering the thunder marks the height of the rains, and the pair shows that this moment is the hinge of the mullai wait: with the chariot heard it brings her colour back, and without it the same roar is when the pallor sets in."},{"a":"tn:118","b":"ku:382","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 382: the friend says the rain that opened the mullai is untimely, for if this were the season he would have come; 118: the heroine says the forest has kept its season and rejects the friend's 'it is not the season'.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kuruntokai gives the friend's argument from his absence to the season's falseness, and 118 gives the heroine's refusal of it from the forest's evidence, so read together they show the untimely-rain consolation as one move in a dispute the heroine could win."},{"a":"tn:119","b":"kn:11","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 11: the kodal putting out clusters like snakes' raised heads announces his swift return; 119: the kodal has gone from ladle to snake's hood to finger, bangle and parasol and has withered.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kar Narpathu stops the kodal at its snake-hood stage, where it is a promise, while 119 follows the same flower to its withering, so together they turn the glory lily into a clock whose hood marks the hope and whose wilting marks the broken word."},{"a":"tn:121","b":"k:1225","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1225: the parted woman asks what enmity she did to evening; 121: she says not one foe but many, the anril, the evening, the rain, the flute, the sea and the dark cloud, have made her their living.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural names evening as the single enemy of the parted woman, and the quatrain multiplies the enemies across landscapes, showing that the evening of the Kural was the type of a whole hostile world of sights and sounds, each taking its share of her."},{"a":"tn:122","b":"kn:14","type":"tension","note":"Kar Narpathu 14: the cool cloud laughs softly as the mullai shows its teeth, and the friend is sure he will come; 122: the sky seems to laugh with its lightning and the heroine's shameless life breaks.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The laughing sky is a good omen in the friend's mouth and mockery in the heroine's, which shows that the same rain-cloud laughter could be heard as welcome or as scorn and that the stock image carried no fixed meaning apart from the speaker."},{"a":"tn:123","b":"k:319","type":"develops","note":"Kural 319: harm done to others in the forenoon returns of itself in the afternoon; 123: what is done in this life bears fruit in this life, as the women who tormented us have now been burned.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's impersonal rule that harm returns soon is here voiced by an injured wife about her rivals, showing how the ethical maxim of quick retribution was taken into akam as a scorned woman's satisfaction rather than a warning to oneself."},{"a":"tn:123","b":"n:85","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 85 says men who forced themselves on other men's wives in a former birth are born eunuchs in this one; 123 says those who think deeds bear fruit in the next birth do not understand, since the fruit comes here.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar defers the reckoning to a later birth while the quatrain insists on this one, so the pair shows a live disagreement in the didactic age about where retribution is seen, with the love poet choosing the visible, present punishment."},{"a":"tn:124","b":"ai:66","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 66: the wife asks who the woman is who snatched away his coming home to his rich house when he was thinking of his toddling son with his little chariot (தேரொடு தளர்நடைப் புதல்வனை). Thinaimalai 124: before the boy appears drawing his toy chariot this way, the man has already bathed and embraced her 'sisters'.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru 66 grants that the toddler with his chariot did pull the father toward home and blames a rival for stopping him, while the venba times the man's straying 'before the boy appears' and so denies the child ever had that pull, which shows the toy-chariot son working as the conventional test of a straying father's heart, passed in one poem and failed in the other."},{"a":"tn:139","b":"ai:403","type":"tension","note":"Ainkurunuru 403: watching the boy, who bears his grandfather's name, pull his little chariot with tottering steps, the man's heart grows great toward the son as well as the wife. Thinaimalai 139: the wife names the field town only as the town of the father of the boy whose chariot does not run (ஊராத்தேரான் தந்தை). On the reading that 139 speaks during the man's absence, the two claim opposite effects for the same sight.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"In the mullai poem the son's toy chariot binds the father to the house, while the marutham wife uses the same child to name a man who is not there, so the pair shows the son as the household's strongest tie and, when that tie fails, the wife's way of addressing the father without speaking to him."},{"a":"tn:138","b":"kl:81","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 81: the boy moves his toy chariot by hand in the yard (கால் வல் தேர் கையின் இயக்க), the father is caught coming from another woman, and the wife ends by going to the boy like a cow to the place where its calf is tied (கன்று யாத்த உழிச் செல்லும் ஆ போல்). Thinaimalai 138: in this boy's father's town the buffalo's milk runs as she thinks of her calf.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Kalittokai 81 gives the cow's pull to its calf to the mother as an open simile, while Thinaimalai 138 leaves the same yearning beast unglossed in the father's own fields, so the pair shows how the marutham poets could turn one image of parental pull from praise of the mother into a silent reproach of the father."},{"a":"tn:131","b":"ai:43","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 43: the woman tells the man of the rich town that his bard is a greater liar than he and swears many oaths (பாணன் பொய்யன் பல்சூ ளினனே). Thinaimalai 131: she tells the bard, whose lute is bent like a prawn, not to fashion words of reconciliation, since she has heard the man's lies (ஊரன்பொய்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru 43 ranks the bard's lying above the man's in a single comparison, while Thinaimalai 131 dates her refusal from the day she heard the man's own lie and so sets the bard's words aside as useless rather than worse, which shows the go-between's credit in marutham falling with his master's and not on its own."},{"a":"tn:134","b":"k:801","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 801 defines old friendship (பழைமை) as friendship that does not cut down the liberties of intimacy (கிழமை). Thinaimalai 134 uses both words (கிழமை, பழமை) as two alternative reasons why she, the humble one, received the man's grace when the many women he took did not.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states as a definition that old intimacy survives the liberties a friend takes, while the wife applies the same two terms to a husband who has taken many women and cannot decide whether his return came from enduring character or from reckoning the use of old ties, so the pair shows the friendship ethic carried into marriage and the doubt it leaves when the one taking liberties is the stronger party.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:152","b":"ai:65","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 65: the wife tells the lord not to embrace her body that has borne a son, since it would spoil his chest. Thinaimalai 152: do not embrace me; our son's wet mouth would wipe off the marks of breasts, jewels and leaf-paint on your chest, and my milk would spoil your garland.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Ainkurunuru's mother refuses the embrace by pretending to protect his chest from her post-partum body; the Thinaimalai keeps that pretext and names what is on the chest, another woman's marks, so the pairing shows the old formula of 'spoiling' was an accusation waiting to be made explicit."},{"a":"tn:152","b":"a:26","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 26: the wife recalls that lately her husband held back from embracing her for fear of spilling the milk meant for their son. Thinaimalai 152: the wife herself refuses his embrace, saying her milk would spoil his garland.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In the Akananuru it is the husband who fears the milk and the wife who is hurt by his caution; in the Thinaimalai the wife takes up the same fear as her weapon, so together they show the nursing mother's milk standing on either side of the quarrel, as his excuse or as her refusal.","gr":3},{"a":"tn:152","b":"k:1311","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1311: all women feast on you in common with their eyes, philanderer; I will not come near your chest. Thinaimalai 152: the wife refuses her husband's marked chest under cover of the son's drool and her milk.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's wife refuses the chest openly and for the least offence, being looked at; the Thinaimalai's wife has proof printed on the chest and still refuses only by indirection, so the couplet's open charge and the quatrain's domestic pretext mark two registers of one refusal, the second keeping the house's peace while making the charge."},{"a":"tn:153","b":"k:1306","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1306: without துனி and புலவி, love is like over-ripe and unripe fruit. Thinaimalai 153: in துனி, புலவி and ஊடல், and in the கனி union after, her eyes never see the lord.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural justifies the graded quarrel as what keeps love ripe, while the Thinaimalai uses the same three words from inside the cycle and finds that none of its stages, quarrel or ripe union, lets her look at him, so the pair sets the doctrine of the quarrel as seasoning against its cost to the one who lives it."},{"a":"tn:153","b":"k:1285","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1285: as the eye does not see the brush that lines it, I do not see his fault when I see him. Thinaimalai 153: my eyes never see the lord, in sulk, in union, or when he leaves in anger.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural finds one moment when the eye cannot see, nearness blinding it to his fault; the Thinaimalai extends that blindness to every phase of the marutham quarrel and to the man himself, so the couplet's single paradox becomes a whole cycle in which closeness and distance alike keep him out of view.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:145","b":"nt:250","type":"qualifies","note":"Natrinai 250: the man, come home with his son, tells the bard how his wife, seeing him approach, shrank away like a startled doe asking 'who are you?'. Thinaimalai 145: amid songs, tunes and horns he came carrying his son to our place like Indra.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Thinaimalai gives the son-bearing return from outside, as a procession fit for Indra, and the Natrinai gives it from the man's side, where the same entry ends in his wife's recoil, so together they show that the son as passport was a public spectacle whose private outcome the poets left open."},{"a":"tn:147","b":"a:46","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 46: the red-eyed buffalo breaks its tether, raids the pond, scatters the fish and eats the lotus, and the friend says he has brought another woman home. Thinaimalai 147: the red-eyed black-horned buffalo plunges into the field pond and comes back crowned with lilies and kayal fish and carrying frogs.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Akananuru states the situation the raiding buffalo stands for, while the Thinaimalai keeps only the image and adds the buffalo coming home dressed in the pond's spoils, which shows the image had become a sign of the straying husband that a later poet could leave unexplained and even play for comedy.","gr":2},{"a":"tn:148","b":"ai:95","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 95: the black-horned buffalo snaps its rope and grazes the long-eared paddy all morning; its lord gives her pain even by day. Thinaimalai 148: the dark-horned buffalo grazes the great field pond, then lows remembering its calf and comes home wearing paddy straw.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"The Ainkurunuru's straying buffalo is left grazing, as the husband is left away, while the Thinaimalai brings the same animal home and gives the calf as the reason, so the pairing shows the marutham buffalo could carry the wife's hope as well as her complaint: the child that draws the stray back."},{"a":"tn:151","b":"nt:380","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 380: the friend refuses the bard, saying they are soiled with ghee and smoke and smell of the new-born son, no match for the decked women of his master's street. Thinaimalai 151: the wife tells the bard her son plays in the dust before this house, not as other women's do, and his master may go and play with 'our younger sisters'.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both refuse the bard by setting the soiled household against the decked rivals, but the Natrinai turns the grime on the women of the house as mock unfitness, while the Thinaimalai turns it on the son and calls the rivals 'our younger sisters', so the same refusal could be humble or proud depending on whom the dirt is laid on.","gr":2},{"a":"ti:1","b":"a:318","type":"develops","note":"Both are the friend forbidding the night visit by a path where elephants go; Akananuru 318 adds 'மன்றல் வேண்டினும் பெறுகுவை', you may have the wedding if you want it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The four-line verse stops at the prohibition, and the long poem shows what the prohibition is for: shutting the night road is the friend's way of pressing for marriage, which Thinaimozhi 1 leaves unsaid.","gr":2},{"a":"ti:2","b":"a:28","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 28 warns that the millet ears are bowing and stalks are showing, so the girl may soon be replaced at the field; Thinaimozhi 2 says the guarding of the fields has ended.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in order the two poems give the harvest as the clock of the secret love, a warning while the ears ripen and the closed field once they are cut, so the end of the field-watch is the end of their meetings."},{"a":"ti:10","b":"a:28","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 28 the mother would replace the girl at the field if she failed to scare the parrots; in Thinaimozhi 10 the mother bars her from the field because she has learnt of the union.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows two routes to the same confinement, neglect of the field-watch and the mother's knowledge, which makes clear that the millet field is the one place the girl is allowed out, and losing it is losing him."},{"a":"ti:6","b":"ti:8","type":"develops","note":"Both answer the man with the same self-naming, குறவர் மகளிரேம், we are daughters of the hill folk; 6 adds that their kin would rage, 8 that no wounded tusker came this way.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The repeated formula works as a fence: in 6 it states the kin's threat outright, and in 8 the same words deflect his hunting pretext, showing that naming the tribe is itself the friend's refusal."},{"a":"ti:7","b":"a:82","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 82 makes the hill a stage where the waterfall is the drum, the bamboo wind the flute and the bees the lute, with a peacock dancing; Thinaimozhi 7 compresses it to one line, the cascade sounding as lute, flute and drum.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In both the orchestra of the hill sets up an audience: in Akananuru 82 many saw him at the field, and in Thinaimozhi 7 the night visit will be known to the whole town, so the stage image carries the danger of the love being seen and heard."},{"a":"ti:7","b":"k:1143","type":"tension","note":"Thinaimozhi 7 uses ஊரறி கௌவை, gossip the town knows, as the harm that a night visit will bring; Kural 1143 has the man welcome the ஊரறிந்த கெளவை as a foretaste of having her. On a plain reading the town's gossip is a threat in one and a gain in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same phrase is a warning in the friend's mouth and a prize in the lover's, so the Kural's couplet reads as his reply to exactly the argument the friend uses to keep him away."},{"a":"ti:8","b":"a:388","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 388 the young hunter asks the girls on the millet platform whether a tusker wounded by his arrow has passed by their field; in Thinaimozhi 8 the friend answers that no bleeding tusker came this way.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The long poem gives the pretext question and the short one gives only the reply, which a reader can only read as a reply because the question is a fixed form; together they show the wounded-elephant question as a standard opening the girls know how to parry."},{"a":"ti:9","b":"k:1265","type":"develops","note":"Both are the woman saying that his coming will take the pallor from her shoulders: Kural 1265, after she has seen her husband with full eyes; Thinaimozhi 9, if the hill lord comes in this vengai evening.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the cure as a rule in the wife's mouth, while the earlier stage in Thinaimozhi ties it to the flowering vengai, the season sign of weddings, so the coming that cures the pallor is, before marriage, the wedding itself."},{"a":"ti:12","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1151, the friend (for Parimelazhagar) to the departing man: tell me if you are not going; tell your quick return to those still alive then. Thinaimozhi 12, the friend to the man leaving for wealth: who can know her helplessness better than you?","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Holding them together shows two moves in the friend's attempt to stop the journey: the couplet threatens with the woman's death, while the venba appeals to the man's own knowledge of her, making him the witness who cannot claim ignorance."},{"a":"ti:13","b":"a:229","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 229: the girl answers her friend that the man who promised to come at once has not come even in young spring, when the koel calls on the budding branch. Thinaimozhi 13: the flowering season has come and the koel has made all his sworn words false.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Akananuru lets the koel mark the date the promise failed, while the venba makes the koel the agent that turns his oath into a lie, so the four-line form compresses the long poem's reasoning into a single accusation aimed at the bird instead of the man."},{"a":"ti:14","b":"k:1264","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1264: thinking of the return of the man who left the love they shared, her heart climbs up as on branches. Thinaimozhi 14: though he left for wealth even after seeing the koel's season, her heart watches for his coming.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's heart rising in hope of his return gains in the venba a bitter premise, that he chose to leave in the very season of love, so the longing is shown to persist against her knowledge of his choice, not out of ignorance of it."},{"a":"ti:15","b":"a:375","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 375: the girl tells her friend her eyes would not weep if she knew the man who crossed the wasteland of killers would return unharmed. Thinaimozhi 15: she asks her friend whether the man who gave up grace for wealth halts on the long waste where the hawk attacks the dove.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In Akananuru 375 fear for his safety is the whole cause of her tears, but the venba puts that fear in the same sentence as the charge that he abandoned grace, showing that the heroine's worry for him survives her own accusation against him."},{"a":"ti:16","b":"nt:9","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 9: the eloping man tells the white-toothed girl to go without strain, lingering at every shade, since the road they take (யாம் செல்லும் ஆறே) runs through cool groves where the koel calls. Thinaimozhi 16: he tells the girl with budding teeth that the place they go (நாம் செல்லும் இடம்) is to be desired, as maraa blooms and bees hum.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both men answer the girl's fear of the waste by describing its blossoms, which shows a fixed speech of the eloping lover; the venba's own turn is to have the bees hum the paalai melody, turning the name of the dreaded land into the music that leads her across it."},{"a":"ti:16","b":"a:355","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 355: in spring bees hum on the budding branches like the strings of a paalai lute touched by a master, the season in which the heroine fears her man's going. Thinaimozhi 16: bees hum the paalai melody as the man leads the girl away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same sound of bees playing the paalai mode marks the spring of a parting in the Akananuru and the spring of an elopement in the venba, so the image does not carry a fixed mood but takes its grief or its promise from the uri it serves.","gr":4},{"a":"ti:17","b":"k:872","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 872: take on the enmity of ploughmen whose plough is the bow rather than of those whose plough is the word. Thinaimozhi 17: the woman fears for her man passing the hamlets of those who live by ploughing with the bow, while her shoulders slacken because he left without grace.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural ranks the bow-ploughmen as the lesser enemy, and the venba agrees in its own way: the bowmen are only a feared danger on his road, while the wound she actually bears, slackened shoulders, comes from his word, the promise and grace he withheld.","gr":5},{"a":"ti:18","b":"a:185","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 185: her bright bangles slip as the man crosses summer hills where seed-bearing bamboo bursts with the twang of warriors' bows. Thinaimozhi 18: on the road where sun-split bamboo sheds pearls, her bangles seem ready to fall.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the Akananuru the bursting bamboo is a sound of violence on his road, while the venba makes the same bursting shed pearls, a treasure dropped in the land where he seeks wealth, set against her bangles falling for nothing, so the image of the split bamboo is turned from danger to the waste of what is precious."},{"a":"ti:19","b":"k:1234","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1234: with her partner gone her shoulders lose their old beauty and fullness and the bangles slip. Thinaimozhi 19: the woman, picturing him halting in the stony waste where the doe cries with the stag, says her shoulders slip with her bangles.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the wasting shoulders as the plain sign of absence, and the venba supplies what she is thinking of while they waste: his fear on the road and the paired deer that keep together, so the symptom is tied to an imagined scene rather than to absence alone."},{"a":"ti:20","b":"nt:279","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 279: the mother asks whether the feet of her jewelled girl, her anklets removed, are weary on the sun-glittering track. Thinaimozhi 20: the mother asks a man on the road whether her daughter can bear to walk the harsh waste with a youth whose bow is like Death.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Natrinai's mother fears only the road and the girl's tender feet, but the venba's mother fears the companion too, calling him Death-like, which shows the mother's grief can doubt the very youth the girl trusted for protection."},{"a":"ti:21","b":"kn:8","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 8: the woodland where the kaayaa opens buds as if dipped in the collyrium of an eye announces the return of the man who went for fame. Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 21: with the collyrium-dark kaayaa in bloom, the lover has come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same collyrium-blue kaayaa that in the Kar Narpathu is still only a herald of the man becomes, in the Thinaimozhi verse, the flower already open when he arrives, so the mullai venbas use one stock blossom to mark both the promise and its keeping."},{"a":"ti:22","b":"k:1265","type":"tension","note":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 22: the friend asks what will become of the pallor now that the rain says he will come. Kural 1265: the woman says the pallor of her shoulders will leave only after she has seen her husband with full eyes; on a plain reading they disagree on whether news of his return can touch the pallor.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend treats the pallor as already beaten by the season's promise, while the couplet's woman refuses any cure short of seeing him, so the couplet sounds like the heroine's unspoken reply to the friend's teasing question."},{"a":"ti:23","b":"ku:380","type":"tension","note":"Both liken the thundering rain to the drums of kings (வேந்தர் ... முரசின்). Kuruntokai 380: the friend says their lover is in a far country, not before them; how shall they bear it? Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 23: the friend says he who went will come, and the rain will be greater tomorrow.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"One image, the sky beating like a king's drum, serves the friend once as proof that he is coming and once as a measure of how far away he still is, which shows that in the rains the friend's comfort is a choice of reading, not a fact the season settles."},{"a":"ti:24","b":"k:1154","type":"qualifies","note":"Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 24: the friend rests her comfort on the man who swore an oath (வஞ்சினம்) and held to it, with the rain as his token. Kural 1154: if he who said 'fear not' leaves, is there any fault in those who trusted his assuring word?","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend builds her comfort on his oath, while the couplet asks who is to blame if such an oath fails, so together they show that the whole mullai argument of waiting rests on trusting a man's word, and the Kural names the risk the friend leaves unsaid.","gr":3},{"a":"ti:25","b":"a:74","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 74: the heroine says she could hold firm on the friend's repeated comfort (he will see the doe with the stag in the rain-fresh scrub, think of you and come today, இன்றே வருவர்) if only the herdsmen's harsh little flute did not torment her at evening. Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 25: the friend points to the buck leaping with his doe and says he will come right now (இன்னே வருவர்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Thinaimozhi verse gives the friend's argument by itself, and the Akananuru heroine quotes that same argument and tells us where it breaks, at evening with the flute, so the four-line comfort is shown as one turn of a longer exchange in which it does not fully hold."},{"a":"ti:26","b":"ku:21","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 21: though the forest's konrai says it is the rainy season, the heroine will not believe it, for he does not lie. Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 26: the friend says the thundering rain is the time he named, fitting his word.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both trust the man's word, but they draw opposite conclusions from it: the friend reads the rain as his promise kept, the Kuruntokai woman reads his absence as proof the rain is not yet the true season, so the same faith can either confirm the season or deny it."},{"a":"ti:27","b":"k:1222","type":"tension","note":"Both name the bewildering evening (மருண்மாலை, மருள்மாலை). Kural 1222: the woman asks the sad-eyed evening whether its partner is as hard-hearted as hers. Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 27: the friend calls the darkening rainy evening the sign the man set for his return.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The same evening the Kural's woman mourns as a fellow sufferer abandoned like herself is presented by the friend as the very token of his return, which shows that the evening's meaning in love poetry depends on who is speaking and whether she still trusts him."},{"a":"ti:28","b":"kn:20","type":"same_image","note":"Both have the thunder (ஏறு) make the gem-bearing serpent cower (மணி நாகம் அனுங்க) as the rain-cloud goes. Kar Narpathu 20: the returning man tells his charioteer the king's work is done. Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 28: the friend tells the heroine the beauty spots will spread on her breasts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A near-identical half-line serves at both ends of the journey, in the returning man's mouth and in the waiting woman's friend's, which shows the serpent cowering at the thunder as a shared formula of the mullai rains rather than one poet's image."},{"a":"ti:30","b":"a:14","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 14: in the evening when good cows with swollen udders ooze sweet milk (தீம்பால் பிலிற்ற) and call to their calves, the wife asks the bard how she will be at dawn if he does not remember them; the bard then sees the chariot coming. Thinaimozhi Aimpathu 30: with the herds oozing sweet milk (தீம்பால் பிலிற்ற), the friend asks 'will our man come?'","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same phrase of cows streaming milk carries the question of his return in both poems, and the long Akananuru supplies the answer the venba leaves open, the chariot on the road, so the milk-heavy herd going home is a fixed cue that the man too must be on his way."},{"a":"ti:33","b":"kl:25","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 25 likens the woman's eyes, bangles and brow, bright while he is near and failing when he leaves, to the bond of base men who praise to the face and slander behind; Thinaimozhi 33 likens the man himself, who ate her youth and moved on, to the friendship of the base.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both poems borrow the moral figure of the false friend for a husband's desertion, but the Kalittokai sets it on the wife's changing body while the venba sets it squarely on the man, so the same ethical simile can either describe the damage or name the culprit."},{"a":"ti:33","b":"k:812","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 812 asks what it matters whether one gains or loses the friendship of those who are friends while there is profit and leave when there is none; Thinaimozhi 33 calls the husband's conduct exactly such a friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural can shrug at the self-serving friend because a friend can be dropped, but the wife cannot treat a husband with that indifference, since what he consumed was her youth, so the akam poem shows where the maxim's calm stops working."},{"a":"ti:34","b":"nt:200","type":"develops","note":"In Natrinai 200 the friend, with the bard listening, has the potter cry through the town that women should guard against this bard's lie-wrapped crooked words; in Thinaimozhi 34 the bard is found standing in the other women's street for his own gain.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together the two show the bard's double standing in marutham: the man's envoy at the wife's door is, to the town and the wife, a known agent of his affairs elsewhere, and the venba adds that he profits from it.","gr":2},{"a":"ti:35","b":"kl:74","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 74 opens with a striped bee that has fed on pond flowers and backwater neythal moving on to the field lotus, and says the man joins one woman at forenoon and seeks another by afternoon; Thinaimozhi 35 likens him openly to the bee that sips fresh flowers and leaves the women only their bodies.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"What the Kalittokai leaves as an opening scene to be matched with the man's day, the four-line venba states as an explicit simile with its result, the emptied body, showing the hidden image of the longer poem made plain in the later form."},{"a":"ti:36","b":"k:1183","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1183 the woman says he took her grace and modesty and gave her sickness and pallor in exchange; in Thinaimozhi 36 the friend tells the man that he both gave the beauty and took it back.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural's exchange is a loss suffered in separation, but the friend makes the same giving and taking an argument against the husband: since he alone gave and took, there is no one else to blame and nothing anyone can do to him, which turns the woman's lament into an accusation.","gr":2},{"a":"ti:37","b":"n:390","type":"develops","note":"In Naladiyar 390 the wife tells the bard not to lie that the man will be kind, since to him she is the tip of the sugarcane, and to tell it to those who are its middle joints; in Thinaimozhi 37 she tells the bard not to keep pressing, since she never had the gracious regard 'our younger sisters' have.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same refusal of the bard, by the same speaker, is filed by the Naladiyar under the chaste wife, so the didactic book reads her sharp tongue to the go-between as a mark of karpu, while the akam venba keeps it as wounded pride with the ironic family word for the rivals."},{"a":"ti:38","b":"kl:74","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kalittokai 74 the wife answers the bard who praises the man as not loveless by calling the bard, the trusting women, the man and his chariot all deluded; in Thinaimozhi 38 she stops the bard's praise of the man's goodness by pointing to the loosened hair his garland lay on.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both wives reject the bard's praise of the husband, but the Kalittokai argues the case at length while the venba answers with a single visible sign, so the shorter form shows the refusal resting on evidence rather than rebuttal."},{"a":"ti:39","b":"n:390","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 390 has the wife call herself the tasteless tip of the sugarcane to the lord of the town; Thinaimozhi 39 has her say she has become the chewed pith of the cane after he ate her beauty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The marutham wife reaches for sugarcane in both books, but the Naladiyar places her at the less sweet end of a cane still whole, a matter of rank among his women, while the Thinaimozhi has the cane already chewed and spat out, so the same image measures either neglect or use and discard."},{"a":"ti:39","b":"k:1078","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1078 says the worthy are of use at a word and the base, like sugarcane, only when crushed; Thinaimozhi 39 has the wife say she has become the crushed cane's refuse.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural looks at crushed cane from the side of the one who extracts the juice, as a way to get use from the mean, while the akam poem speaks from the cane's side, so the same image shows that what is crushed for its sweetness is left as waste."},{"a":"ti:41","b":"k:782","type":"tension","note":"Kural 782: the friendship of the worthy grows like the waxing moon. Thinaimozhi 41: the worthy man's bond has waned since the day she saw him, and his trysts proved false; on a plain reading the heroine's case contradicts the maxim.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The heroine's question ஐயகொல் ஆன்றார் தொடர்பு tests the Kural's rule against one man and finds it failing, so the akam poem uses the ethical maxim as a weapon: either the rule is wrong or he was never one of the worthy."},{"a":"ti:41","b":"nt:11","type":"qualifies","note":"Natrinai 11: the friend tells the heroine, wilting since the tryst he set failed (அவர் செய் குறி பிழைப்ப), to drop the resentful thought that he will not come, for his charioteer is driving along the moonlit shore sparing the crabs. Thinaimozhi 41 is that resentful thought spoken: his trysts have turned false (செய்த குறியும்பொய் யாயின).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together the two give the complaint and its answer in one neythal exchange: the venba lets the heroine conclude from the failed tryst that his bond is false, while the older poem shows the friend refusing that inference and offering his gentleness on the road as proof."},{"a":"ti:41","b":"ku:121","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 121: the heroine grants her friend that for the hill man's failure to keep the tryst (தான்குறி வாயாத் தப்பற்கு) her own shoulders have turned pale, as the branch goes down with the langur that missed its leap. Thinaimozhi 41: for the same failure she questions his nobility.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same failed tryst draws two answers from the heroine: in the Kuruntokai she takes the fault into her own body, while the later venba turns it outward into a charge against his worth, which shows accusation as a second, sharper register of the same situation."},{"a":"ti:47","b":"k:1234","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1234: with the partner gone, the shoulders lose their old beauty and fullness and the bright bangles slip. Thinaimozhi 47: the heroine asks her friend whether he has abandoned her, as pallor spreads and the bangles of her soft shoulders slip.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states the sign as a fact of separation, while the venba makes the heroine read it as a question, has he left me?, so the slipping bangle becomes the evidence by which she learns his desertion rather than its mere effect."},{"a":"ti:48","b":"a:260","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 260 lists the signs of evening on the neythal shore (the sun dimming, herons crying, the crab slipping into its hole, the waves falling quiet) before the heroine asks what will become of her. Thinaimozhi 48 gives a chariot cutting the sand, crabs scattering and the sun hurrying away as the evening comes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The long poem shows that the neythal evening was built as a catalogue of shore signs ending in the heroine's dread, which lets the four-line venba keep only three signs and the bare 'evening will come' and still carry that dread unstated."},{"a":"ti:49","b":"k:1081","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1081: on first seeing her the man asks whether she is a goddess, a peahen or a woman, and his heart reels. Thinaimozhi 49: the man asks whether she was a peacock, a young woman or a goddess of the sea waves, and his heart aches from her eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same three-way doubt in both shows the first-sight ஐயம் was a fixed form of kalavu, and the venba adds what the couplet leaves open: a place for the seeing, the njazhal grove, and a goddess native to that place, the one who lives in the waves."},{"a":"ti:50","b":"a:282","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 282: the father agrees to give the girl to the hill lord whose hunters bring ivory, gold, gems and pearls together, and the household prays for the wedding day. Thinaimozhi 50: the heroine asks whether the sea lord will come to the front yard where coral, pearls and crystal are heaped as if in praise.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 282 makes the suitor's treasure of the land the very thing that wins the father's consent, so beside it the shore's coral, pearls and crystal piled at the heroine's door read as the bride-wealth her question 'will he come?' is waiting for."}]});
